#: hall-1
#. Name of a hall. It must fit a board on a small phone. At most 16 characters.
msgctxt "hall.1.name"
msgid "Mechanics"
msgstr "기계 장치"

#: hall-1
#. Introduction at the top of the page of a hall.
msgctxt "hall.1.intro"
msgid "Here a pixel is an object that you could pick up. Every piece in this hall makes its picture by moving real things: flaps, discs, pins, tiles and drones."
msgstr "여기서는 화소가 손에 쥘 수 있는 물건입니다. 이 전시실의 전시품은 모두 플랩, 원판, 핀, 타일, 드론 같은 실제 물체를 움직여 영상을 만듭니다."

#: hall-2
#. Name of a hall. It must fit a board on a small phone. At most 16 characters.
msgctxt "hall.2.name"
msgid "Glass and glow"
msgstr "유리와 빛"

#: hall-2
#. Introduction at the top of the page of a hall.
msgctxt "hall.2.intro"
msgid "Every piece in this hall is sealed glass with something glowing inside. The glow comes from a beam of electrons, from a gas lit by electricity or from a hot wire. Each of these needs a vacuum or a gas filling to work."
msgstr "이 전시실의 전시품은 모두 안에서 무언가가 빛나는 밀봉된 유리입니다. 그 빛은 전자 빔이나, 전기로 밝힌 기체나, 달군 선에서 나옵니다. 어느 것이든 진공이나 채워 넣은 기체가 있어야 작동합니다."

#: hall-3
#. Name of a hall. It must fit a board on a small phone. At most 16 characters.
msgctxt "hall.3.name"
msgid "Solid light"
msgstr "고체의 빛"

#: hall-3
#. Introduction at the top of the page of a hall.
msgctxt "hall.3.intro"
msgid "Here the light comes straight out of a solid material. These displays need no vacuum, no gas and no hot wire, which is why they could become thin, cool and, in time, bendable."
msgstr "여기서는 빛이 고체 재료에서 곧바로 나옵니다. 이 디스플레이들에는 진공도, 기체도, 달군 선도 필요 없습니다. 그래서 얇고, 뜨겁지 않고, 마침내는 휘어질 수 있게 되었습니다."

#: hall-4
#. Name of a hall. It must fit a board on a small phone. At most 16 characters.
msgctxt "hall.4.name"
msgid "Light valves"
msgstr "라이트 밸브"

#: hall-4
#. Introduction at the top of the page of a hall.
msgctxt "hall.4.intro"
msgid "Nothing in this hall makes its own light. The sun, a lamp or a laser makes the light, and the device blocks it, tilts it or steers it to form a picture. A device that lets light through or blocks it is called a light valve."
msgstr "이 전시실에는 스스로 빛을 내는 것이 없습니다. 빛은 해나 램프나 레이저가 만들고, 장치는 그 빛을 막거나 기울이거나 방향을 틀어 영상을 만듭니다. 빛을 통과시키거나 막는 장치를 라이트 밸브라고 합니다."

#: hall-5
#. Name of a hall. It must fit a board on a small phone. At most 16 characters.
msgctxt "hall.5.name"
msgid "Paper"
msgstr "종이"

#: hall-5
#. Introduction at the top of the page of a hall.
msgctxt "hall.5.intro"
msgid "These displays behave like print. They use the light of the room and make none of their own, and most of them keep their picture with the power off. Switch the daylight off to see the cost: in the dark there is nothing to read."
msgstr "이 디스플레이들은 인쇄물처럼 행동합니다. 주변의 빛을 쓰고 스스로는 빛을 내지 않으며, 대부분 전원을 꺼도 영상이 남습니다. 햇빛을 꺼서 그 대가를 확인해 보세요. 어둠 속에서는 읽을 것이 없습니다."

#: hall-6
#. Name of a hall. It must fit a board on a small phone. At most 16 characters.
msgctxt "hall.6.name"
msgid "Mid-air"
msgstr "공중"

#: hall-6
#. Introduction at the top of the page of a hall.
msgctxt "hall.6.intro"
msgid "These pictures have no screen that you can point to. Some are stage tricks with glass and fog. Others put real points of light in empty space."
msgstr "이 영상들에는 손으로 가리킬 화면이 없습니다. 유리와 안개를 쓰는 무대 속임수도 있고, 빈 공간에 실제 빛의 점을 놓는 것도 있습니다."

#: hall-7
#. Name of a hall. It must fit a board on a small phone. At most 16 characters.
msgctxt "hall.7.name"
msgid "Depth"
msgstr "깊이"

#: hall-7
#. Introduction at the top of the page of a hall.
msgctxt "hall.7.intro"
msgid "You have two eyes, and depth needs a picture for each. Some of these pieces give each eye its own view. Others go further and rebuild the light that a real object would send out."
msgstr "눈은 두 개이고, 깊이를 느끼려면 눈마다 다른 그림이 필요합니다. 이 전시품 가운데 일부는 두 눈에 저마다의 그림을 보여 줍니다. 다른 것들은 더 나아가, 실제 물체가 내보냈을 빛을 다시 만들어 냅니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.name"
msgid "Split-flap board"
msgstr "스플릿 플랩 표시판"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.aka"
msgid "Solari board, Pragotron"
msgstr "솔라리 보드, 프라고트론"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.origin"
msgid "Solari di Udine, Italy. First moving sign at Liège station, Belgium"
msgstr "솔라리 디 우디네, 이탈리아. 최초의 가동식 표시판은 벨기에 리에주역"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.statusNote"
msgid "Mostly replaced by LED and flip-disc boards since the 1990s. A few stations keep theirs, and Solari still builds displays."
msgstr "1990년대부터 대부분 LED 표시판과 플립 디스크 표시판으로 바뀌었습니다. 몇몇 역은 아직 간직하고 있고, 솔라리는 지금도 표시 장치를 만듭니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.lead"
msgid "A drum of hinged flaps sits at every position on the board, and each character is split across two flaps."
msgstr "표시판의 자리마다 경첩으로 이어진 플랩의 드럼이 있고, 글자 하나는 두 장의 플랩에 나뉘어 있습니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.how.1"
msgid "Every position on the board is a small machine of its own. A motor turns a drum of thin flaps, one step at a time, until the wanted flap falls forward. The top half of the character is then on the flap that stayed up, and the bottom half is on the flap that dropped."
msgstr "표시판의 자리 하나하나가 독립된 작은 기계입니다. 모터가 얇은 플랩의 드럼을 한 칸씩 돌려, 원하는 플랩이 앞으로 떨어질 때까지 계속합니다. 그러면 글자의 위쪽 절반은 서 있는 플랩에, 아래쪽 절반은 방금 떨어진 플랩에 놓입니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.how.2"
msgid "Only gravity and a stop hold each flap, so a board keeps its text with the power off. A row clatters when it changes. Stations came to rely on that side effect, because people looked up when they heard it."
msgstr "플랩을 붙잡는 것은 중력과 멈춤쇠뿐이어서, 전원을 꺼도 글자가 남습니다. 한 줄이 바뀔 때는 달그락거리는 소리가 납니다. 사람들이 그 소리를 듣고 고개를 들었기 때문에, 역에서는 이 부수 효과에 기대게 되었습니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.good.1"
msgid "Is as easy to read as print, even in full daylight and across a station hall"
msgstr "한낮의 햇빛 속에서도, 역 대합실 건너편에서도 인쇄물만큼 읽기 쉽다"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.good.2"
msgid "Holds its message with no power at all"
msgstr "전력을 전혀 쓰지 않고 내용을 유지한다"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.good.3"
msgid "Announces every change by its sound"
msgstr "바뀔 때마다 소리로 알린다"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.bad.1"
msgid "Shows only what is printed on its flaps"
msgstr "플랩에 인쇄된 것만 보여 줄 수 있다"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.bad.2"
msgid "Takes seconds, not milliseconds, to change a row"
msgstr "한 줄을 바꾸는 데 밀리초가 아니라 초 단위의 시간이 걸린다"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.bad.3"
msgid "Has moving parts that wear and need maintenance by hand"
msgstr "닳는 가동 부품이 있어 손으로 정비해야 한다"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.look"
msgid "Look down into the open top of the case. On each drum, the waiting flaps fan out behind the two that face you. The last two positions count seconds, so one flap falls every second."
msgstr "위가 열린 케이스 안을 위에서 내려다보세요. 드럼마다, 이쪽을 향한 두 장 뒤로 차례를 기다리는 플랩들이 부채처럼 펼쳐져 있습니다. 마지막 두 자리는 초를 세기 때문에, 1초마다 플랩이 한 장씩 떨어집니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.fact"
msgid "When Boston's transit authority planned an electronic board to replace the flap board at South Station, it chose to keep the noise. The new board was to play the old ticking sound at every change."
msgstr "보스턴 교통 당국은 사우스역의 플랩 표시판을 전자식으로 바꾸려고 계획하면서, 그 소리는 남기기로 했습니다. 새 표시판은 바뀔 때마다 예전의 째깍거리는 소리를 틀어 주게 되어 있었습니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. People section: what Gino Valle did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.people.1"
msgid "Designer credited with the form of the boards made by Solari."
msgstr "솔라리가 만든 표시판의 형태를 디자인한 것으로 알려진 디자이너."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. People section: what John Myer did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.people.2"
msgid "Belgian inventor credited, with Valle, for the Solari board."
msgstr "발레와 함께 솔라리 표시판을 만든 것으로 알려진 벨기에 발명가."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Note under the People section.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.note"
msgid "No inventor is recorded for the mechanism itself. The earliest board on record went up at Liège station in 1956."
msgstr "기구 자체의 발명자는 기록에 없습니다. 기록에 남은 가장 오래된 표시판은 1956년 리에주역에 설치되었습니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.view.hall.label"
msgid "Wait on the concourse"
msgstr "대합실에서 기다리기"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.view.hall.hint"
msgid "People waiting learned to look up at the clatter. Here one leaf falls every second."
msgstr "기다리던 사람들은 달그락 소리에 고개를 드는 법을 배웠습니다. 여기서는 1초에 한 장씩 플랩이 떨어집니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.view.flaps.label"
msgid "Go close to the leaves"
msgstr "플랩 가까이 가기"

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.view.flaps.hint"
msgid "Behind each numeral stands a sheaf of waiting leaves, hinged on pins round a drum. Two pips hold the top one up."
msgstr "숫자마다 뒤에는 차례를 기다리는 플랩 묶음이 있고, 드럼 둘레의 핀에 경첩으로 걸려 있습니다. 작은 돌기 두 개가 맨 위의 한 장을 받칩니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.media.board-updating.caption"
msgid "A departure board at a German railway station, filmed in 2005. Rows of leaves turn over as the list of trains is brought up to date."
msgstr "독일 기차역의 출발 안내 표시판. 2005년 촬영. 열차 목록이 갱신되면서 플랩의 줄이 차례로 넘어갑니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.media.board-updating.alt"
msgid "A large dark board headed Abfahrt, Departure, with white lettering in many rows. Characters blur and change in several rows at once."
msgstr "Abfahrt(출발)라는 제목이 붙은 크고 어두운 표시판. 흰 글자가 여러 줄로 늘어서 있고, 여러 줄에서 동시에 글자가 흐려지며 바뀝니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.media.mechanism.caption"
msgid "The works of a split-flap alarm clock, taken out of its case. A motor at the back turns the wheels of leaves, and the inset shows the metal tab that holds back the top leaf."
msgstr "케이스에서 꺼낸 플랩식 자명종의 기구. 뒤쪽의 모터가 플랩 바퀴를 돌리고, 둥근 확대 그림은 맨 위의 플랩을 붙잡는 금속 걸쇠를 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.media.mechanism.alt"
msgid "A metal frame holding gears, two knobs and wheels of black leaves that read 11 00 in white. A round inset enlarges one corner of a leaf."
msgstr "톱니바퀴와 손잡이 두 개, 검은 플랩 바퀴가 들어 있는 금속 틀. 플랩은 흰 글자로 11 00을 나타냅니다. 둥근 확대 그림이 플랩 한 장의 모서리를 크게 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.media.departures.caption"
msgid "A split-flap departure board for the New Haven Line, photographed at Grand Central in 2014. Each row gives a time, a track, a destination and remarks."
msgstr "뉴헤이븐 노선의 스플릿 플랩 출발 안내판. 2014년 그랜드 센트럴역에서 촬영. 줄마다 시각, 승강장, 행선지, 비고가 나옵니다."

#: hall-1/split-flap
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.split-flap.media.departures.alt"
msgid "A wide black board under a stone wall, headed New Haven Line Departures, with five rows of white capital letters and numbers."
msgstr "돌벽 아래에 걸린 넓고 검은 표시판. New Haven Line Departures라는 제목이 있고, 흰 대문자와 숫자가 다섯 줄로 늘어서 있습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.name"
msgid "Flip-disc display"
msgstr "플립 디스크 표시판"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.aka"
msgid "Flip-dot"
msgstr "플립 도트"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.origin"
msgid "Kenyon Taylor, Ferranti-Packard, Canada, for Trans-Canada Air Lines"
msgstr "케니언 테일러, 페란티 패커드, 캐나다. 트랜스 캐나다 항공을 위해 만들었습니다"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.statusNote"
msgid "LED signs have replaced it in new installations. Flip-disc boards remain in service, and some are hybrids with an LED in every dot."
msgstr "새 설비에서는 LED 표시판이 그 자리를 차지했습니다. 플립 디스크 표시판은 지금도 쓰이고 있으며, 점마다 LED를 넣은 혼합형도 있습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.lead"
msgid "A pulse of magnetism flips each small disc on the board to show its bright face or its dark face."
msgstr "자기 펄스가 표시판의 작은 원판을 하나씩 뒤집어, 밝은 면이나 어두운 면을 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.how.1"
msgid "Each disc turns on an axle and carries a small magnet. A short pulse of current through a coil underneath flips the disc to show its other face. The disc then stays in place with no current, so the sign uses power only while it changes."
msgstr "원판은 저마다 축을 중심으로 돌고, 작은 자석을 달고 있습니다. 아래에 있는 코일에 짧은 전류 펄스를 흘리면 원판이 뒤집혀 반대쪽 면을 보여 줍니다. 그 뒤로는 전류 없이도 제자리에 머무르므로, 표시판은 바뀌는 동안에만 전력을 씁니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.how.2"
msgid "The first big order was for the Montreal Stock Exchange. By 1977, roughly half of the major stock exchanges in the world used the system. It was later used for destination signs on buses, for highway signs and on television game shows."
msgstr "첫 대량 주문은 몬트리올 증권거래소에서 왔습니다. 1977년에는 세계 주요 증권거래소의 절반가량이 이 방식을 쓰고 있었습니다. 나중에는 버스의 행선지 표시, 고속도로 안내판, 텔레비전 퀴즈 프로그램에도 쓰였습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.good.1"
msgid "Is easy to read in direct sun, because it reflects light and makes none"
msgstr "빛을 반사할 뿐 스스로 내지 않아서, 직사광선 아래에서도 읽기 쉽다"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.good.2"
msgid "Uses power only when the picture changes"
msgstr "그림이 바뀔 때에만 전력을 쓴다"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.good.3"
msgid "Is simple and rugged enough for buses and roadsides"
msgstr "버스와 도로변에서 쓸 만큼 단순하고 튼튼하다"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.bad.1"
msgid "Shows each dot in one colour or the other, with nothing in between"
msgstr "점마다 두 색 중 하나만 보여 주고, 중간이 없다"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.bad.2"
msgid "Is invisible at night unless a lamp lights it"
msgstr "밤에는 램프로 비추지 않으면 보이지 않는다"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.bad.3"
msgid "Discs can stick, and a stuck disc stays a wrong pixel until someone repairs it"
msgstr "원판이 걸릴 수 있고, 걸린 원판은 누군가 고칠 때까지 틀린 화소로 남는다"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.look"
msgid "The bottom row counts the seconds and fills one disc at a time. Watch the columns flip one after another when the minute changes. Then switch the daylight off: the board still shows the time, but you cannot see it."
msgstr "맨 아래 줄은 초를 세며 원판이 하나씩 채워집니다. 분이 바뀔 때 세로줄이 차례로 뒤집히는 모습을 보세요. 그런 다음 햇빛을 끄세요. 표시판은 여전히 시각을 나타내지만, 눈에는 보이지 않습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.fact"
msgid "Some highway signs had discs faced with the same retroreflective coating as an ordinary road sign. At night their message was lit by the headlamps of the cars driving towards it."
msgstr "어떤 고속도로 안내판은 일반 도로 표지와 같은 재귀반사 재료를 원판에 입혔습니다. 밤에는 다가오는 자동차의 전조등이 글자를 비추었습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. People section: what Kenyon Taylor did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.people.1"
msgid "Devised the flip-disc at Ferranti-Packard in Canada, for Trans-Canada Air Lines."
msgstr "캐나다의 페란티 패커드에서, 트랜스 캐나다 항공을 위해 플립 디스크를 고안했습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.view.kerb.label"
msgid "Wait at the stop"
msgstr "정류장에서 기다리기"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.view.kerb.hint"
msgid "As on the front of a bus. The board reflects daylight and makes none, and the bottom row fills a disc a second."
msgstr "버스 앞면에 달린 것과 같습니다. 표시판은 햇빛을 반사할 뿐 스스로 빛을 내지 않고, 맨 아래 줄은 1초에 원판 하나씩 채워집니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.view.discs.label"
msgid "Go close to the discs"
msgstr "원판 가까이 가기"

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.view.discs.hint"
msgid "Each disc sits on an axle. A pulse through the copper coils under it kicks it over, and it stays with no current."
msgstr "원판은 저마다 축 위에 놓여 있습니다. 아래의 구리 코일에 펄스를 흘리면 뒤집히고, 전류 없이 그대로 머뭅니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.media.slow-motion.caption"
msgid "A flip-disc display filmed in slow motion from a low angle. Discs turn over one after another, and the edge between the pale faces and the dark ones moves across the board."
msgstr "낮은 각도에서 느린 화면으로 촬영한 플립 디스크 표시판. 원판이 차례로 뒤집히고, 밝은 면과 어두운 면의 경계가 표시판 위를 지나갑니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.media.slow-motion.alt"
msgid "Oblique close view along a grid of square cells. Pale discs fill one side and dark ones the other, and single discs flick over at the edge between them."
msgstr "네모난 칸이 늘어선 격자를 비스듬히 가까이에서 본 모습. 한쪽은 밝은 원판, 반대쪽은 어두운 원판이고, 그 경계에서 원판이 하나씩 뒤집힙니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.media.row.caption"
msgid "A row of seven flip dots. Five show their yellow face, one has turned to black, and the disc of the seventh has been lifted out and lies above the row."
msgstr "플립 도트 일곱 개가 늘어선 줄. 다섯 개는 노란 면을 보이고, 하나는 검은색으로 뒤집혔으며, 일곱 번째는 원판을 빼서 줄 위에 올려놓았습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.media.row.alt"
msgid "Seven black plastic holders in a line on a white ground, each on two green coils with wire legs. Yellow discs sit in five, a black one in the last."
msgstr "흰 바닥 위에 한 줄로 놓인 검은 플라스틱 받침 일곱 개. 저마다 철사 다리가 달린 초록 코일 두 개 위에 얹혀 있습니다. 다섯 개에는 노란 원판이, 마지막 하나에는 검은 원판이 들어 있습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.media.coils.caption"
msgid "A flip-dot matrix seen edge on. Copper coils stand on the circuit board beneath the white discs, a set for every dot."
msgstr "옆에서 본 플립 도트 매트릭스. 흰 원판 아래 회로 기판 위에, 점마다 한 벌씩 구리 코일이 서 있습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.media.coils.alt"
msgid "Side view of a green circuit board carrying rows of small copper wire coils on posts, with flat white discs resting on black frames above them."
msgstr "초록 회로 기판을 옆에서 본 모습. 기둥에 감은 작은 구리선 코일이 여러 줄로 늘어서 있고, 그 위의 검은 틀에 납작한 흰 원판이 놓여 있습니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "From Maurice Taylor's patent of 1964 for a magnetically operated sign. A panel of round openings holds one disc in each. The cut-away shows a disc on its suspension, the magnet it carries and the coil that turns it."
msgstr "자기로 작동하는 표시판에 관한 모리스 테일러의 1964년 특허에서. 둥근 구멍이 뚫린 판의 구멍마다 원판이 하나씩 들어 있습니다. 단면도는 걸이에 매달린 원판과, 원판에 달린 자석과, 그것을 돌리는 코일을 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-1/flip-disc
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.flip-disc.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a board with rows of round openings, a cut-away of one disc with a magnet beside a coil, and a grid of light and dark dots."
msgstr "선으로 그린 그림: 둥근 구멍이 여러 줄로 뚫린 판, 코일 옆에 자석이 달린 원판 하나의 단면, 밝고 어두운 점이 늘어선 격자."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.name"
msgid "Mechanical television"
msgstr "기계식 텔레비전"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.aka"
msgid "Nipkow disc, televisor"
msgstr "닙코 원판, 텔레바이저"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.origin"
msgid "Disc patented by Paul Nipkow, Germany, in the 1880s. First public demonstration by John Logie Baird, London"
msgstr "원판은 파울 닙코가 1880년대에 독일에서 특허를 받았습니다. 최초의 공개 시연은 존 로지 베어드, 런던"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.statusNote"
msgid "Abandoned for electronic systems in the 1930s. The BBC replaced the system made by Baird with an all-electronic one in early 1937."
msgstr "1930년대에 전자식에 밀려났습니다. BBC는 1937년 초에 베어드의 방식을 완전한 전자식으로 바꾸었습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.lead"
msgid "Early television was mechanical: a spinning disc with a spiral of holes cuts the picture into lines."
msgstr "초기의 텔레비전은 기계식이었습니다. 구멍이 나선으로 뚫린 원판이 돌면서 그림을 선으로 자릅니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.how.1"
msgid "Each hole in the disc is slightly closer to the centre than the hole before it. As the disc turns, the holes pass a small window one after another, and each hole traces one line of the picture."
msgstr "원판의 구멍은 저마다 앞의 구멍보다 조금씩 중심에 가깝습니다. 원판이 돌면 구멍들이 작은 창 앞을 차례로 지나가고, 구멍 하나가 그림의 선 하나를 긋습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.how.2"
msgid "Behind the disc, a neon lamp flickers in time with the incoming signal. The eye looks through the moving holes at the lamp and joins the flicker into a picture. The apparatus that Baird showed in 1926 used one disc at the camera and another at the receiver. Each disc had 30 holes, and the two discs turned exactly together."
msgstr "원판 뒤에서는 네온 램프가 들어오는 신호에 맞춰 깜박입니다. 눈은 움직이는 구멍 너머로 램프를 보고, 그 깜박임을 하나의 그림으로 이어 붙입니다. 베어드가 1926년에 선보인 장치는 카메라 쪽에 원판 하나, 수신기 쪽에 원판 하나를 썼습니다. 원판마다 구멍이 30개였고, 두 원판은 정확히 함께 돌았습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.good.1"
msgid "Needs only a motor, a lamp and a punched disc"
msgstr "모터와 램프와 구멍 뚫린 원판만 있으면 된다"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.good.2"
msgid "Worked, and so proved that pictures could be sent at all"
msgstr "실제로 작동했고, 그래서 그림을 보낼 수 있다는 것 자체를 증명했다"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.bad.1"
msgid "Gives a small, dim, orange picture"
msgstr "그림이 작고 어둡고 주황색이다"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.bad.2"
msgid "Has only as many lines as holes will fit on a disc"
msgstr "선의 수는 원판에 뚫을 수 있는 구멍의 수만큼뿐이다"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.bad.3"
msgid "Needs the transmitter disc and the receiver disc to turn exactly together"
msgstr "송신 쪽 원판과 수신 쪽 원판이 정확히 함께 돌아야 한다"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.look"
msgid "The picture window is at the edge of the disc, where the holes move vertically. The picture has only 30 lines, so the model shows the hours above the minutes. Press the Look in button to put your eye to the magnifier. That is how viewers watched it."
msgstr "그림이 보이는 창은 원판의 가장자리에 있고, 거기서 구멍은 세로로 움직입니다. 그림은 선이 30개뿐이어서, 모델은 시를 위에, 분을 아래에 보여 줍니다. 들여다보기 버튼을 누르면 확대경에 눈을 댈 수 있습니다. 당시 사람들은 그렇게 보았습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.fact"
msgid "In his experiments Baird televised the head of a ventriloquist's dummy, known as Stooky Bill, because the lights he needed were too hot for a person. The head is kept in the Science Museum Group collection."
msgstr "베어드는 실험에서 스투키 빌이라고 불린 복화술 인형의 머리를 텔레비전에 비추었습니다. 필요한 조명이 사람에게는 너무 뜨거웠기 때문입니다. 그 머리는 사이언스 뮤지엄 그룹의 소장품으로 남아 있습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. People section: what Paul Nipkow did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.people.1"
msgid "Patented the scanning disc in Germany in the 1880s."
msgstr "1880년대에 독일에서 주사 원판의 특허를 받았습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. People section: what John Logie Baird did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.people.2"
msgid "Gave the first public demonstration of television, in London in 1926."
msgstr "1926년 런던에서 텔레비전을 처음으로 공개 시연했습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.view.eyepiece.label"
msgid "Look in"
msgstr "들여다보기"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.view.eyepiece.hint"
msgid "Thirty lines of flickering neon, seen through the moving holes. Hours above, minutes below."
msgstr "깜박이는 네온의 선 서른 개가 움직이는 구멍 너머로 보입니다. 위가 시, 아래가 분입니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.view.disc.label"
msgid "Follow the spiral"
msgstr "나선 따라가기"

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.view.disc.hint"
msgid "Thirty holes, each one hole's width nearer the centre than the last. One turn of the disc is one picture."
msgstr "구멍은 서른 개. 저마다 앞의 구멍보다 구멍 하나의 너비만큼 중심에 가깝습니다. 원판이 한 바퀴 돌면 그림 한 장입니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.media.receiver.caption"
msgid "A mechanical television receiver from the 1930s at the Tekniska museet. The picture behind the disc was about three centimetres square, so it was watched through the magnifying lenses."
msgstr "1930년대의 기계식 텔레비전 수신기. 스웨덴 국립과학기술박물관 소장. 원판 뒤의 그림은 가로세로 3센티미터쯤이어서, 확대 렌즈를 통해 보았습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.media.receiver.alt"
msgid "A black cabinet with dials, switches and a meter. Above it a large dark disc stands on edge, with round glass lenses mounted in front of it."
msgstr "다이얼과 스위치, 계기가 달린 검은 상자. 그 위에 크고 어두운 원판이 세로로 서 있고, 앞에는 둥근 유리 렌즈가 달려 있습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.media.system-1928.caption"
msgid "A diagram from Radio News, April 1928. At the transmitter a scanning disc and photoelectric cells turn a face into a signal. At the receiver a neon lamp and a second disc rebuild it for the viewer."
msgstr "잡지 라디오 뉴스 1928년 4월호의 도해. 송신 쪽에서는 주사 원판과 광전관이 얼굴을 신호로 바꿉니다. 수신 쪽에서는 네온 램프와 두 번째 원판이 보는 사람을 위해 그것을 다시 짜 맞춥니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.media.system-1928.alt"
msgid "Labelled line drawing: arc light, disc and photoelectric cells facing a head in profile, radio masts, then a neon lamp, disc and lens before a second head."
msgstr "설명이 붙은 선 그림: 아크등과 원판, 광전관이 옆얼굴을 향해 있고, 무선 탑을 지나, 네온 램프와 원판, 렌즈가 두 번째 얼굴 앞에 있습니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "A whole television chain, from John Logie Baird's United States patent of 1929. One spinning disc explores the object, here a cross. A second disc, turning in step, rebuilds the picture on a screen from the light of a lamp."
msgstr "텔레비전의 전체 계통. 존 로지 베어드의 1929년 미국 특허에서. 도는 원판 하나가 물체를 훑는데, 여기서는 십자 모양입니다. 맞춰 도는 두 번째 원판이 램프의 빛으로 스크린에 그림을 다시 짜 맞춥니다."

#: hall-1/mechanical-television
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.mechanical-television.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawing: a cross, a lens and a perforated disc lead to two boxes. Below, two boxes lead to a lamp, a second disc and a screen showing the cross."
msgstr "선 그림: 십자와 렌즈, 구멍 뚫린 원판이 상자 두 개로 이어집니다. 아래에서는 상자 두 개가 램프와 두 번째 원판, 십자가 비친 스크린으로 이어집니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.name"
msgid "Kinetic mirror"
msgstr "키네틱 거울"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.aka"
msgid "Mechanical mirror"
msgstr "기계식 거울"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.origin"
msgid "Daniel Rozin, Wooden Mirror"
msgstr "다니엘 로진, Wooden Mirror"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.statusNote"
msgid "Made as individual artworks, each in its own material: wood, chrome balls, pegs or rubbish."
msgstr "나무, 크롬 구슬, 못, 폐품처럼 작품마다 다른 재료로, 하나뿐인 미술 작품으로 만들어집니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.lead"
msgid "A camera watches you, and several hundred motors tilt pieces of wood until they form your picture."
msgstr "카메라가 여러분을 지켜보고, 수백 개의 모터가 나뭇조각을 기울여 여러분의 모습을 만듭니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.how.1"
msgid "Wooden Mirror has 830 square pieces of wood, a small video camera and a computer. Each piece of wood sits on its own servo motor, a motor that turns to an exact angle. The computer reduces the camera picture to 830 values and tilts each square to match its value."
msgstr "Wooden Mirror는 네모난 나뭇조각 830개와 작은 비디오카메라, 컴퓨터로 이루어져 있습니다. 나뭇조각은 저마다 자기 서보 모터 위에 놓여 있습니다. 서보 모터는 정해진 각도까지 정확히 도는 모터입니다. 컴퓨터는 카메라의 그림을 830개의 값으로 줄이고, 조각마다 그 값에 맞게 기울입니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.how.2"
msgid "When a square tips one way, it catches the light of the room and looks pale. When it tips the other way, it is in shadow and looks dark. The picture consists only of wood and light, and nothing in it glows."
msgstr "조각이 한쪽으로 기울면 방의 빛을 받아 밝아 보입니다. 반대쪽으로 기울면 그늘이 져서 어두워 보입니다. 그림은 나무와 빛만으로 이루어지고, 그 안에서 빛나는 것은 아무것도 없습니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.good.1"
msgid "Works in whatever light the room already has"
msgstr "방에 이미 있는 빛으로 작동한다"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.good.2"
msgid "Makes a picture that you could touch, out of the material itself"
msgstr "재료 그 자체로, 손으로 만질 수 있는 그림을 만든다"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.good.3"
msgid "Needs no instructions, because it behaves like a mirror"
msgstr "거울처럼 행동하므로 설명이 필요 없다"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.bad.1"
msgid "Shows only as much detail as its maker can afford in motors"
msgstr "세밀함은 만든 이가 감당할 수 있는 모터의 수만큼뿐이다"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.bad.2"
msgid "Is slow, audible and mechanical"
msgstr "느리고, 소리가 나고, 기계적이다"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.bad.3"
msgid "Every one is built by hand"
msgstr "하나하나 손으로 만든다"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.look"
msgid "The model shows the head and shoulders of a visitor, moving from side to side across an eight-sided field of tiles. Turn the model until the light falls across the tiles at a low angle. The contrast changes as you turn."
msgstr "모델은 관람객의 머리와 어깨를 보여 주며, 그 모습이 팔각형으로 늘어선 조각들 위를 좌우로 움직입니다. 빛이 조각에 낮은 각도로 비칠 때까지 모델을 돌려 보세요. 돌리는 대로 명암이 달라집니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.fact"
msgid "One of Rozin's later mirrors, made in 2015, is a crowd of 450 motorised toy penguins. When someone walks up, the penguins turn, and their black and white plush draws that person's silhouette."
msgstr "로진이 2015년에 만든 후기의 거울 하나는 모터로 움직이는 장난감 펭귄 450마리의 무리입니다. 누군가 다가가면 펭귄들이 돌아서고, 희고 검은 털이 그 사람의 윤곽을 그립니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. People section: what Daniel Rozin did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.people.1"
msgid "Artist, born in Jerusalem. He built Wooden Mirror in 1999 and has since made mirrors of chrome balls, pegs and toy penguins."
msgstr "예루살렘에서 태어난 미술가. 1999년에 Wooden Mirror를 만들었고, 그 뒤로 크롬 구슬, 못, 장난감 펭귄으로 거울을 만들어 왔습니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.view.stand.label"
msgid "Stand in front of it"
msgstr "앞에 서기"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.view.stand.hint"
msgid "The dark shape is you. A camera in the middle watches, and the tiles tip to match as you move your head."
msgstr "어두운 형상이 여러분입니다. 가운데의 카메라가 지켜보고, 머리를 움직이면 조각들이 그에 맞춰 기웁니다."

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.view.tiles.label"
msgid "Go close to the tiles"
msgstr "조각 가까이 가기"

#: hall-1/kinetic-mirror
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.kinetic-mirror.view.tiles.hint"
msgid "Each tile tips on its own servo. Tipped one way it catches the light, the other way it falls into shadow."
msgstr "조각은 저마다 자기 서보로 기웁니다. 한쪽으로 기울면 빛을 받고, 반대쪽으로 기울면 그늘에 듭니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.name"
msgid "Shape display"
msgstr "형상 디스플레이"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.aka"
msgid "Pin array, inFORM"
msgstr "핀 배열, inFORM"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.origin"
msgid "Daniel Leithinger, Sean Follmer and Hiroshi Ishii, Tangible Media Group, MIT Media Lab"
msgstr "다니엘 라이팅거, 숀 폴머, 이시이 히로시. MIT 미디어랩 탠저블 미디어 그룹"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.statusNote"
msgid "Research prototypes. The pin table has not become a product."
msgstr "연구용 시제품입니다. 핀 테이블은 제품이 되지 않았습니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.lead"
msgid "Motorised pins rise from a table to form the shape itself, instead of drawing a picture of it."
msgstr "모터로 움직이는 핀들이 테이블에서 솟아올라, 형상의 그림을 그리는 대신 형상 그 자체를 만듭니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.how.1"
msgid "A motor drives each pin to a set height. The surface becomes a relief, a raised model of the data: a landscape, a chart or a pair of hands."
msgstr "모터가 핀을 하나씩 정해진 높이까지 움직입니다. 표면은 부조가 되어, 데이터를 솟아오른 모형으로 보여 줍니다. 풍경일 수도, 도표일 수도, 두 손일 수도 있습니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.how.2"
msgid "The table built at MIT also senses what is on it. It can therefore respond to touch, push objects around and reproduce the hands of a person in another room."
msgstr "MIT에서 만든 테이블은 그 위에 놓인 것을 감지하기도 합니다. 그래서 손길에 반응하고, 물건을 밀어 옮기고, 다른 방에 있는 사람의 손을 재현할 수 있습니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.good.1"
msgid "Makes a shape that you can see from any side and feel with your hands"
msgstr "어느 쪽에서든 보이고 손으로 만질 수 있는 형상을 만든다"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.good.2"
msgid "Can move real objects that sit on it"
msgstr "위에 놓인 실제 물건을 움직일 수 있다"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.bad.1"
msgid "Is very coarse, because every pixel needs a motor"
msgstr "화소마다 모터가 필요해서 매우 거칠다"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.bad.2"
msgid "Changes height only, so it cannot form overhangs or undersides"
msgstr "높이만 바꿀 수 있어서, 튀어나온 부분이나 아랫면은 만들지 못한다"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.bad.3"
msgid "Needs a cabinet of motors under a small surface"
msgstr "작은 표면 아래에 모터가 든 큰 함이 필요하다"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.look"
msgid "A bump travels across the pins and carries a red ball with it. The original table is known for this demonstration. Look from a low angle to see how high the pins rise."
msgstr "불룩한 물결이 핀들 위를 지나가며 빨간 공을 실어 나릅니다. 원래의 테이블은 이 시연으로 알려져 있습니다. 낮은 각도에서 보면 핀이 얼마나 높이 올라오는지 알 수 있습니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.fact"
msgid "The lab's earlier pin table, Relief, raised each of its 120 pins with a motorised slider of the kind used on audio mixing desks. The 900 pins of inFORM are moved by the same type of slider, through nylon rods."
msgstr "같은 연구실의 앞선 핀 테이블 Relief는 핀 120개를 하나씩, 음향 믹싱 콘솔에 쓰는 종류의 전동 슬라이더로 들어 올렸습니다. inFORM의 핀 900개도 같은 종류의 슬라이더가 나일론 막대를 통해 움직입니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. People section: what Daniel Leithinger, Sean Follmer did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.people.1"
msgid "Built the pin table inFORM in the Tangible Media Group at the MIT Media Lab."
msgstr "MIT 미디어랩의 탠저블 미디어 그룹에서 핀 테이블 inFORM을 만들었습니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. People section: what Hiroshi Ishii did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.people.2"
msgid "Leads the Tangible Media Group, where the pin tables Relief and inFORM were built."
msgstr "탠저블 미디어 그룹을 이끌고 있습니다. 핀 테이블 Relief와 inFORM이 이곳에서 만들어졌습니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.view.table.label"
msgid "Stand at the table"
msgstr "테이블 앞에 서기"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.view.table.hint"
msgid "The surface is the picture. A bump travels across the pins and carries the red ball with it."
msgstr "표면이 곧 그림입니다. 불룩한 물결이 핀들 위를 지나가며 빨간 공을 실어 나릅니다."

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.view.pins.label"
msgid "Get down among the pins"
msgstr "핀 사이로 내려가기"

#: hall-1/shape-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.shape-display.view.pins.hint"
msgid "Every pin is driven to its own height by a motor underneath. Height is all there is: no overhangs."
msgstr "핀은 저마다 아래의 모터로 자기 높이까지 움직입니다. 있는 것은 높이뿐이고, 튀어나온 부분은 없습니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.name"
msgid "Refreshable braille display"
msgstr "점자 디스플레이"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.origin"
msgid "Oleg and Andrée Tretiakoff, France: braille dots raised by piezoelectric reeds. Klaus Peter Schönherr built a piezoelectric module in Germany in 1975"
msgstr "올레그 트레티아코프와 앙드레 트레티아코프, 프랑스: 압전 리드로 들어 올리는 점자 점. 클라우스 페터 쇤헤어는 1975년 독일에서 압전 모듈을 만들었습니다"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.statusNote"
msgid "In daily use. Still expensive, because every dot is a separate moving part that must survive years of touch."
msgstr "일상에서 쓰이고 있습니다. 여전히 비싼 것은, 점 하나하나가 여러 해의 손길을 견뎌야 하는 별개의 가동 부품이기 때문입니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.lead"
msgid "This display is read by touch: rows of pins rise and fall to spell a line of braille."
msgstr "이 디스플레이는 손끝으로 읽습니다. 핀의 줄이 오르내리며 점자 한 줄을 씁니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.how.1"
msgid "Under each dot is a piezo crystal, a crystal that bends under a voltage. A lever turns that small movement into the rise of a round-tipped pin through a hole. A cell has eight pins, and a typical display has 40 or 80 cells in a single line."
msgstr "점마다 아래에 압전 결정이 있습니다. 전압을 걸면 휘는 결정입니다. 지렛대가 그 작은 움직임을, 끝이 둥근 핀이 구멍으로 올라오는 움직임으로 바꿉니다. 셀 하나에는 핀이 여덟 개 있고, 일반적인 제품은 한 줄에 셀이 40개나 80개 있습니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.how.2"
msgid "It is the only piece in the collection that needs no light at all."
msgstr "이 컬렉션에서 빛이 전혀 필요 없는 전시품은 이것뿐입니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.good.1"
msgid "Works in complete darkness and in full sun"
msgstr "칠흑 같은 어둠에서도, 한낮의 햇빛 아래에서도 작동한다"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.good.2"
msgid "Is silent, private and precise"
msgstr "조용하고, 남에게 보이지 않고, 정확하다"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.good.3"
msgid "Rewrites its one line again and again, so that line can carry a whole book"
msgstr "한 줄을 몇 번이고 다시 쓰므로, 그 한 줄로 책 한 권을 전할 수 있다"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.bad.1"
msgid "Costs a lot for every cell"
msgstr "셀마다 비용이 많이 든다"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.bad.2"
msgid "Usually shows one line at a time, so it has no page layout and no graphics"
msgstr "보통 한 번에 한 줄만 보여 주므로, 지면 구성도 그림도 없다"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.bad.3"
msgid "Is mechanical, and the fingers that read it wear it out"
msgstr "기계식이어서, 읽는 손가락에 닳는다"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.look"
msgid "The model spells the time as braille writes it: first the sign that announces numbers, then the digits, with that sign repeated after each colon. Watch the cells on the right, which change every second."
msgstr "모델은 점자가 쓰는 방식대로 시각을 씁니다. 먼저 숫자가 시작됨을 알리는 기호, 그다음 숫자이고, 쌍점 뒤마다 그 기호가 다시 나옵니다. 오른쪽 셀들을 보세요. 1초마다 바뀝니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.fact"
msgid "By 1985 the United States tax service employed more than 100 blind and partially sighted staff to answer taxpayers' telephone questions. Their tax publications were supplied on cassette tape, to be read as braille on a 20-cell display."
msgstr "1985년 무렵 미국 국세청은 납세자의 전화 문의에 답하는 직원으로 시각 장애인을 100명 넘게 고용하고 있었습니다. 세무 간행물은 카세트테이프로 제공되었고, 직원들은 20셀 디스플레이에서 점자로 읽었습니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. People section: what Oleg Tretiakoff, Andrée Tretiakoff did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.people.1"
msgid "Patented braille dots raised by piezoelectric reeds. They filed first in France, in 1974."
msgstr "압전 리드로 들어 올리는 점자 점의 특허를 받았습니다. 첫 출원은 1974년 프랑스에서 했습니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. People section: what Klaus Peter Schönherr did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.people.2"
msgid "Invented a piezoelectric braille module in Germany in 1975."
msgstr "1975년 독일에서 압전식 점자 모듈을 발명했습니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. People section: what James Bryce did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.people.3"
msgid "Developed the earliest working braille display on record, at IBM around 1950."
msgstr "기록에 남은 가장 오래된 실제 작동하는 점자 디스플레이를 1950년 무렵 IBM에서 개발했습니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.view.touch.label"
msgid "Run a finger along it"
msgstr "손가락으로 훑기"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.view.touch.hint"
msgid "What a fingertip meets: round pins, up or down, eight to a cell. The right-hand cells change every second."
msgstr "손끝에 닿는 것은 올라오거나 내려간 둥근 핀으로, 셀 하나에 여덟 개입니다. 오른쪽 셀들은 1초마다 바뀝니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.view.cell.label"
msgid "See inside one cell"
msgstr "셀 안 들여다보기"

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.view.cell.hint"
msgid "The last cell, taken out and enlarged. A strip of piezo crystal bends under each dot and lifts its pin."
msgstr "마지막 셀을 꺼내 확대한 것입니다. 점마다 아래에서 압전 결정의 띠가 휘며 핀을 들어 올립니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The works of a braille cell, from the patent of Oleg and Andree Tretiakoff of 1977. Thin piezoelectric strips, one for each pin, bend when a voltage is applied and move the pins up and down."
msgstr "점자 셀의 기구. 올레그 트레티아코프와 앙드레 트레티아코프의 1977년 특허에서. 핀마다 하나씩 있는 얇은 압전 띠가 전압을 받으면 휘어, 핀을 위아래로 움직입니다."

#: hall-1/braille-display
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.braille-display.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Cross-section of a box: three upright rods pass through a stack of thin horizontal strips, with coiled wires entering at the left."
msgstr "상자의 단면도: 세로 막대 세 개가 얇은 가로 띠를 쌓은 묶음을 꿰뚫고, 감긴 전선이 왼쪽에서 들어옵니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.name"
msgid "Drone light show"
msgstr "드론 라이트 쇼"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.origin"
msgid "Marshmallow Laser Feast, Meet Your Creator, Cannes. In 2015 Intel and Ars Electronica Futurelab flew 100 drones near Hamburg, Germany"
msgstr "마시멜로 레이저 피스트, Meet Your Creator, 칸. 2015년에는 인텔과 아르스 일렉트로니카 퓨처랩이 독일 함부르크 근처에서 드론 100대를 띄웠습니다"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.statusNote"
msgid "A commercial service. In 2024 the record was 10,197 drones, in Shenzhen."
msgstr "상업 서비스가 되었습니다. 2024년의 기록은 선전에서 세운 10,197대였습니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.lead"
msgid "Every pixel is a small aircraft that carries a lamp and flies a path planned in advance."
msgstr "화소 하나하나가 램프를 싣고 미리 짜 놓은 경로를 나는 작은 비행체입니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.how.1"
msgid "Each drone follows its own planned route and switches its light at set moments. Satellite positioning, corrected from the ground, keeps every drone within centimetres of its planned place. Several hundred points of light can therefore hold a figure in the sky and then rearrange into the next figure."
msgstr "드론은 저마다 자기 경로를 따라가며 정해진 순간에 불을 켜고 끕니다. 지상에서 보정하는 위성 측위가 모든 드론을 정해진 자리에서 몇 센티미터 안에 붙들어 둡니다. 그래서 수백 개의 빛점이 하늘에 한 형상을 유지하다가 다음 형상으로 다시 늘어설 수 있습니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.how.2"
msgid "Shows grew from 100 drones in 2015 to 1,218 at the 2018 Winter Olympics, and to more than 10,000 by 2024."
msgstr "쇼의 규모는 2015년의 100대에서 2018년 동계 올림픽의 1,218대로, 2024년에는 1만 대 이상으로 커졌습니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.good.1"
msgid "Makes a picture hundreds of metres across with no screen and no structure"
msgstr "화면도 구조물도 없이 폭이 수백 미터인 그림을 만든다"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.good.2"
msgid "Is three-dimensional and visible from a whole city"
msgstr "입체적이고, 도시 전체에서 보인다"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.good.3"
msgid "Is quieter and cleaner than fireworks, which it has started to replace"
msgstr "불꽃놀이보다 조용하고 깨끗하며, 불꽃놀이를 대신하기 시작했다"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.bad.1"
msgid "Has very few pixels: the record show had 10,197"
msgstr "화소가 아주 적다. 기록을 세운 쇼도 10,197개였다"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.bad.2"
msgid "Rain and strong wind cancel the show"
msgstr "비와 강한 바람이 쇼를 취소시킨다"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.bad.3"
msgid "Flies for only minutes on each battery, and the fleet needs maintenance"
msgstr "배터리 하나로 몇 분밖에 날지 못하고, 기체들을 정비해야 한다"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.look"
msgid "The model is a small scene: a painted sky at dusk, a take-off grid on a field and 300 aircraft. They write the time inside a ring, then regroup into a turning globe. Zoom in to see that each light hangs under a small aircraft with four rotors. Then switch the daylight off, because drone shows need the dark."
msgstr "모델은 작은 장면입니다. 해 질 녘 하늘을 그린 그림, 들판 위의 이륙용 격자, 그리고 비행체 300대. 비행체들은 고리 안에 시각을 쓰고, 이어서 도는 지구 모양으로 다시 모입니다. 확대하면 빛 하나하나가 회전 날개 네 개짜리 작은 비행체 아래에 달려 있는 것이 보입니다. 그런 다음 햇빛을 끄세요. 드론 쇼에는 어둠이 필요합니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.fact"
msgid "The record flight shown at the opening of the 2018 Winter Olympics was a recording made in advance. A live flight for the crowd in the stadium was planned and then cancelled at the last minute."
msgstr "2018년 동계 올림픽 개막식에서 방영된 기록적인 비행은 미리 찍어 둔 영상이었습니다. 경기장 관중을 위한 실제 비행도 계획했지만, 마지막 순간에 취소되었습니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. People section: what Horst Hörtner did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.people.1"
msgid "Director of the Ars Electronica Futurelab in Linz. His team and Intel flew 100 lit drones near Hamburg in November 2015."
msgstr "린츠에 있는 아르스 일렉트로니카 퓨처랩의 소장. 그의 팀과 인텔은 2015년 11월 함부르크 근처에서 불을 밝힌 드론 100대를 띄웠습니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.view.field.label"
msgid "Stand on the field"
msgstr "들판에 서기"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.view.field.hint"
msgid "Each point of light is an aircraft on its own planned route. They write the time, then regroup into a globe."
msgstr "빛점 하나하나가 자기 경로를 나는 비행체입니다. 시각을 쓰고, 이어서 지구 모양으로 다시 모입니다."

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.view.close.label"
msgid "Fly up to one"
msgstr "한 대 곁으로 날아가기"

#: hall-1/drone-show
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.drone-show.view.close.hint"
msgid "Every light hangs under a small quadcopter, kept within centimetres of its place by satellite positioning."
msgstr "빛은 저마다 작은 쿼드콥터 아래에 달려 있고, 위성 측위로 제자리에서 몇 센티미터 안에 머뭅니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.name"
msgid "Cathode ray tube"
msgstr "음극선관"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.aka"
msgid "CRT, picture tube"
msgstr "CRT, 브라운관, 수상관"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.origin"
msgid "Ferdinand Braun, Germany. Electronic television demonstrated by Takayanagi (1926) and Farnsworth (1927)"
msgstr "페르디난트 브라운, 독일. 전자식 텔레비전은 다카야나기(1926년)와 판즈워스(1927년)가 시연했습니다"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.statusNote"
msgid "Sales of cathode ray tube televisions peaked in 2005 at about 130 million sets. The last large maker stopped production in 2015."
msgstr "브라운관 텔레비전의 판매는 2005년에 약 1.3억 대로 정점에 이르렀습니다. 마지막 대형 제조사는 2015년에 생산을 멈추었습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.lead"
msgid "One beam of electrons, steered by magnets, paints the whole picture on a glowing screen many times a second."
msgstr "자석으로 방향을 바꾸는 전자 빔 한 줄기가, 빛나는 화면 위에 그림 전체를 1초에 여러 번 그립니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.how.1"
msgid "A heated part in the neck of the tube, the cathode, gives off electrons, the particles of electric current. They are focused into a beam and accelerated toward the screen. Coils around the neck act as magnets and bend the beam. Where the beam lands, a coating called a phosphor glows."
msgstr "관의 목 부분에 있는 달군 부품인 음극이 전자를 내놓습니다. 전자는 전류를 이루는 입자입니다. 전자는 하나의 빔으로 모여 화면 쪽으로 가속됩니다. 목 둘레의 코일이 자석 구실을 하여 빔을 휘게 합니다. 빔이 닿는 곳에서는 형광체라는 도막이 빛납니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.how.2"
msgid "To make a picture, the beam sweeps the screen in horizontal lines from top to bottom, again and again, while its strength follows the signal. A colour tube has three beams. Behind the glass sits the shadow mask, a metal sheet full of holes. It lets each beam reach only the phosphor dots of its own colour. Only about 15 percent of the mask is open, so most of the energy of the beams is lost as heat."
msgstr "그림을 만들려면, 빔이 신호에 따라 세기를 바꾸면서 화면을 위에서 아래로, 가로선으로 거듭 훑습니다. 컬러 관에는 빔이 세 줄기 있습니다. 유리 뒤에는 구멍이 가득 뚫린 금속판인 섀도 마스크가 있습니다. 이것은 빔마다 자기 색의 형광체 점에만 닿게 합니다. 마스크에서 열린 부분은 약 15퍼센트뿐이어서, 빔 에너지의 대부분은 열로 사라집니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.good.1"
msgid "Gives off its light at the surface you look at, so the picture is clear from any angle"
msgstr "바라보는 바로 그 표면에서 빛이 나므로, 어느 각도에서나 그림이 또렷하다"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.good.2"
msgid "Responds faster than the eye can follow"
msgstr "눈이 따라갈 수 없을 만큼 빠르게 반응한다"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.good.3"
msgid "Has no fixed grid of pixels in a one-colour tube"
msgstr "단색 관에는 고정된 화소 격자가 없다"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.bad.1"
msgid "Becomes deeper and heavier, with more vacuum to hold, as the screen grows"
msgstr "화면이 커질수록 깊어지고 무거워지며, 유지해야 할 진공도 커진다"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.bad.2"
msgid "Needs a high voltage, and its glass bulb is under pressure from the air outside"
msgstr "높은 전압이 필요하고, 유리 관은 바깥 공기의 압력을 받는다"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.bad.3"
msgid "Redraws its picture constantly and does not hold it, so the picture flickers"
msgstr "그림을 붙잡아 두지 못하고 끊임없이 다시 그리므로, 그림이 깜박인다"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.look"
msgid "Press the Sit in front button. The bright band that moves down the screen is where the beam has just drawn its lines. The model slows this about 60 times. Then turn the model and look from the side at the copper coils on the neck, which steer the beam."
msgstr "정면에 앉기 버튼을 누르세요. 화면을 따라 내려가는 밝은 띠는 빔이 방금 선을 그린 자리입니다. 모델은 이것을 약 60분의 1 속도로 늦추었습니다. 그런 다음 모델을 돌려, 목에 감긴 구리 코일을 옆에서 보세요. 이 코일이 빔의 방향을 바꿉니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.fact"
msgid "A picture tube gives off X-rays while it runs, and the glass contains lead to stop them. The United States Environmental Protection Agency said in 2002 that a typical computer monitor could hold up to eight pounds of lead, about 3.6 kilograms."
msgstr "브라운관은 작동하는 동안 엑스선을 내기 때문에, 그것을 막으려고 유리에 납을 넣습니다. 미국 환경보호청은 2002년에, 일반적인 컴퓨터 모니터에는 납이 최대 8파운드, 약 3.6킬로그램 들어 있을 수 있다고 밝혔습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. People section: what Ferdinand Braun did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.people.1"
msgid "Built the first cathode ray tube, in Germany in 1897."
msgstr "1897년 독일에서 최초의 음극선관을 만들었습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. People section: what Kenjiro Takayanagi did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.people.2"
msgid "Showed a television receiver built on a cathode ray tube, in Japan in 1926."
msgstr "1926년 일본에서, 음극선관으로 만든 텔레비전 수상기를 선보였습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. People section: what Philo Farnsworth did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.people.3"
msgid "Sent the first all-electronic television picture, in San Francisco in 1927."
msgstr "1927년 샌프란시스코에서, 완전한 전자식 텔레비전 영상을 처음으로 보냈습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. People section: what Elma Farnsworth did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.people.4"
msgid "Known as Pem. She worked in Philo Farnsworth's laboratories, where she made the technical drawings for his experiments. Her face was among the first that his system sent."
msgstr "펨이라는 이름으로 알려져 있습니다. 필로 판즈워스의 연구실에서 일하며 실험에 쓸 기술 도면을 그렸습니다. 그의 방식이 처음 보낸 얼굴 가운데 하나가 그녀의 얼굴이었습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. People section: what Marie Van Brittan Brown, Albert Brown did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.people.5"
msgid "A nurse in New York and her husband, an electronics technician. Their home security system of 1966 showed the caller on a television monitor, through a camera sliding between four peepholes. Patented in 1969."
msgstr "뉴욕의 간호사와 전자 기술자인 남편입니다. 두 사람이 1966년에 고안한 가정용 보안 장치는 네 개의 구멍 사이를 오르내리는 카메라로 찾아온 사람을 텔레비전 모니터에 비추었습니다. 특허는 1969년에 등록되었습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. People section: what Mark Dean did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.people.6"
msgid "Engineer at IBM. Two of his three patents on the IBM personal computer are for the adapters that drove its monochrome and its colour monitor."
msgstr "IBM의 기술자입니다. IBM 개인용 컴퓨터에 관한 그의 특허 세 건 가운데 두 건은 흑백 모니터와 컬러 모니터를 구동하는 어댑터에 관한 것입니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.view.seat.label"
msgid "Sit in front"
msgstr "정면에 앉기"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.view.seat.hint"
msgid "The band moving down the face is the raster, slowed about sixty times. One beam paints it line by line."
msgstr "화면을 따라 내려가는 띠가 주사이며, 약 육십 분의 일 속도로 늦추었습니다. 빔 한 줄기가 선을 하나씩 그립니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.view.raster.label"
msgid "Go close to the face"
msgstr "화면 가까이 가기"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.view.raster.hint"
msgid "The picture is made of horizontal lines. The beam sweeps them from top to bottom, over and over."
msgstr "그림은 가로선으로 이루어져 있습니다. 빔이 그 선들을 위에서 아래로 거듭 훑습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.view.gun.label"
msgid "Look into the neck"
msgstr "목 안 들여다보기"

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.view.gun.hint"
msgid "The gun. A heated cathode gives off electrons, and the copper coils round the neck steer the beam."
msgstr "전자총입니다. 달군 음극이 전자를 내놓고, 목 둘레의 구리 코일이 빔의 방향을 바꿉니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.media.braun-tube.caption"
msgid "Ferdinand Braun's cathode ray tube of 1897 on its stand, as printed in a 1921 handbook of wireless telegraphy. The beam ran from the cathode in the narrow end to the screen in the wide one."
msgstr "받침대에 놓인 페르디난트 브라운의 1897년 음극선관. 1921년에 나온 무선 전신 안내서에 실린 사진입니다. 빔은 가는 쪽 끝의 음극에서 넓은 쪽 끝의 화면으로 나아갔습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.media.braun-tube.alt"
msgid "Old printed photograph of a long glass tube lying horizontally on two supports above a baseboard. One end is a wide cylinder, the other a thin neck."
msgstr "오래된 인쇄 사진. 긴 유리관이 밑판 위의 받침 두 개에 가로로 놓여 있습니다. 한쪽 끝은 넓은 원통이고, 다른 쪽은 가는 목입니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.media.tube-and-coils.caption"
msgid "A small picture tube out of its set, with the deflection coils still on its neck. The coils bend the beam so that it sweeps the whole face."
msgstr "수상기에서 꺼낸 작은 수상관. 목에는 편향 코일이 그대로 달려 있습니다. 코일은 빔을 휘게 해서 화면 전체를 훑게 합니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.media.tube-and-coils.alt"
msgid "A glass picture tube lying on a white surface, its grey rectangular screen toward the camera and a ring of copper windings around the narrow neck behind."
msgstr "흰 바닥에 놓인 유리 수상관. 네모난 회색 화면이 카메라를 향해 있고, 뒤쪽의 가는 목에는 구리 권선이 고리처럼 감겨 있습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.media.electron-gun.caption"
msgid "The electron gun in the neck of an oscilloscope tube made in Hungary in the 1970s. The pins at the end carry its connections through the glass."
msgstr "1970년대에 헝가리에서 만든 오실로스코프용 관의 목에 있는 전자총. 끝에 달린 핀들이 유리를 지나 바깥으로 연결됩니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.media.electron-gun.alt"
msgid "The clear glass neck of a tube, seen at an angle. Inside are stacked metal cylinders, plates and fine wires, and a ring of gold pins leaves the end."
msgstr "비스듬히 본 투명한 유리 목. 안에는 금속 원통과 판, 가는 선이 겹겹이 쌓여 있고, 끝에서는 금빛 핀이 고리 모양으로 나와 있습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The idea behind the colour picture tube, from Alfred Schroeder's patent for RCA, filed in 1947. Three electron guns aim through a plate of holes behind the screen, so that each beam reaches only the parts that show its colour."
msgstr "컬러 수상관의 바탕이 된 생각. 알프레드 슈뢰더가 RCA를 위해 1947년에 출원한 특허에서. 전자총 세 개가 화면 뒤의 구멍 뚫린 판을 통해 겨누므로, 빔마다 자기 색을 내는 부분에만 닿습니다."

#: hall-2/cathode-ray-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.cathode-ray-tube.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a picture tube with three guns in its neck and coils round it, three beams crossing at a plate before a screen, and an enlargement labelled with colours."
msgstr "선 그림: 목에 전자총 세 개가 있고 코일이 둘린 수상관, 화면 앞의 판에서 엇갈리는 세 줄기의 빔, 색 이름이 적힌 확대 그림."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.name"
msgid "Colour-wheel television"
msgstr "컬러 휠 텔레비전"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.aka"
msgid "Field-sequential colour, chromoscopic adapter"
msgstr "필드 순차 컬러, 크로모스코프 어댑터"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.origin"
msgid "Guillermo González Camarena, Mexico City. Mexican patent of 19 August 1940, United States patent granted in 1942"
msgstr "기예르모 곤살레스 카마레나, 멕시코시티. 멕시코 특허는 1940년 8월 19일, 미국 특허는 1942년에 등록되었습니다"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.statusNote"
msgid "No television set is built this way now. The principle survives where one fast display is given its three colours in turn."
msgstr "지금은 이런 방식으로 만드는 텔레비전이 없습니다. 이 원리는 빠른 디스플레이 하나에 세 가지 색을 차례로 입히는 곳에 남아 있습니다."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.lead"
msgid "A black-and-white picture tube shows the red, green and blue parts of a picture one after another, and a spinning wheel of filters gives each part its colour."
msgstr "흑백 수상관이 그림의 빨강, 초록, 파랑 부분을 차례로 보여 주고, 도는 필터 원판이 부분마다 색을 입힙니다."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.how.1"
msgid "The tube itself makes only white light. In front of it turns a disc of red, green and blue filters. While the red filter passes, the tube shows the red part of the picture. The green part follows behind the green filter, and then the blue. A second wheel turns in step in front of the camera. The eye cannot follow the changes and sees one picture in full colour."
msgstr "관 자체는 흰빛만 냅니다. 그 앞에서 빨강, 초록, 파랑 필터로 된 원판이 돕니다. 빨강 필터가 지나가는 동안 관은 그림의 빨강 부분을 보여 줍니다. 이어서 초록 필터 뒤에 초록 부분이, 그다음에 파랑 부분이 나옵니다. 카메라 앞에서도 두 번째 원판이 맞춰 돕니다. 눈은 그 바뀜을 따라가지 못하고, 하나의 컬러 그림으로 봅니다."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.how.2"
msgid "González Camarena patented his adapter in Mexico in 1940, at the age of 23. He designed it to be fitted to the black-and-white cameras and receivers already in use. The idea of a colour wheel was older: a German patent application of 1904 already described one. Television later settled on tubes that show the three colours at the same time."
msgstr "곤살레스 카마레나는 1940년, 23세의 나이에 멕시코에서 이 어댑터의 특허를 받았습니다. 그는 이미 쓰이고 있던 흑백 카메라와 수상기에 달 수 있도록 설계했습니다. 색 원판이라는 생각은 더 오래된 것으로, 1904년의 독일 특허 출원에 이미 적혀 있었습니다. 텔레비전은 나중에 세 가지 색을 동시에 보여 주는 관으로 자리를 잡았습니다."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.good.1"
msgid "Adds colour to a black-and-white system with one moving part"
msgstr "가동 부품 하나로 흑백 방식에 색을 더한다"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.good.2"
msgid "Needs no pattern of coloured dots, so the picture is as sharp as the tube"
msgstr "색점 무늬가 필요 없어서, 그림이 관만큼 선명하다"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.good.3"
msgid "Puts all three colours in exactly the same place"
msgstr "세 가지 색이 정확히 같은 자리에 놓인다"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.bad.1"
msgid "The wheel has to be much larger than the picture"
msgstr "원판이 그림보다 훨씬 커야 한다"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.bad.2"
msgid "A fast-moving object breaks up into flashes of red, green and blue"
msgstr "빠르게 움직이는 물체는 빨강, 초록, 파랑의 번쩍임으로 갈라져 보인다"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.bad.3"
msgid "The tube must show three pictures in the time of one, or the picture flickers"
msgstr "관이 그림 한 장의 시간에 세 장을 보여 주어야 하고, 그러지 못하면 그림이 깜박인다"

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.look"
msgid "The model first turns the wheel slowly. Watch the screen behind it: the tube shows three different pictures in turn, and the bars change as each filter passes. Then the wheel runs at speed and the three pictures merge into one. Switch the daylight off to see the screen alone."
msgstr "모델은 먼저 원판을 천천히 돌립니다. 그 뒤의 화면을 보세요. 관은 서로 다른 그림 세 장을 차례로 보여 주고, 필터가 지나갈 때마다 막대가 바뀝니다. 그런 다음 원판이 빠르게 돌고, 세 그림은 하나로 합쳐집니다. 햇빛을 끄면 화면만 보입니다."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.fact"
msgid "The original patent of 1940 is kept in the national archive of Mexico, the Archivo General de la Nación. In it González Camarena calls his invention a simple and novel adapter that could be fitted to any black-and-white television of the day."
msgstr "1940년의 특허 원본은 멕시코의 국립 기록 보관소인 아르치보 헤네랄 데 라 나시온에 보관되어 있습니다. 그 안에서 곤살레스 카마레나는 자신의 발명을, 당시의 어떤 흑백 텔레비전에도 달 수 있는 단순하고 새로운 어댑터라고 부릅니다."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. People section: what Guillermo González Camarena did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.people.1"
msgid "Mexican electrical engineer. He patented the adapter in Mexico in 1940, at the age of 23, and in the United States two years later."
msgstr "멕시코의 전기 기술자. 1940년, 23세의 나이에 멕시코에서 어댑터의 특허를 받았고, 2년 뒤 미국에서도 받았습니다."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. People section: what A. Frankenstein, Werner von Jaworski did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.people.2"
msgid "Applied in Germany in 1904 for a patent on a colour system with a filter wheel."
msgstr "1904년 독일에서, 필터 원판을 쓰는 컬러 방식의 특허를 출원했습니다."

#: hall-2/colour-wheel-television
#. People section: what Peter Goldmark did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.colour-wheel-television.people.3"
msgid "Engineer at the Columbia Broadcasting System, which built its own colour-wheel television in the same years."
msgstr "컬럼비아 방송(CBS)의 기술자. 이 회사는 같은 시기에 독자적인 컬러 휠 텔레비전을 만들었습니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.name"
msgid "Vector display"
msgstr "벡터 디스플레이"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.aka"
msgid "Calligraphic display, XY monitor"
msgstr "캘리그래픽 디스플레이, XY 모니터"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.origin"
msgid "MIT Whirlwind computer, whose Bouncing Ball demonstration ran on one"
msgstr "MIT의 휠윈드 컴퓨터. 튀는 공 시연이 여기에서 돌아갔습니다"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.statusNote"
msgid "Raster screens, which sweep every line as a television does, replaced nearly all of them once memory was cheap enough to hold a whole picture."
msgstr "메모리가 그림 한 장을 통째로 담을 만큼 싸지자, 텔레비전처럼 모든 선을 훑는 래스터 화면이 거의 모두를 대신했습니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.lead"
msgid "Here a television picture tube works like a pen: the beam draws each line directly and skips the empty parts of the screen."
msgstr "여기서는 텔레비전 수상관이 펜처럼 일합니다. 빔이 선을 하나씩 직접 그리고, 화면의 빈 곳은 건너뜁니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.how.1"
msgid "The beam does not sweep every part of the screen. It goes straight from one end of a line to the other end, and then moves to the next line. The whole picture is a list of lines."
msgstr "빔은 화면의 모든 곳을 훑지 않습니다. 선의 한쪽 끝에서 다른 쪽 끝으로 곧장 가고, 그런 다음 다음 선으로 옮겨 갑니다. 그림 전체가 선들의 목록입니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.how.2"
msgid "The lines are smooth, with no steps, and they are bright, because the beam spends all its time on them. But everything must be redrawn many times a second, so a picture with many lines starts to flicker. There is also no practical way to fill an area."
msgstr "선은 계단 없이 매끄럽고, 빔이 모든 시간을 선에 쓰기 때문에 밝습니다. 하지만 모든 것을 1초에 여러 번 다시 그려야 해서, 선이 많은 그림은 깜박이기 시작합니다. 면을 채울 실용적인 방법도 없습니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. How it works, paragraph 3 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.how.3"
msgid "Air defence consoles that worked on this principle went into service in 1958. The Sketchpad drawing program used it in 1963, and arcade games continued to use it into the 1980s."
msgstr "이 원리로 작동하는 방공 콘솔은 1958년에 배치되었습니다. 제도 프로그램 스케치패드는 1963년에 이것을 썼고, 오락실 게임은 1980년대까지 계속 썼습니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.good.1"
msgid "Draws lines with no jagged edges at any angle"
msgstr "어느 각도에서나 톱니 없는 선을 그린다"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.good.2"
msgid "Draws very bright strokes on a fully black background"
msgstr "완전히 검은 바탕에 아주 밝은 획을 그린다"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.bad.1"
msgid "Flickers more as the picture gains lines"
msgstr "그림에 선이 늘수록 더 깜박인다"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.bad.2"
msgid "Draws outlines only, with no filled shapes and only crude text"
msgstr "윤곽만 그릴 뿐, 채운 도형은 없고 글자도 거칠다"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.bad.3"
msgid "Made colour difficult"
msgstr "색을 내기가 어려웠다"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.look"
msgid "The screen shows a clock, drawn stroke by stroke, and a bouncing ball. Watch the trail behind the ball: it is the screen coating, the phosphor, still glowing where the beam has been. On the shelf lies a light pen on its cord, a tool for pointing at the screen."
msgstr "화면에는 획마다 그려지는 시계와 튀는 공이 나옵니다. 공 뒤의 꼬리를 보세요. 화면의 도막인 형광체가, 빔이 지나간 자리에서 아직 빛나고 있는 것입니다. 선반에는 줄이 달린 라이트 펜이 놓여 있습니다. 화면을 가리키는 도구입니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.fact"
msgid "The Vectrex home console of 1982 had a vector tube built in. Its screen was black and white, so colour came from plastic sheets laid over the glass."
msgstr "1982년의 가정용 게임기 벡트렉스에는 벡터 관이 들어 있었습니다. 화면이 흑백이어서, 색은 유리 위에 덧대는 플라스틱 판으로 냈습니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.view.seat.label"
msgid "Sit at the console"
msgstr "콘솔 앞에 앉기"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.view.seat.hint"
msgid "The beam draws each stroke directly, like a pen. The trail behind the ball is phosphor still glowing."
msgstr "빔은 펜처럼 획마다 직접 그립니다. 공 뒤의 꼬리는 아직 빛나고 있는 형광체입니다."

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.view.trace.label"
msgid "Go close to the trace"
msgstr "궤적 가까이 가기"

#: hall-2/vector-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.vector-display.view.trace.hint"
msgid "No dots and no scan lines. Each stroke is one smooth pass of the beam, at any angle."
msgstr "점도 주사선도 없습니다. 획마다 빔이 매끄럽게 한 번 지나간 자취이고, 각도는 자유롭습니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.name"
msgid "Storage tube terminal"
msgstr "축적관 단말기"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.aka"
msgid "DVST, direct-view bistable storage tube"
msgstr "DVST, 직시형 쌍안정 축적관"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.yearText"
msgid "1963 in an oscilloscope, 1968 as a display"
msgstr "1963년 오실로스코프에, 1968년 디스플레이로"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.origin"
msgid "Robert H. Anderson, Tektronix, USA, building on ideas of Andrew Haeff. The tube was refined at Tektronix from the late 1950s"
msgstr "로버트 H. 앤더슨, 텍트로닉스, 미국. 앤드루 헤프의 구상을 바탕으로 했습니다. 관은 1950년대 말부터 텍트로닉스에서 다듬어졌습니다"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.statusNote"
msgid "Replaced during the 1980s, when memory became cheap enough for raster graphics, in which the screen sweeps every line as a television does."
msgstr "1980년대에 메모리가 래스터 그래픽에 쓸 만큼 싸지면서 대체되었습니다. 래스터 방식에서는 화면이 텔레비전처럼 모든 선을 훑습니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.lead"
msgid "This tube stores its own picture: a line, once drawn, stays lit on the glass with no computer memory behind it."
msgstr "이 관은 자기 그림을 스스로 간직합니다. 한 번 그린 선은, 뒤에 컴퓨터 메모리가 없어도 유리 위에서 계속 빛납니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.how.1"
msgid "A writing beam of electrons draws lines as electric charge on a fine grid just behind the screen. A second, wide flood of electrons then covers the whole screen all the time. It reaches the glowing coating, the phosphor, only where the grid has been written. The picture is therefore stored in the tube itself."
msgstr "쓰기용 전자 빔이 화면 바로 뒤의 촘촘한 격자에 선을 전하로 그립니다. 그리고 폭이 넓은 두 번째 전자의 흐름이 화면 전체를 늘 덮고 있습니다. 이 전자는 격자에 쓰인 곳에서만, 빛나는 도막인 형광체에 닿습니다. 그래서 그림은 관 자체에 저장됩니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.how.2"
msgid "That made detailed graphics affordable at a time when computer memory was not. The Tektronix 4010 terminal of 1972 sold for $3,950 and addressed 1,024 by 1,024 points. The disadvantage is that nothing can be removed. To change the picture, the terminal erases the whole screen with a bright flash and draws everything again."
msgstr "덕분에 컴퓨터 메모리가 비싸던 시절에도 세밀한 그래픽을 감당할 수 있는 값에 쓸 수 있었습니다. 1972년의 텍트로닉스 4010 단말기는 3,950달러였고, 가로세로 1,024개씩의 점을 지정할 수 있었습니다. 단점은 아무것도 지울 수 없다는 것입니다. 그림을 바꾸려면 단말기가 밝은 섬광으로 화면 전체를 지우고 모든 것을 다시 그립니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.good.1"
msgid "Shows dense, steady line drawings with no flicker"
msgstr "촘촘하고 안정된 선 그림을 깜박임 없이 보여 준다"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.good.2"
msgid "Needed no memory for the picture at a time when memory was the expensive part"
msgstr "메모리가 비싼 부품이던 시절에, 그림을 담을 메모리가 필요 없었다"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.bad.1"
msgid "Cannot erase one part: to change one thing, it must redraw everything"
msgstr "한 부분만 지울 수 없다. 하나를 바꾸려면 전부 다시 그려야 한다"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.bad.2"
msgid "Shows no animation and no colour"
msgstr "움직이는 그림도 색도 보여 주지 못한다"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.bad.3"
msgid "Gives a dim picture on a glowing green background"
msgstr "빛나는 초록 바탕에 어두운 그림이 나온다"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.look"
msgid "Watch one full cycle. The graph is drawn once and then stays. Next the screen flashes, which erases it, and the drawing starts again. The two wheels beside the keys move a crosshair, two crossed lines that mark a point."
msgstr "한 주기를 끝까지 지켜보세요. 그래프는 한 번 그려지고 그대로 남습니다. 이어서 화면이 번쩍이는데, 그것이 지우기입니다. 그리고 그리기가 다시 시작됩니다. 자판 옆의 바퀴 두 개는 십자선을 움직입니다. 십자선은 한 점을 표시하는 두 줄의 엇갈린 선입니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.fact"
msgid "Its ancestor, Andrew Haeff's Memory Tube of the 1940s, was designed as computer memory, to store numbers and read them out again. Computers adopted a rival tube instead, and the design survived as a screen, read by a person looking at the glass."
msgstr "그 조상인 앤드루 헤프의 1940년대 메모리 튜브는, 숫자를 저장했다가 다시 읽어 내는 컴퓨터 메모리로 설계되었습니다. 컴퓨터는 경쟁하던 다른 관을 택했고, 이 설계는 사람이 유리를 보고 읽는 화면으로 살아남았습니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. People section: what Robert H. Anderson did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.people.1"
msgid "Developed the storage tube at Tektronix."
msgstr "텍트로닉스에서 축적관을 개발했습니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. People section: what Andrew Haeff did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.people.2"
msgid "Built the Memory Tube in the 1940s, the ancestor that Tektronix turned into a screen."
msgstr "1940년대에 메모리 튜브를 만들었습니다. 텍트로닉스가 화면으로 바꾼 그 조상입니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.view.seat.label"
msgid "Sit at the terminal"
msgstr "단말기 앞에 앉기"

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.view.seat.hint"
msgid "The plot is drawn once and stays lit, stored in the tube itself. The flash is the only way to erase it."
msgstr "그래프는 한 번 그려지고, 관 자체에 저장되어 계속 빛납니다. 지우는 방법은 그 섬광뿐입니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The storage tube, from Robert Anderson's patent of 1966 for Tektronix. The second view is cut away to show the inside. The patent itself concerns the thin layer on the face that holds the picture."
msgstr "축적관. 로버트 앤더슨이 텍트로닉스를 위해 받은 1966년 특허에서. 두 번째 그림은 안을 보여 주려고 일부를 잘라 냈습니다. 특허 자체는 그림을 붙잡아 두는 앞면의 얇은 층에 관한 것입니다."

#: hall-2/storage-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.storage-tube.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Two line drawings of a long glass tube that widens to a rectangular face, with pins at the narrow end. In the lower one the wall is partly cut away."
msgstr "긴 유리관을 그린 선 그림 두 개. 관은 네모난 앞면 쪽으로 넓어지고, 가는 끝에 핀이 있습니다. 아래 그림에서는 벽의 일부가 잘려 있습니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.name"
msgid "Nixie tube"
msgstr "닉시관"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.aka"
msgid "Cold cathode numeric indicator"
msgstr "냉음극 숫자 표시관"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.origin"
msgid "Burroughs Corporation, USA, with tubes made by Haydu Brothers"
msgstr "버로스 코퍼레이션, 미국. 관은 헤이두 브러더스가 만들었습니다"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.statusNote"
msgid "Replaced in the 1970s by LEDs and vacuum fluorescent displays. Old tubes are still on sale, and small makers produce new ones."
msgstr "1970년대에 LED와 진공 형광 표시관으로 바뀌었습니다. 오래된 관은 지금도 팔리고 있고, 작은 제조사들이 새것도 만듭니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.lead"
msgid "Ten numerals etched from thin sheet metal sit one behind another in neon gas, and the one that is switched on glows."
msgstr "얇은 금속판을 식각해 만든 숫자 열 개가 네온 기체 속에 앞뒤로 겹쳐 서 있고, 전기가 들어간 하나가 빛납니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.how.1"
msgid "Each numeral is a separate piece of thin sheet metal, etched to its shape. These numerals are the cathodes, the negative contacts. They stand one behind another inside a wire mesh, the anode or positive contact. About 170 volts ionises the neon next to the chosen numeral, which means that it charges the gas electrically. A thin orange layer of glowing gas then clings to that numeral."
msgstr "숫자는 저마다 모양대로 식각한 별개의 얇은 금속판입니다. 이 숫자들이 음극, 곧 음의 전극입니다. 숫자들은 철망 안에 앞뒤로 겹쳐 서 있고, 철망이 양극, 곧 양의 전극입니다. 약 170볼트의 전압이 고른 숫자 곁의 네온을 이온화합니다. 기체가 전기를 띠게 한다는 뜻입니다. 그러면 빛나는 기체의 얇은 주황색 층이 그 숫자에 달라붙습니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.how.2"
msgid "Nothing is heated and nothing is scanned. The numerals are whole shapes and are not built from bars or dots, which is why they look drawn and not assembled. The name comes from NIX I, the label that Burroughs itself gave the tube."
msgstr "달구는 것도, 주사하는 것도 없습니다. 숫자는 막대나 점을 조합한 것이 아니라 통째의 모양입니다. 그래서 조립한 것이 아니라 그린 것처럼 보입니다. 이름은 버로스가 이 관에 직접 붙인 표시인 NIX I에서 왔습니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.good.1"
msgid "Shows fully formed numerals with real depth"
msgstr "실제 깊이가 있는, 온전한 모양의 숫자를 보여 준다"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.good.2"
msgid "Is clear in a dim room, in a colour unlike that of other displays"
msgstr "어둑한 방에서도 또렷하고, 다른 디스플레이에는 없는 색을 띤다"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.bad.1"
msgid "Shows only the ten shapes inside it"
msgstr "안에 든 열 가지 모양만 보여 준다"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.bad.2"
msgid "Needs about 170 volts"
msgstr "약 170볼트가 필요하다"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.bad.3"
msgid "The numerals at the back show through the ones in front"
msgstr "뒤쪽의 숫자가 앞쪽 숫자 사이로 비쳐 보인다"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.look"
msgid "Turn the model until you see a tube from the side. The ten numerals stand in a stack inside the mesh, and the lit one moves forward and back as the seconds count. Then switch the daylight off and watch the orange glow appear on the glass."
msgstr "관이 옆에서 보일 때까지 모델을 돌리세요. 숫자 열 개가 철망 안에 겹쳐 서 있고, 초가 흐름에 따라 불이 들어온 숫자가 앞뒤로 옮겨 갑니다. 그런 다음 햇빛을 끄고, 유리에 주황빛이 떠오르는 모습을 보세요."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.fact"
msgid "The first prototype at Burroughs lasted one night: by morning so much metal from the numerals had settled inside the glass that they could no longer be seen. A little mercury vapour solved the problem, and later tubes ran for more than 200,000 hours."
msgstr "버로스의 첫 시제품은 하룻밤밖에 가지 못했습니다. 아침이 되자 숫자에서 나온 금속이 유리 안쪽에 잔뜩 내려앉아 숫자가 보이지 않게 되었습니다. 약간의 수은 증기가 이 문제를 풀었고, 나중의 관은 20만 시간 넘게 작동했습니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. People section: what David Hagelbarger did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.people.1"
msgid "The engineer most often named as its inventor."
msgstr "발명자로 가장 자주 꼽히는 기술자."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Note under the People section.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.note"
msgid "Credit for the invention is not clear. National Union sold a similar tube, the Inditron, in 1954, and patents for numerals in glow lamps go back to the 1930s."
msgstr "누구의 발명인지는 분명하지 않습니다. 내셔널 유니언은 1954년에 비슷한 관인 인디트론을 팔았고, 글로 방전 램프 안의 숫자에 관한 특허는 1930년대까지 거슬러 올라갑니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.view.stack.label"
msgid "Go close to one tube"
msgstr "관 하나 가까이 가기"

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.view.stack.hint"
msgid "Ten shaped cathodes stacked front to back inside a mesh anode. Neon glows round the one that is energised."
msgstr "모양을 낸 음극 열 장이 철망 양극 안에 앞에서 뒤로 겹쳐 있습니다. 전기가 들어간 한 장 둘레에서 네온이 빛납니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.media.lit-and-unlit.caption"
msgid "Two Soviet IN-18 tubes beside a matchbox for scale. One is lit. In the other the whole stack of thin metal numerals can be seen through the mesh."
msgstr "크기를 가늠하도록 성냥갑 옆에 놓은 소련제 IN-18 관 두 개. 하나는 불이 들어와 있습니다. 다른 하나에서는 얇은 금속 숫자가 겹친 묶음 전체가 철망 너머로 보입니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.media.lit-and-unlit.alt"
msgid "Two glass tubes lying on a wooden table next to a matchbox. The upper one glows orange with a numeral. The lower is unlit, showing fine overlapping metal numerals."
msgstr "나무 탁자 위, 성냥갑 옆에 놓인 유리관 두 개. 위의 관은 숫자 하나가 주황색으로 빛납니다. 아래 관은 꺼져 있어, 서로 겹친 가는 금속 숫자들이 보입니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.media.inside.caption"
msgid "A Nixie tube opened up. The numerals are separate thin metal shapes stacked one behind another, with a screen of fine mesh around them."
msgstr "열어 놓은 닉시관. 숫자는 저마다 별개의 얇은 금속 모양으로 앞뒤로 겹쳐 있고, 촘촘한 철망이 그것을 둘러싸고 있습니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.media.inside.alt"
msgid "Close view of thin pale metal numerals layered on top of each other inside a round mesh cup, standing on a green circuit board."
msgstr "초록 회로 기판 위에 선 둥근 철망 통 안에, 얇고 옅은 금속 숫자가 겹겹이 쌓인 모습을 가까이에서 본 사진."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.media.glow.caption"
msgid "The glow inside a working Nixie tube, seen through the honeycomb mesh at the front. The light is a thin sheath of glowing neon that clings to the metal of the numeral."
msgstr "작동 중인 닉시관 안의 빛. 앞쪽의 벌집 모양 철망 너머로 본 모습입니다. 빛은 숫자의 금속에 달라붙은, 빛나는 네온의 얇은 막입니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.media.glow.alt"
msgid "Extreme close-up of a curved orange band of light behind a hexagonal metal grid, with a second glowing shape out of focus at the left."
msgstr "육각형 금속 격자 뒤에 있는, 휘어진 주황색 빛의 띠를 아주 가까이에서 찍은 사진. 왼쪽에는 또 하나의 빛나는 모양이 흐릿하게 보입니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "From a Burroughs patent filed in 1955 by Leo Landrey. Ten numerals stand in a stack, with ring-shaped anodes between them. In this design the numerals are shaped from fine wire, not etched from sheet metal."
msgstr "레오 랜드리가 1955년에 출원한 버로스의 특허에서. 숫자 열 개가 겹쳐 서 있고, 그 사이사이에 고리 모양의 양극이 있습니다. 이 설계에서 숫자는 금속판을 식각한 것이 아니라 가는 철사로 모양을 냈습니다."

#: hall-2/nixie-tube
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.nixie-tube.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings of a glass tube from the side, from above and in section: numerals marked 0 to 9 stacked one above another between rings, with pins leaving the base."
msgstr "유리관을 옆에서, 위에서, 단면으로 그린 선 그림: 0부터 9까지의 숫자가 고리 사이에 위아래로 겹쳐 있고, 밑에서 핀이 나옵니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.name"
msgid "Vacuum fluorescent display"
msgstr "진공 형광 표시관"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.aka"
msgid "VFD"
msgstr "VFD"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.yearText"
msgid "1957 to 1959"
msgstr "1957년에서 1959년 사이"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.origin"
msgid "Philips, Netherlands (single indicator). Sources date it between 1957 and 1959. First multi-segment tube by Ise Electronics, Japan, 1967"
msgstr "필립스, 네덜란드(단일 표시기). 자료에 따라 1957년에서 1959년 사이로 연대가 다릅니다. 최초의 다중 세그먼트 관은 이세 전자공업, 일본, 1967년"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.statusNote"
msgid "Lost most of its markets to liquid crystal and OLED displays, but is still in production."
msgstr "시장의 대부분을 액정과 OLED 디스플레이에 내주었지만, 지금도 생산되고 있습니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.lead"
msgid "A flat glass box holds bars and symbols that glow blue-green, as on the video recorders and microwave ovens of its time."
msgstr "납작한 유리 상자 안에서 막대와 기호가 청록색으로 빛납니다. 그 시절의 비디오 녹화기와 전자레인지에서 보던 것입니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.how.1"
msgid "It is a small, low-voltage relative of the cathode ray tube. Fine heated wires, stretched across the front, give off electrons. Behind the wires, a mesh grid switches each digit on or off. Behind the grid sit the segments, the separate bars and symbols of the display. Each segment is an anode, a positive contact, and carries a coat of phosphor, a material that glows when electrons hit it. A segment glows when the grid lets electrons reach it."
msgstr "이것은 음극선관의 작고 전압이 낮은 친척입니다. 앞쪽에 팽팽하게 걸린 가는 달군 선들이 전자를 내놓습니다. 선 뒤에서는 그물 모양의 그리드가 자릿수마다 켜고 끕니다. 그리드 뒤에는 세그먼트, 곧 표시를 이루는 따로따로의 막대와 기호가 있습니다. 세그먼트는 저마다 양극, 곧 양의 전극이고, 형광체가 발라져 있습니다. 형광체는 전자가 부딪히면 빛나는 재료입니다. 그리드가 전자를 통과시키면 세그먼트가 빛납니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.how.2"
msgid "In the late 1980s, makers produced hundreds of millions every year for calculators, car radios and kitchen appliances."
msgstr "1980년대 말에는 제조사들이 계산기, 자동차 라디오, 주방 가전용으로 해마다 수억 개를 만들었습니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.good.1"
msgid "Is bright, with high contrast, and readable at a wide angle"
msgstr "밝고, 명암비가 높고, 넓은 각도에서 읽을 수 있다"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.good.2"
msgid "Works from a few tens of volts"
msgstr "수십 볼트로 작동한다"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.good.3"
msgid "Can have segments of any shape that the maker prints"
msgstr "제조사가 인쇄할 수 있는 모양이면 세그먼트는 어떤 모양이든 될 수 있다"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.bad.1"
msgid "Shows only the fixed shapes built into it"
msgstr "미리 만들어 넣은 정해진 모양만 보여 준다"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.bad.2"
msgid "Needs a vacuum and a heated wire"
msgstr "진공과 달군 선이 필요하다"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.bad.3"
msgid "Dims first where its phosphor has glowed the most"
msgstr "형광체가 가장 오래 빛난 곳부터 먼저 어두워진다"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.look"
msgid "Zoom in on the front of the glass. The thin horizontal wires are the heated wires, called filaments, and the fine mesh behind them is the grid. Every connecting wire leaves the glass along the bottom edge."
msgstr "유리의 앞면을 확대해 보세요. 가로로 걸린 가는 선들이 달군 선으로, 필라멘트라고 부릅니다. 그 뒤의 촘촘한 그물이 그리드입니다. 연결선은 모두 유리의 아래쪽 가장자리로 빠져나갑니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.fact"
msgid "Japanese calculator makers owed Burroughs high patent fees for the nixie tube, so the head of calculator development at the firm now called Sharp asked a tube engineer he knew for a cheaper, low-voltage display. Prototypes of the VFD were ready in two months."
msgstr "일본의 계산기 제조사들은 닉시관 때문에 버로스에 높은 특허료를 내고 있었습니다. 그래서 지금의 샤프에 해당하는 회사의 계산기 개발 책임자가, 알고 지내던 진공관 기술자에게 더 싸고 전압이 낮은 표시기를 부탁했습니다. 진공 형광 표시관의 시제품은 두 달 만에 나왔습니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. People section: what Tadashi Nakamura did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.people.1"
msgid "Founder of Ise Electronics in Japan. He invented the display and put it on sale in 1967."
msgstr "일본 이세 전자공업의 창업자. 이 표시관을 발명해 1967년에 시장에 내놓았습니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. People section: what Tadashi Sasaki did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.people.2"
msgid "Led calculator development at Hayakawa Electric, now Sharp, and asked Nakamura for a low-voltage display."
msgstr "하야카와 전기, 지금의 샤프에서 계산기 개발을 이끌었고, 나카무라에게 전압이 낮은 표시기를 부탁했습니다."

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.view.grid.label"
msgid "Look across the glass"
msgstr "유리를 따라 보기"

#: hall-2/vacuum-fluorescent
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.vacuum-fluorescent.view.grid.hint"
msgid "Thin hot wires in front, a mesh grid behind them, then the phosphor segments. Every lead leaves at the bottom."
msgstr "앞에는 가는 달군 선, 그 뒤에 그물 그리드, 그다음에 형광체 세그먼트가 있습니다. 인출선은 모두 아래로 나갑니다."

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.name"
msgid "Numitron"
msgstr "누미트론"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.aka"
msgid "Incandescent filament display"
msgstr "백열 필라멘트 표시관"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.origin"
msgid "RCA, USA"
msgstr "RCA, 미국"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.statusNote"
msgid "Replaced by LED and liquid crystal digits within a few years."
msgstr "몇 해 만에 LED와 액정 숫자 표시로 바뀌었습니다."

#: hall-2/numitron
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.lead"
msgid "One glass bulb holds seven tiny lamp filaments, arranged as the seven bars that form a digit."
msgstr "유리구 하나 안에 아주 작은 전구 필라멘트 일곱 개가, 숫자를 이루는 일곱 개의 막대 모양으로 놓여 있습니다."

#: hall-2/numitron
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 1.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.how.1"
msgid "Each bar, or segment, is a short filament like the one in an old light bulb. All seven sit in one glass bulb with the air pumped out. Current through the right filaments makes them glow white-hot in the shape of a numeral. It is a light bulb with seven wires instead of one."
msgstr "막대, 곧 세그먼트 하나하나는 옛 전구의 것과 같은 짧은 필라멘트입니다. 일곱 개 모두가 공기를 빼낸 유리구 하나에 들어 있습니다. 알맞은 필라멘트에 전류를 흘리면 하얗게 달아올라 숫자 모양으로 빛납니다. 선이 하나가 아니라 일곱 개인 전구입니다."

#: hall-2/numitron
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.good.1"
msgid "Contains nothing more unusual than a lamp filament"
msgstr "전구 필라멘트보다 특별한 것이 아무것도 들어 있지 않다"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.good.2"
msgid "Gives a warm white light that filters can turn into any colour"
msgstr "따뜻한 흰빛을 내고, 필터로 어떤 색이든 만들 수 있다"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.bad.1"
msgid "Shows numerals only"
msgstr "숫자만 보여 준다"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.bad.2"
msgid "Has filaments that burn out"
msgstr "필라멘트가 끊어진다"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.bad.3"
msgid "Arrived just as LEDs made it unnecessary"
msgstr "LED가 이것을 쓸모없게 만든 바로 그때 나왔다"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.look"
msgid "Compare it with the Nixie tube in this hall. Both are glass bulbs that show numerals. Here, however, each numeral consists of seven bars, and the light comes from a hot wire."
msgstr "이 전시실의 닉시관과 견주어 보세요. 둘 다 숫자를 보여 주는 유리구입니다. 다만 여기서는 숫자가 일곱 개의 막대로 이루어지고, 빛은 달군 선에서 나옵니다."

#: hall-2/numitron
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.fact"
msgid "In March 1970 the editors of Popular Electronics called the new tube so simple that they wondered why no one had thought of it before. It ran on 5 volts or less and was expected to last more than 100,000 hours."
msgstr "1970년 3월, 잡지 파퓰러 일렉트로닉스의 편집자들은 이 새 관이 너무나 단순해서, 왜 이제껏 아무도 생각하지 못했는지 궁금하다고 썼습니다. 5볼트 이하로 작동했고, 10만 시간 넘게 갈 것으로 기대되었습니다."

#: hall-2/numitron
#. People section: what Lewis Latimer did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.people.1"
msgid "Not a maker of this display. The lamp filament came first, and in 1882 he patented a way of making the carbon filaments of early electric lamps so that fewer broke in the furnace."
msgstr "이 디스플레이를 만든 사람은 아닙니다. 전구의 필라멘트가 먼저 있었습니다. 1882년에, 초기 전구의 탄소 필라멘트를 가마 안에서 덜 부러지게 만드는 방법의 특허를 받았습니다."

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.view.filaments.label"
msgid "Go close to one bulb"
msgstr "유리구 하나 가까이 가기"

#: hall-2/numitron
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.numitron.view.filaments.hint"
msgid "Seven lamp filaments strung between posts. The lit ones are glowing wire, and that is all the light there is."
msgstr "기둥 사이에 걸린 전구 필라멘트 일곱 개. 불이 들어온 것은 달아오른 선이고, 빛은 그것이 전부입니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.name"
msgid "Plasma display panel"
msgstr "플라스마 디스플레이 패널"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.origin"
msgid "Donald Bitzer, Gene Slottow and Robert Willson, University of Illinois, for the PLATO teaching system"
msgstr "도널드 비처, 진 슬로토, 로버트 윌슨. 일리노이 대학교, 교육 시스템 PLATO를 위해 만들었습니다"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.statusNote"
msgid "Panasonic, LG and Samsung stopped making plasma televisions in 2014."
msgstr "파나소닉, LG, 삼성은 2014년에 플라스마 텔레비전 생산을 멈추었습니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.lead"
msgid "Two sheets of glass hold a grid of tiny gas cells, and each lit cell glows like a small neon sign."
msgstr "유리판 두 장 사이에 아주 작은 기체 셀이 격자로 놓여 있고, 불이 들어온 셀은 작은 네온사인처럼 빛납니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.how.1"
msgid "A high voltage across a cell turns the gas inside it into plasma, a gas that carries electric charge and glows. In the first panels that glow was the picture: orange dots on black. The panel was flat and rugged, and it could hold a picture without help from a computer. That is why a university built it for classroom terminals."
msgstr "셀에 높은 전압을 걸면 안의 기체가 플라스마가 됩니다. 플라스마는 전하를 나르며 빛나는 기체입니다. 처음의 패널에서는 그 빛이 곧 그림이었습니다. 검은 바탕의 주황색 점입니다. 패널은 납작하고 튼튼했으며, 컴퓨터의 도움 없이도 그림을 유지할 수 있었습니다. 한 대학이 교실용 단말기를 위해 이것을 만든 까닭입니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.how.2"
msgid "In colour panels the gas gives off invisible ultraviolet light. This light strikes a coating inside each cell, a phosphor, which glows red, green or blue. Fujitsu showed a full-colour 21 inch panel in 1992 and a 42 inch panel in 1995. For a decade, plasma was the way to have a large flat television."
msgstr "컬러 패널에서는 기체가 눈에 보이지 않는 자외선을 냅니다. 이 빛이 셀 안쪽의 도막인 형광체에 부딪히고, 형광체가 빨강, 초록, 파랑으로 빛납니다. 후지쯔는 1992년에 21인치 풀 컬러 패널을, 1995년에 42인치 패널을 선보였습니다. 그 뒤 십 년 동안, 크고 납작한 텔레비전을 가지려면 플라스마였습니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.good.1"
msgid "Makes its own light in each cell, so blacks are deep and viewing angles are wide"
msgstr "셀마다 스스로 빛을 내므로, 검은색이 깊고 시야각이 넓다"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.good.2"
msgid "Was flat and large long before liquid crystal panels could be"
msgstr "액정 패널이 그럴 수 있기 훨씬 전부터 납작하고 컸다"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.bad.1"
msgid "Uses a lot of power and is heavy"
msgstr "전력을 많이 쓰고 무겁다"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.bad.2"
msgid "Keeps a lasting mark of a picture that stays on screen for a long time, called burn-in"
msgstr "화면에 오래 머문 그림의 자국이 남는다. 번인이라고 한다"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.bad.3"
msgid "Has cells that cannot be made very small, so small panels with fine detail are not possible"
msgstr "셀을 아주 작게 만들 수 없어서, 세밀한 작은 패널은 만들 수 없다"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.look"
msgid "The model shows the early kind: a square orange panel in a classroom terminal. A frame of invisible infrared beams around the screen lets the terminal detect where a finger is. Press the Go close to the dots button to see the individual dots."
msgstr "모델은 초기의 형태를 보여 줍니다. 교실용 단말기에 달린 네모난 주황색 패널입니다. 화면 둘레의 틀에는 눈에 보이지 않는 적외선 빔이 지나가서, 단말기는 손가락이 어디에 있는지 알아낼 수 있습니다. 점 가까이 가기 버튼을 누르면 점 하나하나가 보입니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.fact"
msgid "The classroom plasma panel was clear enough to see through. Behind it the terminal carried a projector and a sheet of film holding up to 256 tiny pictures, and the computer could project any one of them onto the back of the screen."
msgstr "교실용 플라스마 패널은 건너편이 비쳐 보일 만큼 투명했습니다. 단말기는 패널 뒤에 영사기와, 작은 그림을 256장까지 담은 필름을 갖추고 있었고, 컴퓨터는 그 가운데 어느 것이든 화면 뒤쪽에 비출 수 있었습니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. People section: what Donald Bitzer, Gene Slottow, Robert Willson did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.people.1"
msgid "Invented the panel at the University of Illinois in 1964, for the PLATO teaching system. Willson was a graduate student."
msgstr "1964년 일리노이 대학교에서, 교육 시스템 PLATO를 위해 이 패널을 발명했습니다. 윌슨은 대학원생이었습니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. People section: what Tsutae Shinoda did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.people.2"
msgid "Led the team at Fujitsu in Japan that made the colour plasma panel practical. Its 21-inch colour television went on sale in 1993."
msgstr "일본 후지쯔에서 컬러 플라스마 패널을 실용화한 팀을 이끌었습니다. 그 21인치 컬러 텔레비전은 1993년에 발매되었습니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.view.seat.label"
msgid "Sit in front"
msgstr "정면에 앉기"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.view.seat.hint"
msgid "A classroom terminal. Every orange dot is a small neon discharge. Infrared beams across the frame find a finger."
msgstr "교실용 단말기입니다. 주황색 점 하나하나가 작은 네온 방전입니다. 틀을 가로지르는 적외선 빔이 손가락을 찾아냅니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.view.dots.label"
msgid "Go close to the dots"
msgstr "점 가까이 가기"

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.view.dots.hint"
msgid "Each dot is one gas cell between two sheets of glass. A lit cell is a small neon sign."
msgstr "점 하나하나가 유리판 두 장 사이의 기체 셀입니다. 불이 들어온 셀은 작은 네온사인입니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The plasma panel drawn apart, from the patent of Donald Bitzer, Gene Slottow and Robert Willson, filed in 1966. A sheet of glass pierced with holes lies between two outer sheets that carry crossed rows of conductors."
msgstr "분해해서 그린 플라스마 패널. 도널드 비처, 진 슬로토, 로버트 윌슨이 1966년에 출원한 특허에서. 구멍이 뚫린 유리판이, 엇갈린 도선 줄을 실은 바깥쪽 판 두 장 사이에 놓여 있습니다."

#: hall-2/plasma-panel
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.plasma-panel.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: three sheets drawn apart, the middle one full of round holes and the outer two ruled with lines at right angles, and a cross-section through four cells."
msgstr "선 그림: 떨어뜨려 그린 판 세 장. 가운데 판에는 둥근 구멍이 가득하고, 바깥 두 장에는 서로 직각인 선이 그어져 있습니다. 그리고 셀 네 개를 지나는 단면도."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.name"
msgid "Field emission display"
msgstr "전계 방출 디스플레이"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.aka"
msgid "FED, the flat CRT"
msgstr "FED, 평면 CRT"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.origin"
msgid "Candescent Technologies, USA, one of several companies that worked with Spindt tips (microscopic metal tips that give off electrons)"
msgstr "캔데선트 테크놀로지스, 미국. 스핀트 팁(전자를 내놓는 미세한 금속 돌기)을 다룬 여러 회사 가운데 하나입니다"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.statusNote"
msgid "Never reached volume production. Candescent went bankrupt in 2004 after spending about $600 million. Sony's effort closed in 2009."
msgstr "양산에 이르지 못했습니다. 캔데선트는 약 6억 달러를 쓴 뒤 2004년에 파산했습니다. 소니의 사업은 2009년에 끝났습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.lead"
msgid "This display is a cathode ray tube flattened to a few millimetres, with a tiny source of electrons behind every dot."
msgstr "이 디스플레이는 몇 밀리미터 두께로 납작하게 누른 음극선관으로, 점마다 뒤에 아주 작은 전자원이 있습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.how.1"
msgid "A cathode ray tube has one hot source of electrons and a long path to the screen. Here the back plate carries a grid of microscopic cold sources: sharp metal tips, or microscopic tubes of carbon called nanotubes. A voltage on a layer above the tips, the gate, pulls electrons off them. The electrons cross a thin vacuum gap and strike a glowing coating, the phosphor, on the front glass, exactly as in a cathode ray tube."
msgstr "음극선관에는 달군 전자원 하나와 화면까지의 긴 길이 있습니다. 여기서는 뒤판에 미세한 차가운 전자원이 격자로 놓여 있습니다. 날카로운 금속 돌기이거나, 나노튜브라고 하는 미세한 탄소 관입니다. 돌기 위의 층인 게이트에 전압을 걸면 돌기에서 전자가 끌려 나옵니다. 전자는 얇은 진공 틈을 건너, 앞 유리의 빛나는 도막인 형광체에 부딪힙니다. 이 부분은 음극선관과 똑같습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.how.2"
msgid "It promised the picture quality of a tube in the depth of a flat panel, and prototypes worked. But the tips wore away, the vacuum was hard to keep, and liquid crystal panels became cheap first."
msgstr "평판의 두께로 관의 화질을 낼 수 있다는 약속이었고, 시제품은 작동했습니다. 하지만 돌기는 닳았고, 진공은 유지하기 어려웠으며, 액정 패널이 먼저 싸졌습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.good.1"
msgid "Gives the fast, wide-angle picture of a tube and makes its own light"
msgstr "관처럼 빠르고 시야각이 넓은 그림을 내며, 스스로 빛을 낸다"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.good.2"
msgid "Is only a few millimetres deep"
msgstr "두께가 몇 밀리미터뿐이다"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.bad.1"
msgid "Has tips that wear under the voltages it needs"
msgstr "필요한 전압 아래에서 돌기가 닳는다"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.bad.2"
msgid "Needs spacers to hold a wide, thin vacuum gap open, and the vacuum must stay clean for years"
msgstr "넓고 얇은 진공 틈을 벌려 둘 스페이서가 필요하고, 진공이 여러 해 동안 깨끗해야 한다"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.bad.3"
msgid "Lost on cost to liquid crystal panels before it was ever sold in volume"
msgstr "양산해서 팔리기도 전에 원가에서 액정 패널에 졌다"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.look"
msgid "The model shows one corner of a panel, opened and greatly enlarged. Follow the layers upward. First come the tips, then the gate with a hole over each tip. Glass spacers hold up the front plate, which has the phosphor on its underside. The lit dots count seconds."
msgstr "모델은 패널의 한 귀퉁이를 열어 크게 확대해 보여 줍니다. 층을 아래에서 위로 따라가 보세요. 먼저 돌기가 있고, 그 위에 돌기마다 구멍이 뚫린 게이트가 있습니다. 유리 스페이서가 앞판을 받치고, 앞판의 아랫면에 형광체가 있습니다. 불이 들어온 점들은 초를 셉니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.fact"
msgid "Nobody sharpens the tips. In the method patented at Stanford Research Institute, metal is deposited into holes two thousandths of a millimetre wide while a second material slowly closes each hole from its edge, and the shrinking opening shapes the metal into a cone."
msgstr "돌기는 누가 갈아서 세우는 것이 아닙니다. 스탠퍼드 연구소에서 특허를 받은 방법에서는, 너비가 천분의 2밀리미터인 구멍에 금속을 쌓는 동안 다른 재료가 구멍을 가장자리에서부터 천천히 막아 갑니다. 좁아지는 구멍이 금속을 원뿔 모양으로 빚습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. People section: what Charles Spindt, Kenneth Shoulders, Louis Heynick did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.people.1"
msgid "Patented the way of making the microscopic tips, at Stanford Research Institute."
msgstr "스탠퍼드 연구소에서, 미세한 돌기를 만드는 방법의 특허를 받았습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. People section: what Akintunde Akinwande did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.people.2"
msgid "Engineer, educated in Nigeria and at Stanford. He joined Honeywell in 1986 and worked there on thin-film field emitters and displays, and at MIT on arrays of emitters switched one by one."
msgstr "나이지리아와 스탠퍼드 대학교에서 공부한 공학자. 1986년에 허니웰에 들어가 박막 전계 방출 소자와 디스플레이를 연구했고, MIT에서는 하나씩 따로 켜고 끄는 방출 소자의 배열을 연구했습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.view.tips.label"
msgid "Go down to the tips"
msgstr "돌기까지 내려가기"

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.view.tips.hint"
msgid "A cold cathode tip under every hole in the gate. Electrons leave the tips and cross the gap to the phosphor above."
msgstr "게이트의 구멍마다 아래에 냉음극 돌기가 하나씩 있습니다. 전자는 돌기를 떠나 틈을 건너 위의 형광체에 닿습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The Spindt cathode, from the Stanford Research Institute patent of 1973. A sandwich of metal, insulator and metal is pierced with holes, and a sharp cone stands in each one."
msgstr "스핀트 음극. 스탠퍼드 연구소의 1973년 특허에서. 금속, 절연체, 금속을 겹친 층에 구멍이 뚫려 있고, 구멍마다 날카로운 원뿔이 서 있습니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Perspective drawing of a layered block with six round holes and a small pointed cone in each, wired to a box marked variable potential source. Below, one hole in cross-section."
msgstr "층을 이룬 덩어리의 투시도. 둥근 구멍 여섯 개마다 작고 뾰족한 원뿔이 있고, 가변 전위원이라고 적힌 상자에 배선되어 있습니다. 아래에는 구멍 하나의 단면도."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.media.patent-2.caption"
msgid "A flat tube with a cold cathode, from a United States Army patent filed in 1967. Electron emitters are spread over crossed strips of conductor. Pulsing one strip of each set makes the emitters at their crossing fire."
msgstr "냉음극을 쓴 납작한 관. 미국 육군이 1967년에 출원한 특허에서. 전자 방출원이 엇갈린 도체 띠 위에 퍼져 있습니다. 각 조에서 띠 하나씩에 펄스를 주면, 그 교차점의 방출원이 작동합니다."

#: hall-2/field-emission
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.field-emission.media.patent-2.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a cut-away corner of a plate covered in fine dots over crossed strips, a round flat tube from above with part broken away, and its thin cross-section."
msgstr "선 그림: 엇갈린 띠 위를 고운 점이 덮은 판의 잘라 낸 귀퉁이, 일부를 뜯어내고 위에서 본 둥글고 납작한 관, 그리고 그 얇은 단면."

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.name"
msgid "Eggcrate display"
msgstr "전구 표시판"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.aka"
msgid "Lamp matrix"
msgstr "에그크레이트, 램프 매트릭스"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.yearText"
msgid "about 1968"
msgstr "1968년경"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.origin"
msgid "Maker not recorded"
msgstr "제작자는 기록에 없습니다"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.statusNote"
msgid "Used on television game shows into the late 1990s, and on scoreboards and road signs until flip-disc, fibre optic and LED signs replaced it."
msgstr "텔레비전 퀴즈 프로그램에서는 1990년대 말까지 쓰였고, 점수판과 도로 표지에서는 플립 디스크, 광섬유, LED 표시판으로 바뀔 때까지 쓰였습니다."

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.lead"
msgid "Ordinary light bulbs sit in a grid, each in its own cell of a mask that keeps its light from spilling onto its neighbours."
msgstr "평범한 전구들이 격자로 놓여 있고, 저마다 마스크의 자기 칸에 들어 있어 빛이 이웃으로 새지 않습니다."

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.how.1"
msgid "Filament light bulbs form a grid, usually five bulbs wide and seven high for each character. A mask with a hole around each bulb, originally made of foam rubber, stops one lamp from lighting its neighbours. The mask looks like an egg tray, which gave the display its name."
msgstr "필라멘트 전구를 격자로 늘어놓습니다. 글자 하나에 보통 가로 다섯 개, 세로 일곱 개입니다. 전구마다 둘레에 구멍이 난 마스크가, 전구 하나가 이웃을 비추지 못하게 막습니다. 마스크는 원래 발포 고무로 만들었습니다. 그 모양이 달걀판을 닮아서, 영어로 에그크레이트라는 이름이 붙었습니다."

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.how.2"
msgid "A filament takes a moment to heat and a moment to cool, so the digits brighten and fade gradually. That soft change is typical of the scoreboards and the game show totals of the period."
msgstr "필라멘트는 달아오르는 데도, 식는 데도 잠깐의 시간이 걸려서, 숫자는 서서히 밝아지고 서서히 사라집니다. 그 부드러운 변화는 그 시절의 점수판과 퀴즈 프로그램 상금 표시에서 흔히 보던 것입니다."

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.good.1"
msgid "Is very bright, and big enough for a stadium"
msgstr "아주 밝고, 경기장에서 쓸 만큼 크다"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.good.2"
msgid "Uses parts that any electrician stocks"
msgstr "어느 전기 기사나 갖고 있는 부품을 쓴다"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.bad.1"
msgid "Bulbs fail one at a time, and each failed bulb is a missing dot"
msgstr "전구는 하나씩 나가고, 나간 전구는 곧 빠진 점이 된다"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.bad.2"
msgid "Runs hot and uses a lot of power"
msgstr "뜨거워지고 전력을 많이 쓴다"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.bad.3"
msgid "Is coarse: it shows a few characters and no pictures"
msgstr "거칠다. 글자 몇 개를 보여 줄 뿐 그림은 보여 주지 못한다"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.look"
msgid "Watch the minute change, or watch the colon blink. The lamps do not switch on and off sharply. They brighten and fade."
msgstr "분이 바뀌는 모습이나 쌍점이 깜박이는 모습을 보세요. 전구는 딱 켜지고 딱 꺼지지 않습니다. 밝아졌다가 사그라듭니다."

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.fact"
msgid "An older lamp sign, the news strip that wrapped round the Times Tower in New York from 1928, had 14,800 bulbs. An operator set each headline by hand in thin letter slabs, and a chain pulled them across contacts that switched the lamps."
msgstr "더 오래된 전구 표시판으로, 1928년부터 뉴욕의 타임스 타워를 둘러싸고 있던 뉴스 띠가 있습니다. 전구가 14,800개였습니다. 조작원이 기사 제목마다 얇은 글자판을 손으로 짜 넣으면, 사슬이 그것을 접점 위로 끌고 가며 전구를 켜고 껐습니다."

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.view.below.label"
msgid "Look up at the board"
msgstr "표시판 올려다보기"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.view.below.hint"
msgid "A scoreboard as the crowd met it. The lamps do not snap on and off: they swell and die away."
msgstr "관중이 보던 그대로의 점수판입니다. 전구는 딱 켜지고 꺼지지 않고, 부풀듯 밝아졌다가 스러집니다."

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.view.lamps.label"
msgid "Go close to the lamps"
msgstr "전구 가까이 가기"

#: hall-2/eggcrate
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.eggcrate.view.lamps.hint"
msgid "Ordinary bulbs, each in its own cell of the baffle so that its light does not spill on to its neighbours."
msgstr "평범한 전구가 저마다 칸막이의 자기 칸에 들어 있어서, 빛이 이웃으로 새지 않습니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.name"
msgid "LED display"
msgstr "LED 디스플레이"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.origin"
msgid "Hewlett-Packard, USA. The 5082-7000 numeric indicator of 1969 was designed by Howard Borden and Gerald Pighini"
msgstr "휼렛 패커드, 미국. 1969년의 숫자 표시기 5082-7000은 하워드 보든과 제럴드 피기니가 설계했습니다"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.statusNote"
msgid "The standard for outdoor signs, stadium screens and video walls."
msgstr "옥외 간판, 경기장 전광판, 비디오 월의 표준입니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.lead"
msgid "Each pixel is one light-emitting diode, or three for colour, and the picture is bright enough to read in direct sunlight."
msgstr "화소 하나가 발광 다이오드 하나, 컬러라면 세 개이며, 그림은 직사광선 아래에서도 읽을 수 있을 만큼 밝습니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.how.1"
msgid "A light-emitting diode (LED) turns electric current directly into light of one colour. Place a red, a green and a blue LED close together and vary the brightness of each. From far enough away, the three merge into any colour."
msgstr "발광 다이오드(LED)는 전류를 곧바로 한 가지 색의 빛으로 바꿉니다. 빨강, 초록, 파랑 LED를 가까이 붙여 놓고 저마다 밝기를 바꿉니다. 충분히 멀리서 보면 셋은 섞여 어떤 색이든 됩니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.how.2"
msgid "For decades there was no blue LED. Displays were red, and later red and green. Efficient blue and green LEDs arrived in the early 1990s. That work earned Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics, and full-colour video screens followed."
msgstr "수십 년 동안 파란 LED는 없었습니다. 디스플레이는 빨간색이었고, 나중에는 빨강과 초록이었습니다. 효율 좋은 파랑과 초록 LED는 1990년대 초에 나왔습니다. 이 일로 아카사키, 아마노, 나카무라는 2014년 노벨 물리학상을 받았고, 풀 컬러 영상 화면이 그 뒤를 이었습니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.good.1"
msgid "Is bright enough for direct sunlight"
msgstr "직사광선 아래에서도 충분히 밝다"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.good.2"
msgid "Can be any size and shape, because tiles join without visible seams"
msgstr "타일을 이음매가 보이지 않게 이어 붙일 수 있어, 어떤 크기와 모양도 된다"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.good.3"
msgid "Lasts a long time and is easy to service"
msgstr "수명이 길고 손보기 쉽다"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.bad.1"
msgid "Shows its individual lamps when you stand close"
msgstr "가까이 서면 램프가 하나하나 보인다"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.bad.2"
msgid "Costs much more as the lamps move closer together"
msgstr "램프 간격이 좁아질수록 값이 크게 오른다"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.bad.3"
msgid "Is hot and heavy at the size of a stadium screen"
msgstr "경기장 전광판만 한 크기에서는 뜨겁고 무겁다"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.look"
msgid "The model is a coarse outdoor tile. The digits are amber. The bar under them runs through all the colours of the spectrum, which needed the blue LED. Zoom in until the picture separates into single lamps."
msgstr "모델은 눈이 거친 옥외용 타일입니다. 숫자는 호박색입니다. 그 아래 막대는 스펙트럼의 모든 색을 지나가는데, 여기에는 파란 LED가 필요했습니다. 그림이 램프 하나하나로 갈라질 때까지 확대해 보세요."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.fact"
msgid "The Pulsar watch of 1972 showed the time in LEDs and cost $2,100, about as much as a small car. The diodes used so much power that the face stayed dark until the wearer pushed a button."
msgstr "1972년의 펄서 손목시계는 시각을 LED로 보여 주었고, 값은 소형차 한 대와 비슷한 2,100달러였습니다. 다이오드가 전력을 너무 많이 써서, 찬 사람이 단추를 누를 때까지 시계 면은 꺼져 있었습니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. People section: what Howard Borden, Gerald Pighini did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.people.1"
msgid "Designed the Hewlett-Packard numeric indicator of 1969."
msgstr "1969년의 휼렛 패커드 숫자 표시기를 설계했습니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. People section: what Isamu Akasaki, Hiroshi Amano, Shuji Nakamura did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.people.2"
msgid "Made efficient blue LEDs in Japan. Full-colour screens followed, and so did the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics."
msgstr "일본에서 효율 좋은 파란 LED를 만들었습니다. 풀 컬러 화면이 뒤따랐고, 2014년 노벨 물리학상도 뒤따랐습니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.view.below.label"
msgid "Stand under it"
msgstr "아래에 서기"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.view.below.hint"
msgid "Outdoor signs hang high. The shade over each row keeps the sun off the lamps and never hides them from below."
msgstr "옥외 간판은 높이 걸립니다. 줄마다 달린 차양이 램프에 햇빛이 드는 것을 막지만, 아래에서 보는 눈길은 가리지 않습니다."

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.view.lamps.label"
msgid "Go close to the lamps"
msgstr "램프 가까이 가기"

#: hall-3/led-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.led-display.view.lamps.hint"
msgid "Three lamps to a pixel: red, green and blue. Amber is red and green lit together, with the blue left dark."
msgstr "화소 하나에 램프 세 개: 빨강, 초록, 파랑. 호박색은 파랑을 끄고 빨강과 초록을 함께 켠 색입니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.oled.name"
msgid "OLED"
msgstr "OLED"

#: hall-3/oled
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.oled.aka"
msgid "Organic light-emitting diode"
msgstr "유기 발광 다이오드"

#: hall-3/oled
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.oled.origin"
msgid "Ching Wan Tang and Steven Van Slyke, Eastman Kodak, USA"
msgstr "칭 W. 탕, 스티븐 밴 슬라이크. 이스트먼 코닥, 미국"

#: hall-3/oled
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.oled.statusNote"
msgid "In most phones and watches, and in the more expensive televisions."
msgstr "대부분의 휴대폰과 시계, 그리고 값비싼 텔레비전에 쓰입니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.oled.lead"
msgid "A film of carbon-based material glows when current passes through it, and the film is thin enough to print on plastic."
msgstr "탄소를 바탕으로 한 재료의 막이 전류가 흐르면 빛나고, 그 막은 플라스틱에 인쇄할 수 있을 만큼 얇습니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.oled.how.1"
msgid "The carbon-based layer, called organic, lies between two electrodes, the contacts that carry the current. Negative charges enter the layer from one side and positive charges from the other. Where they meet in the layer, they release light. Every pixel is its own lamp."
msgstr "탄소를 바탕으로 한 층은 유기층이라고 하며, 전류를 나르는 접점인 두 전극 사이에 놓여 있습니다. 한쪽에서는 음전하가, 다른 쪽에서는 양전하가 층으로 들어옵니다. 둘이 층 안에서 만나는 곳에서 빛이 나옵니다. 화소 하나하나가 자기 자신의 램프입니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.oled.how.2"
msgid "An OLED panel therefore needs no backlight, the lamp behind a liquid crystal display. A pixel that should be black stays off, and the whole panel can be a fraction of a millimetre thick."
msgstr "그래서 OLED 패널에는 액정 디스플레이 뒤에 있는 램프인 백라이트가 필요 없습니다. 검어야 할 화소는 꺼진 채로 있고, 패널 전체가 1밀리미터의 몇 분의 일 두께일 수 있습니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.oled.good.1"
msgid "Shows true black, because an unlit pixel gives off no light"
msgstr "꺼진 화소는 빛을 전혀 내지 않으므로, 진짜 검은색을 보여 준다"

#: hall-3/oled
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.oled.good.2"
msgid "Responds very fast"
msgstr "아주 빠르게 반응한다"

#: hall-3/oled
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.oled.good.3"
msgid "Is thin and light, and can bend"
msgstr "얇고 가벼우며 휠 수 있다"

#: hall-3/oled
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.oled.bad.1"
msgid "Static pictures leave a ghost, called burn-in, because lit pixels age"
msgstr "켜진 화소가 노화하기 때문에, 멈춘 그림은 번인이라는 잔상을 남긴다"

#: hall-3/oled
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.oled.bad.2"
msgid "Blue wears out first"
msgstr "파랑이 가장 먼저 닳는다"

#: hall-3/oled
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.oled.bad.3"
msgid "Needs a seal against air and moisture"
msgstr "공기와 습기를 막는 봉지가 필요하다"

#: hall-3/oled
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.oled.look"
msgid "Switch the daylight off. The black parts of this panel disappear into the dark while the colours stay. No screen in the museum that has a backlight can do that."
msgstr "햇빛을 끄세요. 이 패널의 검은 부분은 어둠 속으로 사라지고 색만 남습니다. 박물관에서 백라이트가 있는 화면은 어느 것도 그렇게 하지 못합니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.oled.fact"
msgid "Ching Tang's job at Kodak was to make solar cells, and by his own account he failed. Two thin layers of his solar cell material gave off light instead, though the first device worked for only a few seconds at a time."
msgstr "코닥에서 칭 탕이 맡은 일은 태양 전지를 만드는 것이었고, 본인의 말로는 실패했습니다. 그 대신 태양 전지 재료의 얇은 층 두 장이 빛을 냈습니다. 다만 첫 소자는 한 번에 몇 초밖에 작동하지 않았습니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. People section: what Ching Wan Tang, Steven Van Slyke did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.oled.people.1"
msgid "Made the first efficient OLED at Eastman Kodak, published in 1987. Tang, born in Hong Kong, had set out to make solar cells."
msgstr "이스트먼 코닥에서 최초의 효율 좋은 OLED를 만들어 1987년에 발표했습니다. 홍콩에서 태어난 탕은 원래 태양 전지를 만들려고 했습니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.oled.view.seat.label"
msgid "Sit in front"
msgstr "정면에 앉기"

#: hall-3/oled
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.oled.view.seat.hint"
msgid "Switch the daylight off. The black of the panel goes with it and only the colours are left."
msgstr "햇빛을 끄세요. 패널의 검은색도 함께 사라지고 색만 남습니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.oled.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The layers of an organic light-emitting device, from the Kodak patent of 1989 by Ching Tang and Steven VanSlyke. Positive and negative charges enter from the two electrodes and meet in the thin organic layers between."
msgstr "유기 발광 소자의 층. 칭 탕과 스티븐 밴 슬라이크의 1989년 코닥 특허에서. 양전하와 음전하가 두 전극에서 들어와, 그 사이의 얇은 유기층에서 만납니다."

#: hall-3/oled
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.oled.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Two diagrams: a slab wired to a voltage source, with a row of minus signs above a row of plus signs inside it, and a stack of five hatched layers."
msgstr "도해 두 개: 전압원에 배선된 판 안에서 빼기 기호의 줄이 더하기 기호의 줄 위에 있는 그림과, 빗금 친 층 다섯 개를 쌓은 그림."

#: hall-3/microled
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.microled.name"
msgid "MicroLED"
msgstr "마이크로 LED"

#: hall-3/microled
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.microled.origin"
msgid "Hongxing Jiang and Jingyu Lin, Kansas State University, USA"
msgstr "훙싱 장, 징위 린. 캔자스 주립 대학교, 미국"

#: hall-3/microled
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.microled.statusNote"
msgid "Sold as large tiled video walls. Small consumer screens remain out of reach: Apple reportedly cancelled a microLED watch in 2024."
msgstr "타일을 이어 붙인 대형 비디오 월로 팔립니다. 소비자용 작은 화면은 아직 손이 닿지 않습니다. 애플은 2024년에 마이크로 LED 시계를 취소한 것으로 전해졌습니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.microled.lead"
msgid "An LED wall is shrunk until each lamp is too small to see, which keeps the brightness and avoids the ghost pictures called burn-in."
msgstr "LED 벽을 램프 하나하나가 보이지 않을 만큼 줄인 것입니다. 밝기는 그대로이고, 번인이라는 잔상도 피합니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.microled.how.1"
msgid "The pixels are LEDs like those in a stadium screen, made microscopic. They are inorganic, which means not carbon-based. They are brighter and last longer than the organic material of an OLED, and they do not burn in."
msgstr "화소는 경기장 전광판에 있는 것과 같은 LED를 현미경으로나 보일 크기로 만든 것입니다. 무기물, 곧 탄소를 바탕으로 하지 않는 재료입니다. OLED의 유기 재료보다 밝고 오래가며, 번인이 생기지 않습니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.microled.how.2"
msgid "The difficulty is assembly. The maker must pick up, place and test millions of separate chips, and replace the faulty ones. One published estimate puts the step of moving the chips into place, called transfer, at 18 days for a single phone screen. That is why the product on sale is a wall made of tiles."
msgstr "어려운 것은 조립입니다. 제조사는 수백만 개의 따로따로인 칩을 집어서 놓고 검사하고, 불량품을 갈아 끼워야 합니다. 칩을 제자리로 옮기는 공정을 전사라고 하는데, 발표된 한 추정치는 휴대폰 화면 한 장에 18일이 걸린다고 봅니다. 그래서 팔리는 제품은 타일로 된 벽입니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.microled.good.1"
msgid "Is very bright and lasts a very long time"
msgstr "아주 밝고 아주 오래간다"

#: hall-3/microled
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.microled.good.2"
msgid "Does not burn in"
msgstr "번인이 생기지 않는다"

#: hall-3/microled
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.microled.good.3"
msgid "Comes in modules, so a wall can be any size"
msgstr "모듈식이어서 벽을 어떤 크기로도 만들 수 있다"

#: hall-3/microled
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.microled.bad.1"
msgid "Needs millions of tiny chips moved into place, which is slow and costly"
msgstr "아주 작은 칩 수백만 개를 제자리로 옮겨야 해서 느리고 비싸다"

#: hall-3/microled
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.microled.bad.2"
msgid "Needs every faulty chip found and replaced"
msgstr "불량 칩을 모두 찾아 갈아 끼워야 한다"

#: hall-3/microled
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.microled.bad.3"
msgid "Has a price for companies, not for homes"
msgstr "값이 기업용이지 가정용이 아니다"

#: hall-3/microled
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.microled.look"
msgid "One tile is out of the wall. Look at it: it leans against the stand, dark, with the suction lifter still on it. The empty place in the wall shows the circuit board behind the tiles and the magnets that hold a tile. A wall is repaired in this way, one square at a time."
msgstr "타일 하나가 벽에서 빠져 있습니다. 보세요. 흡착식 분리 도구가 붙은 채로, 꺼진 상태로 받침대에 기대어 있습니다. 벽의 빈자리에는 타일 뒤의 회로 기판과, 타일을 붙잡는 자석이 보입니다. 벽은 이렇게 네모 한 장씩 고칩니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.microled.fact"
msgid "The inventors are a married couple, and the idea began as a question at their dinner table: what would happen to an LED shrunk to the scale of micrometres? Their first patent expired just as the first microLED screens reached consumers."
msgstr "발명자는 부부이고, 이 생각은 저녁 식탁에서 나온 질문으로 시작되었습니다. LED를 마이크로미터 크기로 줄이면 어떻게 될까? 두 사람의 첫 특허는 최초의 마이크로 LED 화면이 소비자에게 닿던 바로 그 무렵에 만료되었습니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. People section: what Hongxing Jiang, Jingyu Lin did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.microled.people.1"
msgid "Physicists and a married couple. They made the first microLEDs at Kansas State University in 2000."
msgstr "물리학자이자 부부입니다. 2000년 캔자스 주립 대학교에서 최초의 마이크로 LED를 만들었습니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.microled.view.socket.label"
msgid "Look into the socket"
msgstr "빈자리 들여다보기"

#: hall-3/microled
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.microled.view.socket.hint"
msgid "A tile has been lifted out. Behind it: the backplane, power and data sockets, and the four magnets that hold a tile."
msgstr "타일 하나를 들어냈습니다. 그 뒤에는 뒷면 기판, 전원과 데이터 소켓, 타일을 붙잡는 자석 네 개가 있습니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.microled.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "Micro-size LEDs, from the Kansas State University patent of 2002. Each one is a small disc of gallium nitride on a shared base. The enlargement gives its width: 5 to 20 thousandths of a millimetre."
msgstr "마이크로 크기의 LED. 캔자스 주립 대학교의 2002년 특허에서. 하나하나는 공통의 바탕 위에 놓인 질화 갈륨의 작은 원판입니다. 확대 그림에 너비가 적혀 있습니다. 5에서 20마이크로미터입니다."

#: hall-3/microled
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.microled.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Perspective drawing of four small ringed discs and a contact pad on a layered slab, with one disc enlarged and marked 5 to 20 micrometres across."
msgstr "층을 이룬 판 위에 고리가 있는 작은 원판 네 개와 접점 하나가 놓인 투시도. 원판 하나가 확대되어 있고, 너비 5에서 20마이크로미터라고 적혀 있습니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.name"
msgid "Quantum-dot display"
msgstr "양자점 디스플레이"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.aka"
msgid "QLED, QD-OLED"
msgstr "QLED, QD-OLED"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.origin"
msgid "Vicki Colvin, M. C. Schlamp and Paul Alivisatos, University of California, Berkeley: an LED made with quantum dots"
msgstr "비키 콜빈, M. C. 슐람프, 폴 알리비사토스. 캘리포니아 대학교 버클리: 양자점으로 만든 LED"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.statusNote"
msgid "In televisions and monitors as a film that turns blue light into red and green. Screens whose dots are driven directly by a current have been shown as prototypes."
msgstr "텔레비전과 모니터에서, 파란빛을 빨강과 초록으로 바꾸는 필름으로 쓰입니다. 양자점을 전류로 직접 구동하는 화면은 시제품으로 공개되었습니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.lead"
msgid "Crystals a few millionths of a millimetre across glow in a colour set by their size alone: the small ones green, the larger ones red."
msgstr "너비가 백만분의 몇 밀리미터인 결정이, 오직 크기로 정해지는 색으로 빛납니다. 작은 것은 초록, 큰 것은 빨강입니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.how.1"
msgid "A quantum dot is a crystal of semiconductor so small that it no longer behaves like the same material in bulk. The smaller the dot, the more tightly it holds its electrons, and the bluer the light it gives off. Dots of one material can therefore be made to glow in different colours, simply by growing them to different sizes."
msgstr "양자점은 반도체 결정인데, 너무 작아서 같은 재료의 덩어리처럼 행동하지 않습니다. 점이 작을수록 전자를 더 단단히 붙잡고, 내놓는 빛은 더 파랗습니다. 그래서 한 가지 재료의 점도, 다른 크기로 키우기만 하면 다른 색으로 빛나게 할 수 있습니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.how.2"
msgid "In most screens that use them, the dots do not make the picture on their own. Blue light from LEDs or from an OLED layer shines on a film of dots, and the dots turn part of it into very pure red and green. In 1994 a group at Berkeley took another route and made a layer of dots glow by passing a current through it. Screens built that way are the next step."
msgstr "양자점을 쓰는 화면 대부분에서, 점은 혼자서 그림을 만들지 않습니다. LED나 OLED 층에서 나온 파란빛이 점의 필름을 비추고, 점은 그 일부를 아주 순수한 빨강과 초록으로 바꿉니다. 1994년에 버클리의 한 연구진은 다른 길을 택해, 점의 층에 전류를 흘려 빛나게 했습니다. 그렇게 만드는 화면이 다음 단계입니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.good.1"
msgid "Gives very pure red, green and blue, and so a wide range of colours"
msgstr "아주 순수한 빨강, 초록, 파랑을 내므로 색의 범위가 넓다"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.good.2"
msgid "One material makes several colours, and only the size of the crystal changes"
msgstr "한 가지 재료로 여러 색을 내고, 바뀌는 것은 결정의 크기뿐이다"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.good.3"
msgid "Turns blue light into another colour, where a colour filter would throw most of the light away"
msgstr "컬러 필터라면 빛의 대부분을 버렸을 자리에서, 파란빛을 다른 색으로 바꾼다"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.bad.1"
msgid "The dots with the purest colours long depended on cadmium, a toxic metal"
msgstr "색이 가장 순수한 점은 오랫동안 독성 금속인 카드뮴에 기대었다"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.bad.2"
msgid "Air and moisture damage the dots, so they have to be sealed in"
msgstr "공기와 습기가 점을 상하게 하므로 봉해 넣어야 한다"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.bad.3"
msgid "Blue dots driven by a current still wear out too quickly"
msgstr "전류로 구동하는 파란 점은 아직 너무 빨리 닳는다"

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.look"
msgid "In front is one pixel, cut open and enlarged many thousands of times. Blue light rises from the lamps on the board. In the left well, large dots turn it red. In the middle well, small dots turn it green. The right well has no dots, and the blue passes through. The bar on top shows the three colours mixed, as the eye sees them."
msgstr "앞에 있는 것은 잘라 열어 수천 배로 확대한 화소 하나입니다. 기판의 램프에서 파란빛이 올라옵니다. 왼쪽 우물에서는 큰 점이 그 빛을 빨강으로 바꿉니다. 가운데 우물에서는 작은 점이 초록으로 바꿉니다. 오른쪽 우물에는 점이 없어서 파란빛이 그대로 지나갑니다. 위의 막대는 세 가지 색이 섞여 눈에 보이는 색을 나타냅니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.fact"
msgid "The colour of quantum dots was first seen in glass. Aleksey Yekimov coloured glass with tiny crystals of copper chloride and showed that the colour depended on the size of the crystals. He shared the 2023 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it."
msgstr "양자점의 색은 유리에서 처음 관찰되었습니다. 알렉세이 예키모프는 염화 구리의 미세한 결정으로 유리에 색을 내고, 그 색이 결정의 크기에 달려 있음을 보였습니다. 그는 이 일로 2023년 노벨 화학상을 공동 수상했습니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. People section: what Vicki Colvin, M. C. Schlamp, Paul Alivisatos did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.people.1"
msgid "Made an LED from quantum dots and a plastic that conducts, at the University of California, Berkeley. Colvin is the first author of the paper of 1994."
msgstr "캘리포니아 대학교 버클리에서, 양자점과 전기가 통하는 플라스틱으로 LED를 만들었습니다. 콜빈은 1994년 논문의 제1저자입니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. People section: what Aleksey Yekimov did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.people.2"
msgid "Russian physicist. He showed in coloured glass that the size of a tiny crystal sets its colour."
msgstr "러시아의 물리학자. 색유리 안에서, 미세한 결정의 크기가 그 색을 정한다는 것을 보였습니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. People section: what Louis Brus did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.people.3"
msgid "Proved the same effect in particles floating freely in a liquid."
msgstr "액체 속에 자유롭게 떠 있는 입자에서 같은 효과를 확인했습니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. People section: what Moungi Bawendi did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.people.4"
msgid "American, French and Tunisian chemist. In 1993 he found the way to make dots of almost perfect quality. The three shared the 2023 Nobel Prize in Chemistry."
msgstr "미국, 프랑스, 튀니지에 뿌리를 둔 화학자. 1993년에 거의 완벽한 품질의 점을 만드는 방법을 찾아냈습니다. 세 사람은 2023년 노벨 화학상을 함께 받았습니다."

#: hall-3/quantum-dot
#. People section: what Xiaogang Peng, Yizheng Jin did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.quantum-dot.people.5"
msgid "At Zhejiang University in 2014 they made a quantum-dot diode from liquids, layer on layer. It glowed deep red, turned a fifth of its electrons into light and was rated for over 100,000 hours."
msgstr "2014년 저장 대학교에서, 액체를 한 층씩 쌓아 양자점 다이오드를 만들었습니다. 짙은 빨간빛을 내고, 전자의 5분의 1을 빛으로 바꾸었으며, 수명은 10만 시간이 넘는 것으로 평가되었습니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.name"
msgid "Thin-film electroluminescent panel"
msgstr "박막 전계 발광 패널"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.aka"
msgid "TFEL, EL display"
msgstr "TFEL, EL 디스플레이"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.origin"
msgid "Commercialised in the 1980s by Sharp (Japan), Finlux (Finland) and Planar (USA)"
msgstr "1980년대에 샤프(일본), 핀룩스(핀란드), 플래너(미국)가 상용화했습니다"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.statusNote"
msgid "One manufacturer remains. Used where a screen must survive cold, vibration and decades of service."
msgstr "제조사는 한 곳만 남았습니다. 화면이 추위와 진동, 수십 년의 사용을 견뎌야 하는 곳에 쓰입니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.lead"
msgid "Thin layers on glass glow yellow at every point where two crossed conducting strips put a voltage across them."
msgstr "유리 위의 얇은 층들이, 엇갈린 두 도체 띠가 전압을 거는 점마다 노랗게 빛납니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.how.1"
msgid "A film of zinc sulphide with a little manganese added lies between insulating layers. Outside those layers are two sets of conducting strips, the electrodes, at right angles to each other. A high alternating voltage across any crossing excites the film at that point, and the film gives off a yellow-orange light. This effect is called electroluminescence."
msgstr "망가니즈를 조금 넣은 황화 아연의 막이 절연층 사이에 놓여 있습니다. 그 바깥에는 서로 직각으로 엇갈린 두 벌의 도체 띠, 곧 전극이 있습니다. 어느 교차점에든 높은 교류 전압을 걸면 그 점의 막이 들뜨고, 막은 누르스름한 주황색 빛을 냅니다. 이 현상을 전계 발광이라고 합니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.how.2"
msgid "The whole stack is solid and a few thousandths of a millimetre thick, and it contains nothing that can leak, freeze or burn out. Electroluminescent digits flew on the Apollo guidance computer and in Concorde, and they showed the floor number in lifts for decades."
msgstr "쌓인 층 전체는 고체이고 두께는 천분의 몇 밀리미터이며, 새거나 얼거나 타 버릴 것이 아무것도 들어 있지 않습니다. 전계 발광 숫자 표시는 아폴로의 유도 컴퓨터와 콩코드에 실려 날았고, 엘리베이터에서는 수십 년 동안 층수를 알렸습니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 4. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.good.1"
msgid "Works from deep cold to the heat of an engine compartment"
msgstr "혹한에서 엔진실의 열기까지 어디서나 작동한다"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 4. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.good.2"
msgid "Is sharp, fast and readable at any angle"
msgstr "선명하고 빠르며, 어느 각도에서나 읽을 수 있다"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 4. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.good.3"
msgid "Lasts for decades"
msgstr "수십 년을 간다"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. What it is good at, point 4 of 4. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.good.4"
msgid "Can be made transparent"
msgstr "투명하게 만들 수 있다"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.bad.1"
msgid "Shows one colour only, in practice"
msgstr "실제로는 한 가지 색만 보여 준다"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.bad.2"
msgid "Needs a high voltage to drive it"
msgstr "구동하려면 높은 전압이 필요하다"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.bad.3"
msgid "Is dimmer and costlier than the liquid crystal panels that took over its markets"
msgstr "그 시장을 차지한 액정 패널보다 어둡고 비싸다"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.look"
msgid "The model is an instrument panel, complete with its frame. Look at the colour of the display. No filter makes that colour: it is the light that manganese in zinc sulphide gives off."
msgstr "모델은 틀까지 갖춘 계기판입니다. 표시의 색을 보세요. 필터가 만든 색이 아닙니다. 황화 아연 속의 망가니즈가 내는 빛입니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.fact"
msgid "Atomic layer deposition, which builds a film one layer of atoms at a time and now helps to make computer chips, was devised by a Finnish physicist in 1974 for one purpose: to make these panels. The first panel showed flights at Helsinki Airport."
msgstr "원자층 증착은 막을 원자 한 층씩 쌓는 방법으로, 지금은 컴퓨터 칩을 만드는 데도 쓰입니다. 이것은 1974년에 핀란드의 한 물리학자가 오직 한 가지 목적, 곧 이 패널을 만들려고 고안한 것입니다. 첫 패널은 헬싱키 공항에서 항공편을 알렸습니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. People section: what Tuomo Suntola did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.people.1"
msgid "Finnish physicist. In 1974 he devised atomic layer deposition, which grows a film one layer of atoms at a time, to make these panels."
msgstr "핀란드의 물리학자. 1974년에, 이 패널을 만들려고, 막을 원자 한 층씩 키우는 원자층 증착을 고안했습니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. People section: what Dawon Kahng did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.people.2"
msgid "Born in Seoul. In 1968 at Bell Laboratories he showed that thin films of compounds like zinc sulphide glow when fast electrons strike metal atoms placed in them. These panels make light that way."
msgstr "서울에서 태어났습니다. 1968년 벨 연구소에서, 황화 아연 같은 화합물의 얇은 막은 그 안에 넣은 금속 원자에 빠른 전자가 부딪치면 빛난다는 것을 보였습니다. 이 패널도 그렇게 빛을 냅니다."

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.view.dots.label"
msgid "Go close to the dots"
msgstr "점 가까이 가기"

#: hall-3/thin-film-el
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.thin-film-el.view.dots.hint"
msgid "Each dot is one crossing of two electrode strips. The yellow is what the film itself emits, not a filter."
msgstr "점 하나하나가 두 전극 띠의 교차점입니다. 노란색은 막 자체가 내는 빛이지, 필터가 아닙니다."

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.name"
msgid "Transparent display"
msgstr "투명 디스플레이"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.aka"
msgid "See-through display"
msgstr "시스루 디스플레이"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.origin"
msgid "First commercial products: a phone with a clear screen in 2009, transparent LCD panels from 2011"
msgstr "최초의 상용 제품: 2009년에 화면이 투명한 휴대폰, 2011년부터 투명 LCD 패널"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.statusNote"
msgid "Used in shop windows, museum cases and vehicle windscreens."
msgstr "상점 진열창, 박물관 진열장, 자동차 앞 유리에 쓰입니다."

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.lead"
msgid "A screen that you can see through must either add light to the view behind it or take light away."
msgstr "건너편이 비쳐 보이는 화면은, 뒤에 있는 풍경에 빛을 더하거나 거기서 빛을 덜어 내야 합니다."

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.how.1"
msgid "A see-through panel can work in two ways. An emissive panel, OLED or microLED, makes its own light. It has gaps between its pixels and adds its light on top of whatever is behind it. It cannot show black, because black would mean removing light that comes from the room."
msgstr "투명한 패널은 두 가지 방식으로 작동할 수 있습니다. 발광형 패널은 OLED나 마이크로 LED로, 스스로 빛을 냅니다. 화소 사이에 틈이 있고, 뒤에 무엇이 있든 그 위에 자기 빛을 더합니다. 검은색은 보여 주지 못합니다. 검은색을 내려면 방에서 오는 빛을 없애야 하기 때문입니다."

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.how.2"
msgid "An absorptive panel is a liquid crystal display with its backlight, the lamp behind it, taken away. It can darken the view but makes no light, so it needs a bright box or a window behind it. A third approach is to project onto a clear film that scatters only the light of the projector."
msgstr "흡수형 패널은 뒤의 램프인 백라이트를 떼어 낸 액정 디스플레이입니다. 풍경을 어둡게 할 수는 있지만 빛을 내지는 못하므로, 뒤에 밝은 상자나 창이 필요합니다. 세 번째 방법은 프로젝터의 빛만 흩뜨리는 투명 필름에 비추는 것입니다."

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.good.1"
msgid "Puts information on the object itself, not beside it"
msgstr "정보를 물건 옆이 아니라 물건 바로 위에 놓는다"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.good.2"
msgid "Emissive types glow against a dark scene"
msgstr "발광형은 어두운 장면을 배경으로 빛난다"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.bad.1"
msgid "Emissive types cannot show black, and they look pale against a bright background"
msgstr "발광형은 검은색을 보여 주지 못하고, 밝은 배경 앞에서는 흐릿해 보인다"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.bad.2"
msgid "Absorptive types make no light of their own"
msgstr "흡수형은 스스로 빛을 내지 못한다"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.bad.3"
msgid "Is never as clear as plain glass"
msgstr "맨유리만큼 투명해지는 일은 없다"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.look"
msgid "The model is a shop case whose front pane is the screen. An alarm clock stands inside, and the pane draws the same time in figures over the clock. Switch the daylight off and on. The graphics are strong in the dark and faint in daylight, and the clock behind them stays visible all the time."
msgstr "모델은 앞 유리가 화면인 진열장입니다. 안에는 자명종이 서 있고, 유리는 같은 시각을 숫자로 시계 위에 그립니다. 햇빛을 껐다 켰다 해 보세요. 그림은 어둠 속에서는 또렷하고 햇빛 속에서는 희미하며, 그 뒤의 시계는 내내 보입니다."

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.fact"
msgid "In 2020 metro trains on one line in Beijing and one in Shenzhen were given 55-inch transparent OLED screens in their windows. The screens showed passengers train times, transfers, flights, weather and news."
msgstr "2020년에 베이징의 지하철 한 노선과 선전의 한 노선에서, 열차 창에 55인치 투명 OLED 화면이 달렸습니다. 화면은 승객에게 열차 시각, 환승, 항공편, 날씨, 뉴스를 보여 주었습니다."

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.view.window.label"
msgid "Stand at the window"
msgstr "유리 앞에 서기"

#: hall-3/transparent-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.transparent-display.view.window.hint"
msgid "The figures are light added on the glass, and the clock stands behind it. Move your head and they slide apart."
msgstr "숫자는 유리 위에 더해진 빛이고, 시계는 그 뒤에 서 있습니다. 머리를 움직이면 둘이 서로 어긋납니다."

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.name"
msgid "Flexible display"
msgstr "플렉시블 디스플레이"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.aka"
msgid "Rollable, foldable"
msgstr "롤러블, 폴더블"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.yearText"
msgid "the 1970s"
msgstr "1970년대"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.origin"
msgid "Flexible electronic paper at the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center, USA. Curved OLED phones from 2013 and folding ones from 2019"
msgstr "휘는 전자 종이: 제록스 팰로앨토 연구소, 미국. 휜 OLED 휴대폰은 2013년부터, 접는 휴대폰은 2019년부터"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.statusNote"
msgid "Curved and folding phones are on sale. Screens that roll up completely are much rarer."
msgstr "휜 휴대폰과 접는 휴대폰이 팔리고 있습니다. 완전히 말리는 화면은 훨씬 드뭅니다."

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.lead"
msgid "The thin films of a flat panel sit on plastic instead of glass, so that the sheet can bend."
msgstr "평판의 얇은 막들이 유리 대신 플라스틱 위에 놓여 있어서, 판을 휠 수 있습니다."

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.how.1"
msgid "A display is a stack of layers a few thousandths of a millimetre thick. On glass, the stack is rigid. On a plastic film, the same stack can curve, fold or roll, as long as nothing in it cracks and nothing lets air in."
msgstr "디스플레이는 두께가 천분의 몇 밀리미터인 층을 쌓은 것입니다. 유리 위에서는 그 층이 단단합니다. 플라스틱 필름 위에서는 같은 층을 휘고, 접고, 말 수 있습니다. 안에서 아무것도 깨지지 않고 공기가 들어가지 않는 한에서입니다."

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.how.2"
msgid "Electronic paper was the first display to bend, in the laboratory in the 1970s and in products in the 2000s. OLED followed, with curved phone screens in 2013 and folding screens in 2019."
msgstr "가장 먼저 휜 디스플레이는 전자 종이로, 연구실에서는 1970년대, 제품에서는 2000년대의 일이었습니다. OLED가 그 뒤를 이어, 2013년에 휜 휴대폰 화면이, 2019년에 접는 화면이 나왔습니다."

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.good.1"
msgid "Packs a large screen into a small space"
msgstr "큰 화면을 작은 공간에 넣는다"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.good.2"
msgid "Is light and hard to shatter"
msgstr "가볍고 잘 깨지지 않는다"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.good.3"
msgid "Can follow a curved surface"
msgstr "휜 표면을 따라갈 수 있다"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.bad.1"
msgid "Wears with every fold, so makers specify how tightly and how many times it may bend"
msgstr "접을 때마다 닳으므로, 제조사는 얼마나 세게 몇 번까지 휘어도 되는지 정해 둔다"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.bad.2"
msgid "Scratches where glass would not, because the surface is plastic"
msgstr "표면이 플라스틱이어서, 유리라면 생기지 않을 흠집이 생긴다"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.bad.3"
msgid "Is hard to seal, because the sheet moves"
msgstr "판이 움직이기 때문에 봉하기가 어렵다"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.look"
msgid "The sheet rises out of a roller housing in an S-shaped curve. Turn the model to see the back: there is nothing there but film."
msgstr "판은 롤러가 든 통에서 S자 곡선을 그리며 올라옵니다. 모델을 돌려 뒷면을 보세요. 거기에는 필름 말고 아무것도 없습니다."

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.fact"
msgid "A flexible OLED screen is made on a sheet of rigid glass. Plastic is coated onto the glass, the display is built on top, and at the end an ultraviolet laser shines through the glass and frees the finished screen."
msgstr "휘는 OLED 화면은 단단한 유리판 위에서 만듭니다. 유리에 플라스틱을 입히고, 그 위에 디스플레이를 만들고, 마지막에 자외선 레이저를 유리 너머로 쏘아 완성된 화면을 떼어 냅니다."

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. People section: what Nicholas Sheridon did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.people.1"
msgid "Made Gyricon, the first flexible electronic paper, at the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center."
msgstr "제록스 팰로앨토 연구소에서, 최초의 휘는 전자 종이인 자이리콘을 만들었습니다."

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.view.edge.label"
msgid "Look along the edge"
msgstr "가장자리를 따라 보기"

#: hall-3/flexible-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.flexible-display.view.edge.hint"
msgid "The sheet is the whole display: thin films on plastic, with nothing behind. It winds on to the drum below."
msgstr "이 판이 디스플레이의 전부입니다. 플라스틱 위의 얇은 막일 뿐, 뒤에는 아무것도 없습니다. 아래의 드럼에 감깁니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.name"
msgid "Camera obscura"
msgstr "카메라 옵스큐라"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.aka"
msgid "Dark chamber, pinhole image"
msgstr "어둠상자, 바늘구멍 상"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.yearText"
msgid "about 300 BCE"
msgstr "기원전 300년경"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.origin"
msgid "One of the earliest known accounts is in the Mohist Canons, compiled in China between the late fourth and the middle of the third century BCE. No inventor is known"
msgstr "알려진 가장 오래된 기록 가운데 하나가 『묵경』에 있으며, 이 글은 기원전 4세기 말에서 기원전 3세기 중반 사이에 중국에서 엮였습니다. 발명자는 알려져 있지 않습니다"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.statusNote"
msgid "Built today as a school science project and used by artists. The photographic camera descends from it."
msgstr "오늘날에는 학교의 과학 실습으로 만들고, 미술가들도 씁니다. 사진기는 여기에서 나왔습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.lead"
msgid "A small hole in the wall of a dark room lets in light from outside, and the view appears on the opposite wall, upside down."
msgstr "어두운 방의 벽에 뚫린 작은 구멍으로 바깥의 빛이 들어오고, 맞은편 벽에 바깥 풍경이 거꾸로 나타납니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 4.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.how.1"
msgid "Light travels in straight lines. Every point of the scene outside sends light in all directions, but only a narrow bundle from each point passes the hole, and it lands on its own spot on the far wall. The spots together are the picture. All the lines cross in the hole. So what is high outside lands low, and what lies to one side of the hole lands on the other."
msgstr "빛은 곧게 나아갑니다. 바깥 풍경의 모든 점은 사방으로 빛을 보내지만, 구멍을 지나는 것은 점마다 가느다란 빛다발 하나뿐이고, 그 빛다발은 안쪽 벽의 제자리에 떨어집니다. 그 자리들이 모여 상이 됩니다. 모든 선은 구멍에서 엇갈립니다. 그래서 바깥에서 높은 것은 낮은 곳에, 구멍의 한쪽에 있는 것은 반대쪽에 떨어집니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 4.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.how.2"
msgid "The size of the hole is a compromise. A small hole gives a sharp picture, because each point outside lights only a small spot, but it lets in little light. A larger hole gives a brighter picture and a more blurred one. A hole that is too small blurs the picture again, because light spreads when it passes a very narrow opening."
msgstr "구멍의 크기는 절충입니다. 구멍이 작으면 바깥의 한 점이 작은 자리만 비추므로 상이 또렷하지만, 들어오는 빛이 적습니다. 구멍이 크면 상이 더 밝고 더 흐립니다. 구멍이 너무 작아도 상은 다시 흐려집니다. 빛은 아주 좁은 틈을 지나면 퍼지기 때문입니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. How it works, paragraph 3 of 4.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.how.3"
msgid "A lens in the opening changes the terms. The opening can be wide, and the lens gathers the light from each point into one spot again. In 1568 the Venetian Daniele Barbaro described the convex glass of an old man's spectacles set in the hole, and a cover over part of the lens to make the picture clearer."
msgstr "구멍에 렌즈를 끼우면 조건이 달라집니다. 구멍을 넓혀도 렌즈가 각 점에서 온 빛을 다시 한 점으로 모읍니다. 1568년 베네치아의 다니엘레 바르바로는 노인용 안경의 볼록 렌즈를 구멍에 끼우는 것과, 상을 더 선명하게 하려고 렌즈의 일부를 가리는 것을 기록했습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. How it works, paragraph 4 of 4.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.how.4"
msgid "The Mohist Canons say that the shadow is turned over because the light crosses at a point, travelling like arrows shot from a bow. Early in the eleventh century Ibn al-Haytham, writing in Cairo, described candles set in front of an opening into a dark place: each spot of light they cast appears directly opposite one candle. He also studied the crescent that the sun casts through a small hole during an eclipse. The Latin name means dark chamber. Johannes Kepler used it first, in 1604."
msgstr "『묵경』은 빛이 한 점에서 엇갈리기 때문에 그림자가 뒤집힌다고 말하며, 빛이 활에서 쏜 화살처럼 나아간다고 합니다. 11세기 초 카이로에서 글을 쓰던 이븐 알하이삼은 어두운 곳으로 난 구멍 앞에 촛불들을 놓는 일을 기록했습니다. 촛불이 만드는 빛의 점은 저마다 촛불 하나의 바로 맞은편에 나타납니다. 그는 일식 때 해가 작은 구멍으로 드리우는 초승달 모양도 연구했습니다. 라틴어 이름은 어두운 방이라는 뜻입니다. 이 이름은 요하네스 케플러가 1604년에 처음 썼습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.good.1"
msgid "Needs no power and has no part that moves or wears out"
msgstr "전력이 필요 없고, 움직이거나 닳는 부품이 없다"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.good.2"
msgid "Shows the scene in its own colours and with all its movement"
msgstr "풍경을 제 빛깔 그대로, 움직임까지 모두 보여 준다"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.good.3"
msgid "Shows near and far things equally sharp when the opening is a bare hole"
msgstr "구멍만 있을 때는 가까운 것과 먼 것이 똑같이 또렷하다"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 4. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.bad.1"
msgid "Gives a dim picture, because a hole small enough for a sharp picture lets in very little light"
msgstr "상이 어둡다. 또렷한 상을 낼 만큼 작은 구멍은 빛을 아주 조금만 들여보낸다"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 4. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.bad.2"
msgid "Works only in a darkened room and only while there is light outside"
msgstr "어둡게 한 방에서만, 그리고 바깥이 밝은 동안에만 쓸 수 있다"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 4. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.bad.3"
msgid "Turns the picture upside down"
msgstr "상이 거꾸로 뒤집힌다"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Where it fails, point 4 of 4. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.bad.4"
msgid "Keeps nothing: the picture is there only while the light is"
msgstr "아무것도 남기지 않는다. 상은 빛이 있는 동안에만 있다"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.look"
msgid "The model is a small chamber of boards, blackened inside. The hole is in the wall that faces the windows, and the wall opposite is a sheet of thin paper. On it is the view from those windows, upside down: the sky at the bottom, the mountain and the town above. From outside, the roof and the nearer side wall are left off so that you can look in. Press the Step inside button to see the chamber closed and dark. A brass disc outside turns from a small hole to a lens and back: with the lens the picture is brighter and sharper. Then switch the daylight off. The chamber makes no light, and the picture goes with the day. Only the lamps of the town stay, as faint points, while the lens is in place."
msgstr "모델은 널빤지로 만든 작은 방으로, 안쪽을 검게 칠했습니다. 구멍은 창을 향한 벽에 있고, 맞은편 벽은 얇은 종이 한 장입니다. 그 종이에 창밖 풍경이 거꾸로 비칩니다. 하늘이 아래에, 산과 마을이 위에 있습니다. 밖에서 볼 때는 안을 들여다볼 수 있도록 지붕과 가까운 쪽 옆벽을 떼어 두었습니다. 안으로 들어가기 버튼을 누르면 닫힌 어두운 방을 볼 수 있습니다. 바깥에서는 황동 원판이 작은 구멍에서 렌즈로, 다시 구멍으로 돕니다. 렌즈일 때 상이 더 밝고 또렷합니다. 그런 다음 햇빛을 끄세요. 이 방은 빛을 내지 않으므로 상은 낮의 빛과 함께 사라집니다. 렌즈가 제자리에 있는 동안에만 마을의 불빛이 희미한 점으로 남습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.fact"
msgid "A gap between the leaves of a tree works as the hole of a camera obscura. During a partial eclipse of the sun, the patches of light on the ground under the tree turn into crescents, each one a picture of the sun."
msgstr "나뭇잎 사이의 틈은 카메라 옵스큐라의 구멍과 같은 구실을 합니다. 부분 일식 때 나무 아래 땅에 떨어지는 빛의 점들은 초승달 모양이 됩니다. 그 하나하나가 해의 상입니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. People section: what Mozi did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.people.1"
msgid "Taught in China about 430 BCE, and the Mohist school is named after him. Its later members wrote the Canons, which hold an early account of a picture turned over by a small hole."
msgstr "기원전 430년경 중국에서 가르쳤고, 묵가는 그의 이름을 딴 학파입니다. 후대의 묵가가 엮은 『묵경』에는 작은 구멍 때문에 뒤집히는 상에 관한 이른 기록이 있습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. People section: what Ibn al-Haytham did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.people.2"
msgid "Born in Basra about 965, died in Cairo about 1040. In a dark chamber he studied how light passes through an opening, and how the sun in eclipse looks through a small hole."
msgstr "965년경 바스라에서 태어나 1040년경 카이로에서 세상을 떠났습니다. 어두운 방에서 빛이 구멍을 지나는 모습과, 일식 때의 해가 작은 구멍을 통해 어떻게 보이는지를 연구했습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. People section: what Gemma Frisius did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.people.3"
msgid "Physician and instrument maker. He watched the solar eclipse of 24 January 1544 at Leuven in a darkened room. His book of 1545 has the oldest known printed drawing of one."
msgstr "의사이자 기구 제작자. 1544년 1월 24일의 일식을 뢰번에서 어둡게 한 방 안에서 관측했습니다. 1545년에 낸 책에는 이런 방을 그린, 알려진 가장 오래된 인쇄 그림이 실려 있습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. People section: what Daniele Barbaro did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.people.4"
msgid "Venetian nobleman. In 1568 he described a spectacle lens set in the hole, and a cover over part of the lens to make the picture clearer."
msgstr "베네치아의 귀족. 1568년에 구멍에 끼운 안경 렌즈와, 상을 더 선명하게 하려고 렌즈의 일부를 가리는 덮개를 기록했습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. People section: what Johannes Kepler did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.people.5"
msgid "Was the first to use the name camera obscura, in 1604. He made himself a portable dark tent with a lens, for sketching landscapes."
msgstr "1604년에 카메라 옵스큐라라는 이름을 처음 썼습니다. 풍경을 스케치하려고 렌즈를 단 휴대용 어두운 천막을 직접 만들었습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Note under the People section.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.note"
msgid "No inventor is known. The Mohist text speaks of the turned shadow and the crossing light, and does not describe a chamber."
msgstr "발명자는 알려져 있지 않습니다. 묵가의 글은 뒤집힌 그림자와 엇갈리는 빛을 말할 뿐, 방은 묘사하지 않습니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.view.inside.label"
msgid "Step inside"
msgstr "안으로 들어가기"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.view.inside.hint"
msgid "The chamber is closed and dark. The view from the windows lies on the paper upside down: sky below, town above."
msgstr "방은 닫혀 있고 어둡습니다. 창밖 풍경이 종이 위에 거꾸로 비칩니다. 하늘이 아래, 마을이 위입니다."

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.view.hole.label"
msgid "Go close to the hole"
msgstr "구멍 가까이 가기"

#: hall-4/camera-obscura
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.camera-obscura.view.hole.hint"
msgid "A brass disc turns between a small hole and a lens. The lens has the wider opening, so it lets in more light."
msgstr "황동 원판이 작은 구멍과 렌즈 사이를 돕니다. 렌즈 쪽 구멍이 더 넓어서 빛이 더 많이 들어옵니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.name"
msgid "Shadow play"
msgstr "그림자극"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.aka"
msgid "Shadow theatre, shadow puppets"
msgstr "그림자 연극, 그림자 인형"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.yearText"
msgid "about 1000 CE or earlier"
msgstr "서기 1000년경 또는 그 이전"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.origin"
msgid "Recorded in Java and in China about a thousand years ago. Where it began, and how much older it is, are not settled. No inventor is known"
msgstr "약 천 년 전 자와와 중국에 기록이 있습니다. 어디에서 시작되었는지, 그보다 얼마나 더 오래되었는지는 밝혀지지 않았습니다. 발명자는 알려져 있지 않습니다"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.statusNote"
msgid "Performed today in Indonesia, China, Türkiye and other countries. UNESCO lists wayang, Chinese shadow puppetry and Karagöz as intangible cultural heritage."
msgstr "오늘날에도 인도네시아, 중국, 튀르키예 등 여러 나라에서 공연됩니다. 유네스코는 와양, 중국 그림자극, 카라괴즈를 무형문화유산으로 등재했습니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.lead"
msgid "A lamp shines on a cloth screen from behind, and flat figures held between the two cast the shadows that the audience watches."
msgstr "램프가 천으로 된 막을 뒤에서 비추고, 그 사이에 든 납작한 인형이 관객이 보는 그림자를 드리웁니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 4.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.how.1"
msgid "The screen is thin cloth stretched in a frame, and one lamp burns behind it. The puppeteer holds flat figures on rods between the lamp and the cloth. Where a figure blocks the light the cloth stays dark, and the audience on the other side sees its outline. Holes cut through a figure let the light pass and draw an eye, a feather or the pattern of a garment as bright lines."
msgstr "막은 틀에 팽팽하게 맨 얇은 천이고, 그 뒤에 등불 하나가 탑니다. 인형사는 막대에 단 납작한 인형을 등불과 천 사이에 듭니다. 인형이 빛을 가린 곳은 천이 어두운 채로 남고, 반대편의 관객은 그 윤곽을 봅니다. 인형에 뚫은 구멍은 빛을 통과시켜 눈이나 깃털이나 옷의 무늬를 밝은 선으로 그립니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 4.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.how.2"
msgid "Distance does the rest. A figure held against the cloth casts a shadow of its own size, with a sharp edge. The further it is from the cloth, the larger its shadow. The edge also goes soft, because a flame is not a point: beside the full shadow lies a band that part of the flame still reaches. A flame moves, too, so the shadows are never quite still."
msgstr "나머지는 거리가 합니다. 천에 바짝 댄 인형은 제 크기 그대로, 가장자리가 또렷한 그림자를 드리웁니다. 천에서 멀어질수록 그림자는 커집니다. 가장자리도 부드러워집니다. 불꽃은 점이 아니어서, 온전한 그림자 옆에 불꽃의 일부만 닿는 띠가 생기기 때문입니다. 불꽃은 움직이기도 하므로, 그림자는 결코 완전히 멈추지 않습니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. How it works, paragraph 3 of 4.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.how.3"
msgid "In Java and Bali the theatre is called wayang kulit. Its figures are of buffalo hide, pierced and painted, and one puppeteer, the dalang, works them all by a coconut oil lamp, today usually an electric one. Chinese figures are cut from leather or paper in colour, with twelve to twenty-four moving joints. In the Turkish theatre, named after its hero Karagöz, the hide is worked until light passes through it and is then coloured, so the shadows have colour."
msgstr "자와와 발리에서는 이 연극을 와양 쿨릿이라고 합니다. 인형은 물소 가죽에 구멍을 뚫고 색을 칠한 것이며, 달랑이라는 인형사 한 사람이 코코넛 기름 등불 아래에서 모든 인형을 놀립니다. 오늘날 등불은 대개 전등입니다. 중국의 인형은 가죽이나 종이를 오려 색을 입힌 것으로, 움직이는 관절이 열두 개에서 스물네 개 있습니다. 주인공 카라괴즈의 이름으로 불리는 튀르키예의 연극에서는 가죽을 빛이 비칠 때까지 다듬은 뒤 색을 입히므로, 그림자에도 색이 있습니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. How it works, paragraph 4 of 4.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.how.4"
msgid "Where it began and how old it is are not settled. One encyclopedia names China and the islands of Java and Bali as the likely places. Another names India, or Central Asia and China, more than two thousand years ago. What is recorded is later. A Javanese poem of the eleventh century describes a leather shadow figure, and the first clear mention in China is from the Northern Song dynasty, 960 to 1127. The oldest texts that survive for the Arabic shadow theatre are three plays by Ibn Daniyal, who settled in Cairo and died about 1311. Karagöz is said to have been first played in Egypt for the Ottoman sultan Selim I, who reigned from 1512 to 1520."
msgstr "어디에서 시작되었고 얼마나 오래되었는지는 밝혀지지 않았습니다. 한 백과사전은 중국과 자와섬, 발리섬을 유력한 곳으로 꼽습니다. 다른 백과사전은 이천 년도 더 전의 인도, 또는 중앙아시아와 중국을 꼽습니다. 기록으로 남은 것은 그보다 뒤의 일입니다. 11세기 자와의 시에 가죽 그림자 인형이 묘사되어 있고, 중국에서 분명한 언급이 처음 나오는 것은 북송(960년부터 1127년) 때입니다. 아랍 그림자극에서 지금까지 전하는 가장 오래된 대본은 이븐 다니얄의 작품 세 편입니다. 그는 카이로에 정착했고 1311년경에 세상을 떠났습니다. 카라괴즈는 1512년부터 1520년까지 재위한 오스만 제국의 술탄 셀림 1세 앞에서, 이집트에서 처음 공연되었다고 전해집니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.good.1"
msgid "Needs only a lamp, a cloth and figures that one person can carry"
msgstr "등불과 천, 그리고 한 사람이 들고 다닐 수 있는 인형만 있으면 된다"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.good.2"
msgid "Changes the size of a figure in a moment, by moving it between the cloth and the lamp"
msgstr "인형을 천과 등불 사이에서 움직이기만 하면 크기가 곧바로 바뀐다"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.good.3"
msgid "Lets one performer work a whole cast of figures"
msgstr "연희자 한 사람이 등장하는 인형을 모두 놀릴 수 있다"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.bad.1"
msgid "Shows outlines only: whatever is not cut through the figure is not seen"
msgstr "윤곽만 보여 준다. 인형에 뚫어 놓지 않은 것은 보이지 않는다"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.bad.2"
msgid "Needs darkness on the side of the audience"
msgstr "관객 쪽이 어두워야 한다"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.bad.3"
msgid "Depends on a living performer, and has to compete with television and video for its audience"
msgstr "살아 있는 연희자가 있어야 하고, 텔레비전이나 비디오와 관객을 두고 겨루어야 한다"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.look"
msgid "The model shows a cloth screen in a wooden frame, with an oil lamp hanging behind it. Three flat figures stand on rods between the lamp and the cloth: a tree, a deer and a bird. From the front you see only their shadows. The tree and the deer are held against the cloth, so their shadows are sharp. The bird passes close to the cloth, then comes back out by the lamp: its shadow grows large and soft, and the deer lifts its head. Watch the edges tremble with the flame. By day the shadows are pale, so switch the daylight off. Then press the Go behind the screen button to see the figures themselves, pierced and painted."
msgstr "모델은 나무 틀에 맨 천 막과, 그 뒤에 매단 기름 등불을 보여 줍니다. 등불과 천 사이에는 막대에 단 납작한 인형 셋이 있습니다. 나무와 사슴과 새입니다. 앞에서는 그림자만 보입니다. 나무와 사슴은 천에 바짝 대어 있어서 그림자가 또렷합니다. 새는 천 가까이를 지나갔다가 등불 쪽으로 돌아옵니다. 그러면 새의 그림자가 크고 부드러워지고, 사슴이 고개를 듭니다. 그림자의 가장자리가 불꽃을 따라 떨리는 것을 보세요. 낮에는 그림자가 옅으니 햇빛을 끄세요. 그런 다음 막 뒤로 가기 버튼을 누르면, 구멍을 뚫고 색을 칠한 인형 자체를 볼 수 있습니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.fact"
msgid "In Java the lamp has a name of its own, the blencong, and a keeper of its own: a stage assistant tends the flame so that the shadows on the screen stay clear. In many performances today electric light has taken its place."
msgstr "자와에서는 이 등불에 블렌총이라는 이름이 있고, 돌보는 사람도 따로 있습니다. 무대 조수가 불꽃을 살펴서 막의 그림자가 또렷하게 보이도록 합니다. 오늘날에는 많은 공연에서 전등이 그 자리를 대신합니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. People section: what Ibn Daniyal did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.people.1"
msgid "Eye doctor, born in Mosul in 1248. He settled in Cairo and wrote three shadow plays there, the oldest texts that survive for the Arabic shadow theatre."
msgstr "안과 의사. 1248년 모술에서 태어났습니다. 카이로에 정착해 그곳에서 그림자극 세 편을 썼습니다. 아랍 그림자극에서 지금까지 전하는 가장 오래된 대본입니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. People section: what François Dominique Séraphin did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.people.2"
msgid "Showed shadow plays at Versailles from 1771 and moved his theatre to Paris in 1784. In France such shows were advertised as ombres chinoises, Chinese shadows."
msgstr "1771년부터 베르사유에서 그림자극을 공연했고, 1784년에 극장을 파리로 옮겼습니다. 프랑스에서는 이런 공연을 옹브르 시누아즈, 곧 중국 그림자라는 이름으로 광고했습니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. People section: what Henri Rivière did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.people.3"
msgid "Worked with up to twenty assistants on the shadow plays of the cabaret Le Chat Noir in Paris, which staged 45 of them between 1885 and 1896. From 1887 the figures were of zinc."
msgstr "파리의 카바레 르 샤 누아르의 그림자극을 많게는 스무 명의 조수와 함께 만들었습니다. 이 카바레는 1885년부터 1896년 사이에 그림자극 45편을 올렸습니다. 1887년부터는 인형을 아연판으로 만들었습니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. People section: what Kara Walker did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.people.4"
msgid "American artist, known for silhouettes cut from black paper. Since 2004 she has made films in which jointed paper puppets, worked by hand on sticks, play out stories of slavery in America."
msgstr "미국의 미술가. 검은 종이를 오려 만든 실루엣으로 알려져 있습니다. 2004년부터, 막대에 달린 관절 있는 종이 인형을 손으로 움직여 미국 노예제의 이야기를 펼치는 영상 작품을 만들고 있습니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Note under the People section.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.note"
msgid "No inventor is recorded for wayang, for the Chinese shadow theatre or for Karagöz. In Java the craft has been handed down within families of puppeteers, musicians and puppet makers."
msgstr "와양에도, 중국 그림자극에도, 카라괴즈에도 발명자의 기록은 없습니다. 자와에서는 이 기술이 인형사, 악사, 인형 제작자의 집안 안에서 전해져 왔습니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.view.audience.label"
msgid "Sit with the audience"
msgstr "관객과 함께 앉기"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.view.audience.hint"
msgid "Only shadows reach this side. Held against the cloth, a figure is small and sharp. Out by the lamp it grows and blurs."
msgstr "이쪽에는 그림자만 옵니다. 천에 바짝 댄 인형은 작고 또렷합니다. 등불 가까이에서는 커지고 흐려집니다."

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.view.behind.label"
msgid "Go behind the screen"
msgstr "막 뒤로 가기"

#: hall-4/shadow-play
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.shadow-play.view.behind.hint"
msgid "The figures are flat, pierced and painted, each on its rod. One flame lights them all, and it never holds still."
msgstr "인형은 납작하고, 구멍을 뚫고 색을 칠했으며, 저마다 막대에 달려 있습니다. 불꽃 하나가 모두를 비추고, 그 불꽃은 잠시도 가만있지 않습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.name"
msgid "Liquid crystal display"
msgstr "액정 디스플레이"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.aka"
msgid "LCD, twisted nematic"
msgstr "LCD, 트위스티드 네마틱"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.origin"
msgid "Martin Schadt and Wolfgang Helfrich, Hoffmann-La Roche, Switzerland. Patent filed 4 December 1970"
msgstr "마르틴 샤트, 볼프강 헬프리히. 호프만 라 로슈, 스위스. 특허 출원일은 1970년 12월 4일"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.statusNote"
msgid "In most monitors, televisions and instruments. Liquid crystal televisions overtook cathode ray tube televisions in worldwide sales at the end of 2007."
msgstr "대부분의 모니터와 텔레비전, 계기에 쓰입니다. 액정 텔레비전은 2007년 말에 세계 판매량에서 브라운관 텔레비전을 앞질렀습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.lead"
msgid "A thin layer of liquid crystal twists light, a voltage removes the twist, and two polarising filters turn that difference into bright and dark."
msgstr "액정의 얇은 층이 빛을 비틀고, 전압이 그 비틀림을 없애며, 편광 필터 두 장이 그 차이를 밝음과 어둠으로 바꿉니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.how.1"
msgid "The panel makes no light. The light comes from a lamp behind it, the backlight, or from the room. It first passes a polariser, a filter that lets through only light that vibrates in one direction. Next comes a thin layer of liquid crystal, a liquid whose molecules line up in order. Here they form a quarter-turn spiral that rotates the light."
msgstr "패널은 빛을 내지 않습니다. 빛은 뒤에 있는 램프인 백라이트나 방에서 옵니다. 빛은 먼저 편광판을 지납니다. 편광판은 한 방향으로 진동하는 빛만 통과시키는 필터입니다. 다음은 액정의 얇은 층입니다. 액정은 분자들이 가지런히 줄을 서는 액체입니다. 여기서 분자들은 4분의 1바퀴의 나선을 이루어 빛을 돌려놓습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.how.2"
msgid "A second polariser, set crosswise to the first, lets only rotated light through. A voltage straightens the spiral. The light is then no longer rotated, and the second polariser blocks it."
msgstr "첫 번째와 엇갈리게 놓은 두 번째 편광판은 돌아간 빛만 통과시킵니다. 전압을 걸면 나선이 곧게 펴집니다. 그러면 빛은 더는 돌아가지 않고, 두 번째 편광판이 그 빛을 막습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. How it works, paragraph 3 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.how.3"
msgid "Colour comes from a red, green or blue filter over each cell. Everything else in a modern panel serves that one effect. The transistor (a tiny switch) behind every cell and the backlight make it faster and more even."
msgstr "색은 셀마다 얹은 빨강, 초록, 파랑 필터에서 나옵니다. 현대의 패널에 있는 나머지는 모두 이 한 가지 효과를 위한 것입니다. 셀마다 뒤에 있는 트랜지스터(아주 작은 스위치)와 백라이트가 그것을 더 빠르고 더 고르게 만듭니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.good.1"
msgid "Is thin and cheap, and comes in every size"
msgstr "얇고 싸며, 온갖 크기로 나온다"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.good.2"
msgid "Uses very little power when it works with room light instead of a backlight"
msgstr "백라이트 대신 방의 빛으로 작동할 때는 전력을 아주 조금 쓴다"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.good.3"
msgid "Lasts a long time"
msgstr "오래간다"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.bad.1"
msgid "Shows black by blocking the backlight, so black is never fully dark"
msgstr "백라이트를 막아서 검은색을 내므로, 검은색이 완전히 어둡지는 않다"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.bad.2"
msgid "Changes in contrast and colour when viewed from an angle"
msgstr "비스듬히 보면 명암비와 색이 달라진다"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.bad.3"
msgid "Smears motion, because the liquid takes time to turn"
msgstr "액체가 돌아서는 데 시간이 걸려서, 움직임이 번진다"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.look"
msgid "The model pulls the panel apart into its layers. From the back they are: the lamp, a polariser, the liquid crystal between glass, the colour filter and the second polariser. Look from the side to see the layers, then from the front, where they combine into a picture."
msgstr "모델은 패널을 층층이 떼어 놓았습니다. 뒤에서부터 램프, 편광판, 유리 사이의 액정, 컬러 필터, 두 번째 편광판입니다. 옆에서 층들을 보고, 그런 다음 앞에서 보세요. 앞에서는 층들이 합쳐져 그림이 됩니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.fact"
msgid "The twist comes from rubbing. Traditionally, a thin plastic film on each glass plate is rubbed in one direction, at right angles to the other plate, and the molecules line up along the strokes. Why this works was debated for most of a century."
msgstr "비틀림은 문지르는 데서 나옵니다. 전통적인 방법으로는, 유리판마다 입힌 얇은 플라스틱 막을 한 방향으로 문지르되 다른 판과는 직각이 되게 합니다. 그러면 분자들이 문지른 결을 따라 줄을 섭니다. 이것이 왜 통하는지는 거의 한 세기 동안 논쟁거리였습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. People section: what Martin Schadt, Wolfgang Helfrich did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.people.1"
msgid "Filed the patent for the twisted cell at Hoffmann-La Roche in Switzerland on 4 December 1970."
msgstr "1970년 12월 4일, 스위스의 호프만 라 로슈에서 비틀린 셀의 특허를 출원했습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. People section: what James Fergason did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.people.2"
msgid "Filed a similar patent in the United States in 1971. The dispute over who came first was settled out of court."
msgstr "1971년 미국에서 비슷한 특허를 출원했습니다. 누가 먼저였는가를 둘러싼 다툼은 법정 밖에서 마무리되었습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. People section: what George Heilmeier did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.people.3"
msgid "Built an earlier kind of liquid crystal display at RCA in the 1960s, which worked by scattering light."
msgstr "1960년대에 RCA에서, 빛을 산란시켜 작동하는 더 이른 종류의 액정 디스플레이를 만들었습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. People section: what Sivaramakrishna Chandrasekhar, B. K. Sadashiva, K. A. Suresh did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.people.4"
msgid "In 1977 at the Raman Research Institute in Bangalore they found liquid crystals whose molecules are discs, not rods. Films of such liquid crystals later widened the angle a screen can be read from."
msgstr "1977년 방갈로르의 라만 연구소에서, 분자가 막대가 아니라 원반 모양인 액정을 찾아냈습니다. 이런 액정으로 만든 막은 나중에 화면을 읽을 수 있는 각도를 넓혔습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. People section: what Mohamed Atalla, Dawon Kahng did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.people.5"
msgid "Showed the first working MOS transistor at Bell Laboratories in 1960. The thin-film transistor behind every cell works in a similar way. Atalla was born in Port Said, Kahng in Seoul."
msgstr "1960년 벨 연구소에서, 실제로 작동하는 최초의 MOS 트랜지스터를 발표했습니다. 셀마다 뒤에 있는 박막 트랜지스터도 비슷한 방식으로 작동합니다. 아탈라는 포트사이드에서, 강대원은 서울에서 태어났습니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.view.layers.label"
msgid "Go between the layers"
msgstr "층 사이로 들어가기"

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.view.layers.hint"
msgid "The crystal layer alone shows only grey. It sets how far each cell twists the light. The colour comes from the filter."
msgstr "액정 층만으로는 회색밖에 보이지 않습니다. 이 층은 셀마다 빛을 얼마나 비틀지를 정합니다. 색은 필터에서 나옵니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The twisted cell, from James Fergason's patent of 1973. The second figure shows the two glass plates rubbed at right angles to each other. The third shows the cell between two polarisers, with light passing through."
msgstr "비틀린 셀. 제임스 퍼거슨의 1973년 특허에서. 두 번째 그림은 서로 직각으로 문지른 유리판 두 장을 보여 줍니다. 세 번째 그림은 편광판 두 장 사이의 셀을 빛이 지나가는 모습입니다."

#: hall-4/liquid-crystal
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.liquid-crystal.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a cell in cross-section wired to a battery, two plates ruled with lines in crossed directions, and a square cell between two discs before a light source."
msgstr "선 그림: 전지에 배선된 셀의 단면, 엇갈린 방향으로 선을 그은 판 두 장, 광원 앞에서 원판 두 장 사이에 놓인 네모난 셀."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.name"
msgid "Digital micromirror device"
msgstr "디지털 마이크로미러 소자"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.aka"
msgid "DMD, DLP"
msgstr "DMD, DLP"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.origin"
msgid "Larry Hornbeck, Texas Instruments, USA. The first projectors built around the chip followed in 1996 or 1997"
msgstr "래리 혼벡, 텍사스 인스트루먼츠, 미국. 이 칩을 쓴 첫 프로젝터들은 1996년이나 1997년에 나왔습니다"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.statusNote"
msgid "In many cinema projectors and in many other projectors."
msgstr "많은 영화관 프로젝터와 그 밖의 많은 프로젝터에 쓰입니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.lead"
msgid "Microscopic mirrors cover a chip, one for each pixel, and each mirror tips toward the projection lens or away from it."
msgstr "현미경으로나 보이는 거울들이 화소마다 하나씩 칩을 덮고 있고, 거울마다 투사 렌즈 쪽으로 기울거나 반대쪽으로 기웁니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.how.1"
msgid "Every mirror has two positions. In one position it reflects the light of the lamp through the projection lens. In the other it reflects the light into an absorber. There is nothing in between. To make a grey, the mirror flips on and off thousands of times a second, and the eye averages the flashes."
msgstr "거울은 저마다 두 가지 자세만 취합니다. 한 자세에서는 램프의 빛을 투사 렌즈로 반사합니다. 다른 자세에서는 빛을 흡수체로 반사합니다. 중간은 없습니다. 회색을 만들려면 거울이 1초에 수천 번 켜졌다 꺼지고, 눈이 그 번쩍임을 평균합니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.how.2"
msgid "Colour needs three chips, or one chip with a spinning wheel of colour filters, or coloured lamps that flash in turn. The single-chip kind can show brief coloured fringes on bright points that move fast. This is called the rainbow effect."
msgstr "색을 내려면 칩 세 개를 쓰거나, 칩 하나에 도는 컬러 필터 원판을 붙이거나, 색이 있는 램프를 차례로 깜박입니다. 칩이 하나인 방식은 빠르게 움직이는 밝은 점에서 잠깐 색 띠가 보일 수 있습니다. 이것을 무지개 효과라고 합니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.good.1"
msgid "Reflects light, so it wastes little of it and withstands a powerful lamp"
msgstr "빛을 반사하므로 낭비가 적고, 강한 램프도 견딘다"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.good.2"
msgid "Switches in microseconds"
msgstr "마이크로초 만에 전환한다"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.good.3"
msgid "Is a sealed chip with nothing in it that fades"
msgstr "밀봉된 칩이어서, 안에 바랠 것이 없다"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.bad.1"
msgid "Is a projector part, not a screen: it needs a lamp, a lens and a dark room"
msgstr "프로젝터의 부품이지 화면이 아니다. 램프와 렌즈와 어두운 방이 필요하다"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.bad.2"
msgid "Can break colour into rainbow fringes in the single-chip kind"
msgstr "칩이 하나인 방식에서는 색이 무지개 띠로 갈라질 수 있다"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.bad.3"
msgid "Comes from a single maker"
msgstr "제조사가 한 곳뿐이다"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.look"
msgid "The chip sits on the stand. Above it, the model shows a patch of its mirrors greatly enlarged, counting seconds. Turn the model: the mirrors that tip toward you look bright. Then switch the daylight off. The chip makes no light, so at night it disappears."
msgstr "칩은 받침대 위에 있습니다. 그 위에 모델은 거울의 일부를 크게 확대해 보여 주고, 거울들은 초를 셉니다. 모델을 돌려 보세요. 이쪽으로 기운 거울이 밝아 보입니다. 그런 다음 햇빛을 끄세요. 칩은 빛을 내지 않으므로 밤에는 사라집니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.fact"
msgid "The first product built around the chip was not a projector. It was a high-speed printer for airline tickets, which used a single row of 840 mirrors."
msgstr "이 칩으로 만든 첫 제품은 프로젝터가 아니었습니다. 항공권을 찍는 고속 프린터였고, 거울 840개를 한 줄로 늘어놓아 썼습니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. People section: what Larry Hornbeck did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.people.1"
msgid "Invented the mirror chip at Texas Instruments in 1987."
msgstr "1987년 텍사스 인스트루먼츠에서 거울 칩을 발명했습니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.view.mirrors.label"
msgid "Go close to the mirrors"
msgstr "거울 가까이 가기"

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.view.mirrors.hint"
msgid "A few mirrors, enlarged several thousand times. Each rocks on its diagonal: one way sends light to the lens."
msgstr "수천 배로 확대한 거울 몇 장입니다. 저마다 대각선을 축으로 까딱이고, 한쪽으로 기울면 빛을 렌즈로 보냅니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "One early micromirror, from Larry Hornbeck's patent of 1987. The pixel is a square flap held over a shallow well by a hinge at one corner. A voltage below pulls the flap down."
msgstr "초기의 마이크로미러 하나. 래리 혼벡의 1987년 특허에서. 화소는 한 모서리의 경첩으로 얕은 우물 위에 걸린 네모난 날개입니다. 아래에 건 전압이 날개를 끌어 내립니다."

#: hall-4/micromirror
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.micromirror.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Three line drawings of one square plate with four small holes, joined to its frame at a corner: in perspective over a hollow, in cross-section, and from above."
msgstr "작은 구멍 네 개가 뚫린 네모난 판을 그린 선 그림 세 개. 판은 한 모서리에서 틀과 이어져 있습니다. 우묵한 곳 위의 투시도, 단면도, 위에서 본 그림입니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.name"
msgid "Liquid crystal on silicon"
msgstr "실리콘 액정"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.aka"
msgid "LCoS"
msgstr "LCoS"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.origin"
msgid "Grew out of the liquid crystal light valve developed at Hughes Research Laboratories, USA"
msgstr "미국 휴스 연구소에서 개발한 액정 라이트 밸브에서 발전했습니다"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.statusNote"
msgid "In the more expensive projectors, camera viewfinders and headsets. It also steers light in optical fibre networks."
msgstr "값비싼 프로젝터, 카메라 뷰파인더, 헤드셋에 쓰입니다. 광섬유 통신망에서 빛의 방향을 바꾸는 데에도 쓰입니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.lead"
msgid "A layer of liquid crystal lies directly on a mirrored silicon chip, so that light goes in and comes back out carrying the picture."
msgstr "액정 층이 거울처럼 만든 실리콘 칩 위에 바로 놓여 있어서, 빛이 들어갔다가 그림을 싣고 다시 나옵니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.how.1"
msgid "The chip carries a mirror and a drive circuit under every pixel. Light arrives through a glass cube that splits light by its polarisation, the direction in which the light vibrates. The light passes through the liquid crystal, reflects off the mirror and returns through the liquid crystal. Where the liquid crystal has turned the polarisation, the cube sends the light on to the lens. Where it has not, the light goes back toward the lamp."
msgstr "칩에는 화소마다 아래에 거울과 구동 회로가 있습니다. 빛은 편광에 따라 빛을 가르는 유리 정육면체를 지나서 들어옵니다. 편광은 빛이 진동하는 방향입니다. 빛은 액정을 지나 거울에서 반사되고, 다시 액정을 지나 돌아옵니다. 액정이 편광을 돌려놓은 곳에서는 정육면체가 빛을 렌즈로 보냅니다. 돌려놓지 않은 곳에서는 빛이 램프 쪽으로 돌아갑니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.how.2"
msgid "The circuitry lies behind the mirror instead of between the pixels, so the pixels can be tiny and tightly packed."
msgstr "회로가 화소 사이가 아니라 거울 뒤에 있어서, 화소를 아주 작고 촘촘하게 만들 수 있습니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.good.1"
msgid "Fits a very large number of pixels on a small chip"
msgstr "작은 칩에 아주 많은 화소를 넣는다"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.good.2"
msgid "Gives a smooth picture with almost no visible pixel grid"
msgstr "화소 격자가 거의 보이지 않는 매끄러운 그림을 낸다"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.good.3"
msgid "Is small and uses little power"
msgstr "작고 전력을 적게 쓴다"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.bad.1"
msgid "Needs polarised light and a splitter cube, which reduces brightness"
msgstr "편광된 빛과 분리용 정육면체가 필요해서 밝기가 줄어든다"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.bad.2"
msgid "Held ghost pictures in early panels"
msgstr "초기 패널에서는 잔상이 남았다"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.bad.3"
msgid "Is only as good as its lamp, like every device that makes no light of its own"
msgstr "스스로 빛을 내지 않는 장치가 다 그렇듯, 램프만큼만 좋다"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.look"
msgid "Follow the light. It goes from the lamp to the cube, down to the chip and back up. It then leaves through the lens and lands on the card as a picture."
msgstr "빛을 따라가 보세요. 빛은 램프에서 정육면체로 가고, 칩까지 내려갔다가 다시 올라옵니다. 그런 다음 렌즈로 나가 카드 위에 그림으로 맺힙니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.fact"
msgid "Its ancestor, the Hughes light valve, was from the late 1970s the preferred projector for industry and for military command and control rooms. In 1999 it shared with the micromirror chip the first showings of digital cinema to paying audiences."
msgstr "그 조상인 휴스의 라이트 밸브는 1970년대 말부터 산업 현장과 군의 지휘 통제실에서 가장 선호한 프로젝터였습니다. 1999년에는, 돈을 낸 관객 앞에서 디지털 영화를 처음 상영하는 일을 마이크로미러 칩과 나누어 맡았습니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.view.cube.label"
msgid "Follow the light"
msgstr "빛 따라가기"

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.view.cube.hint"
msgid "The cube turns the light of the lamp down onto the chip. Only light the chip has twisted leaves through the lens."
msgstr "정육면체가 램프의 빛을 아래의 칩으로 돌립니다. 칩이 비튼 빛만 렌즈로 나갑니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The Hughes liquid crystal light valve in cross-section, from a patent of 1974. A picture arrives on one side, and projection light is reflected from the other. The layer of liquid crystal between them copies the picture onto that light."
msgstr "휴스 액정 라이트 밸브의 단면. 1974년 특허에서. 한쪽으로 그림이 들어오고, 반대쪽에서는 투사용 빛이 반사됩니다. 그 사이의 액정 층이 그림을 그 빛에 옮겨 싣습니다."

#: hall-4/lcos
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.lcos.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Cross-section of a thick sandwich of hatched layers round a central fluid layer. Arrows point in from the right, and double-headed arrows stand on the left."
msgstr "가운데의 액체 층을 빗금 친 층들이 두껍게 감싼 단면도. 오른쪽에서 화살표들이 안쪽을 가리키고, 왼쪽에는 양쪽 화살표가 서 있습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.name"
msgid "Eidophor"
msgstr "아이도포르"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.aka"
msgid "Oil film projector"
msgstr "유막 프로젝터"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.origin"
msgid "Fritz Fischer, ETH Zurich, Switzerland. Prototype shown in 1943"
msgstr "프리츠 피셔, 취리히 연방 공과대학교, 스위스. 시제품은 1943년에 공개되었습니다"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.statusNote"
msgid "Used for arena screens and control rooms until liquid crystal and micromirror projectors replaced it in the 1990s."
msgstr "경기장의 대형 화면과 관제실에서 쓰이다가, 1990년대에 액정과 마이크로미러 프로젝터로 바뀌었습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.lead"
msgid "The first television projector bright enough for a cinema screen used an electron beam to write ripples into a film of oil."
msgstr "영화관 스크린에 쓸 만큼 밝았던 최초의 텔레비전 프로젝터는, 전자 빔으로 기름 막에 잔물결을 썼습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.how.1"
msgid "A powerful lamp shines onto a grille of mirrored bars. The bars reflect the light down onto a slowly turning mirror with a thin coat of oil. If the oil is smooth, the light returns straight to the bars and never leaves the machine."
msgstr "강한 램프가 거울 막대를 늘어놓은 격자를 비춥니다. 막대는 빛을 아래로, 기름을 얇게 입히고 천천히 도는 거울 위로 반사합니다. 기름이 매끄러우면 빛은 곧장 막대로 돌아가고, 기계 밖으로 나가지 못합니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.how.2"
msgid "An electron beam scans the oil and leaves a pattern of electric charge, which ripples the surface. Rippled oil bends the light slightly. On its way back, that light passes between the bars, through the lens and onto the screen. The deeper the ripple, the brighter the spot."
msgstr "전자 빔이 기름을 주사하며 전하의 무늬를 남기고, 그 무늬가 표면에 잔물결을 일으킵니다. 물결친 기름은 빛을 살짝 꺾습니다. 그 빛은 돌아가는 길에 막대 사이를 빠져나가, 렌즈를 지나 스크린에 닿습니다. 물결이 깊을수록 그 점은 더 밝습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.good.1"
msgid "Takes its brightness from a lamp, not a glowing tube, which is enough for a cinema or a stadium"
msgstr "밝기를 빛나는 관이 아니라 램프에서 얻으므로, 영화관이나 경기장에도 충분하다"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.good.2"
msgid "Was decades ahead of any alternative for large-screen video"
msgstr "대형 화면 영상에서는 다른 어떤 방식보다 수십 년 앞서 있었다"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.bad.1"
msgid "Was the size of a wardrobe and needed a vacuum pump"
msgstr "옷장만 한 크기였고 진공 펌프가 필요했다"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.bad.2"
msgid "Needed engineers to set it up, and industrial three-phase power"
msgstr "설치하려면 기술자가, 전원으로는 산업용 삼상 전력이 필요했다"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.bad.3"
msgid "Showed any dust or contamination in the oil on the screen"
msgstr "기름 속의 먼지나 오염이 그대로 스크린에 비쳤다"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.look"
msgid "The model shows the machine with its panels off. Follow the light from the lamp house at the left across to the bars. It goes down to the dish of oil, which turns under a glass bell, and back up through the lens. The top mirror then sends it to the small screen."
msgstr "모델은 덮개를 벗긴 기계를 보여 줍니다. 왼쪽의 램프 함에서 막대까지 빛을 따라가 보세요. 빛은 유리 덮개 아래에서 도는 기름 접시까지 내려갔다가, 렌즈를 지나 다시 올라옵니다. 그러면 위의 거울이 그 빛을 작은 스크린으로 보냅니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.fact"
msgid "Three Eidophors worked behind the big screens of the Apollo Mission Control room in Houston. NASA describes them as the main means of putting video on the large screen in front of the flight controllers."
msgstr "휴스턴의 아폴로 임무 관제실에서는 대형 스크린 뒤에서 아이도포르 세 대가 일했습니다. NASA는 이것을, 비행 관제사들 앞의 큰 화면에 영상을 올리는 주된 수단이었다고 설명합니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. People section: what Fritz Fischer did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.people.1"
msgid "Conceived the projector at ETH Zurich in 1939. A prototype was shown in 1943."
msgstr "1939년 취리히 연방 공과대학교에서 이 프로젝터를 구상했습니다. 시제품은 1943년에 공개되었습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.view.oil.label"
msgid "Go close to the oil"
msgstr "기름 가까이 가기"

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.view.oil.hint"
msgid "A film of oil on a turning mirror. The electron beam dimples it, and only light bent by a dimple passes the bars."
msgstr "도는 거울 위의 기름 막입니다. 전자 빔이 거기에 오목한 자국을 내고, 그 자국에서 꺾인 빛만 막대 사이를 지나갑니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.media.prototype.caption"
msgid "The second prototype of the Eidophor large-screen television projector, about 1948. The ETH library's record lists the picture tube, the Eidophor cassette, the schlieren optics, a high-vacuum pump and a cooling machine."
msgstr "대형 화면 텔레비전 프로젝터 아이도포르의 두 번째 시제품, 1948년경. 취리히 연방 공과대학교 도서관의 기록에는 수상관, 아이도포르 카세트, 슐리렌 광학계, 고진공 펌프, 냉각기가 적혀 있습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.media.prototype.alt"
msgid "Black and white photograph of a machine filling a room: racks of electronics at the left, pipes, pumps and gauges at the right, a tall column in the middle."
msgstr "방을 가득 채운 기계를 찍은 흑백 사진. 왼쪽에 전자 장비 선반, 오른쪽에 배관과 펌프와 계기, 가운데에 높은 기둥이 있습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.media.mission-control.caption"
msgid "Mission control in Houston on 22 July 1969, during the return of Apollo 11. The file page identifies the large picture at the front of the room as an Eidophor display."
msgstr "1969년 7월 22일, 아폴로 11호가 돌아오는 동안의 휴스턴 임무 관제실. 파일 페이지는 방 앞쪽의 큰 그림을 아이도포르 화면이라고 밝힙니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.media.mission-control.alt"
msgid "A dim control room. Flight controllers seen from behind sit at consoles with small screens, facing a wall where a large projected picture shows figures on the Moon."
msgstr "어둑한 관제실. 뒷모습이 보이는 비행 관제사들이 작은 화면이 달린 콘솔 앞에 앉아, 벽을 향해 있습니다. 벽에는 달 위의 사람 모습을 비춘 큰 투사 영상이 있습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.media.machine.caption"
msgid "An Eidophor projector with its power supply and its projection lens, seen from the side. One panel is open on the works inside."
msgstr "전원 장치와 투사 렌즈를 갖춘 아이도포르 프로젝터를 옆에서 본 모습. 덮개 하나가 열려 있어 안의 기구가 보입니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.media.machine.alt"
msgid "A tall blue-grey cabinet marked Eidophor, with a control panel of switches and meters, an open compartment, and a large black lens barrel at floor level."
msgstr "Eidophor라고 적힌 키 큰 청회색 함. 스위치와 계기가 달린 조작반, 열린 칸, 바닥 높이에 달린 크고 검은 렌즈 통이 있습니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The Eidophor, from Fritz Fischer's United States patent of 1945. An electron beam writes on a film of liquid carried round on a turning plate, and light from a lamp passes through that film on the way to the screen."
msgstr "아이도포르. 프리츠 피셔의 1945년 미국 특허에서. 전자 빔이 도는 판에 실려 가는 액체 막에 쓰고, 램프의 빛이 그 막을 지나 스크린으로 갑니다."

#: hall-4/eidophor
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.eidophor.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a glass vessel with two angled electron guns over a round dish, a light path rising through it to a mirror and a screen, and a turntable."
msgstr "선 그림: 둥근 접시 위로 비스듬한 전자총 두 개가 있는 유리 용기, 그것을 지나 거울과 스크린으로 올라가는 빛의 길, 그리고 회전판."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.name"
msgid "Scanned laser"
msgstr "주사 레이저"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.aka"
msgid "Laser show projector, galvanometer scanner"
msgstr "레이저 쇼 프로젝터, 갈바노미터 스캐너"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.origin"
msgid "Early laser show: Joseph Ostoja-Kotkowski, Sound and Image, Adelaide Festival of Arts"
msgstr "초기의 레이저 쇼: 요제프 오스토야코트코프스키, Sound and Image, 애들레이드 예술 축제"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.statusNote"
msgid "Used at concerts, in planetariums and for alignment in industry. The trade body for laser shows dates from 1986."
msgstr "공연과 천문관, 산업 현장의 정렬 작업에 쓰입니다. 레이저 쇼 업계 단체는 1986년에 생겼습니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.lead"
msgid "Two small mirrors on fast motors steer one laser beam, and the eye joins the moving dot into a drawing."
msgstr "빠른 모터에 달린 작은 거울 두 장이 레이저 빔 한 줄기의 방향을 바꾸고, 눈은 움직이는 점을 이어 하나의 그림으로 봅니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.how.1"
msgid "One mirror turns the beam left and right, and the other turns it up and down. A computer drives both mirrors so that the spot of light traces a figure again and again. Between strokes, the beam switches off. Scanners are rated by the number of points they can reach each second, commonly tens of thousands."
msgstr "거울 하나는 빔을 좌우로, 다른 하나는 위아래로 돌립니다. 컴퓨터가 두 거울을 구동해, 빛점이 같은 도형을 거듭거듭 긋게 합니다. 획과 획 사이에서는 빔이 꺼집니다. 스캐너의 성능은 1초에 닿을 수 있는 점의 수로 매기며, 보통 수만 개입니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.how.2"
msgid "Mirrors that reflect only one colour merge a red, a green and a blue laser into one beam. In haze, a thin mist, the beam itself becomes the show: fans, tunnels and sheets of light."
msgstr "한 가지 색만 반사하는 거울들이 빨강, 초록, 파랑 레이저를 한 줄기의 빔으로 합칩니다. 헤이즈라고 하는 옅은 안개 속에서는 빔 자체가 볼거리가 됩니다. 부채, 터널, 빛의 장막입니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.good.1"
msgid "Gives pure, strong colour and a beam that stays sharp at any distance"
msgstr "순수하고 강한 색을 내고, 빔은 어느 거리에서나 날카롭다"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.good.2"
msgid "Draws on anything, including clouds, buildings and smoke"
msgstr "구름이든 건물이든 연기든, 무엇에나 그린다"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.bad.1"
msgid "Makes line drawings only, and flickers more as the drawing gains lines"
msgstr "선 그림만 그리고, 그림에 선이 늘수록 더 깜박인다"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.bad.2"
msgid "Must obey eye safety rules, which decide where the beam may go and for how long"
msgstr "눈의 안전 규칙을 지켜야 하고, 그 규칙이 빔이 가도 되는 곳과 시간을 정한다"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.bad.3"
msgid "Is useless in daylight"
msgstr "햇빛 아래에서는 쓸모가 없다"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.look"
msgid "Follow the beams from left to right. Three lasers at the left merge into one white beam, which goes to the pair of mirrors at the right. The beam that leaves the mirrors jumps from place to place. The figure on the wall shows where the beam has been."
msgstr "빔을 왼쪽에서 오른쪽으로 따라가 보세요. 왼쪽의 레이저 세 대가 한 줄기의 흰 빔으로 합쳐져, 오른쪽의 거울 한 쌍으로 갑니다. 거울을 떠난 빔은 이곳저곳으로 뜁니다. 벽의 도형은 빔이 지나간 자리를 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.fact"
msgid "In the United States, laser shows are regulated by the Food and Drug Administration. Its rules hold a show projector to 5 milliwatts of visible light, and anything stronger needs the agency's written approval before a show may be given."
msgstr "미국에서는 식품의약국이 레이저 쇼를 규제합니다. 그 규칙은 쇼용 프로젝터의 가시광선을 5밀리와트로 묶고, 그보다 강한 것은 쇼를 열기 전에 식품의약국의 서면 승인을 받아야 합니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. People section: what Joseph Ostoja-Kotkowski did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.people.1"
msgid "Staged an early laser show, Sound and Image, at the Adelaide Festival of Arts in 1968."
msgstr "1968년 애들레이드 예술 축제에서, 초기의 레이저 쇼인 Sound and Image를 올렸습니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. People section: what Elsa Garmire did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.people.2"
msgid "Physicist at the California Institute of Technology. Her laser images, filmed in 1972, became the model for Laserium."
msgstr "캘리포니아 공과대학교의 물리학자. 1972년에 촬영된 그녀의 레이저 영상이 레이저리움의 본보기가 되었습니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. People section: what Ivan Dryer did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.people.3"
msgid "Filmmaker who worked with Garmire and opened Laserium at Griffith Observatory in Los Angeles in 1973."
msgstr "가마이어와 함께 일한 영화 제작자. 1973년 로스앤젤레스의 그리피스 천문대에서 레이저리움을 열었습니다."

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.view.galvos.label"
msgid "Go close to the mirrors"
msgstr "거울 가까이 가기"

#: hall-4/scanned-laser
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.scanned-laser.view.galvos.hint"
msgid "Two small mirrors on fast motors. One swings the beam left and right, the other up and down. The eye sees the path."
msgstr "빠른 모터에 달린 작은 거울 두 장입니다. 하나는 빔을 좌우로, 다른 하나는 위아래로 흔듭니다. 눈에 보이는 것은 그 자취입니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.name"
msgid "Projection mapping"
msgstr "프로젝션 매핑"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.aka"
msgid "Spatial augmented reality"
msgstr "공간 증강 현실"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.origin"
msgid "Singing busts in the Haunted Mansion, Disneyland, USA"
msgstr "미국 디즈니랜드의 헌티드 맨션에 있는 노래하는 흉상"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.statusNote"
msgid "A standard technique for buildings, stage sets and museum objects."
msgstr "건물, 무대 장치, 박물관 전시물에 쓰는 표준 기법입니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.lead"
msgid "An ordinary projector shows a picture cut to fit the object it lands on, so that the object itself seems to change."
msgstr "평범한 프로젝터가, 빛이 닿는 물체에 맞게 오려 낸 그림을 비추어, 물체 자체가 바뀌는 것처럼 보이게 합니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.how.1"
msgid "There is no special hardware. The work is in the picture. The makers measure the object, then reshape and mask the picture so that every part of it lands on the surface it was made for."
msgstr "특별한 장비는 없습니다. 일은 그림에 있습니다. 만드는 사람들은 물체를 재고, 그림의 모든 부분이 그것을 위해 만든 면에 닿도록 그림을 변형하고 가립니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.how.2"
msgid "In 1969 that meant filming the faces of singers and projecting the film onto plaster busts of the same faces. In 1980 the artist Michael Naimark filmed a furnished room with a rotating camera. He then put a projector in the place of the camera and played the film back onto the room. Researchers named the field spatial augmented reality in 1998 and published the Shader Lamps method in 2001."
msgstr "1969년에 그것은 가수들의 얼굴을 촬영해서, 같은 얼굴을 본뜬 석고 흉상에 그 필름을 비추는 일이었습니다. 1980년에는 미술가 마이클 네이마크가 가구가 놓인 방을 도는 카메라로 촬영했습니다. 그런 다음 카메라가 있던 자리에 프로젝터를 놓고, 필름을 그 방에 다시 비추었습니다. 연구자들은 1998년에 이 분야에 공간 증강 현실이라는 이름을 붙였고, 2001년에 셰이더 램프 방법을 발표했습니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.good.1"
msgid "Turns any object or building into a display without touching it"
msgstr "어떤 물체나 건물이든 손대지 않고 디스플레이로 바꾼다"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.good.2"
msgid "Can be as large as the available projectors allow"
msgstr "쓸 수 있는 프로젝터가 허락하는 만큼 얼마든지 크게 할 수 있다"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.bad.1"
msgid "Needs darkness, or very powerful projectors"
msgstr "어둠이나 아주 강한 프로젝터가 필요하다"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.bad.2"
msgid "Works only while the object and the projector stay exactly where they were measured"
msgstr "물체와 프로젝터가 잰 자리에 정확히 그대로 있는 동안에만 작동한다"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.bad.3"
msgid "Shows a shadow wherever someone stands in the beam"
msgstr "누군가 빔 안에 서면 그 자리에 그림자가 진다"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.look"
msgid "The model shows white plaster shapes and a projector on a tripod. The picture is calculated from the position of the projector, so each shape receives its own pattern and no light falls on the floor. By day the picture is barely visible. Switch the daylight off."
msgstr "모델은 흰 석고 입체들과 삼각대 위의 프로젝터를 보여 줍니다. 그림은 프로젝터의 위치에서 계산한 것이어서, 입체마다 자기 무늬를 받고 바닥에는 빛이 떨어지지 않습니다. 낮에는 그림이 거의 보이지 않습니다. 햇빛을 끄세요."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.fact"
msgid "Before playing the film back, Naimark spray-painted the room and everything in it white, to make a screen of exactly the right shape. The two people in the film were not there to be painted, so they looked like ghosts."
msgstr "필름을 다시 비추기 전에, 네이마크는 방과 그 안의 모든 것을 흰 스프레이로 칠해, 꼭 맞는 모양의 스크린을 만들었습니다. 필름 속의 두 사람은 그 자리에 없어 칠해지지 않았고, 그래서 유령처럼 보였습니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. People section: what Leota Toombs did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.people.1"
msgid "Artist at WED Enterprises, the Disney design workshop. Her filmed face is projected onto a blank head inside the crystal ball of the Haunted Mansion."
msgstr "디즈니의 설계 공방인 WED 엔터프라이즈의 미술가. 촬영된 그녀의 얼굴이 헌티드 맨션의 수정 구슬 안에 있는 민얼굴 머리에 비칩니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. People section: what Michael Naimark did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.people.2"
msgid "Artist. For Displacements he filmed a room, painted it white and projected the film back onto it."
msgstr "미술가. Displacements에서 그는 방을 촬영하고, 흰색으로 칠하고, 그 필름을 방에 다시 비추었습니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. People section: what Ramesh Raskar, Greg Welch, Kok-Lim Low, Deepak Bandyopadhyay did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.people.3"
msgid "Described the technique in 2001 under the name shader lamps."
msgstr "2001년에 셰이더 램프라는 이름으로 이 기법을 발표했습니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. People section: what Lanny Smoot did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.people.4"
msgid "Disney research fellow with more than 100 patents. He is credited with making Madame Leota, the head in the crystal ball of the Haunted Mansion, float in her séance room."
msgstr "100건이 넘는 특허를 가진 디즈니의 리서치 펠로. 헌티드 맨션의 수정 구슬 속 머리인 마담 리오타를 강령회 방에서 떠다니게 만든 사람으로 꼽힙니다."

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.view.lens.label"
msgid "Stand at the projector"
msgstr "프로젝터 옆에 서기"

#: hall-4/projection-mapping
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.projection-mapping.view.lens.hint"
msgid "From the lens it is one flat picture, cut to fit. Each solid gets its own part, and the gaps between them get black."
msgstr "렌즈에서 보면, 꼭 맞게 오려 낸 평평한 그림 한 장입니다. 입체마다 자기 몫을 받고, 그 사이의 틈에는 검은색이 닿습니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.name"
msgid "Gyricon"
msgstr "자이리콘"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.aka"
msgid "Rotating ball display"
msgstr "회전 구슬 디스플레이"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.yearText"
msgid "the 1970s"
msgstr "1970년대"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.origin"
msgid "Nicholas Sheridon, Xerox Palo Alto Research Center, USA"
msgstr "니컬러스 셰리던, 제록스 팰로앨토 연구소, 미국"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.statusNote"
msgid "Gyricon, a subsidiary of Xerox, sold signs for a few years and closed in December 2005."
msgstr "제록스의 자회사 자이리콘은 몇 해 동안 표지판을 팔다가 2005년 12월에 문을 닫았습니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.lead"
msgid "The first electronic paper was a sheet full of tiny beads, each black on one half and white on the other."
msgstr "최초의 전자 종이는 절반은 검고 절반은 흰 아주 작은 구슬을 가득 채운 판이었습니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.how.1"
msgid "Each bead floats in its own bubble of oil inside a clear, rubbery sheet. The two halves of a bead carry different electric charges. A voltage across the sheet therefore turns the beads to show one half or the other. Without the voltage, the beads stay as they are."
msgstr "구슬은 저마다 투명하고 고무 같은 판 속의 기름방울 안에 떠 있습니다. 구슬의 두 반쪽은 서로 다른 전하를 띱니다. 그래서 판에 전압을 걸면 구슬이 돌아 한쪽 반이나 다른 쪽 반을 보여 줍니다. 전압이 없으면 구슬은 그대로 있습니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.how.2"
msgid "Like ink, it is read by reflected light, and the sheet can bend. It failed on cost: the company could not make the electronics behind the sheet cheaply enough."
msgstr "잉크처럼 반사된 빛으로 읽고, 판은 휠 수 있습니다. 실패한 까닭은 비용이었습니다. 회사는 판 뒤의 전자 회로를 충분히 싸게 만들지 못했습니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.good.1"
msgid "Looks like print and is readable in sunlight"
msgstr "인쇄물처럼 보이고 햇빛 아래에서도 읽을 수 있다"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.good.2"
msgid "Keeps its picture with no power"
msgstr "전력 없이 그림을 유지한다"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.good.3"
msgid "Was flexible from the start"
msgstr "처음부터 휠 수 있었다"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.bad.1"
msgid "Shows two colours only"
msgstr "두 가지 색만 보여 준다"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.bad.2"
msgid "Needed a cheap grid of electronics behind it, which did not yet exist"
msgstr "뒤에 값싼 전자 회로 격자가 필요했는데, 그때는 아직 없었다"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.look"
msgid "The model shows the sheet greatly enlarged. Watch the beads turn over as the seconds change. Then look from the side to see that they are spheres."
msgstr "모델은 판을 크게 확대해 보여 줍니다. 초가 바뀔 때 구슬이 뒤집히는 모습을 보세요. 그런 다음 옆에서 보면 그것이 공 모양임을 알 수 있습니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.fact"
msgid "A 1993 Xerox patent describes a way to make the beads. Molten white wax runs over one face of a spinning disc and black wax over the other. At the rim they meet without mixing and fly off as droplets, half white, half black."
msgstr "1993년의 제록스 특허는 구슬을 만드는 방법 하나를 설명합니다. 녹인 흰 밀랍을 도는 원판의 한 면에, 검은 밀랍을 다른 면에 흘립니다. 둘은 가장자리에서 섞이지 않고 만나, 절반은 희고 절반은 검은 방울이 되어 날아갑니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. People section: what Nicholas Sheridon did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.people.1"
msgid "Invented the sheet of turning beads at the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center in the 1970s."
msgstr "1970년대에 제록스 팰로앨토 연구소에서, 도는 구슬의 판을 발명했습니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.view.beads.label"
msgid "Go close to the beads"
msgstr "구슬 가까이 가기"

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.view.beads.hint"
msgid "Each bead is black on one half and white on the other, in its own pocket of oil. The field turns it, and it stays."
msgstr "구슬은 저마다 절반은 검고 절반은 희며, 자기 기름 주머니 안에 있습니다. 전기장이 구슬을 돌리고, 구슬은 그대로 머뭅니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "Nicholas Sheridon's twisting ball display, from his patent of 1978. A sheet full of small balls lies between two plates of electrodes. The enlargement shows two balls, each half dark, free to turn in its own cavity."
msgstr "니컬러스 셰리던의 회전 구슬 디스플레이. 그의 1978년 특허에서. 작은 구슬이 가득한 판이 전극판 두 장 사이에 놓여 있습니다. 확대 그림은 절반이 어두운 구슬 두 개가 저마다의 공동 안에서 자유롭게 도는 모습입니다."

#: hall-5/gyricon
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.gyricon.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a stack of plates drawn apart, a cross-section with a row of small balls between two plates, and an enlargement of two half-shaded balls in round cavities."
msgstr "선 그림: 떨어뜨려 그린 판 묶음, 판 두 장 사이에 작은 구슬이 한 줄로 놓인 단면, 둥근 공동에 든 반쯤 음영 진 구슬 두 개의 확대 그림."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.name"
msgid "Electrophoretic ink"
msgstr "전기 영동 잉크"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.aka"
msgid "E Ink, electronic paper"
msgstr "E Ink, 전자 종이"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.origin"
msgid "Barrett Comiskey, J.D. Albert and Joseph Jacobson, MIT Media Lab, USA"
msgstr "배럿 코미스키, J. D. 앨버트, 조지프 제이컵슨. MIT 미디어랩, 미국"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.statusNote"
msgid "Used in e-readers, shelf labels in shops and signs."
msgstr "전자책 단말기, 상점의 선반 가격표, 표지판에 쓰입니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.lead"
msgid "Millions of tiny capsules each hold black and white pigment, which an electric field pulls to the top or to the bottom."
msgstr "수백만 개의 아주 작은 캡슐마다 검은 안료와 흰 안료가 들어 있고, 전기장이 그것을 위나 아래로 끌어당깁니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.how.1"
msgid "White particles and black particles carry opposite electric charges. They float in a clear liquid inside a capsule about as wide as a hair. A voltage across the capsule draws one colour up to the side you look at and the other colour down, out of sight. Without the voltage, the particles stay where they are."
msgstr "흰 입자와 검은 입자는 반대의 전하를 띱니다. 입자들은 머리카락 굵기쯤 되는 캡슐 안의 투명한 액체에 떠 있습니다. 캡슐에 전압을 걸면 한 색은 바라보는 쪽으로 올라오고, 다른 색은 보이지 않는 아래로 내려갑니다. 전압이 없으면 입자는 제자리에 머뭅니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.how.2"
msgid "The pigment is real pigment, lit by the room, so the page looks like paper. The particles have to move through liquid, so the display is slow. A page turn often flashes black and white to clear the previous picture."
msgstr "안료는 진짜 안료이고 방의 빛을 받으므로, 지면은 종이처럼 보입니다. 입자가 액체 속을 움직여야 해서 표시는 느립니다. 쪽을 넘길 때는 앞의 그림을 지우려고 검은색과 흰색으로 한 번 번쩍이는 일이 많습니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.good.1"
msgid "Looks like print, and is easiest to read in bright light"
msgstr "인쇄물처럼 보이고, 밝은 빛에서 가장 읽기 쉽다"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.good.2"
msgid "Uses power only to change the page"
msgstr "쪽을 바꿀 때에만 전력을 쓴다"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.good.3"
msgid "Is thin and can flex"
msgstr "얇고 휠 수 있다"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.bad.1"
msgid "Is slow, which suits pages but not video"
msgstr "느리다. 지면에는 맞지만 동영상에는 맞지 않는다"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.bad.2"
msgid "Needs a lamp at night"
msgstr "밤에는 램프가 필요하다"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.bad.3"
msgid "Loses brightness in its colour versions"
msgstr "컬러판에서는 밝기가 떨어진다"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.look"
msgid "Watch the page reset every 15 seconds with the full black and white flash. Then switch the daylight off. The page is still there, but you cannot read it."
msgstr "지면이 15초마다 검은색과 흰색으로 번쩍이며 새로 그려지는 모습을 보세요. 그런 다음 햇빛을 끄세요. 지면은 그대로 있지만 읽을 수 없습니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.fact"
msgid "In 2008 the magazine Esquire put a battery-powered electronic ink panel on the cover of 100,000 copies of one issue. Each panel was to be fitted by hand, and the copies were to travel in refrigerated trucks to preserve the batteries."
msgstr "2008년에 잡지 에스콰이어는 한 호의 10만 부 표지에, 전지로 작동하는 전자 잉크 패널을 붙였습니다. 패널은 한 장씩 손으로 달고, 전지를 아끼기 위해 잡지는 냉장 트럭으로 운반할 계획이었습니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. People section: what Barrett Comiskey, J. D. Albert, Joseph Jacobson did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.people.1"
msgid "Developed the ink in capsules at the MIT Media Lab in 1996."
msgstr "1996년 MIT 미디어랩에서, 캡슐에 넣은 잉크를 개발했습니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. People section: what John Rogers, Zhenan Bao did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.people.2"
msgid "At Bell Laboratories in 2001 they stamped 256 plastic transistors onto a plastic sheet, one for each dot, and laid this ink over them: electronic paper that bends. Bao, a chemist, designs conducting plastics."
msgstr "2001년 벨 연구소에서 두 사람은 플라스틱 시트 위에 플라스틱 트랜지스터 256개를 도장으로 찍었습니다. 화면의 점 하나에 하나씩입니다. 그 위에 이 잉크를 입히자 휘어지는 전자 종이가 되었습니다. 바오는 전기가 통하는 플라스틱을 설계하는 화학자입니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.view.read.label"
msgid "Pick it up to read"
msgstr "집어 들고 읽기"

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.view.read.hint"
msgid "It reads by room light, like print. Watch a page change: every capsule goes black, then white, then to the new page."
msgstr "인쇄물처럼 방의 빛으로 읽습니다. 지면이 바뀌는 모습을 보세요. 모든 캡슐이 검게, 다음에는 희게, 그리고 새 지면으로 바뀝니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "One capsule of electronic ink between its two electrodes, from the patent of Joseph Jacobson, Barrett Comiskey and Jonathan Albert of 1999. Light and dark particles float in a clear fluid. In the second figure they carry opposite charges."
msgstr "두 전극 사이에 놓인 전자 잉크 캡슐 하나. 조지프 제이컵슨, 배럿 코미스키, 조너선 앨버트의 1999년 특허에서. 밝은 입자와 어두운 입자가 투명한 유체에 떠 있습니다. 두 번째 그림에서는 둘이 반대의 전하를 띱니다."

#: hall-5/electrophoretic-ink
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.electrophoretic-ink.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Two diagrams of a circle between two bars. White dots gather at the top of each circle and black dots at the bottom, with plus and minus signs in one."
msgstr "막대 두 개 사이에 원이 있는 도해 두 개. 원마다 위쪽에 흰 점이, 아래쪽에 검은 점이 모여 있고, 한 그림에는 더하기와 빼기 기호가 있습니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.name"
msgid "Cholesteric display"
msgstr "콜레스테릭 디스플레이"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.aka"
msgid "Bistable reflective LCD, writing tablet"
msgstr "쌍안정 반사형 LCD, 전자 메모 패드"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.yearText"
msgid "about 1994"
msgstr "1994년경"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.origin"
msgid "Deng-Ke Yang, J. William Doane and colleagues, Liquid Crystal Institute, Kent State University, USA"
msgstr "덩커 양, J. 윌리엄 돈과 동료들. 켄트 주립 대학교 액정 연구소, 미국"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.statusNote"
msgid "Made as a continuous roll of film since 2009, mostly for pressure-sensitive writing tablets."
msgstr "2009년부터 이어진 두루마리 필름으로 생산되며, 주로 압력에 반응하는 전자 메모 패드에 쓰입니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.lead"
msgid "This liquid crystal reflects one colour in one state and lets light through in the other, and it keeps either state without power."
msgstr "이 액정은 한 상태에서는 한 가지 색을 반사하고 다른 상태에서는 빛을 통과시키며, 어느 상태든 전력 없이 유지합니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.how.1"
msgid "The molecules of a cholesteric liquid crystal arrange themselves in a helix, a spiral like a coiled spring. When all the spirals stand upright, the layer reflects light of one colour. It needs neither the polarising filters nor the backlight of an ordinary liquid crystal display. The pitch of the helix, the length of one turn, sets the colour. When the spirals lie in all directions, light passes through to a black backing."
msgstr "콜레스테릭 액정의 분자들은 나선 모양으로 늘어섭니다. 감긴 용수철 같은 소용돌이입니다. 모든 나선이 곧게 서면 층은 한 가지 색의 빛을 반사합니다. 보통의 액정 디스플레이에 있는 편광 필터도, 백라이트도 필요 없습니다. 나선의 피치, 곧 한 바퀴의 길이가 색을 정합니다. 나선이 사방으로 누우면 빛은 지나가 검은 뒤판에 닿습니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.how.2"
msgid "Both arrangements are stable, so the picture stays when the power is off. A pulse of voltage switches between them. Pressure does the same: when a stylus, a pen without ink, presses on the film, the spirals stand up. The writing tablets work in this way."
msgstr "두 배열 모두 안정적이어서, 전원을 꺼도 그림이 남습니다. 전압 펄스가 둘 사이를 오가게 합니다. 압력도 같은 일을 합니다. 잉크 없는 펜인 스타일러스로 필름을 누르면 나선이 일어섭니다. 전자 메모 패드는 이렇게 작동합니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.good.1"
msgid "Keeps its picture without power, indefinitely"
msgstr "전력 없이, 언제까지나 그림을 유지한다"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.good.2"
msgid "Reflects brightly for a liquid crystal, because it has no polarising filters"
msgstr "편광 필터가 없어서, 액정치고는 밝게 반사한다"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.good.3"
msgid "Is simple enough to coat onto plastic film"
msgstr "플라스틱 필름에 바를 수 있을 만큼 단순하다"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.bad.1"
msgid "Shows one colour for each layer"
msgstr "층 하나에 한 가지 색만 보여 준다"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.bad.2"
msgid "Erases the whole tablet at once, not line by line"
msgstr "한 줄씩이 아니라 판 전체를 한꺼번에 지운다"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.look"
msgid "Watch the stylus write the time. The strokes stay. After a pause, the whole sheet clears at once."
msgstr "스타일러스가 시각을 쓰는 모습을 보세요. 획은 남습니다. 잠시 뒤에 판 전체가 한꺼번에 지워집니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.fact"
msgid "Some beetles get their colour in the same way. A 2009 study linked the metallic green of the jewel beetle Chrysina gloriosa to a shell built like a cholesteric liquid crystal, reflecting light of one circular polarisation only."
msgstr "어떤 딱정벌레는 같은 방식으로 색을 냅니다. 2009년의 한 연구는 딱정벌레 크리시나 글로리오사의 금속성 초록색을, 콜레스테릭 액정처럼 짜인 껍데기와 연결했습니다. 그 껍데기는 한 방향의 원편광만 반사합니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. People section: what Deng-Ke Yang, J. William Doane did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.people.1"
msgid "Made the reflective display that keeps its picture, at the Liquid Crystal Institute of Kent State University."
msgstr "켄트 주립 대학교 액정 연구소에서, 그림을 유지하는 반사형 디스플레이를 만들었습니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.view.slate.label"
msgid "Lean over the tablet"
msgstr "패드 위로 몸 숙이기"

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.view.slate.hint"
msgid "Pressure flips the crystal to its reflecting state, and the stroke stays with no power. One pulse wipes the sheet."
msgstr "압력이 액정을 반사하는 상태로 뒤집고, 획은 전력 없이 남습니다. 펄스 한 번이면 판이 지워집니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The two stable states of a cholesteric cell, from a Kent State University patent of 2000. In the planar texture the droplets of liquid crystal reflect coloured light. In the focal conic texture the light passes through and scatters forward."
msgstr "콜레스테릭 셀의 두 가지 안정 상태. 켄트 주립 대학교의 2000년 특허에서. 플레이너 조직에서는 액정 방울이 색이 있는 빛을 반사합니다. 포컬 코닉 조직에서는 빛이 지나가며 앞쪽으로 흩어집니다."

#: hall-5/cholesteric-lcd
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.cholesteric-lcd.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Two cross-sections of a cell holding three oval droplets, labelled planar texture and focal conic texture. Arrows show light turned back by the first and passing through the second."
msgstr "타원형 방울 세 개가 든 셀의 단면도 두 개. 플레이너 조직, 포컬 코닉 조직이라고 적혀 있습니다. 화살표는 빛이 한쪽에서는 되돌아가고 다른 쪽에서는 지나가는 모습을 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.name"
msgid "Electrowetting display"
msgstr "전기 습윤 디스플레이"

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.origin"
msgid "Robert Hayes and Johan Feenstra, Philips Research, Eindhoven, Netherlands"
msgstr "로버트 헤이스, 요한 페인스트라. 필립스 연구소, 에인트호번, 네덜란드"

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.statusNote"
msgid "Demonstrated at video speed in 2003. It has not become a mass-market product."
msgstr "2003년에 동영상 속도로 시연되었습니다. 대중 제품이 되지는 못했습니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.lead"
msgid "A film of coloured oil covers each pixel until a voltage makes water push the oil aside."
msgstr "색을 입힌 기름 막이 화소를 덮고 있다가, 전압이 걸리면 물이 기름을 옆으로 밀어냅니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.how.1"
msgid "Every cell holds a drop of dyed oil under water, on a white floor with a coating that repels water. With no voltage, the oil spreads into a film and the cell shows the colour of the oil. With a voltage, the floor starts to attract water. The water pushes the oil into a corner and uncovers the white."
msgstr "셀마다 염료로 물들인 기름방울이 물 아래에 들어 있고, 바닥은 희며 물을 밀어내는 도막이 입혀져 있습니다. 전압이 없으면 기름은 막으로 퍼지고, 셀은 기름의 색을 보여 줍니다. 전압을 걸면 바닥이 물을 끌어당기기 시작합니다. 물이 기름을 구석으로 밀어 넣고, 흰 바닥이 드러납니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.how.2"
msgid "Liquids move quickly over such short distances, so the cell switches fast enough for video. Electronic paper that moves particles cannot do that. The authors of the 2003 paper reported a reflective picture four times brighter than a reflective liquid crystal display."
msgstr "이렇게 짧은 거리에서는 액체가 빨리 움직이므로, 셀은 동영상에 쓸 만큼 빠르게 전환합니다. 입자를 움직이는 전자 종이는 그렇게 하지 못합니다. 2003년 논문의 저자들은 반사형 액정 디스플레이보다 네 배 밝은 반사 그림을 보고했습니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.good.1"
msgid "Reflects room light and is fast enough for moving pictures"
msgstr "방의 빛을 반사하면서도 움직이는 그림에 쓸 만큼 빠르다"

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.good.2"
msgid "Gives bright, strong colour from real dye"
msgstr "진짜 염료에서 나오는 밝고 진한 색을 낸다"

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.bad.1"
msgid "Has to hold liquids in millions of cells for years"
msgstr "수백만 개의 셀에 여러 해 동안 액체를 가두어 두어야 한다"

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.bad.2"
msgid "Needs a small voltage to hold its picture, so it does not work fully without power"
msgstr "그림을 유지하는 데 작은 전압이 필요해서, 전력 없이 온전히 작동하지는 않는다"

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.bad.3"
msgid "Did not reach volume production"
msgstr "양산에 이르지 못했다"

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.look"
msgid "The model shows 12 cells, greatly enlarged and tilted toward you. Watch the oil when a cell switches. The oil does not disappear: it gathers in the corner and stands higher."
msgstr "모델은 크게 확대해 이쪽으로 기울인 셀 12개를 보여 줍니다. 셀이 전환할 때 기름을 보세요. 기름은 사라지지 않습니다. 구석에 모여 더 높이 솟습니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.fact"
msgid "The same effect can focus a camera. In 2004 Philips researchers described a lens formed where salty water meets oil in a glass tube 3 millimetres wide. A voltage changed its curve, and it refocused in 10 milliseconds with no moving parts."
msgstr "같은 효과로 카메라의 초점을 맞출 수도 있습니다. 2004년에 필립스의 연구자들은, 너비 3밀리미터의 유리관 안에서 소금물과 기름이 만나는 곳에 생기는 렌즈를 발표했습니다. 전압이 그 곡면을 바꾸었고, 가동 부품 없이 10밀리초 만에 초점을 다시 맞추었습니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. People section: what Gerardo Beni, Susan Hackwood did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.people.1"
msgid "Proposed the display at Bell Laboratories in 1981 and gave the effect its name. Beni was born in Florence and Hackwood in England."
msgstr "1981년 벨 연구소에서 이 디스플레이를 제안하고, 이 효과에 이름을 붙였습니다. 베니는 피렌체에서, 해크우드는 잉글랜드에서 태어났습니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. People section: what Robert Hayes, Johan Feenstra did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.people.2"
msgid "Made it work at video speed at Philips Research in Eindhoven in 2003."
msgstr "2003년 에인트호번의 필립스 연구소에서, 동영상 속도로 작동하게 만들었습니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.view.cells.label"
msgid "Go close to the cells"
msgstr "셀 가까이 가기"

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.view.cells.hint"
msgid "A film of dyed oil covers each cell. A voltage makes the water push the oil into a corner, and the cell clears."
msgstr "염료로 물들인 기름 막이 셀을 덮고 있습니다. 전압을 걸면 물이 기름을 구석으로 밀어 넣고, 셀이 맑아집니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "One electrowetting cell in cross-section, from the patent of Bokke Feenstra, Robert Hayes and Menno Prins, filed in 2003. With no voltage a layer of coloured oil covers the floor of the cell. With a voltage it breaks into drops."
msgstr "전기 습윤 셀 하나의 단면. 보커 페인스트라, 로버트 헤이스, 메노 프린스가 2003년에 출원한 특허에서. 전압이 없으면 색을 입힌 기름 층이 셀의 바닥을 덮습니다. 전압을 걸면 기름은 방울로 갈라집니다."

#: hall-5/electrowetting
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.electrowetting.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Two cross-sections of stacked layers. In the upper one a thin dotted layer runs right across. In the lower one it has drawn up into four humps, beside a battery."
msgstr "겹친 층의 단면도 두 개. 위 그림에서는 얇은 점선 층이 끝에서 끝까지 이어집니다. 아래 그림에서는 그것이 네 개의 혹으로 뭉쳐 있고, 옆에 전지가 있습니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.name"
msgid "Interferometric modulator"
msgstr "간섭 변조기"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.aka"
msgid "IMOD, Mirasol"
msgstr "IMOD, 미라솔"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.origin"
msgid "Mark Miles, Etalon and later Iridigm, USA. First patent granted in 1998"
msgstr "마크 마일스. 에탈론, 나중에는 이리다임, 미국. 첫 특허는 1998년에 등록되었습니다"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.statusNote"
msgid "Sold in a few e-readers from 2011 and in a smartwatch, then discontinued."
msgstr "2011년부터 몇몇 전자책 단말기와 스마트워치 하나에 쓰여 팔리다가 단종되었습니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.lead"
msgid "Colour without pigment: a tiny mirror on springs sits a precise distance behind glass, and that distance sets the colour."
msgstr "안료 없는 색입니다. 용수철에 얹힌 아주 작은 거울이 유리 뒤의 정확한 거리에 놓여 있고, 그 거리가 색을 정합니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.how.1"
msgid "Some light reflects from the back of the glass, and some reflects from the mirror behind it. The two reflections interfere: their waves add together or cancel. The size of the gap decides which colour is reinforced, and the other colours cancel. A soap film and a butterfly wing get their colour in the same way."
msgstr "빛의 일부는 유리의 뒷면에서 반사되고, 일부는 그 뒤의 거울에서 반사됩니다. 두 반사는 간섭합니다. 파동이 서로 보태지거나 지워집니다. 틈의 크기가 어느 색이 강해질지를 정하고, 다른 색들은 지워집니다. 비눗방울 막과 나비 날개도 같은 방식으로 색을 냅니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.how.2"
msgid "Each pixel has three such cells, with gaps set for red, green and blue. A voltage pulls a mirror against the glass, and that cell turns black. Without power, the mirror stays where it was last put."
msgstr "화소 하나에는 이런 셀이 세 개 있고, 틈은 저마다 빨강, 초록, 파랑에 맞추어져 있습니다. 전압을 걸면 거울이 유리에 달라붙고, 그 셀은 검게 됩니다. 전력이 없으면 거울은 마지막으로 놓인 자리에 머뭅니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.good.1"
msgid "Reflects room light, so it is readable in direct sun"
msgstr "방의 빛을 반사하므로 직사광선 아래에서도 읽을 수 있다"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.good.2"
msgid "Holds its picture with almost no power"
msgstr "전력을 거의 쓰지 않고 그림을 유지한다"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.good.3"
msgid "Is fast for a reflective display"
msgstr "반사형 디스플레이치고는 빠르다"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.bad.1"
msgid "Showed pale colours next to a screen that makes its own light"
msgstr "스스로 빛을 내는 화면 옆에서는 색이 흐릿해 보였다"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.bad.2"
msgid "Needs a microscopic moving mirror under each of the three colour cells of every pixel"
msgstr "모든 화소의 세 가지 색 셀마다 아래에 미세한 움직이는 거울이 필요하다"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.bad.3"
msgid "Had limited colour in most of the products that reached the market"
msgstr "시장에 나온 제품 대부분에서 색이 제한적이었다"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.look"
msgid "The model is greatly enlarged. Look from the side to see the three different gaps behind the glass. Watch the mirrors move forward against the glass, which turns their cells black."
msgstr "모델은 크게 확대한 것입니다. 옆에서 보면 유리 뒤에 서로 다른 틈 세 가지가 보입니다. 거울이 유리 쪽으로 다가가 붙으면서 그 셀이 검게 되는 모습을 보세요."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.fact"
msgid "A cell switched to black has not stopped reflecting. With its mirror pulled in, the cavity reinforces ultraviolet light, which the eye cannot see, so the cell looks black."
msgstr "검게 전환된 셀이 반사를 멈춘 것은 아닙니다. 거울이 당겨지면 공동은 눈에 보이지 않는 자외선을 강하게 반사하게 되고, 그래서 셀이 검게 보입니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. People section: what Mark Miles did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.people.1"
msgid "Invented the modulator and founded Etalon, then Iridigm, to develop it. Qualcomm bought Iridigm in 2004."
msgstr "이 변조기를 발명했고, 이를 개발하려고 에탈론을, 이어서 이리다임을 세웠습니다. 퀄컴은 2004년에 이리다임을 인수했습니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.view.gaps.label"
msgid "Look across the gaps"
msgstr "틈을 옆에서 보기"

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.view.gaps.hint"
msgid "A mirror on springs behind glass. The width of the gap picks the colour. Pulled against the glass, the cell is black."
msgstr "유리 뒤에 용수철로 얹힌 거울입니다. 틈의 너비가 색을 고릅니다. 유리에 달라붙으면 셀은 검게 됩니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "Mark Miles's interferometric cell, from his patent of 1998. A thin membrane is held on posts above the surface below, and a voltage between the two pulls it down. The gap between them sets the colour the cell reflects."
msgstr "마크 마일스의 간섭 셀. 그의 1998년 특허에서. 얇은 막이 기둥에 얹혀 아래의 면 위에 떠 있고, 둘 사이에 건 전압이 막을 끌어 내립니다. 둘 사이의 틈이 셀이 반사하는 색을 정합니다."

#: hall-5/interferometric-modulator
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.interferometric-modulator.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Two perspective drawings of a strip of membrane on two posts over a flat base, dipping between them, under a flat plate. Wires are marked 0 volts and 7 volts."
msgstr "평평한 바닥 위의 기둥 두 개에 띠 모양의 막이 얹혀 그 사이에서 처진 모습의 투시도 두 개. 위에는 평평한 판이 있습니다. 전선에는 0볼트와 7볼트라고 적혀 있습니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.name"
msgid "Electrochromic display"
msgstr "전기 변색 디스플레이"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.aka"
msgid "Printed indicator, smart glass"
msgstr "인쇄형 표시기, 스마트 유리"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.origin"
msgid "S. K. Deb, work on tungsten oxide films"
msgstr "S. K. 데브. 산화 텅스텐 막에 관한 연구"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.statusNote"
msgid "Common as dimming mirrors and windows. As a display it is a printed label, not a screen."
msgstr "저절로 어두워지는 거울과 창으로 흔히 쓰입니다. 디스플레이로서는 화면이 아니라 인쇄한 라벨입니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.lead"
msgid "This film changes colour when a small voltage pushes charged atoms into it, and it keeps the new colour with the power off."
msgstr "이 막은 작은 전압이 전하를 띤 원자를 안으로 밀어 넣으면 색이 바뀌고, 전원을 꺼도 새 색을 유지합니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.how.1"
msgid "An electrochromic material darkens when a voltage pushes ions, electrically charged atoms, into it. It clears when the voltage pulls them out again. Tungsten oxide is the classic example. The film is closer to a battery than to a screen. Charging colours it, and it holds the colour when the circuit is open."
msgstr "전기 변색 재료는 전압이 이온, 곧 전하를 띤 원자를 안으로 밀어 넣으면 어두워집니다. 전압이 이온을 다시 끌어내면 맑아집니다. 산화 텅스텐이 대표적인 예입니다. 이 막은 화면보다 전지에 가깝습니다. 충전하면 색이 들고, 회로를 열어도 색을 유지합니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.how.2"
msgid "The common uses are not displays at all: car mirrors that dim themselves, and aircraft windows that darken when a passenger presses a button. Printed onto paper or plastic, the same chemistry makes simple indicators and labels."
msgstr "흔한 쓰임새는 디스플레이도 아닙니다. 저절로 어두워지는 자동차 거울과, 승객이 단추를 누르면 어두워지는 여객기 창입니다. 같은 화학 반응을 종이나 플라스틱에 인쇄하면 간단한 표시기와 라벨이 됩니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.good.1"
msgid "Keeps its colour with no power"
msgstr "전력 없이 색을 유지한다"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.good.2"
msgid "Can be printed like ink on paper or film"
msgstr "잉크처럼 종이나 필름에 인쇄할 수 있다"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.good.3"
msgid "Runs from a very low voltage"
msgstr "아주 낮은 전압으로 작동한다"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.bad.1"
msgid "Is slow: a change takes seconds"
msgstr "느리다. 한 번 바뀌는 데 몇 초가 걸린다"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.bad.2"
msgid "Shows fixed shapes, not a grid of pixels"
msgstr "정해진 모양을 보여 줄 뿐, 화소 격자가 아니다"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.bad.3"
msgid "Offers only a few colours"
msgstr "낼 수 있는 색이 몇 가지뿐이다"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.look"
msgid "The model is a printed label that shows the minutes in printed bars. Wait for the minute to change: the old digit fades and the new digit darkens slowly, over several seconds. The window pane behind the label is the same film on glass."
msgstr "모델은 분을 인쇄된 막대로 보여 주는 인쇄 라벨입니다. 분이 바뀌기를 기다리세요. 앞의 숫자가 옅어지고, 새 숫자가 몇 초에 걸쳐 천천히 짙어집니다. 라벨 뒤의 창유리는 같은 막을 유리에 입힌 것입니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.fact"
msgid "Prussian blue, a pigment first made in Berlin in the early 1700s and still used in paints and printing inks, is electrochromic. The electrochemistry of thin films of it was first reported only in 1978."
msgstr "프러시안 블루는 18세기 초에 베를린에서 처음 만들어져 지금도 물감과 인쇄 잉크에 쓰이는 안료인데, 전기 변색 재료이기도 합니다. 그 박막의 전기 화학은 1978년에야 처음 보고되었습니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. People section: what S. K. Deb did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.people.1"
msgid "Published the work on tungsten oxide films, in 1969, from which electrochromic displays grew."
msgstr "1969년에 산화 텅스텐 막에 관한 연구를 발표했고, 전기 변색 디스플레이는 거기에서 자라났습니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. People section: what Paula Hammond, Dean DeLongchamp did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.people.2"
msgid "At MIT they built electrochromic films one molecular layer at a time, which switched faster and with more contrast than films made in other ways. DeLongchamp's thesis of 2003 points to a full-colour display."
msgstr "MIT에서 두 사람은 전기 변색 막을 분자층을 한 층씩 쌓아 만들었습니다. 이 막은 다른 방법으로 만든 막보다 더 빠르게, 더 높은 대비로 바뀌었습니다. 들롱샴의 2003년 학위 논문은 풀컬러 디스플레이로 가는 길을 가리킵니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.view.label.label"
msgid "Go close to the label"
msgstr "라벨 가까이 가기"

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.view.label.hint"
msgid "Ions soak into the printed film and darken it. That takes seconds, not milliseconds, and then it holds with no power."
msgstr "이온이 인쇄된 막에 스며들어 막을 어둡게 합니다. 밀리초가 아니라 몇 초가 걸리고, 그 뒤로는 전력 없이 유지됩니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "A layered electrochromic cell, from the patent of Satyendra Deb and Robert Shaw of 1970. Thin films lie between two electrodes on glass. A switch sends current one way to colour the cell and the other way to bleach it."
msgstr "층을 이룬 전기 변색 셀. 사티엔드라 데브와 로버트 쇼의 1970년 특허에서. 유리 위의 두 전극 사이에 얇은 막들이 놓여 있습니다. 스위치가 전류를 한쪽으로 보내면 셀에 색이 들고, 반대쪽으로 보내면 색이 빠집니다."

#: hall-5/electrochromic
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.electrochromic.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Two line drawings of a flat layered slab wired to a battery. In the lower one a switch is marked color and bleach."
msgstr "전지에 배선된, 층을 이룬 납작한 판의 선 그림 두 개. 아래 그림의 스위치에는 color와 bleach라고 적혀 있습니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.name"
msgid "Pepper's ghost"
msgstr "페퍼의 유령"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.origin"
msgid "Henry Dircks and John Henry Pepper, Royal Polytechnic Institution, London"
msgstr "헨리 더크스, 존 헨리 페퍼. 런던 왕립 폴리테크닉"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.statusNote"
msgid "Used in theme parks, museums and teleprompters, and in concerts that are advertised as holograms."
msgstr "테마파크, 박물관, 프롬프터에 쓰이고, 홀로그램이라고 광고하는 공연에도 쓰입니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.lead"
msgid "A tilted sheet of glass reflects something bright that the audience cannot see directly."
msgstr "비스듬히 세운 유리판이, 관객이 직접 볼 수 없는 밝은 무언가를 비춥니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.how.1"
msgid "Glass lets most light through and reflects a little. A large, clean sheet of glass stands across a dim stage at a tilt. A lamp lights a figure that is hidden below or to the side. The audience looks through the glass at the stage. It also sees the faint reflection of the hidden figure, which seems to stand among the furniture."
msgstr "유리는 빛의 대부분을 통과시키고 조금만 반사합니다. 크고 깨끗한 유리판 한 장을 어둑한 무대를 가로질러 비스듬히 세웁니다. 램프가 아래나 옆에 숨은 인물을 비춥니다. 관객은 유리 너머로 무대를 봅니다. 동시에 숨은 인물의 희미한 반사도 보게 되고, 그 인물은 가구 사이에 서 있는 것처럼 보입니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.how.2"
msgid "The effect was described in the 1500s. Dircks developed a version for the theatre from 1858. Pepper made it practical and first showed it in December 1862, in a scene from Dickens. The two men patented it the following year. The stage shows of today replace the hidden actor with a video screen and the glass with stretched film."
msgstr "이 효과는 16세기에 기록되었습니다. 더크스는 1858년부터 극장용 방식을 개발했습니다. 페퍼가 그것을 실용적으로 만들어, 1862년 12월에 디킨스의 한 장면에서 처음 선보였습니다. 두 사람은 이듬해에 특허를 받았습니다. 오늘날의 무대는 숨은 배우 대신 영상 화면을, 유리 대신 팽팽하게 당긴 필름을 씁니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.good.1"
msgid "Shows a bright, full-colour figure that seems to stand in real space"
msgstr "실제 공간에 서 있는 듯한, 밝은 컬러의 인물을 보여 준다"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.good.2"
msgid "Needs no special glasses, and a whole audience can see it"
msgstr "특수 안경이 필요 없고, 관객 전체가 볼 수 있다"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.good.3"
msgid "Mixes with real actors and scenery"
msgstr "실제 배우와 무대 장치와 어우러진다"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.bad.1"
msgid "Gives a flat reflection at one fixed depth, not a three-dimensional picture"
msgstr "정해진 한 깊이에 있는 평평한 반사일 뿐, 입체 영상이 아니다"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.bad.2"
msgid "Needs a dark stage, a hidden bright area and careful control of what the audience can see"
msgstr "어두운 무대와 숨긴 밝은 공간, 그리고 관객이 무엇을 볼 수 있는지에 대한 세심한 통제가 필요하다"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.bad.3"
msgid "Is not a hologram, although concerts that use it are advertised as holograms"
msgstr "이것을 쓰는 공연이 홀로그램이라고 광고되지만, 홀로그램이 아니다"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.look"
msgid "Press the Sit down button for a seat in the audience. A pale figure walks on the stage, and the chair shows through it. Move your head: the figure keeps its place, as a true mirror image does, and ends at the edge of the glass. Then press the Whole piece button and look into the pit in front of the stage. The real figure lies there in limelight, a bright stage lamp, under the leaning glass."
msgstr "자리에 앉기 버튼을 누르면 객석에 앉습니다. 창백한 인물이 무대를 걷고, 그 몸 너머로 의자가 비쳐 보입니다. 머리를 움직여 보세요. 인물은 진짜 거울상처럼 제자리를 지키고, 유리의 가장자리에서 끊깁니다. 그런 다음 전체 보기 버튼을 누르고, 무대 앞의 구덩이를 들여다보세요. 진짜 인물은 거기, 기울어진 유리 아래에서, 라임라이트라는 밝은 무대 조명을 받으며 누워 있습니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.fact"
msgid "Pepper wrote that even Michael Faraday, taken behind the scenes, said he did not understand how the ghost was made. Pepper put Faraday's hand on one of the huge glass plates, and only then did Faraday say he understood."
msgstr "페퍼는, 무대 뒤로 안내받은 마이클 패러데이조차 유령이 어떻게 만들어지는지 모르겠다고 말했다고 썼습니다. 페퍼가 패러데이의 손을 커다란 유리판 한 장에 대어 주자, 그제야 패러데이는 알겠다고 말했습니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. People section: what Henry Dircks did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.people.1"
msgid "Engineer who devised the illusion."
msgstr "이 착시를 고안한 기술자."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. People section: what John Henry Pepper did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.people.2"
msgid "Staged it at the Royal Polytechnic Institution in London in 1862, and it took his name."
msgstr "1862년 런던의 왕립 폴리테크닉에서 이것을 무대에 올렸고, 여기에 그의 이름이 붙었습니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.view.seat.label"
msgid "Sit down"
msgstr "자리에 앉기"

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.media.stage-1865.caption"
msgid "The stage arrangement for Pepper's ghost, from Le Monde Illustré of 21 January 1865. A figure in white below the stage, lit by a lamp, is reflected in a tilted sheet of glass and appears beside the actor."
msgstr "페퍼의 유령을 위한 무대 배치. 르 몽드 일뤼스트레 1865년 1월 21일 자에서. 무대 아래에서 램프를 받는 흰옷의 인물이 비스듬한 유리판에 비쳐, 배우 옆에 나타납니다."

#: hall-6/peppers-ghost
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.peppers-ghost.media.stage-1865.alt"
msgid "Engraving in cross-section: an audience, a sloping pane of glass, an actor facing a pale figure on stage, under the stage a man aiming a lantern at a draped figure."
msgstr "단면으로 그린 판화: 관객, 비스듬한 유리판, 무대 위에서 창백한 인물과 마주한 배우, 그리고 무대 아래에서 천을 두른 인물에게 등불을 겨눈 남자."

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.name"
msgid "Illusion transmitter"
msgstr "환영 전송기"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.aka"
msgid "Real-image mirror display"
msgstr "실상 거울 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.origin"
msgid "Valerie Thomas, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, USA. Patent granted on 21 October 1980"
msgstr "밸러리 토머스, NASA 고더드 우주 비행 센터, 미국. 특허는 1980년 10월 21일에 등록되었습니다"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.statusNote"
msgid "The optics society SPIE reports that it is still in use at NASA, and that it has been applied in television and in imaging for surgery."
msgstr "광학 학회 SPIE는 이것이 지금도 NASA에서 쓰이고 있으며, 텔레비전과 수술용 영상에도 응용되었다고 전합니다."

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.lead"
msgid "A curved mirror stands a picture of an object in the air in front of it, and a camera and a second mirror make the picture appear again somewhere else."
msgstr "굽은 거울이 물체의 상을 자기 앞의 허공에 세우고, 카메라와 두 번째 거울이 그 상을 다른 곳에 다시 나타나게 합니다."

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.how.1"
msgid "A flat mirror shows a picture that seems to lie behind the glass. A concave mirror, curved like the inside of a bowl, can do the opposite. It sends the light from an object back to a point in front of itself. There the light forms a real image: a picture that hangs in the air, with depth, where a hand can pass through it."
msgstr "평평한 거울은 유리 뒤에 있는 듯한 상을 보여 줍니다. 오목 거울은 그릇의 안쪽처럼 굽은 거울로, 그 반대를 할 수 있습니다. 물체에서 온 빛을 자기 앞의 한 점으로 되돌려 보냅니다. 거기서 빛은 실상을 만듭니다. 허공에 걸려 있고, 깊이가 있고, 손이 지나갈 수 있는 상입니다."

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.how.2"
msgid "At an exhibition in 1976, Thomas saw such a mirror make a light bulb seem to glow after it had been taken out of its socket. She asked whether the picture could be sent. In her system a video camera looks at the first mirror. Its signal goes to a projector at the far end, which is aimed at a second concave mirror, and that mirror stands the picture in the air again."
msgstr "1976년의 한 전시회에서 토머스는, 그런 거울이 소켓에서 빼낸 전구를 여전히 빛나는 것처럼 보이게 하는 모습을 보았습니다. 그녀는 그 상을 보낼 수 있을지 물었습니다. 그녀의 방식에서는 비디오카메라가 첫 번째 거울을 바라봅니다. 그 신호는 먼 쪽의 프로젝터로 가고, 프로젝터는 두 번째 오목 거울을 향해 있으며, 그 거울이 상을 다시 허공에 세웁니다."

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.good.1"
msgid "Shows depth without glasses"
msgstr "안경 없이 깊이를 보여 준다"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.good.2"
msgid "Puts the picture in front of the apparatus, not behind glass"
msgstr "상을 유리 뒤가 아니라 장치 앞에 놓는다"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.good.3"
msgid "Needs only mirrors, a camera and a projector"
msgstr "거울과 카메라와 프로젝터만 있으면 된다"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.bad.1"
msgid "Can be seen only from inside the cone of light that leaves the mirror"
msgstr "거울에서 나오는 빛의 원뿔 안에서만 볼 수 있다"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.bad.2"
msgid "The picture cannot be larger than the mirror behind it"
msgstr "상이 뒤에 있는 거울보다 클 수 없다"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.bad.3"
msgid "Any flaw or tilt in the mirror bends the picture"
msgstr "거울에 흠이나 기울어짐이 있으면 상이 일그러진다"

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.look"
msgid "On the left a real bulb hangs hidden under the socket, and its picture stands in the empty socket above. On the right there is no bulb at all. Watch the left bulb go dark: the picture on the right follows a moment later. Then switch the daylight off."
msgstr "왼쪽에는 진짜 전구가 소켓 아래에 숨어 매달려 있고, 그 상이 위의 빈 소켓에 서 있습니다. 오른쪽에는 전구가 아예 없습니다. 왼쪽 전구가 꺼지는 모습을 보세요. 오른쪽의 상도 한순간 뒤에 따라 꺼집니다. 그런 다음 햇빛을 끄세요."

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.fact"
msgid "When she invented it, Thomas was managing the image-processing systems for Landsat, the first satellite to send multi-spectral pictures of the Earth's resources from space. She had joined NASA as a data analyst."
msgstr "이것을 발명했을 때 토머스는, 지구 자원의 다중 분광 영상을 우주에서 처음으로 보낸 위성인 랜드샛의 영상 처리 시스템을 관리하고 있었습니다. 그녀는 데이터 분석가로 NASA에 들어갔습니다."

#: hall-6/illusion-transmitter
#. People section: what Valerie Thomas did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.illusion-transmitter.people.1"
msgid "Born in Maryland in 1943. One of only two women to major in physics at Morgan State University, she joined NASA as a data analyst and invented the illusion transmitter there."
msgstr "1943년 메릴랜드에서 태어났습니다. 모건 주립 대학교에서 물리학을 전공한 여성은 그녀를 포함해 단 두 명이었습니다. 데이터 분석가로 NASA에 들어가, 그곳에서 환영 전송기를 발명했습니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.name"
msgid "Fog screen"
msgstr "포그 스크린"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.aka"
msgid "Walk-through screen"
msgstr "걸어서 통과하는 스크린"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.origin"
msgid "Karri Palovuori and Ismo Rakkolainen, Tampere University of Technology, Finland"
msgstr "카리 팔로부오리, 이스모 라콜라이넨. 탐페레 공과대학교, 핀란드"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.statusNote"
msgid "Rented for product launches, exhibitions and stage entrances."
msgstr "제품 발표회, 전시회, 무대 등장 연출에 대여됩니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.lead"
msgid "A thin sheet of fog, held flat by curtains of moving air, serves as a projection screen that you can walk through."
msgstr "움직이는 공기의 장막이 평평하게 붙잡아 주는 얇은 안개 막이, 걸어서 통과할 수 있는 투사 스크린이 됩니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.how.1"
msgid "A unit overhead makes dry fog from plain water. It releases the fog downward between two layers of smooth air that flows without turbulence. The air stops the fog from spreading, so the fog falls as a thin, flat sheet. A projector behind the sheet lights it."
msgstr "머리 위의 장치가 맹물로 마른 안개를 만듭니다. 장치는 그 안개를, 난류 없이 매끄럽게 흐르는 두 겹의 공기 사이로 아래를 향해 내보냅니다. 공기가 안개의 퍼짐을 막아, 안개는 얇고 평평한 막으로 떨어집니다. 막 뒤의 프로젝터가 그것을 비춥니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.how.2"
msgid "The picture is brightest when you look toward the projector. It stays usable up to about 60 degrees to the side. From the same side as the projector the picture is faint, so each face of the sheet needs its own projector."
msgstr "그림은 프로젝터 쪽을 바라볼 때 가장 밝습니다. 옆으로 약 60도까지는 볼 만합니다. 프로젝터와 같은 쪽에서는 그림이 흐릿해서, 막의 양면에는 저마다 프로젝터가 필요합니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.good.1"
msgid "Makes a picture that hangs in a doorway and that people can walk through"
msgstr "문간에 걸려 있고 사람이 걸어서 통과할 수 있는 그림을 만든다"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.good.2"
msgid "Has no surface to touch, break or clean"
msgstr "만지거나 깨뜨리거나 닦을 표면이 없다"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.bad.1"
msgid "Smears fine detail, because the fog is turbulent"
msgstr "안개가 난류를 일으켜 세밀한 부분이 번진다"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.bad.2"
msgid "Has a narrow viewing angle and needs a dim room"
msgstr "시야각이 좁고 어둑한 방이 필요하다"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.bad.3"
msgid "Suffers from draughts and needs a steady supply of water"
msgstr "외풍에 약하고, 물을 꾸준히 대 주어야 한다"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.look"
msgid "Look through the sheet toward the projector. Then turn the model until you see the sheet from the side, and watch the picture fade. By day there is almost nothing to see, so switch the daylight off."
msgstr "막 너머로 프로젝터 쪽을 보세요. 그런 다음 막이 옆에서 보일 때까지 모델을 돌리고, 그림이 희미해지는 모습을 보세요. 낮에는 볼 것이 거의 없으니 햇빛을 끄세요."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.fact"
msgid "Its developers report that the first thing most people do on seeing one is put their hands into it. The fog feels like slightly cool air, and the picture forms again behind anyone who walks through."
msgstr "개발자들에 따르면, 이것을 본 사람 대부분이 가장 먼저 하는 일은 손을 넣어 보는 것입니다. 안개는 조금 서늘한 공기처럼 느껴지고, 걸어서 지나간 사람 뒤에서 그림은 다시 만들어집니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. People section: what Karri Palovuori, Ismo Rakkolainen did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.people.1"
msgid "Developed the fog screen that people can walk through, at Tampere University of Technology in 2002."
msgstr "2002년 탐페레 공과대학교에서, 사람이 걸어서 통과할 수 있는 포그 스크린을 개발했습니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Note under the People section.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.note"
msgid "Projection onto fountains and curtains of water is older. A patent for an ornamental fountain screen dates from 1899."
msgstr "분수와 물의 장막에 영상을 비추는 일은 더 오래되었습니다. 장식용 분수 스크린의 특허는 1899년의 것입니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.view.fog.label"
msgid "Step up to the fog"
msgstr "안개 앞으로 다가서기"

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.view.fog.hint"
msgid "The picture hangs in falling fog, ragged by the time it reaches the floor. It is brightest facing the projector."
msgstr "그림은 떨어지는 안개에 걸려 있고, 바닥에 닿을 즈음에는 너덜너덜해집니다. 프로젝터를 마주 볼 때 가장 밝습니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The principle of the fog screen, from the patent of Karri Palovuori and Ismo Rakkolainen of 2004. A thin flat sheet of fog is carried along inside a flow of air, and a projector throws its picture onto it."
msgstr "포그 스크린의 원리. 카리 팔로부오리와 이스모 라콜라이넨의 2004년 특허에서. 얇고 평평한 안개 막이 공기의 흐름 안에 실려 가고, 프로젝터가 거기에 그림을 비춥니다."

#: hall-6/fog-screen
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.fog-screen.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Perspective line drawing: a flat dotted sheet stretched between two box shapes inside an outlined slab, with a small lamp shape above sending rays toward it."
msgstr "선으로 그린 투시도: 윤곽만 그린 판 안에서, 점선으로 된 평평한 막이 상자 모양 두 개 사이에 걸려 있고, 위의 작은 램프 모양에서 광선이 그쪽으로 향합니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.name"
msgid "Persistence of vision display"
msgstr "잔상 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.aka"
msgid "POV display, LED fan"
msgstr "POV 디스플레이, LED 팬"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.origin"
msgid "Joseph Plateau used the effect behind it in his spinning picture discs. Spinning LED versions are recent"
msgstr "조제프 플라토는 그 바탕이 되는 효과를 도는 그림 원판에 썼습니다. 도는 LED를 쓰는 방식은 최근의 것입니다"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.statusNote"
msgid "Sold as spinning LED fans for shop windows, often advertised as holograms."
msgstr "진열창용 회전 LED 팬으로 팔리며, 흔히 홀로그램이라고 광고됩니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.lead"
msgid "One row of lights moves so fast that the eye sees the whole area it sweeps as lit."
msgstr "한 줄의 빛이 아주 빠르게 움직여서, 눈에는 그것이 지나가는 면 전체가 빛나는 것처럼 보입니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.how.1"
msgid "A bar of LEDs spins like a propeller. Each LED is switched on and off many times in every turn, timed to the position of the bar. It leaves dots of light at the right places around the circle. The eye cannot follow the bar and sees the dots as a steady picture."
msgstr "LED가 늘어선 막대가 프로펠러처럼 돕니다. LED는 저마다 막대의 위치에 맞추어, 한 바퀴 도는 동안 여러 번 켜지고 꺼집니다. 그래서 원둘레의 알맞은 자리에 빛점을 남깁니다. 눈은 막대를 따라가지 못하고, 그 점들을 멈춘 그림으로 봅니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.how.2"
msgid "Most of the disc is empty air most of the time, so you can see through the picture to whatever is behind it. That is why the picture seems to float."
msgstr "원판의 대부분은 대부분의 시간 동안 빈 공기입니다. 그래서 그림 너머로 뒤에 있는 것이 보입니다. 그림이 떠 있는 것처럼 보이는 까닭입니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.good.1"
msgid "Makes a large, bright picture from very few lights"
msgstr "아주 적은 빛으로 크고 밝은 그림을 만든다"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.good.2"
msgid "Is see-through, so the picture seems to float in the air"
msgstr "건너편이 비쳐 보여서, 그림이 공중에 떠 있는 것처럼 보인다"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.good.3"
msgid "Costs little"
msgstr "값이 싸다"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.bad.1"
msgid "Has a fast spinning blade, which needs a guard and makes noise"
msgstr "빠르게 도는 날이 있어서 보호망이 필요하고 소리가 난다"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.bad.2"
msgid "Flickers on camera and at the edge of your vision"
msgstr "카메라에서, 그리고 시야의 가장자리에서 깜박인다"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.bad.3"
msgid "Is flat, although it is often advertised as a hologram"
msgstr "흔히 홀로그램이라고 광고되지만 평면이다"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.look"
msgid "Watch the rotor speed up and slow down, again and again. At low speed you can see that it is only a bar with lamps on it. As it speeds up, the bar disappears and the picture appears."
msgstr "회전자가 빨라졌다 느려졌다 하기를 되풀이하는 모습을 보세요. 느릴 때는 그것이 램프가 달린 막대일 뿐임을 알 수 있습니다. 빨라지면 막대는 사라지고 그림이 나타납니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.fact"
msgid "Isaac Newton described the effect with a burning coal. Moved quickly round in a circle, he wrote in his Opticks, it makes the whole circle look like fire, because the sensation of the coal lasts until it comes round again."
msgstr "아이작 뉴턴은 이 효과를 불붙은 석탄으로 설명했습니다. 석탄을 빠르게 원을 그리며 돌리면 원 전체가 불처럼 보인다고 그는 광학에 썼습니다. 석탄의 감각이, 석탄이 한 바퀴 돌아올 때까지 남아 있기 때문입니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. People section: what Joseph Plateau did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.people.1"
msgid "Used the effect in his spinning picture discs of 1832."
msgstr "1832년에 도는 그림 원판에 이 효과를 썼습니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Note under the People section.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.note"
msgid "Persistence of vision is the traditional name, not a full explanation. The way the eye and brain merge flicker and perceive motion is more complex. No first maker of the spinning LED kind is agreed."
msgstr "잔상(시각의 지속)은 전통적인 이름이지, 온전한 설명이 아닙니다. 눈과 뇌가 깜박임을 하나로 합치고 움직임을 지각하는 방식은 더 복잡합니다. 도는 LED 방식을 누가 처음 만들었는지는 의견이 모이지 않았습니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.view.bar.label"
msgid "Go close to the bar"
msgstr "막대 가까이 가기"

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.view.bar.hint"
msgid "One bar of lamps, eighteen each side of the hub. Wait for it to slow down: the picture comes apart into a bar again."
msgstr "램프가 늘어선 막대 하나로, 축의 양쪽에 열여덟 개씩 있습니다. 느려지기를 기다려 보세요. 그림이 흩어져 다시 막대가 됩니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.media.disc.caption"
msgid "A paper phenakistiscope disc of about 1830. Spun and watched through its ten slots, the drawings merge into a cat leaping over a stool and a donkey moving its ears."
msgstr "1830년경의 종이 페나키스토스코프 원판. 돌리면서 열 개의 틈으로 보면, 그림들이 이어져 의자를 뛰어넘는 고양이와 귀를 움직이는 당나귀가 됩니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.media.disc.alt"
msgid "A round cream card with a ring of ten slots near the edge. Around it run small coloured drawings of cats and stools, and an inner ring of donkey heads."
msgstr "가장자리 가까이에 틈 열 개가 고리처럼 뚫린 둥근 크림색 판지. 그 둘레로 고양이와 의자를 그린 작은 채색 그림이 늘어서 있고, 안쪽 고리에는 당나귀 머리가 늘어서 있습니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.media.mirror-viewing.caption"
msgid "How a phenakistiscope was used, from Gaston Tissandier's Les récréations scientifiques, 1884. The viewer holds the spinning disc up to a mirror and looks through its slots at the reflection."
msgstr "페나키스토스코프를 쓰는 방법. 가스통 티상디에의 Les récréations scientifiques, 1884년에서. 보는 사람은 도는 원판을 거울 앞에 들고, 틈 사이로 거울에 비친 모습을 봅니다."

#: hall-6/persistence-of-vision
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.persistence-of-vision.media.mirror-viewing.alt"
msgid "Engraving of a man in profile holding a slotted disc on a handle to his eye. A mirror before him reflects the pictured side of the disc and his hands."
msgstr "손잡이가 달린, 틈이 난 원판을 눈에 댄 남자의 옆모습 판화. 앞의 거울에 원판의 그림 쪽 면과 그의 두 손이 비칩니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.name"
msgid "Swept-volume display"
msgstr "체적 주사 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.origin"
msgid "Swept-screen system by Hirsch, 1961. Best known product: Perspecta, Actuality Systems, USA, 2002"
msgstr "허시의 스윕 스크린 방식, 1961년. 가장 잘 알려진 제품: 퍼스펙타, 액추얼리티 시스템스, 미국, 2002년"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.statusNote"
msgid "A few specialist products."
msgstr "전문가용 제품이 몇 가지 있습니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.lead"
msgid "A screen spins through a volume of space while a fast projector lights each point as the screen passes it."
msgstr "스크린이 공간의 한 부피를 돌며 지나가고, 빠른 프로젝터가 스크린이 지나는 점마다 빛을 비춥니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.how.1"
msgid "A flat screen that turns about its own axis passes every point inside a cylinder or a sphere once or twice in each turn. At each angle, a projector shows the matching slice of a three-dimensional scene on the screen. When this happens fast enough, the slices merge into an object that looks solid."
msgstr "자기 축을 중심으로 도는 평평한 스크린은, 원기둥이나 구 안의 모든 점을 한 바퀴에 한두 번씩 지나갑니다. 각도마다 프로젝터는 입체 장면 가운데 그 자리에 해당하는 단면을 스크린에 비춥니다. 이것이 충분히 빠르게 일어나면, 단면들이 합쳐져 속이 찬 물체처럼 보입니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.how.2"
msgid "Perspecta spun its screen at 900 revolutions a minute inside a 25 centimetre dome and addressed about 100 million points. Several people can stand around it, and each person sees the object from their own side. They need no glasses, and their eyes focus on the object itself."
msgstr "퍼스펙타는 지름 25센티미터의 돔 안에서 스크린을 분당 900회전으로 돌렸고, 약 1억 개의 점을 지정했습니다. 여러 사람이 둘레에 설 수 있고, 저마다 자기 쪽에서 물체를 봅니다. 안경이 필요 없고, 눈은 물체 그 자체에 초점을 맞춥니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.good.1"
msgid "Gives real depth from every direction at once"
msgstr "모든 방향에서 동시에 진짜 깊이를 보여 준다"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.good.2"
msgid "Lets the eyes focus where the object is, so there is no strain"
msgstr "물체가 있는 곳에 눈의 초점이 맞으므로 피로하지 않다"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.good.3"
msgid "Needs nothing worn on the head"
msgstr "머리에 아무것도 쓸 필요가 없다"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.bad.1"
msgid "Makes everything see-through, because points of light cannot hide what is behind them"
msgstr "빛점은 뒤에 있는 것을 가릴 수 없어서, 모든 것이 비쳐 보인다"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.bad.2"
msgid "Has a large, fast-moving part inside a dome"
msgstr "돔 안에 크고 빠르게 움직이는 부품이 있다"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.bad.3"
msgid "Needs a very large amount of data"
msgstr "아주 많은 양의 데이터가 필요하다"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.look"
msgid "The model turns the screen slowly so that you can see it. Points near the screen brighten as it passes. The figure is a helix, a spiral. Turn the model all the way round to see that the spiral really has depth."
msgstr "모델은 여러분이 볼 수 있도록 스크린을 천천히 돌립니다. 스크린이 지나갈 때 가까운 점들이 밝아집니다. 도형은 나선입니다. 모델을 한 바퀴 다 돌려 보면, 나선에 정말로 깊이가 있음을 알 수 있습니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.fact"
msgid "Hirsch called his machine a generescope and had air traffic control in mind. His patent says it would let a group of observers see the positions and movements of aircraft in three dimensions."
msgstr "허시는 자신의 기계를 제너레스코프라고 불렀고, 항공 교통 관제를 염두에 두었습니다. 그의 특허는, 이것이 여러 관측자에게 항공기의 위치와 움직임을 3차원으로 보여 줄 것이라고 말합니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. People section: what Max Hirsch did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.people.1"
msgid "Patented a swept-screen display in 1961. He called it a generescope and had air traffic control in mind."
msgstr "1961년에 스윕 스크린 디스플레이의 특허를 받았습니다. 그것을 제너레스코프라고 불렀고, 항공 교통 관제를 염두에 두었습니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. People section: what Gregg Favalora did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.people.2"
msgid "Founder of Actuality Systems, which put the Perspecta display on sale in 2002."
msgstr "액추얼리티 시스템스의 창업자. 이 회사는 2002년에 퍼스펙타 디스플레이를 시장에 내놓았습니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.view.dome.label"
msgid "Go close to the dome"
msgstr "돔 가까이 가기"

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.view.dome.hint"
msgid "The points are inside the dome, not on it. Move round them: each is lit only as the spinning screen passes through it."
msgstr "점들은 돔 위가 아니라 돔 안에 있습니다. 둘레를 돌아 보세요. 점은 저마다 도는 스크린이 지나갈 때에만 빛납니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "Max Hirsch's generescope, from his patent of 1961. A picture tube in the column projects through mirrors onto a screen that turns inside the dome, so that each spot of light gets a place in depth."
msgstr "맥스 허시의 제너레스코프. 그의 1961년 특허에서. 기둥 안의 수상관이 거울을 거쳐, 돔 안에서 도는 스크린에 비춥니다. 그래서 빛점마다 깊이 속에 자리를 얻습니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawing: a cross-section of a domed cabinet over a tall column that holds a tube and lenses, and below it a sketch of rays passing between tilted mirrors."
msgstr "선 그림: 관과 렌즈가 든 높은 기둥 위에 돔이 달린 함을 얹은 단면도. 그 아래에는 기울어진 거울 사이를 지나는 광선의 약도."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.media.patent-2.caption"
msgid "Inside the Perspecta display, from the Actuality Systems patent of 2003. The picture comes up through the hollow shaft of the motor, and three mirrors fold it onto the round screen that spins above."
msgstr "퍼스펙타 디스플레이의 내부. 액추얼리티 시스템스의 2003년 특허에서. 그림은 모터의 속이 빈 축을 따라 올라오고, 거울 세 장이 그것을 꺾어 위에서 도는 둥근 스크린으로 보냅니다."

#: hall-6/swept-volume
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.swept-volume.media.patent-2.alt"
msgid "Perspective drawing: a round screen standing on edge on a vertical axis, a ring-shaped motor at its foot, and three flat mirrors around it joined by dashed light paths."
msgstr "투시도: 세로축 위에 모로 선 둥근 스크린, 그 밑동의 고리 모양 모터, 둘레의 평평한 거울 세 장. 거울들은 점선의 빛 길로 이어져 있습니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.name"
msgid "Static-volume display"
msgstr "정적 체적 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.origin"
msgid "Downing and colleagues, light from crossed infrared beams in doped glass (glass with other elements added). DepthCube, LightSpace Technologies, 2004"
msgstr "다우닝과 동료들. 다른 원소를 넣은 유리 안에서, 엇갈린 적외선 빔이 내는 빛. 뎁스큐브, 라이트스페이스 테크놀로지스, 2004년"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.statusNote"
msgid "Demonstrated in several forms. It has no moving parts and no mass market."
msgstr "여러 형태로 시연되었습니다. 가동 부품이 없고, 대중 시장도 없습니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.lead"
msgid "Any point inside a block of space can be made to give off light or to scatter light, and nothing moves."
msgstr "공간 한 덩어리 안의 어느 점이든 빛을 내게 하거나 빛을 흩뜨리게 할 수 있고, 움직이는 것은 아무것도 없습니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.how.1"
msgid "One approach is a stack of liquid crystal sheets, each of which can switch from clear to milky. A fast projector behind the stack shows one slice of the scene at a time. Only the matching sheet turns milky and catches that slice. DepthCube used 20 sheets."
msgstr "한 가지 방법은 액정 판을 쌓는 것입니다. 판은 저마다 투명에서 뿌연 상태로 바뀔 수 있습니다. 쌓은 판 뒤의 빠른 프로젝터가 장면의 단면을 한 번에 한 장씩 비춥니다. 그 단면에 해당하는 판만 뿌옇게 되어 그림을 받습니다. 뎁스큐브는 판 20장을 썼습니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.how.2"
msgid "Another approach uses a block of special glass that glows only where two invisible infrared beams cross. When the crossing point is steered through the block, it draws in three dimensions. The prototype was a cube about 15 millimetres on each side, made of three layers of glass: one glows red, one green and one blue."
msgstr "다른 방법은, 눈에 보이지 않는 적외선 빔 두 줄기가 엇갈리는 곳에서만 빛나는 특수 유리 덩어리를 씁니다. 교차점을 덩어리 안에서 이리저리 옮기면, 3차원으로 그림을 그립니다. 시제품은 한 변이 약 15밀리미터인 정육면체로, 유리 세 겹으로 만들었습니다. 한 겹은 빨강, 한 겹은 초록, 한 겹은 파랑으로 빛납니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.good.1"
msgid "Gives true depth with no moving parts"
msgstr "가동 부품 없이 진짜 깊이를 보여 준다"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.good.2"
msgid "Serves several viewers at once, without glasses"
msgstr "안경 없이 여러 사람이 동시에 본다"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.bad.1"
msgid "Has only as many steps of depth as it has sheets"
msgstr "깊이의 단계는 판의 수만큼뿐이다"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.bad.2"
msgid "Gives a translucent picture, like all displays that fill a volume"
msgstr "부피를 채우는 디스플레이가 다 그렇듯, 그림이 반투명하다"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.bad.3"
msgid "Is bulky for the size of its picture"
msgstr "그림의 크기에 견주어 덩치가 크다"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.look"
msgid "The figure in the stack is a knot. The lit sheet moves from front to back, and the model slows it to a few steps a second. Look along the stack from the side to see the knot divided into slices. Beside the stack stands the block of glass, many times its real size. Its two beams are drawn in red so that they can be seen. The point where they cross draws a knot too, in the colour of the layer it passes through."
msgstr "쌓은 판 안의 도형은 매듭입니다. 빛이 들어온 판은 앞에서 뒤로 옮겨 가고, 모델은 그것을 1초에 몇 단계로 늦추었습니다. 쌓은 판을 옆에서 보면, 매듭이 단면으로 나뉜 것이 보입니다. 그 옆에는 유리 덩어리가 있는데, 실물보다 여러 배 크게 만들었습니다. 빔 두 줄기는 눈에 보이도록 빨간색으로 그렸습니다. 두 빔이 엇갈리는 점도 매듭을 그리며, 지나가는 층의 색으로 빛납니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.fact"
msgid "Three industries were interested in the stacked-sheet display, and each wanted something else. Doctors wanted a picture free of all artefacts and the oil industry a much larger one. Slot machine makers needed a price near 50 dollars. It cost more than 10,000."
msgstr "판을 쌓는 방식에는 세 업계가 관심을 보였지만, 원하는 것은 저마다 달랐습니다. 의사들은 인공물이 전혀 없는 그림을, 석유 업계는 훨씬 큰 그림을 원했습니다. 슬롯머신 제조사들은 50달러에 가까운 값이 필요했습니다. 실제 값은 1만 달러가 넘었습니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. People section: what Elizabeth Downing, Lambertus Hesselink, John Ralston, Roger Macfarlane did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.people.1"
msgid "Drew coloured points of light inside a block of glass with crossed infrared beams. The work was published from Stanford University in 1996, with Downing as first author."
msgstr "엇갈린 적외선 빔으로, 유리 덩어리 안에 색이 있는 빛점을 그렸습니다. 이 연구는 1996년 스탠퍼드 대학교에서 발표되었고, 다우닝이 제1저자였습니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.view.sheets.label"
msgid "Look along the sheets"
msgstr "판을 옆에서 보기"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.view.sheets.hint"
msgid "Twelve liquid crystal sheets. One at a time turns milky and catches its slice of the picture. The rest stay clear."
msgstr "액정 판 열두 장. 한 번에 한 장씩 뿌옇게 되어 그림의 자기 단면을 받습니다. 나머지는 투명한 채로 있습니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.view.glass.label"
msgid "Go close to the glass"
msgstr "유리 가까이 가기"

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.view.glass.hint"
msgid "Three layers of glass, one for each colour. It glows only where the two beams cross, and that point draws the figure."
msgstr "유리 세 겹으로, 한 겹이 한 가지 색을 맡습니다. 두 빔이 엇갈리는 곳에서만 빛나고, 그 점이 도형을 그립니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "A single point of light inside a block of glass, from Elizabeth Downing's patent of 1997. The glass glows only where the two infrared laser beams cross."
msgstr "유리 덩어리 안의 단 하나의 빛점. 엘리자베스 다우닝의 1997년 특허에서. 유리는 적외선 레이저 빔 두 줄기가 엇갈리는 곳에서만 빛납니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawing of a cube labelled 3D display image volume. Two lines marked laser 1 and laser 2 enter from below and cross at a small star inside."
msgstr "3D display image volume이라고 적힌 정육면체의 선 그림. laser 1과 laser 2라고 적힌 선 두 줄이 아래에서 들어와, 안의 작은 별에서 엇갈립니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.media.patent-2.caption"
msgid "How a stack of switchable screens builds a picture in depth, from Alan Sullivan's patent of 2000. The projector shows one slice at a time, and only the screen that should catch that slice is turned cloudy."
msgstr "전환할 수 있는 스크린을 쌓아 깊이 있는 그림을 만드는 방법. 앨런 설리번의 2000년 특허에서. 프로젝터는 단면을 한 번에 한 장씩 비추고, 그 단면을 받아야 할 스크린만 흐려집니다."

#: hall-6/static-volume
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.static-volume.media.patent-2.alt"
msgid "Four small diagrams of a projector facing four upright sheets. In each a different sheet carries part of a picture, among them a mountain and a car."
msgstr "서 있는 판 네 장을 향한 프로젝터의 작은 도해 네 개. 도해마다 다른 판이 그림의 일부를 싣고 있고, 그 가운데에는 산과 자동차가 있습니다."

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.name"
msgid "Optical trap display"
msgstr "광학 트랩 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.aka"
msgid "Photophoretic trap display"
msgstr "광영동 트랩 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.origin"
msgid "Daniel Smalley and colleagues, Brigham Young University, USA"
msgstr "대니얼 스몰리와 동료들. 브리검 영 대학교, 미국"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.statusNote"
msgid "A laboratory demonstration. The pictures are small."
msgstr "연구실의 시연입니다. 그림은 작습니다."

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.lead"
msgid "A laser beam holds one tiny speck and drags it through the air, and coloured lasers light it as it moves."
msgstr "레이저 빔이 아주 작은 알갱이 하나를 붙잡아 공중에서 끌고 다니고, 색이 있는 레이저가 움직이는 알갱이를 비춥니다."

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.how.1"
msgid "A nearly invisible laser beam traps one particle of cellulose, the fibre of plants and paper, by heating it. This effect is called photophoresis. When the beam moves, the particle follows."
msgstr "거의 보이지 않는 레이저 빔이 셀룰로스 입자 하나를 가열하여 붙잡습니다. 셀룰로스는 식물과 종이의 섬유입니다. 이 효과를 광영동이라고 합니다. 빔이 움직이면 입자가 따라갑니다."

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.how.2"
msgid "The beam sweeps the particle along a path many times a second while red, green and blue lasers light it. The eye sees the path, not the speck. The points of the picture are about one hundredth of a millimetre across. The light comes from the place where the picture is, so the picture is visible from almost any direction."
msgstr "빔은 입자를 정해진 길을 따라 1초에 여러 번 끌고 다니고, 그동안 빨강, 초록, 파랑 레이저가 입자를 비춥니다. 눈에 보이는 것은 알갱이가 아니라 그 길입니다. 그림의 점은 너비가 100분의 1밀리미터쯤입니다. 빛은 그림이 있는 바로 그 자리에서 나오므로, 그림은 거의 어느 방향에서나 보입니다."

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.good.1"
msgid "Makes a picture that is really there in mid-air, visible from all around"
msgstr "정말로 공중에 있고, 사방에서 보이는 그림을 만든다"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.good.2"
msgid "Gives fine detail and strong laser colour"
msgstr "세밀하고, 레이저의 강한 색을 낸다"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.good.3"
msgid "Puts nothing between you and the picture"
msgstr "보는 사람과 그림 사이에 아무것도 두지 않는다"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.bad.1"
msgid "Draws only a limited length of line each second with one particle, so pictures are small and simple"
msgstr "입자 하나로는 1초에 그을 수 있는 선의 길이가 한정되어, 그림이 작고 단순하다"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.bad.2"
msgid "Fails in a draught"
msgstr "외풍이 불면 실패한다"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.bad.3"
msgid "Needs lasers and precision optics for a very small picture"
msgstr "아주 작은 그림을 위해 레이저와 정밀 광학계가 필요하다"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.look"
msgid "Find the violet beam from the lens: it holds the speck and moves with it. The coloured figure is the path that the speck has just travelled. Switch the daylight off, because the figure shows best in the dark."
msgstr "렌즈에서 나오는 보랏빛 빔을 찾아보세요. 그것이 알갱이를 붙잡고 함께 움직입니다. 색이 있는 도형은 알갱이가 방금 지나간 길입니다. 도형은 어두울 때 가장 잘 보이니 햇빛을 끄세요."

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.fact"
msgid "Its makers called it the Princess Leia project, after the floating message in Star Wars. Among the first things they drew were rings of light around a person's arm and a figure in a lab coat crouched in Leia's pose."
msgstr "만든 사람들은 스타워즈에 나오는 공중의 메시지를 따서, 이것을 레아 공주 프로젝트라고 불렀습니다. 처음 그린 것 가운데에는 사람의 팔을 두른 빛의 고리와, 레아의 자세로 웅크린 실험복 차림의 인물이 있었습니다."

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. People section: what Daniel Smalley did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.people.1"
msgid "Leads the group at Brigham Young University that built the display, published in 2018."
msgstr "브리검 영 대학교에서 이 디스플레이를 만든 연구진을 이끌고 있습니다. 2018년에 발표되었습니다."

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.view.speck.label"
msgid "Go close to the speck"
msgstr "알갱이 가까이 가기"

#: hall-6/optical-trap
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.optical-trap.view.speck.hint"
msgid "One speck of cellulose, held in a laser beam and dragged round. Three small lasers colour it as it passes."
msgstr "레이저 빔에 붙잡혀 끌려다니는 셀룰로스 알갱이 하나. 지나갈 때 작은 레이저 세 대가 색을 입힙니다."

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.name"
msgid "Acoustic levitation display"
msgstr "음향 부양 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.aka"
msgid "Multimodal acoustic trap display"
msgstr "다중 감각 음향 트랩 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.origin"
msgid "Ryuji Hirayama, Diego Martinez Plasencia, Nobuyuki Masuda and Sriram Subramanian, University of Sussex, UK"
msgstr "히라야마 류지, 디에고 마르티네스 플라센시아, 마스다 노부유키, 스리람 수브라마니안. 서식스 대학교, 영국"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.statusNote"
msgid "A laboratory demonstration."
msgstr "연구실의 시연입니다."

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.lead"
msgid "Ultrasound, sound too high to hear, holds a small bead in mid-air and moves it around a shape faster than the eye can follow."
msgstr "초음파, 곧 너무 높아서 들리지 않는 소리가 작은 구슬을 공중에 붙잡아, 눈이 따라갈 수 없는 속도로 도형을 따라 움직입니다."

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.how.1"
msgid "Two grids of small ultrasound speakers face each other. With the right timing for each speaker, the sound forms a pocket that holds a small, lightweight bead. A change in the timing moves the pocket."
msgstr "작은 초음파 스피커를 늘어놓은 격자 두 개가 서로 마주 봅니다. 스피커마다 때를 알맞게 맞추면, 소리가 작고 가벼운 구슬을 붙잡는 주머니를 만듭니다. 때를 바꾸면 주머니가 움직입니다."

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.how.2"
msgid "The bead moves around its path at up to several metres per second. Coloured light shines on it, so the eye sees a glowing figure. The same speakers can also focus sound onto your hand, where you feel it as pressure, and they can make sound that you can hear. The picture can therefore be felt and heard as well as seen."
msgstr "구슬은 최고 초속 몇 미터로 자기 길을 돕니다. 색이 있는 빛이 구슬을 비추므로, 눈에는 빛나는 도형이 보입니다. 같은 스피커는 소리를 손에 모아 압력으로 느끼게 할 수도 있고, 귀에 들리는 소리를 낼 수도 있습니다. 그래서 그림은 보는 것뿐 아니라 만지고 들을 수도 있습니다."

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.good.1"
msgid "Makes a picture in mid-air, with sound and touch from the same hardware"
msgstr "같은 장치로 공중의 그림과 소리와 촉감을 만든다"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.good.2"
msgid "Does all three jobs with one physical principle"
msgstr "세 가지 일을 한 가지 물리 원리로 해낸다"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.bad.1"
msgid "Can move one bead along only a limited path, so figures are small and simple"
msgstr "구슬 하나를 움직일 수 있는 길이 한정되어, 도형이 작고 단순하다"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.bad.2"
msgid "Needs speakers above and below the picture"
msgstr "그림의 위와 아래에 스피커가 필요하다"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.bad.3"
msgid "Air currents disturb it"
msgstr "공기의 흐름에 흐트러진다"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.look"
msgid "Find the bright point: that is the bead. The butterfly shape behind it shows where the bead has been in the last fraction of a second."
msgstr "밝은 점을 찾아보세요. 그것이 구슬입니다. 그 뒤의 나비 모양은 구슬이 바로 직전의 한순간 동안 지나간 자리를 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.fact"
msgid "One demonstration was a countdown timer hanging in the air. A user could see it, hear it, start and stop it with a finger gesture, and feel a touch in reply each time."
msgstr "시연 가운데 하나는 공중에 걸린 카운트다운 타이머였습니다. 사용자는 그것을 보고, 듣고, 손가락 동작으로 시작하고 멈출 수 있었으며, 그때마다 응답으로 손끝에 닿는 느낌을 받았습니다."

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. People section: what Ryuji Hirayama, Diego Martinez Plasencia, Nobuyuki Masuda, Sriram Subramanian did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.people.1"
msgid "Built the display at the University of Sussex and published it in 2019."
msgstr "서식스 대학교에서 이 디스플레이를 만들어 2019년에 발표했습니다."

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.view.bead.label"
msgid "Go close to the bead"
msgstr "구슬 가까이 가기"

#: hall-6/acoustic-levitation
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.acoustic-levitation.view.bead.hint"
msgid "One bead, held by ultrasound from the arrays above and below, flown round the outline fast enough to be seen whole."
msgstr "위아래 배열의 초음파에 붙잡힌 구슬 하나가, 윤곽을 따라 전체가 보일 만큼 빠르게 날아다닙니다."

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.name"
msgid "Laser plasma display"
msgstr "레이저 플라스마 디스플레이"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.aka"
msgid "Aerial plasma voxels"
msgstr "공중 플라스마 복셀"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.origin"
msgid "Kimura and colleagues, Japan. Version with far shorter (femtosecond) pulses by Yoichi Ochiai and colleagues, 2015"
msgstr "기무라와 동료들, 일본. 훨씬 짧은(펨토초) 펄스를 쓰는 방식은 오치아이 요이치와 동료들, 2015년"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.statusNote"
msgid "Laboratory demonstrations with Class 4 lasers, the most hazardous class."
msgstr "가장 위험한 등급인 4등급 레이저를 쓰는 연구실 시연입니다."

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.lead"
msgid "A lens focuses a laser pulse so tightly that the air at the focus briefly becomes a glowing spark of plasma."
msgstr "렌즈가 레이저 펄스를 아주 작은 한 점에 모아서, 초점의 공기가 잠깐 빛나는 플라스마 불꽃이 됩니다."

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.how.1"
msgid "A lens brings a very short, very intense laser pulse to a point. At that point, and nowhere else along the beam, the light is strong enough to tear electrons from the molecules of the air. The air there becomes plasma, a glowing gas that carries electric charge. That tiny spark is the pixel. The focus then moves and the laser fires again."
msgstr "렌즈가 아주 짧고 아주 강한 레이저 펄스를 한 점에 모읍니다. 바로 그 점에서만, 빔의 다른 어느 곳도 아닌 그곳에서만, 빛은 공기 분자에서 전자를 떼어 낼 만큼 강해집니다. 그곳의 공기는 플라스마, 곧 전하를 나르며 빛나는 기체가 됩니다. 그 작은 불꽃이 화소입니다. 그런 다음 초점이 옮겨 가고, 레이저가 다시 발사됩니다."

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.how.2"
msgid "The 2006 system made about 100 points a second. Its pulses lasted nanoseconds, billionths of a second, and were strong enough to burn. The 2015 version used far shorter pulses, each of which carries much less energy. It reached up to 200,000 points a second and made sparks that could be touched briefly. Its pictures were a few millimetres across."
msgstr "2006년의 방식은 1초에 약 100개의 점을 만들었습니다. 펄스는 나노초, 곧 10억분의 1초 단위로 이어졌고, 화상을 입힐 만큼 강했습니다. 2015년의 방식은 훨씬 짧은 펄스를 썼고, 펄스 하나하나의 에너지는 훨씬 작았습니다. 1초에 최대 20만 개의 점에 이르렀고, 잠깐은 만질 수 있는 불꽃을 만들었습니다. 그림의 너비는 몇 밀리미터였습니다."

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.good.1"
msgid "Makes light in empty air, with no screen, no particle and no fog"
msgstr "화면도, 입자도, 안개도 없이 빈 공기 속에 빛을 만든다"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.good.2"
msgid "Is visible from every side"
msgstr "모든 방향에서 보인다"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.bad.1"
msgid "Makes pictures of only a few cubic millimetres"
msgstr "그림이 몇 세제곱밀리미터에 지나지 않는다"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.bad.2"
msgid "Needs a powerful laser, and stray reflections of it can harm the eyes"
msgstr "강한 레이저가 필요하고, 빗나간 반사광이 눈을 다치게 할 수 있다"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.bad.3"
msgid "Shows one colour only, the bluish white of ionised (electrically charged) air"
msgstr "한 가지 색만 보여 준다. 이온화된(전기를 띤) 공기의 푸르스름한 흰색이다"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.look"
msgid "Find the two faint lines that mark the edges of the narrowing beam. A point flashes where they meet. The cube is the trace that many such points leave in the eye."
msgstr "좁아지는 빔의 가장자리를 나타내는 희미한 선 두 줄을 찾아보세요. 둘이 만나는 곳에서 점이 번쩍입니다. 정육면체는 그런 점 여럿이 눈에 남긴 자취입니다."

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.fact"
msgid "The picture can be heard. Each point of plasma gives off sound as well as light, and the brighter it glows the louder it is. A sound meter 2 centimetres from the 2015 display read up to 77 decibels."
msgstr "이 그림은 들을 수도 있습니다. 플라스마 점은 저마다 빛과 함께 소리를 내고, 밝게 빛날수록 소리도 큽니다. 2015년 디스플레이에서 2센티미터 떨어진 곳의 소음계는 최대 77데시벨을 가리켰습니다."

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. People section: what Yoichi Ochiai did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.people.1"
msgid "Led the version of 2015, which uses far shorter laser pulses."
msgstr "훨씬 짧은 레이저 펄스를 쓰는 2015년의 방식을 이끌었습니다."

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.view.points.label"
msgid "Go close to the points"
msgstr "점 가까이 가기"

#: hall-6/laser-plasma
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.laser-plasma.view.points.hint"
msgid "Each point is air, flashing where the pulsed beam comes to a focus. Only one exists at a time. The rest is afterimage."
msgstr "점은 저마다 공기이며, 펄스 빔이 초점을 맺는 곳에서 번쩍입니다. 한 번에 있는 것은 하나뿐이고, 나머지는 눈에 남은 잔상입니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.name"
msgid "Stereoscope and 3D glasses"
msgstr "입체경과 3D 안경"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.aka"
msgid "Anaglyph, polarised and shutter glasses"
msgstr "애너글리프, 편광 안경, 셔터 안경"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.origin"
msgid "Charles Wheatstone, London"
msgstr "찰스 휘트스톤, 런던"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.statusNote"
msgid "Used in 3D cinema. The same principle works inside headsets."
msgstr "3D 영화에 쓰입니다. 같은 원리가 헤드셋 안에서도 작동합니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.lead"
msgid "Each eye sees one of two flat pictures taken from slightly different positions."
msgstr "조금 다른 위치에서 찍은 평평한 그림 두 장을, 두 눈이 한 장씩 봅니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.how.1"
msgid "Your eyes are a few centimetres apart, so they see slightly different views. The brain interprets the differences as depth. When each eye sees its own picture, the same thing happens with photographs."
msgstr "두 눈은 몇 센티미터 떨어져 있어서, 조금씩 다른 모습을 봅니다. 뇌는 그 차이를 깊이로 받아들입니다. 눈마다 자기 그림을 보면, 사진으로도 같은 일이 일어납니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.how.2"
msgid "Wheatstone did it with mirrors in 1838. Every later method is a different way to send each picture to the right eye. One uses lenses and a card held away from the eyes. Another uses red and cyan filters over a print in two colours, first described in 1853. Others use polarising filters, which pass only light that vibrates in one direction, or shutters that cover each eye in turn. A New York cinema tried shutters as early as 1922."
msgstr "휘트스톤은 1838년에 거울로 이것을 해냈습니다. 그 뒤의 방법은 모두, 그림을 알맞은 눈에 보내는 서로 다른 방식입니다. 렌즈와, 눈에서 떨어뜨려 드는 카드를 쓰는 방법이 있습니다. 두 색으로 찍은 인쇄물 위에 빨강과 청록 필터를 대는 방법도 있는데, 1853년에 처음 기록되었습니다. 한 방향으로 진동하는 빛만 통과시키는 편광 필터를 쓰거나, 두 눈을 번갈아 가리는 셔터를 쓰는 방법도 있습니다. 뉴욕의 한 영화관은 일찍이 1922년에 셔터를 시험했습니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.good.1"
msgid "Gives convincing depth from two ordinary pictures"
msgstr "평범한 그림 두 장으로 그럴듯한 깊이를 낸다"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.good.2"
msgid "Is simple, cheap and well understood"
msgstr "단순하고 싸며, 원리가 잘 알려져 있다"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.bad.1"
msgid "Needs something to wear or to look into"
msgstr "무언가를 쓰거나 들여다보아야 한다"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.bad.2"
msgid "Tires the eyes, because they turn inward toward the object but focus on the screen"
msgstr "눈은 물체 쪽으로 안으로 모이는데 초점은 화면에 맞추므로, 눈이 피로하다"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.bad.3"
msgid "Shows no change when you move your head, so you cannot look around an object"
msgstr "머리를 움직여도 달라지는 것이 없어서, 물체 둘레를 돌아볼 수 없다"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.look"
msgid "A hand-held viewer stands on a post, with a stereo card of a factory in its holder. Press the Look in button to put an eye to the lens: the flat card becomes a scene with depth. To show that depth on a flat screen, the model lets the chimney slide across the sheds as you drag. A real card does not do this. The red and cyan glasses in front do the same job with colour."
msgstr "손에 드는 뷰어가 기둥 위에 서 있고, 공장을 찍은 입체 카드가 꽂혀 있습니다. 들여다보기 버튼을 눌러 렌즈에 눈을 대 보세요. 평평한 카드가 깊이 있는 장면이 됩니다. 그 깊이를 평평한 화면에서 보여 주려고, 모델은 드래그하면 굴뚝이 창고들 위로 미끄러지게 했습니다. 진짜 카드는 그렇게 되지 않습니다. 앞에 놓인 빨강과 청록 안경은 같은 일을 색으로 합니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.fact"
msgid "Wheatstone published the stereoscope in 1838, months before photography was announced. So the first pictures seen in it were drawings: pairs of outlines of solid shapes such as a cube and a cone."
msgstr "휘트스톤은 1838년에 입체경을 발표했는데, 사진술이 공표되기 몇 달 전이었습니다. 그래서 여기에서 처음 본 그림은 소묘였습니다. 정육면체와 원뿔 같은 입체의 윤곽을 두 장씩 짝지어 그린 것입니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. People section: what Charles Wheatstone did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.people.1"
msgid "Published the stereoscope in London in 1838, months before photography was announced."
msgstr "1838년 런던에서 입체경을 발표했습니다. 사진술이 공표되기 몇 달 전이었습니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. People section: what Kenneth Dunkley did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.people.2"
msgid "Physicist in Pennsylvania. His patent of 1989 is for viewing glasses with no lenses: two pinholes and two empty frames. It says they give an ordinary flat picture the look of depth."
msgstr "펜실베이니아의 물리학자. 1989년의 특허는 렌즈가 없는 안경에 관한 것입니다. 작은 구멍 두 개와 빈 틀 두 개가 전부입니다. 특허는 이 안경이 평범한 평면 그림을 깊이가 있는 것처럼 보이게 한다고 밝힙니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. People section: what Charles Harrison did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.people.3"
msgid "Industrial designer in Chicago. In 1958 he redesigned the View-Master, a stereoscope for the hand, in light moulded plastic, and it went from a photographer's accessory to a children's toy."
msgstr "시카고의 산업 디자이너. 1958년에 손에 드는 입체경인 View-Master를 가벼운 성형 플라스틱으로 다시 디자인했습니다. 사진가의 부속품이던 것이 어린이의 장난감이 되었습니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.view.eyepiece.label"
msgid "Look in"
msgstr "들여다보기"

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "A viewer for the Teleview system, from Laurens Hammond's patent of 1924. A small motor spins a shutter in front of the eyes, in step with the projector, so that each eye sees only the pictures meant for it."
msgstr "텔레뷰 방식의 뷰어. 로런스 해먼드의 1924년 특허에서. 작은 모터가 영사기에 맞추어 눈앞의 셔터를 돌려, 눈마다 자기 몫의 그림만 보게 합니다."

#: hall-7/stereoscope
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.stereoscope.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a round flat case on a curved stalk, with a window across its lower half and a cut-out disc inside, and beside it a section through its motor."
msgstr "선 그림: 굽은 대 위에 달린 둥글고 납작한 통. 아래쪽 절반에 창이 있고, 안에는 잘라 낸 원판이 들어 있습니다. 옆에는 모터의 단면도."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.name"
msgid "Parallax barrier"
msgstr "시차 장벽"

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.origin"
msgid "Frederic Ives, USA, after a description by Auguste Berthier in 1896"
msgstr "프레더릭 아이브스, 미국. 1896년 오귀스트 베르티에의 기술을 따랐습니다"

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.statusNote"
msgid "In a hand-held games console, some phones and car screens that show the driver and the passenger different pictures."
msgstr "한 휴대용 게임기, 일부 휴대폰, 그리고 운전자와 동승자에게 다른 그림을 보여 주는 자동차 화면에 쓰입니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.lead"
msgid "A mask of fine slits stands in front of the pixels, so that each eye, from its own angle, sees a different set of columns."
msgstr "가는 틈이 난 가리개가 화소 앞에 서 있어서, 두 눈은 저마다의 각도에서 다른 세로줄을 봅니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.how.1"
msgid "The picture behind the mask combines two pictures. Both are cut into narrow vertical strips, and the strips alternate. From the left eye, the slits line up with one set of strips, and the bars between the slits hide the other set. From the right eye, a few centimetres to the side, it is the reverse."
msgstr "가리개 뒤의 그림은 두 그림을 합친 것입니다. 둘 다 좁은 세로 띠로 잘려 있고, 띠들이 번갈아 놓입니다. 왼눈에서는 틈이 한 벌의 띠와 맞아떨어지고, 틈 사이의 막대가 다른 한 벌을 가립니다. 몇 센티미터 옆의 오른눈에서는 그 반대입니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.how.2"
msgid "It needs no glasses, only a striped mask. The mask can itself be a liquid crystal layer, so it can be switched off. The cost is that each eye gets half the columns, the bars block half the light, and the effect works only from the right position."
msgstr "안경은 필요 없고, 줄무늬 가리개만 있으면 됩니다. 가리개 자체를 액정 층으로 만들 수도 있어서, 끌 수도 있습니다. 그 대가로 눈마다 세로줄의 절반만 받고, 막대가 빛의 절반을 막으며, 효과는 알맞은 자리에서만 납니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.good.1"
msgid "Gives depth without glasses from a very simple addition to a screen"
msgstr "화면에 아주 단순한 것을 하나 더해서, 안경 없이 깊이를 낸다"

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.good.2"
msgid "Can be switched off to give a normal picture again"
msgstr "끄면 다시 보통 그림이 된다"

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.bad.1"
msgid "Gives each eye half the pixels and half the brightness"
msgstr "눈마다 화소의 절반과 밝기의 절반만 받는다"

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.bad.2"
msgid "Works for one viewer in one place"
msgstr "한 자리에 있는 한 사람에게만 통한다"

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.bad.3"
msgid "Swaps or blurs the two views when the viewer moves sideways"
msgstr "보는 사람이 옆으로 움직이면 두 그림이 바뀌거나 흐려진다"

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.look"
msgid "The model enlarges the slits so that you can see them. Turn it a few degrees to the left and to the right. The barrier is a real layer in front of the screen, so the effect works here too. From one side you see an L, and from the other side an R."
msgstr "모델은 틈이 보이도록 크게 만들었습니다. 왼쪽과 오른쪽으로 몇 도씩 돌려 보세요. 장벽은 화면 앞에 있는 실제의 층이어서, 효과는 여기서도 납니다. 한쪽에서는 L이, 다른 쪽에서는 R이 보입니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.fact"
msgid "Ives's first one was a glass transparency. Held 12 inches from the eyes, it looked like the front of a box with a statuette and two glass vases inside. He traced the idea to his work on the line screens used to print photographs."
msgstr "아이브스의 첫 작품은 유리 투명 양화였습니다. 눈에서 12인치 떨어뜨려 들면, 작은 조각상과 유리 꽃병 두 개가 든 상자의 앞면처럼 보였습니다. 그는 이 생각이, 사진을 인쇄하는 데 쓰는 망점 스크린을 다룬 자신의 일에서 나왔다고 밝혔습니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. People section: what Frederic Ives did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.people.1"
msgid "Made the first parallax stereogram, in the United States in 1901."
msgstr "1901년 미국에서 최초의 시차 입체 그림을 만들었습니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. People section: what Auguste Berthier did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.people.2"
msgid "Described the principle in 1896."
msgstr "1896년에 이 원리를 기술했습니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.view.spot.label"
msgid "Stand in front"
msgstr "정면에 서기"

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.view.spot.hint"
msgid "Move your head. Through the slits, the columns for one eye give way to the columns for the other: L turns to R."
msgstr "머리를 움직여 보세요. 틈 너머에서 한쪽 눈의 세로줄이 다른 쪽 눈의 세로줄로 바뀌고, L이 R이 됩니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "Frederic Ives's parallax stereogram, from his patent of 1903. A camera with two openings takes both views at once through a screen of fine lines. A like screen over the finished picture sorts them out for the two eyes."
msgstr "프레더릭 아이브스의 시차 입체 그림. 그의 1903년 특허에서. 구멍이 두 개인 카메라가 가는 선의 스크린을 통해 두 모습을 한꺼번에 찍습니다. 완성된 그림 위에 같은 스크린을 얹으면, 두 모습이 두 눈으로 갈립니다."

#: hall-7/parallax-barrier
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.parallax-barrier.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a bellows camera from above, rays from a ball and a cube crossing at its lens, small diagrams of prisms, and a ruled plate from front and side."
msgstr "선 그림: 위에서 본 주름상자 카메라, 렌즈에서 엇갈리는 공과 정육면체의 광선, 프리즘의 작은 도해, 앞과 옆에서 본 줄 그은 판."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.name"
msgid "Lenticular display"
msgstr "렌티큘러 디스플레이"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.aka"
msgid "Lenticular print"
msgstr "렌티큘러 인쇄"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.origin"
msgid "Walter Hess, patent for a sheet of cylindrical lenses"
msgstr "발터 헤스. 원통 렌즈를 늘어놓은 판의 특허"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.statusNote"
msgid "Common as printed cards and posters. Lenticular screens are a specialist product."
msgstr "인쇄한 카드와 포스터로 흔합니다. 렌티큘러 화면은 전문가용 제품입니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.lead"
msgid "A sheet of tiny ribbed lenses lies over a picture made of strips, and each rib shows a different strip depending on where you stand."
msgstr "골이 진 아주 작은 렌즈의 판이 띠로 이루어진 그림 위에 놓여 있고, 서 있는 자리에 따라 골마다 다른 띠를 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.how.1"
msgid "Several pictures are cut into strips and interleaved, so that one strip of each picture lies behind every rib. A rib is a long, thin lens that magnifies whichever strip is in line with your eye. When you move, all the ribs switch to the next strip together."
msgstr "여러 그림을 띠로 잘라 번갈아 놓아, 골마다 뒤에 그림 하나의 띠가 하나씩 오게 합니다. 골은 길고 가는 렌즈로, 눈과 한 줄에 놓인 띠를 크게 보여 줍니다. 움직이면 모든 골이 함께 다음 띠로 넘어갑니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.how.2"
msgid "With two pictures, the card flips from one to the other. With a sequence of pictures, it shows movement. With views of a scene from slightly different positions, each eye gets its own view, which gives depth. Unlike a barrier of slits, the lenses redirect light and do not block it."
msgstr "그림이 두 장이면 카드는 하나에서 다른 하나로 바뀝니다. 이어지는 그림들이면 움직임을 보여 줍니다. 조금씩 다른 자리에서 본 장면들이면 눈마다 자기 모습을 받아, 깊이가 생깁니다. 틈의 장벽과 달리, 렌즈는 빛의 방향을 바꿀 뿐 막지 않습니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.good.1"
msgid "Gives depth or motion from a printed card, with no power and no glasses"
msgstr "전력도 안경도 없이, 인쇄한 카드로 깊이나 움직임을 보여 준다"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.good.2"
msgid "Is brighter than a barrier of slits"
msgstr "틈의 장벽보다 밝다"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.good.3"
msgid "Offers several views, so you can look around an object a little"
msgstr "시점이 여럿이어서, 물체 둘레를 조금 돌아볼 수 있다"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.bad.1"
msgid "Divides its detail among the views"
msgstr "세밀함을 시점들이 나누어 갖는다"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.bad.2"
msgid "Repeats its views, with a visible jump from one zone to the next"
msgstr "시점이 되풀이되고, 한 구역에서 다음 구역으로 넘어갈 때 눈에 띄게 뛴다"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.bad.3"
msgid "Works at one viewing distance only"
msgstr "정해진 한 거리에서만 통한다"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.look"
msgid "The card shows an eye, printed in nine views. Turn the model: the eye follows you across the room and jumps from one view to the next."
msgstr "카드에는 아홉 시점으로 인쇄한 눈이 있습니다. 모델을 돌려 보세요. 눈이 방을 가로지르는 여러분을 따라오고, 한 시점에서 다음 시점으로 뜁니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.fact"
msgid "Ribbed lenses once made colour home movies. Kodacolor film, sold by Kodak from 1928, had tiny lenses embossed on it. With red, green and blue filter bands over the camera lens, they recorded each colour in its own strip of the picture."
msgstr "골이 진 렌즈는 한때 컬러 홈 무비를 만들었습니다. 코닥이 1928년부터 판 코다컬러 필름에는 아주 작은 렌즈가 돋을새김되어 있었습니다. 카메라 렌즈 위에 빨강, 초록, 파랑 필터 띠를 대면, 그 렌즈들이 색마다 그림의 자기 띠에 기록했습니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. People section: what Walter Hess did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.people.1"
msgid "Eye doctor in Rapperswil, Switzerland. He filed his patent for stereoscopic pictures under a sheet of small lenses in 1912, and in 1949 won a Nobel Prize for his work on the brain."
msgstr "스위스 라퍼스빌의 안과 의사. 1912년에 작은 렌즈의 판을 덧댄 입체 사진의 특허를 출원했고, 1949년에는 뇌 연구로 노벨상을 받았습니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. People section: what Hank Willis Thomas did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.people.2"
msgid "Artist in New York. His lenticular prints carry text that changes as the visitor walks past. In one, Black Imitates White becomes White Imitates Black."
msgstr "뉴욕의 미술가. 그의 렌티큘러 인쇄 작품에는 관람객이 앞을 지나가면 바뀌는 글이 들어 있습니다. 한 작품에서는 Black Imitates White(검정이 하양을 흉내 낸다)가 White Imitates Black(하양이 검정을 흉내 낸다)으로 바뀝니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.view.pass.label"
msgid "Walk past it"
msgstr "앞을 지나가기"

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.view.pass.hint"
msgid "Drag to walk past. Each rib shows the stripe that faces you, so the eye in the print follows you. There are nine views."
msgstr "드래그해서 앞을 지나가 보세요. 골마다 이쪽을 향한 띠를 보여 주어서, 인쇄된 눈이 여러분을 따라옵니다. 시점은 아홉 개입니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "Walter Hess's ribbed viewing sheet, from his patent filed in 1912. The ribs are small cylindrical lenses. The second figure shows one rib bringing light from one direction to a single line on the picture behind it."
msgstr "발터 헤스의 골이 진 관찰용 판. 1912년에 출원한 그의 특허에서. 골은 작은 원통 렌즈입니다. 두 번째 그림은 골 하나가 한 방향에서 온 빛을, 뒤에 있는 그림 위의 한 줄로 모으는 모습입니다."

#: hall-7/lenticular
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.lenticular.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a sheet covered in fine vertical ribs, and an enlarged edge view of two ribs with rays converging through one to a point on the flat back."
msgstr "선 그림: 가는 세로 골로 덮인 판, 그리고 골 두 개를 옆에서 확대한 그림. 광선이 골 하나를 지나 평평한 뒷면의 한 점으로 모입니다."

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.name"
msgid "Integral imaging"
msgstr "집적 영상"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.aka"
msgid "Light field display, fly's eye lens"
msgstr "라이트 필드 디스플레이, 파리 눈 렌즈"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.origin"
msgid "Gabriel Lippmann, France, who called it integral photography"
msgstr "가브리엘 리프만, 프랑스. 그는 이것을 집적 사진이라고 불렀습니다"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.statusNote"
msgid "Prototypes and a few specialist products. The number of pixels that it needs is still hard to supply."
msgstr "시제품과 전문가용 제품이 몇 가지 있습니다. 필요한 화소 수를 대는 일이 아직도 어렵습니다."

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.lead"
msgid "A sheet of tiny round lenses, modelled on the eye of an insect, sends a different picture in every direction."
msgstr "곤충의 눈을 본뜬 아주 작고 둥근 렌즈의 판이, 방향마다 다른 그림을 내보냅니다."

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.how.1"
msgid "Behind each lens is a miniature picture of the scene as seen from the position of that lens. When you look at the sheet, your eye catches one point of each miniature. Which point it catches depends on where you are. Together, the points rebuild the rays of light that would have come from the real scene."
msgstr "렌즈마다 뒤에는, 그 렌즈의 자리에서 본 장면의 축소 그림이 있습니다. 판을 바라보면 눈은 축소 그림마다 한 점씩을 붙잡습니다. 어느 점을 붙잡는지는 보는 사람이 있는 자리에 달려 있습니다. 그 점들이 모여, 실제 장면에서 왔을 광선을 다시 짜 맞춥니다."

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.how.2"
msgid "The lenses are round, not ribbed, so the view changes when you move up and down as well as from side to side. Lippmann proposed the method in 1908. More than a century later, the limit is the same: every direction needs its own pixels."
msgstr "렌즈는 골 모양이 아니라 둥글어서, 좌우로 움직일 때뿐 아니라 위아래로 움직일 때에도 모습이 바뀝니다. 리프만은 1908년에 이 방법을 제안했습니다. 한 세기가 넘게 지난 지금도 한계는 같습니다. 방향마다 자기 화소가 필요합니다."

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.good.1"
msgid "Gives depth both up and down and from side to side, with no glasses"
msgstr "안경 없이, 위아래로도 좌우로도 깊이를 낸다"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.good.2"
msgid "Lets several people look at once"
msgstr "여러 사람이 한꺼번에 볼 수 있다"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.bad.1"
msgid "Needs a very large number of pixels for a modest picture"
msgstr "소박한 그림 하나에도 아주 많은 화소가 필요하다"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.bad.2"
msgid "Shows depth sharply only near the sheet"
msgstr "깊이가 또렷한 것은 판 가까이에서만이다"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.bad.3"
msgid "Changes its view in steps unless the lenses are very fine"
msgstr "렌즈가 아주 촘촘하지 않으면 모습이 계단처럼 바뀐다"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.look"
msgid "Turn the model to look at the panel from the side and from above. The three layers of the picture shift against each other in small steps, vertically as well as horizontally."
msgstr "모델을 돌려 패널을 옆에서, 그리고 위에서 보세요. 그림의 세 층이 가로로도 세로로도, 작은 계단으로 서로 어긋납니다."

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.fact"
msgid "Its descendants include a camera. In 2005 a Stanford team put a sheet of 296 by 296 tiny lenses in front of a camera sensor, recorded the direction of the light as well as its position, and refocused the photographs afterwards."
msgstr "그 후손 가운데에는 카메라도 있습니다. 2005년에 스탠퍼드의 한 연구진은 가로세로 296개씩의 아주 작은 렌즈가 늘어선 판을 카메라 센서 앞에 놓고, 빛의 위치와 함께 방향도 기록해서, 찍고 난 뒤에 사진의 초점을 다시 맞추었습니다."

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. People section: what Gabriel Lippmann did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.people.1"
msgid "Proposed it in France in 1908 and called it integral photography."
msgstr "1908년 프랑스에서 이것을 제안하고, 집적 사진이라고 불렀습니다."

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.view.round.label"
msgid "Look round it"
msgstr "둘러보기"

#: hall-7/integral-imaging
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.integral-imaging.view.round.hint"
msgid "Move your head sideways, then up and down. The scene shifts both ways, in small steps: one view for each lens position."
msgstr "머리를 좌우로, 그런 다음 위아래로 움직여 보세요. 장면은 두 방향 모두 작은 계단으로 어긋납니다. 렌즈 자리 하나에 시점 하나입니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.holography.name"
msgid "Holography"
msgstr "홀로그래피"

#: hall-7/holography
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.holography.origin"
msgid "Dennis Gabor, British Thomson-Houston, Rugby, England"
msgstr "데니스 가보르. 브리티시 톰슨 휴스턴, 럭비, 잉글랜드"

#: hall-7/holography
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.holography.statusNote"
msgid "Static holograms are everyday objects, for example on bank cards. A holographic video display is still a research problem."
msgstr "멈춘 홀로그램은 일상의 물건으로, 이를테면 은행 카드에 붙어 있습니다. 홀로그래피 동영상 디스플레이는 아직 연구 과제입니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.holography.lead"
msgid "A hologram records the light itself, not a picture: lit correctly, the plate sends out the same light waves that the object did."
msgstr "홀로그램은 그림이 아니라 빛 그 자체를 기록합니다. 알맞게 비추면, 판은 물체가 내보냈던 것과 같은 빛의 파동을 내보냅니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.holography.how.1"
msgid "A laser beam is split in two. One part lights the object and scatters onto a photographic plate. The other part goes straight to the plate. Where the two parts meet, they interfere: their waves add together or cancel. The plate records the microscopic pattern of stripes that results."
msgstr "레이저 빔을 둘로 나눕니다. 한쪽은 물체를 비추고, 흩어져 사진 건판에 닿습니다. 다른 쪽은 곧장 건판으로 갑니다. 둘이 만나는 곳에서 빛은 간섭합니다. 파동이 서로 보태지거나 지워집니다. 건판은 그렇게 생기는 미세한 줄무늬를 기록합니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.holography.how.2"
msgid "When the laser shines on the developed plate, the stripes bend the light back into the original waves. Looking through the plate is then optically the same as looking at the object. The object has depth, it shifts as you move, and your eye focuses on it at its real distance."
msgstr "현상한 건판에 레이저를 비추면, 줄무늬가 빛을 꺾어 원래의 파동으로 되돌립니다. 그때 건판 너머로 보는 것은 광학적으로 물체를 보는 것과 같습니다. 물체에는 깊이가 있고, 움직이면 어긋나 보이며, 눈은 실제 거리에 초점을 맞춥니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. How it works, paragraph 3 of 3.
msgctxt "piece.holography.how.3"
msgid "Gabor invented the method in 1948 to improve electron microscopes, and he won the Nobel Prize for it in 1971. The method needed the laser. In 1962, Leith and Upatnieks in the United States and Denisyuk in the Soviet Union made the first practical holograms of solid objects."
msgstr "가보르는 1948년에 전자 현미경을 개선하려고 이 방법을 발명했고, 1971년에 그 공로로 노벨상을 받았습니다. 이 방법에는 레이저가 필요했습니다. 1962년에 미국의 리스와 우파트니엑스, 소련의 데니슈크가 입체 물체의 실용적인 홀로그램을 처음으로 만들었습니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.holography.good.1"
msgid "Gives the eye every sign of depth that it uses, all of them correct at once"
msgstr "눈이 쓰는 깊이의 단서를 모두, 한꺼번에, 모두 올바르게 준다"

#: hall-7/holography
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.holography.good.2"
msgid "Works as a window, not a picture: you can look around the object and look closer"
msgstr "그림이 아니라 창처럼 작동한다. 물체 둘레를 돌아볼 수도, 가까이 들여다볼 수도 있다"

#: hall-7/holography
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.holography.bad.1"
msgid "Needs pixels almost as small as light waves, and very much data, for a moving version"
msgstr "움직이는 영상으로 만들려면 빛의 파장만큼 작은 화소와 엄청난 양의 데이터가 필요하다"

#: hall-7/holography
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.holography.bad.2"
msgid "Usually shows one colour, with a grainy pattern called speckle"
msgstr "보통 한 가지 색만 보여 주고, 스페클이라는 알갱이 무늬가 낀다"

#: hall-7/holography
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.holography.bad.3"
msgid "Shares its name with almost any picture that seems to float"
msgstr "떠 있는 듯한 그림이면 거의 무엇이든 같은 이름으로 불린다"

#: hall-7/holography
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.holography.look"
msgid "A laser behind the plate lights it at the angle the plate was made for. Look through the glass: a toy engine stands behind it, in one colour and grainy with speckle. Turn the model from side to side and up and down. You see around the engine smoothly, without the small steps of the Integral imaging panel in this hall. From behind the plate there is nothing to see."
msgstr "건판 뒤의 레이저가, 건판을 만들 때의 각도로 그것을 비춥니다. 유리 너머를 보세요. 장난감 기관차가 그 뒤에 서 있고, 한 가지 색에 스페클 알갱이가 끼어 있습니다. 모델을 좌우와 위아래로 돌려 보세요. 기관차 둘레가 매끄럽게 보이고, 이 전시실의 집적 영상 패널 같은 작은 계단이 없습니다. 건판 뒤쪽에서는 아무것도 보이지 않습니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.holography.fact"
msgid "Gabor named the hologram from the Greek holos, the whole, because it held the whole information. The name fits in another way: any small piece of a hologram of a diffusely lit scene can rebuild the entire picture, only with more noise."
msgstr "가보르는 홀로그램이라는 이름을 전체를 뜻하는 그리스어 홀로스에서 따왔습니다. 정보 전체를 담고 있기 때문입니다. 이 이름은 다른 뜻에서도 들어맞습니다. 고르게 퍼진 빛을 받은 장면의 홀로그램은, 어느 작은 조각으로도 그림 전체를 다시 짜 맞출 수 있습니다. 다만 잡음이 늘어납니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. People section: what Dennis Gabor did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.holography.people.1"
msgid "Invented holography in 1948 at British Thomson-Houston in Rugby, England."
msgstr "1948년 잉글랜드 럭비의 브리티시 톰슨 휴스턴에서 홀로그래피를 발명했습니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. People section: what Emmett Leith, Juris Upatnieks did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.holography.people.2"
msgid "Published the off-axis method of recording holograms in 1962."
msgstr "1962년에 홀로그램을 기록하는 비축 방식을 발표했습니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. People section: what Margaret Benyon did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.holography.people.3"
msgid "Painter, born in Birmingham and raised in Kenya. She made holograms as art from 1968 and showed them a year later."
msgstr "버밍엄에서 태어나 케냐에서 자란 화가. 1968년부터 홀로그램을 미술로 만들었고, 한 해 뒤에 전시했습니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. People section: what Stephen Benton, Mary Lou Jepsen did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.holography.people.4"
msgid "The first holographic video display was built in Benton's group at the MIT Media Lab in 1989. Jepsen's thesis of that year sets out its design."
msgstr "최초의 홀로그래피 동영상 디스플레이는 1989년 MIT 미디어랩의 벤턴 연구진에서 만들어졌습니다. 그해에 나온 젭슨의 학위 논문이 그 설계를 담고 있습니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. People section: what Ali Javan did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.holography.people.5"
msgid "Born in Tehran. At Bell Laboratories in 1960 he, William Bennett and Don Herriott ran the first gas laser, the first to give a steady beam. Laser light is what holograms are made with."
msgstr "테헤란에서 태어났습니다. 1960년 벨 연구소에서 윌리엄 베넷, 돈 헤리엇과 함께 최초의 기체 레이저를 작동시켰습니다. 끊기지 않는 빛줄기를 낸 첫 레이저였습니다. 홀로그램은 레이저 빛으로 만듭니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. People section: what Nasser Peyghambarian, Pierre-Alexandre Blanche did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.holography.people.6"
msgid "At the University of Arizona in 2010 their group showed a rewritable hologram: a sheet of polymer took a new colour picture every two seconds. In 2008 it took four minutes."
msgstr "2010년 애리조나 대학교에서, 두 사람의 연구진이 다시 쓸 수 있는 홀로그램을 선보였습니다. 고분자 시트가 2초마다 새 컬러 영상을 담았습니다. 2008년에는 4분이 걸렸습니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.holography.view.plate.label"
msgid "Look through the plate"
msgstr "건판 너머로 보기"

#: hall-7/holography
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.holography.view.plate.hint"
msgid "The toy engine is not behind the glass. Move your head: it turns as the real one would. Best with the daylight off."
msgstr "장난감 기관차는 유리 뒤에 있지 않습니다. 머리를 움직여 보세요. 진짜처럼 돌아 보입니다. 햇빛을 끄면 가장 잘 보입니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.holography.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "From Dennis Gabor's United States patent of 1949. Light from a point source passes a small object and reaches a photographic plate. There the direct light and the scattered light meet and leave a pattern of rings."
msgstr "데니스 가보르의 1949년 미국 특허에서. 점광원에서 나온 빛이 작은 물체를 지나 사진 건판에 닿습니다. 거기서 곧장 온 빛과 흩어진 빛이 만나 고리 무늬를 남깁니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.holography.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: rays fanning from a point marked S past a point marked O to an upright plate, and beside it a square plate marked with concentric rings."
msgstr "선 그림: S라고 적힌 점에서 퍼져 나온 광선이 O라고 적힌 점을 지나, 세워 놓은 판에 닿습니다. 옆에는 동심원 고리가 그려진 네모난 판."

#: hall-7/holography
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.holography.media.patent-2.caption"
msgid "Making and viewing a hologram, from the patent that Emmett Leith and Juris Upatnieks filed in 1964. A prism turns part of the beam aside as a reference. Lit again, the plate gives a real image and a virtual one."
msgstr "홀로그램을 만들고 보는 방법. 에밋 리스와 유리스 우파트니엑스가 1964년에 출원한 특허에서. 프리즘이 빔의 일부를 기준광으로 옆으로 돌립니다. 다시 비추면, 건판은 실상 하나와 허상 하나를 보여 줍니다."

#: hall-7/holography
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.holography.media.patent-2.alt"
msgid "Two labelled diagrams: a light source, an object and a prism in front of a recording plate, then the plate lit by a beam, with an image on each side."
msgstr "설명이 붙은 도해 두 개: 기록용 건판 앞에 광원과 물체와 프리즘이 있는 그림, 그리고 빔을 받은 건판의 양쪽에 상이 있는 그림."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.name"
msgid "Head-mounted display"
msgstr "헤드 마운트 디스플레이"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.aka"
msgid "HMD, headset"
msgstr "HMD, 헤드셋"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.origin"
msgid "Ivan Sutherland and Bob Sproull, Harvard University, USA: the first driven by computer graphics"
msgstr "아이번 서덜랜드, 밥 스프롤. 하버드 대학교, 미국: 컴퓨터 그래픽으로 구동한 최초의 것"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.statusNote"
msgid "Consumer products since the 1990s, sold in large numbers since the 2010s."
msgstr "1990년대부터 소비자용 제품이 있었고, 2010년대부터 많이 팔리고 있습니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.lead"
msgid "The wearer has a small screen and a lens in front of each eye, and the picture changes as the head turns."
msgstr "쓴 사람의 두 눈앞에 작은 화면과 렌즈가 하나씩 있고, 머리를 돌리면 그림이 바뀝니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.how.1"
msgid "It is a stereoscope with screens instead of cards. A lens in front of each eye magnifies a small screen and makes its picture appear at a comfortable distance. Sensors report where the head is pointing, and the pictures are redrawn to match. The scene therefore seems to stay still while you look around it."
msgstr "이것은 카드 대신 화면을 쓰는 입체경입니다. 눈앞의 렌즈가 작은 화면을 크게 보이게 하고, 그 그림이 편안한 거리에 있는 것처럼 보이게 합니다. 센서가 머리가 향한 곳을 알려 주고, 그림은 거기에 맞추어 다시 그려집니다. 그래서 둘러보는 동안에도 장면은 제자리에 있는 것처럼 보입니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.how.2"
msgid "A half-silvered mirror, which reflects part of the light and passes the rest, can let the outside world in. Cameras can also film the outside world and mix it into the picture. Picture tubes mounted on helmets existed by 1961. The 1968 system by Sutherland and Sproull was the first to show graphics drawn by a computer."
msgstr "빛의 일부는 반사하고 나머지는 통과시키는 반투명 거울을 쓰면, 바깥세상을 들일 수 있습니다. 카메라로 바깥세상을 찍어 그림에 섞을 수도 있습니다. 헬멧에 수상관을 단 장치는 1961년에 이미 있었습니다. 서덜랜드와 스프롤의 1968년 장치는 컴퓨터가 그린 그래픽을 처음으로 보여 주었습니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.good.1"
msgid "Fills the field of view and follows the head"
msgstr "시야를 가득 채우고 머리를 따라온다"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.good.2"
msgid "Gives a private display that can appear to be any size"
msgstr "어떤 크기로든 보일 수 있는, 혼자만의 화면을 준다"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.bad.1"
msgid "Sits on the face"
msgstr "얼굴 위에 얹힌다"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.bad.2"
msgid "Makes the eyes focus at one fixed distance while they turn inward for another"
msgstr "눈이 정해진 한 거리에 초점을 맞춘 채, 다른 거리를 향해 안으로 모여야 한다"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.bad.3"
msgid "The wearer feels any delay between head and picture immediately"
msgstr "머리와 그림 사이에 조금이라도 지연이 있으면, 쓴 사람은 곧바로 느낀다"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.look"
msgid "The model shows a headset on a dummy head. Beside it are the parts for one eye: a lens and the small screen behind it. The picture on that screen bulges outward, because the lens squeezes it back into shape. Press the Put it on button to see what the wearer sees."
msgstr "모델은 마네킹 머리에 씌운 헤드셋을 보여 줍니다. 그 옆에는 한쪽 눈의 부품, 곧 렌즈와 그 뒤의 작은 화면이 있습니다. 그 화면의 그림은 바깥으로 불룩합니다. 렌즈가 그것을 눌러 제 모양으로 되돌리기 때문입니다. 써 보기 버튼을 누르면, 쓴 사람이 보는 것을 볼 수 있습니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.fact"
msgid "One scene shown in the 1968 headset was a room drawn in lines around the wearer: walls marked N, S, E and W, a ceiling marked C and a floor marked F. Sutherland reported that people quickly accepted being inside it."
msgstr "1968년의 헤드셋에서 보여 준 장면 하나는, 쓴 사람 둘레에 선으로 그린 방이었습니다. 벽에는 N, S, E, W, 천장에는 C, 바닥에는 F라고 적혀 있었습니다. 서덜랜드는 사람들이 그 안에 있다는 것을 금세 받아들였다고 보고했습니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. People section: what Ivan Sutherland, Bob Sproull did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.people.1"
msgid "Built the first headset driven by computer graphics, at Harvard University in 1968."
msgstr "1968년 하버드 대학교에서, 컴퓨터 그래픽으로 구동하는 최초의 헤드셋을 만들었습니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. People section: what Samuel Achilefu did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.people.2"
msgid "Born in Nigeria. His team at Washington University in St. Louis built goggles with a screen for surgeons: a dye makes cancer cells glow on it. First worn in an operation in 2014."
msgstr "나이지리아에서 태어났습니다. 세인트루이스 워싱턴 대학교의 그의 연구진은 외과 의사를 위해 화면이 달린 고글을 만들었습니다. 염료가 암세포를 화면에서 빛나게 합니다. 2014년에 처음으로 수술에서 쓰였습니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.view.worn.label"
msgid "Put it on"
msgstr "써 보기"

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "Morton Heilig's television mask, from his patent of 1960. A small television tube and a wide lens sit in front of each eye. The patent also gives the wearer earphones and nozzles that blow air."
msgstr "모턴 하일리그의 텔레비전 마스크. 그의 1960년 특허에서. 두 눈앞에 작은 텔레비전 관과 넓은 렌즈가 하나씩 놓입니다. 특허는 쓰는 사람에게 이어폰과, 바람을 내뿜는 노즐도 줍니다."

#: hall-7/head-mounted
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.head-mounted.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Line drawings: a side view of a head with a lens against the eye and a cone-shaped tube behind it, and a box-shaped mask with two round eyepieces."
msgstr "선 그림: 눈에 렌즈를 대고 그 뒤에 원뿔 모양의 관이 달린 머리의 옆모습, 그리고 둥근 접안부 두 개가 달린 상자 모양의 마스크."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.name"
msgid "Virtual retinal display"
msgstr "가상 망막 디스플레이"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.aka"
msgid "Retinal scan display"
msgstr "망막 주사 디스플레이"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.origin"
msgid "Kazuo Yoshinaka, Nippon Electric, Japan. Developed further at the University of Washington from 1991"
msgstr "요시나카 가즈오, 일본 전기, 일본. 1991년부터 워싱턴 대학교에서 더 발전했습니다"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.statusNote"
msgid "A few products, including eyewear aimed at people with low vision."
msgstr "제품이 몇 가지 있고, 저시력자를 위한 안경형 기기도 있습니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.lead"
msgid "There is no screen: a beam of light scanned through the pupil draws the picture directly on the retina, at the back of the eye."
msgstr "화면이 없습니다. 눈동자를 지나며 주사하는 빛의 빔이, 눈 뒤쪽의 망막에 그림을 바로 그립니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.how.1"
msgid "A low-power beam sweeps line by line, like the beam in a picture tube, but it points into the eye. The lens of the eye is the last part of the optics. The retina, the light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye, is the screen."
msgstr "약한 빔이 수상관의 빔처럼 한 줄씩 훑지만, 향하는 곳은 눈 안입니다. 눈의 수정체가 광학계의 마지막 부품입니다. 눈 뒤쪽의 빛을 느끼는 층인 망막이 화면입니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.how.2"
msgid "All of the light goes into the eye, so the display needs very little light, and the picture can still be bright enough for daylight. There is no panel to shrink, so the hardware can be tiny."
msgstr "빛이 모두 눈으로 들어가므로, 이 디스플레이에는 빛이 아주 조금만 있으면 되고, 그러면서도 그림은 햇빛 속에서 볼 만큼 밝을 수 있습니다. 줄여야 할 패널이 없어서, 장치는 아주 작을 수 있습니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.good.1"
msgid "Is bright enough to show over a sunny scene"
msgstr "햇빛이 비치는 풍경 위에 겹쳐 보여도 될 만큼 밝다"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.good.2"
msgid "Needs only very small optics"
msgstr "아주 작은 광학계만 있으면 된다"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. What it is good at, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.good.3"
msgid "Is sold as an aid for people with low vision"
msgstr "저시력자를 위한 보조 기기로 팔린다"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.bad.1"
msgid "Can lose the picture when the eye moves, because the beam must enter the pupil"
msgstr "빔이 눈동자로 들어가야 해서, 눈이 움직이면 그림을 놓칠 수 있다"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.bad.2"
msgid "Has to stay far below the power that could harm the eye"
msgstr "눈을 다치게 할 수 있는 세기보다 훨씬 낮게 머물러야 한다"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.bad.3"
msgid "Is hard to get right, and few products exist"
msgstr "제대로 만들기 어렵고, 제품이 드물다"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.look"
msgid "The model is a classroom model of an eye, with a quarter cut away. Look into the cut. One beam enters through the pupil, and the picture forms line by line on the back wall of the eye. The picture is upside down, as every image inside an eye is."
msgstr "모델은 4분의 1을 잘라 낸, 교실에서 쓰는 눈 모형입니다. 잘린 곳을 들여다보세요. 빔 한 줄기가 눈동자로 들어가고, 눈의 뒤쪽 벽에 그림이 한 줄씩 맺힙니다. 그림은 거꾸로입니다. 눈 안의 상은 모두 그렇습니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.fact"
msgid "All the light that enters an eye from an ordinary desktop screen amounts to less than a millionth of a watt. That is small beside what one light-emitting diode can supply."
msgstr "보통의 책상 위 화면에서 눈으로 들어오는 빛을 모두 더해도 백만분의 1와트가 되지 않습니다. 발광 다이오드 하나가 낼 수 있는 양에 견주면 작습니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. People section: what Kazuo Yoshinaka did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.people.1"
msgid "Proposed the display at Nippon Electric in Japan in 1986."
msgstr "1986년 일본의 일본 전기에서 이 디스플레이를 제안했습니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.view.retina.label"
msgid "Look in at the retina"
msgstr "망막 들여다보기"

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Line shown while the visitor is at that place.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.view.retina.hint"
msgid "There is no screen. The beam goes in through the pupil and paints the picture on the back wall of the eye, upside down."
msgstr "화면이 없습니다. 빔은 눈동자로 들어가, 눈의 뒤쪽 벽에 거꾸로 선 그림을 그립니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.media.patent-1.caption"
msgid "The virtual retinal display as a block diagram, from the University of Washington patent of 1995. Light is modulated, scanned across and down, and sent into the eye. The lens of the eye itself focuses it on the retina."
msgstr "블록 도해로 그린 가상 망막 디스플레이. 워싱턴 대학교의 1995년 특허에서. 빛은 변조되고, 가로와 세로로 주사되어, 눈 안으로 보내집니다. 눈의 수정체 자체가 그 빛을 망막에 모읍니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.media.patent-1.alt"
msgid "Block diagrams: boxes marked photon generator, modulator, scanner and projection optics lead to a drawing of an eye, with an eye tracker above. A second diagram adds video circuits."
msgstr "블록 도해: photon generator, modulator, scanner, projection optics라고 적힌 상자들이 눈 그림으로 이어지고, 위에는 시선 추적기가 있습니다. 두 번째 도해에는 영상 회로가 더해져 있습니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. Caption of a picture or film from an archive.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.media.patent-2.caption"
msgid "One of the scanners in the same patent. A curved mirror on a block of piezoelectric material sweeps the beam one way, and a second, turning mirror sweeps it the other. Together they draw a raster."
msgstr "같은 특허에 나오는 스캐너 하나. 압전 재료 덩어리에 얹힌 굽은 거울이 빔을 한 방향으로 훑고, 도는 두 번째 거울이 다른 방향으로 훑습니다. 둘이 함께 주사 면을 그립니다."

#: hall-7/retinal-display
#. The same picture described for someone who cannot see it.
msgctxt "piece.retinal-display.media.patent-2.alt"
msgid "Perspective drawing: a light beam strikes the curved step of a block labelled piezoelectric material, rises to a tilted square mirror, and fans out to a dashed frame marked raster."
msgstr "투시도: 빛의 빔이 piezoelectric material이라고 적힌 덩어리의 굽은 단에 부딪히고, 기울어진 네모난 거울로 올라가, raster라고 적힌 점선 틀로 퍼집니다."

#: hall-7/head-up
#. Name of a piece.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.name"
msgid "Head-up display"
msgstr "헤드업 디스플레이"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. Other names the piece goes by.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.aka"
msgid "HUD"
msgstr "HUD"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. The date of the piece, as its label gives it.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.yearText"
msgid "first flown 1958, in service 1961"
msgstr "1958년 첫 비행, 1961년 실전 배치"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. Who made it first, and where. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.origin"
msgid "Blackburn Buccaneer strike aircraft, UK, descended from wartime reflector gunsights"
msgstr "공격기 블랙번 버커니어, 영국. 전쟁 시기의 반사식 조준기에서 이어져 왔습니다"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. Where this kind of display stands today.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.statusNote"
msgid "Standard in military and airline cockpits, and more and more common in cars. The first one in a production car appeared in 1988."
msgstr "군용기와 여객기의 조종실에서는 표준이고, 자동차에서도 점점 흔해지고 있습니다. 양산차에는 1988년에 처음 실렸습니다."

#: hall-7/head-up
#. First sentence of the label.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.lead"
msgid "A sloping sheet of glass reflects symbols that are focused far away, so that they appear to sit on the world outside."
msgstr "비스듬한 유리판이 먼 곳에 초점이 맞은 기호를 비추어, 기호가 바깥세상 위에 놓인 것처럼 보이게 합니다."

#: hall-7/head-up
#. How it works, paragraph 1 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.how.1"
msgid "A small, bright display points up at an angled glass plate called the combiner. The combiner reflects the display toward the eyes and lets the outside view through. Up to this point, it works like Pepper's ghost."
msgstr "작고 밝은 표시 장치가, 컴바이너라고 하는 비스듬한 유리판을 향해 위를 보고 있습니다. 컴바이너는 표시를 눈 쪽으로 반사하고, 바깥 풍경은 통과시킵니다. 여기까지는 페퍼의 유령과 같은 방식입니다."

#: hall-7/head-up
#. How it works, paragraph 2 of 2.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.how.2"
msgid "The difference is a lens that makes the light from each point of the display parallel. The symbols then appear to be at a great distance. The pilot does not need to refocus between the runway and the numbers. The symbols also stay in line with the world outside when the head moves."
msgstr "차이는, 표시의 점마다 나오는 빛을 평행하게 만드는 렌즈입니다. 그러면 기호는 아주 먼 곳에 있는 것처럼 보입니다. 조종사는 활주로와 숫자 사이에서 초점을 다시 맞출 필요가 없습니다. 머리가 움직여도 기호는 바깥세상과 줄이 맞습니다."

#: hall-7/head-up
#. What it is good at, point 1 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.good.1"
msgid "Gives information without the need to look down or refocus"
msgstr "아래를 보거나 초점을 다시 맞추지 않고도 정보를 얻는다"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. What it is good at, point 2 of 2. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.good.2"
msgid "Can line its symbols up with real objects outside"
msgstr "기호를 바깥의 실제 물체와 겹쳐 놓을 수 있다"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. Where it fails, point 1 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.bad.1"
msgid "Is visible only from a small range of head positions"
msgstr "머리가 좁은 범위 안에 있을 때에만 보인다"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. Where it fails, point 2 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.bad.2"
msgid "Covers only a narrow field of view"
msgstr "좁은 시야만 덮는다"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. Where it fails, point 3 of 3. No full stop at the end.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.bad.3"
msgid "Must be bright enough to read against the sky"
msgstr "하늘을 배경으로도 읽을 만큼 밝아야 한다"

#: hall-7/head-up
#. What to look for in the 3D model. Where it names a button, use the words you gave that button.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.look"
msgid "The projector sits in the top of the cover over the instruments, and the combiner leans over it. Press the Sit down button to take the seat of the pilot. The green symbols are focused far away, so they stay still against the background when you move your head. From any other position you see part of them or nothing."
msgstr "투사기는 계기를 덮은 덮개의 위쪽에 있고, 컴바이너가 그 위로 기울어 있습니다. 자리에 앉기 버튼을 눌러 조종사의 자리에 앉아 보세요. 초록 기호는 먼 곳에 초점이 맞아 있어서, 머리를 움직여도 배경에 대해 제자리에 있습니다. 다른 자리에서는 기호의 일부만 보이거나 아무것도 보이지 않습니다."

#: hall-7/head-up
#. A short story about the piece, under the heading Worth knowing.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.fact"
msgid "The system first called a head-up display answered a question put to J. M. Naish at Farnborough in 1956: how to fly fast and low in poor visibility without looking down. A television picture in the instrument panel was considered and rejected."
msgstr "처음으로 헤드업 디스플레이라고 불린 장치는, 1956년 판버러에서 J. M. 네이시에게 던져진 물음에 대한 답이었습니다. 시야가 나쁠 때 아래를 보지 않고 어떻게 빠르고 낮게 날 것인가. 계기판에 텔레비전 그림을 넣는 방안도 검토했지만 물리쳤습니다."

#: hall-7/head-up
#. People section: what J. M. Naish did. The names stand above this line.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.people.1"
msgid "Worked out the system first called a head-up display, at Farnborough from 1956."
msgstr "1956년부터 판버러에서, 처음으로 헤드업 디스플레이라고 불린 장치를 만들어 냈습니다."

#: hall-7/head-up
#. Button that moves the visitor to a place in the 3D model. At most 28 characters.
msgctxt "piece.head-up.view.seat.label"
msgid "Sit down"
msgstr "자리에 앉기"

#: archive
#. What was done to an archive picture or film before it was put on the site. Keep the words in curly brackets as they are.
msgctxt "changes.1"
msgid "Cut to {n} seconds, re-encoded"
msgstr "{n}초로 자르고 다시 인코딩함"

#: archive
#. What was done to an archive picture or film before it was put on the site.
msgctxt "changes.2"
msgid "Resized"
msgstr "크기를 조정함"

#: archive
#. What was done to an archive picture or film before it was put on the site. Keep the words in curly brackets as they are.
msgctxt "changes.3"
msgid "Cut to {n} seconds, sound removed, re-encoded"
msgstr "{n}초로 자르고 소리를 없앤 뒤 다시 인코딩함"

#: archive
#. What was done to an archive picture or film before it was put on the site.
msgctxt "changes.4"
msgid "Cropped, marks from the rest of the sheet painted out, resized"
msgstr "잘라 내고, 도면의 다른 부분에서 온 표시를 지우고, 크기를 조정함"

#: archive
#. What was done to an archive picture or film before it was put on the site.
msgctxt "changes.5"
msgid "Cropped, resized"
msgstr "잘라 내고 크기를 조정함"

#: archive
#. What was done to an archive picture or film before it was put on the site.
msgctxt "changes.6"
msgid "Cropped, turned upright, resized"
msgstr "잘라 내고, 똑바로 세우고, 크기를 조정함"

#: archive
#. What was done to an archive picture or film before it was put on the site.
msgctxt "changes.7"
msgid "Cropped, marks from the rest of the sheet painted out, turned upright, resized"
msgstr "잘라 내고, 도면의 다른 부분에서 온 표시를 지우고, 똑바로 세우고, 크기를 조정함"

#: archive
#. A year standing alone under an archive picture, as in "1930". Most languages leave it as it is, and some add a word to it. Keep the words in curly brackets as they are.
msgctxt "year"
msgid "{n}"
msgstr "{n}"

#: archive
#. Before a year that is not certain, as in "about 1930". Keep the words in curly brackets as they are.
msgctxt "about"
msgid "about {n}"
msgstr "{n}년경"

#: tour-women
#. Title of a guided tour.
msgctxt "tour.women.title"
msgid "Women in the museum"
msgstr "박물관의 여성들"

#: tour-women
#. One sentence under the title of the tour, saying what it is.
msgctxt "tour.women.lead"
msgid "A guided walk through all seven halls. Thirteen displays, from braille pins to a hologram, each with at least one woman among its inventors or contributors."
msgstr "일곱 전시실을 모두 도는 가이드 투어입니다. 점자 핀에서 홀로그램까지 디스플레이 열세 점을 봅니다. 저마다 발명자나 기여자 가운데 여성이 적어도 한 명 있습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, before the first stop: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.start.title"
msgid "Before the first stop"
msgstr "첫 지점에 앞서"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, before the first stop: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 2. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.start.text.1"
msgid "Welcome to the museum. This tour follows the green line on the floor. It runs through all seven halls and stops at thirteen displays. Each has at least one woman among its inventors or contributors."
msgstr "박물관에 오신 것을 환영합니다. 이 투어는 바닥의 초록 선을 따라갑니다. 일곱 전시실을 모두 지나며 디스플레이 열세 점 앞에 멈춥니다. 저마다 발명자나 기여자 가운데 여성이 적어도 한 명 있습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, before the first stop: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 2. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.start.text.2"
msgid "The tour waits at each stop. Press the key for the next stop when you are ready. The first is the braille display in hall 1, where the picture is made of pins."
msgstr "투어는 지점마다 기다립니다. 준비가 되면 다음 지점 버튼을 누르세요. 첫 지점은 제1전시실의 점자 디스플레이입니다. 그곳에서는 그림이 핀으로 이루어집니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Refreshable braille display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.braille-display.title"
msgid "Read by touch"
msgstr "손끝으로 읽기"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Refreshable braille display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.braille-display.text.1"
msgid "This display is read by touch. Rows of pins rise and fall to spell a line of braille. The model is spelling the time. Watch the cells on the right. They change every second."
msgstr "이 디스플레이는 손끝으로 읽습니다. 핀의 줄이 오르내리며 점자 한 줄을 씁니다. 모델은 시각을 쓰고 있습니다. 오른쪽 셀들을 보세요. 1초마다 바뀝니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Refreshable braille display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.braille-display.text.2"
msgid "Under each dot is a crystal that bends when a voltage reaches it, and a lever turns that small bend into the rise of a pin. Andrée Tretiakoff and Oleg Tretiakoff patented this way of raising the dots. They filed first in France, in 1974."
msgstr "점마다 그 아래에 전압이 닿으면 휘는 결정이 있고, 지렛대가 그 작은 휨을 핀이 올라오는 움직임으로 바꿉니다. 앙드레 트레티아코프와 올레그 트레티아코프가 점을 들어 올리는 이 방법의 특허를 받았습니다. 첫 출원은 1974년 프랑스에서 했습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Refreshable braille display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.braille-display.text.3"
msgid "It is the only piece in the museum that needs no light at all. Every other stop on the green line works with light. The next is the cathode ray tube in hall 2, where a beam of electrons makes a screen glow."
msgstr "박물관에서 빛이 전혀 필요 없는 전시품은 이것 하나뿐입니다. 초록 선의 다른 지점은 모두 빛으로 작동합니다. 다음은 제2전시실의 음극선관입니다. 그곳에서는 전자 빔이 화면을 빛나게 합니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Cathode ray tube: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.cathode-ray-tube.title"
msgid "A beam that draws"
msgstr "그림을 그리는 빔"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Cathode ray tube: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.cathode-ray-tube.text.1"
msgid "Look at the screen. The bright band moving down it is where one beam of electrons has just drawn its lines. The model slows it about 60 times. From the side you can see the copper coils that steer the beam."
msgstr "화면을 보세요. 화면을 따라 내려가는 밝은 띠는 전자 빔 한 줄기가 방금 선을 그린 자리입니다. 모델은 이것을 약 60분의 1 속도로 늦추었습니다. 옆에서 보면 빔의 방향을 바꾸는 구리 코일이 보입니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Cathode ray tube: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.cathode-ray-tube.text.2"
msgid "The plate on the block names Ferdinand Braun, who built the first tube of this kind in 1897. Thirty years later, in San Francisco, Philo Farnsworth sent the first television picture made entirely by electronics. Elma Farnsworth, known as Pem, worked in his laboratories and made the technical drawings for the experiments. Her face was among the first that the system sent."
msgstr "받침대의 명판에는 페르디난트 브라운의 이름이 있습니다. 1897년에 이런 관을 처음 만든 사람입니다. 30년 뒤 샌프란시스코에서 필로 판즈워스가 완전한 전자식 텔레비전 영상을 처음으로 보냈습니다. 펨이라는 이름으로 알려진 엘마 판즈워스는 그의 연구실에서 일하며 실험에 쓸 기술 도면을 그렸습니다. 그 방식이 처음 보낸 얼굴 가운데 하나가 그녀의 얼굴이었습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Cathode ray tube: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.cathode-ray-tube.text.3"
msgid "In hall 1 the picture was made of pins. Here one beam draws all of it. The next stop is the MicroLED wall in hall 3, where the picture is made of lamps."
msgstr "제1전시실에서는 그림이 핀으로 이루어졌습니다. 여기서는 빔 한 줄기가 그림 전체를 그립니다. 다음 지점은 제3전시실의 마이크로 LED 벽입니다. 그곳에서는 그림이 램프로 이루어집니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece MicroLED: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.microled.title"
msgid "A question at dinner"
msgstr "저녁 식탁의 질문"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece MicroLED: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.microled.text.1"
msgid "The wall in front of you is made of LEDs, each too small to see. One tile is out and leans against the stand, with the suction lifter still on it. A wall like this is repaired one square at a time."
msgstr "앞에 있는 벽은 LED로 이루어져 있고, 하나하나는 너무 작아서 보이지 않습니다. 타일 하나가 빠져서, 흡착식 분리 도구가 붙은 채로 받침대에 기대어 있습니다. 이런 벽은 네모 한 장씩 고칩니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece MicroLED: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.microled.text.2"
msgid "Jingyu Lin and Hongxing Jiang are physicists. In 2000, at Kansas State University, they made the first microLEDs. They are also a married couple, and the idea began as a question at their dinner table: what would happen to an LED shrunk to thousandths of a millimetre?"
msgstr "징위 린과 훙싱 장은 물리학자입니다. 2000년 캔자스 주립 대학교에서 두 사람은 최초의 마이크로 LED를 만들었습니다. 두 사람은 부부이기도 하고, 이 생각은 저녁 식탁에서 나온 질문에서 시작되었습니다. LED를 천분의 몇 밀리미터로 줄이면 어떻게 될까요?"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece MicroLED: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.microled.text.3"
msgid "Stay in hall 3. The next stop, the quantum-dot display, works with crystals far smaller than these lamps."
msgstr "제3전시실에 머무르세요. 다음 지점인 양자점 디스플레이는 이 램프들보다 훨씬 작은 결정으로 작동합니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Quantum-dot display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.quantum-dot.title"
msgid "Colour from size"
msgstr "크기가 정하는 색"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Quantum-dot display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.quantum-dot.text.1"
msgid "This is one pixel, cut open and greatly enlarged. Blue light rises from the lamps on the board. In the left well, large crystals turn it red. In the middle well, small ones turn it green. The right well has no crystals, and the blue passes through. The size of a crystal alone sets its colour."
msgstr "이것은 잘라 열어 크게 확대한 화소 하나입니다. 기판의 램프에서 파란빛이 올라옵니다. 왼쪽 우물에서는 큰 결정이 그 빛을 빨강으로 바꿉니다. 가운데 우물에서는 작은 결정이 초록으로 바꿉니다. 오른쪽 우물에는 결정이 없어서 파란빛이 그대로 지나갑니다. 결정의 크기만으로 색이 정해집니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Quantum-dot display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.quantum-dot.text.2"
msgid "The crystals are called quantum dots. Most screens use them as this model does, to turn blue light into red and green. In 1994, at Berkeley, Vicki Colvin and two colleagues took another route. They made a layer of dots glow by passing a current through it. Colvin is the first author of the paper."
msgstr "이 결정을 양자점이라고 합니다. 대부분의 화면은 이 모델처럼, 파란빛을 빨강과 초록으로 바꾸는 데 양자점을 씁니다. 1994년 버클리에서 비키 콜빈과 두 동료는 다른 길을 택했습니다. 양자점의 층에 전류를 흘려 빛나게 한 것입니다. 콜빈은 그 논문의 제1저자입니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Quantum-dot display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.quantum-dot.text.3"
msgid "The cathode ray tube, the LED wall and this pixel all make their own light. In hall 4 the sun, a lamp or a laser makes the light, and the display shapes it. Go to the shadow play, where the lamp is a flame behind a cloth."
msgstr "음극선관, LED 벽, 그리고 이 화소는 모두 스스로 빛을 냅니다. 제4전시실에서는 해나 램프나 레이저가 빛을 내고, 디스플레이는 그 빛을 다듬습니다. 그림자극으로 가세요. 그곳의 램프는 천 뒤의 불꽃입니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Shadow play: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.shadow-play.title"
msgid "A flame and a cloth"
msgstr "불꽃과 천"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Shadow play: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.shadow-play.text.1"
msgid "Look at the cloth. A flame burns behind it, and three flat figures stand between the flame and the cloth: a tree, a deer and a bird. What you see are their shadows. Watch the bird. When it flies towards the lamp, its shadow grows and its edge goes soft."
msgstr "천을 보세요. 그 뒤에서 불꽃이 타고, 불꽃과 천 사이에 납작한 인형 셋이 서 있습니다. 나무와 사슴과 새입니다. 보이는 것은 그 그림자입니다. 새를 지켜보세요. 새가 등불 쪽으로 날아가면 그림자가 커지고 가장자리가 흐려집니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Shadow play: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.shadow-play.text.2"
msgid "Nobody knows who first did this. It is recorded in Java and in China about a thousand years ago, and it is performed today. Holes cut through a figure let the light pass and draw an eye or a feather inside the shadow. Kara Walker, an American artist known for silhouettes cut from black paper, has made films this way since 2004. Her jointed paper puppets, worked by hand on sticks, play out stories of slavery in America."
msgstr "이것을 누가 처음 했는지는 아무도 모릅니다. 약 천 년 전의 자바와 중국에 기록이 있고, 오늘날에도 공연됩니다. 인형에 뚫은 구멍은 빛을 통과시켜 그림자 안에 눈이나 깃털을 그립니다. 검은 종이를 오려 만든 실루엣으로 알려진 미국의 미술가 카라 워커는 2004년부터 이 방식으로 영상 작품을 만들고 있습니다. 막대에 달린 관절 있는 종이 인형을 손으로 움직여 미국 노예제의 이야기를 펼칩니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Shadow play: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.shadow-play.text.3"
msgid "The first stop needed no light at all. This one needs only a flame. The next stop, in this hall, draws with a beam of light. Go to the scanned laser."
msgstr "첫 지점에는 빛이 전혀 필요 없었습니다. 이 지점에는 불꽃 하나면 됩니다. 이 전시실에 있는 다음 지점은 빛줄기로 그림을 그립니다. 주사 레이저로 가세요."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Scanned laser: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.scanned-laser.title"
msgid "Drawing with a laser"
msgstr "레이저로 그리기"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Scanned laser: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.scanned-laser.text.1"
msgid "Follow the beams from left to right. Three lasers merge into one white beam. Two small mirrors on fast motors steer it, and your eye joins the moving dot into a drawing on the wall."
msgstr "빔을 왼쪽에서 오른쪽으로 따라가 보세요. 레이저 세 대가 한 줄기의 흰 빔으로 합쳐집니다. 빠른 모터에 달린 작은 거울 두 장이 그 방향을 바꾸고, 눈은 움직이는 점을 이어 벽 위의 그림으로 봅니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Scanned laser: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.scanned-laser.text.2"
msgid "The plate names Joseph Ostoja-Kotkowski, who staged an early laser show in Adelaide. Elsa Garmire, a physicist at the California Institute of Technology, made laser images. They were filmed in 1972, and they became the model for Laserium, the laser show that opened at Griffith Observatory in Los Angeles."
msgstr "명판에는 요제프 오스토야코트코프스키의 이름이 있습니다. 애들레이드에서 초기의 레이저 쇼를 올린 사람입니다. 캘리포니아 공과대학교의 물리학자 엘사 가마이어는 레이저 영상을 만들었습니다. 그 영상은 1972년에 촬영되었고, 로스앤젤레스의 그리피스 천문대에서 문을 연 레이저 쇼인 레이저리움의 본보기가 되었습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Scanned laser: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.scanned-laser.text.3"
msgid "In hall 2 a beam of electrons drew the picture inside a tube. This beam is light, and it can draw on anything: clouds, buildings, smoke. A few steps on, in the same hall, a picture is cut to fit a set of white plaster shapes."
msgstr "제2전시실에서는 전자 빔이 관 안에서 그림을 그렸습니다. 이 빔은 빛이고, 구름이든 건물이든 연기든 어디에나 그릴 수 있습니다. 같은 전시실에서 몇 걸음만 가면, 흰 석고 입체들에 맞게 오려 낸 그림이 있습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Projection mapping: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.projection-mapping.title"
msgid "A picture cut to fit"
msgstr "꼭 맞게 오려 낸 그림"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Projection mapping: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.projection-mapping.text.1"
msgid "Find the switch marked Daylight and turn it off. By day this picture is barely visible. The projector on the tripod is an ordinary one. The work is in the picture, which is cut to fit the shapes it lands on. Each shape gets its own pattern, and no light falls on the floor."
msgstr "햇빛이라고 적힌 스위치를 찾아 끄세요. 낮에는 이 그림이 거의 보이지 않습니다. 삼각대 위의 프로젝터는 평범한 프로젝터입니다. 공은 그림에 들어 있습니다. 그림은 빛이 닿는 입체에 맞게 오려 낸 것입니다. 입체마다 자기 무늬를 받고, 바닥에는 빛이 떨어지지 않습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Projection mapping: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.projection-mapping.text.2"
msgid "In 1969 the Haunted Mansion at Disneyland used this for its singing busts. The faces of singers were filmed, and the film was projected onto plaster busts of the same faces. In the same house a crystal ball holds a blank head. Leota Toombs, an artist at the Disney design workshop, gave it her face: the film projected onto that head is of her."
msgstr "1969년 디즈니랜드의 헌티드 맨션은 노래하는 흉상에 이 방법을 썼습니다. 가수들의 얼굴을 촬영하고, 그 필름을 같은 얼굴을 본뜬 석고 흉상에 비추었습니다. 같은 저택에는 민얼굴 머리가 든 수정 구슬이 있습니다. 디즈니의 설계 공방에서 일한 미술가 리오타 툼스가 그 머리에 자기 얼굴을 주었습니다. 그 머리에 비치는 필름은 그녀를 찍은 것입니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Projection mapping: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.projection-mapping.text.3"
msgid "Before you go on, turn the daylight back on. The next stop is in hall 5, where the displays behave like print: they need the light of the room. Go to the electrophoretic ink, the page of an e-reader."
msgstr "다음으로 가기 전에 햇빛을 다시 켜세요. 다음 지점은 제5전시실에 있습니다. 그곳의 디스플레이들은 인쇄물처럼 행동해서, 주변의 빛이 필요합니다. 전기 영동 잉크로 가세요. 전자책 단말기의 지면입니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrophoretic ink: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrophoretic-ink.title"
msgid "A page that bends"
msgstr "휘어지는 지면"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrophoretic ink: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrophoretic-ink.text.1"
msgid "This is the page of an e-reader. It makes no light. Millions of capsules hold black and white pigment, and a voltage pulls one colour to the top. Watch the page for a moment. Every 15 seconds it resets, with a flash of black and then of white. In between, it holds the page with no power."
msgstr "이것은 전자책 단말기의 지면입니다. 스스로 빛을 내지 않습니다. 수백만 개의 캡슐에 검은 안료와 흰 안료가 들어 있고, 전압이 한 색을 위로 끌어올립니다. 지면을 잠시 지켜보세요. 15초마다 검은색, 이어서 흰색으로 번쩍이며 새로 그려집니다. 그 사이에는 전력 없이 지면을 유지합니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrophoretic ink: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrophoretic-ink.text.2"
msgid "The plate names Barrett Comiskey, J. D. Albert and Joseph Jacobson, who developed this ink at the MIT Media Lab in 1996. Five years later, at Bell Laboratories, John Rogers and Zhenan Bao laid it on a sheet of plastic. Bao is a chemist who designs plastics that carry a current. With a rubber stamp they printed 256 plastic transistors on the sheet, one for each dot of the picture. The page could bend."
msgstr "명판에는 배럿 코미스키, J. D. 앨버트, 조지프 제이컵슨의 이름이 있습니다. 1996년 MIT 미디어랩에서 이 잉크를 개발한 사람들입니다. 5년 뒤 벨 연구소에서 존 로저스와 제난 바오가 이 잉크를 플라스틱 시트 위에 입혔습니다. 바오는 전기가 통하는 플라스틱을 설계하는 화학자입니다. 두 사람은 고무도장으로 시트 위에 플라스틱 트랜지스터 256개를 찍었습니다. 화면의 점 하나에 하나씩입니다. 지면은 휘어질 수 있었습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrophoretic ink: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrophoretic-ink.text.3"
msgid "The ink is slow, which suits a page. The next stop, in this hall, is a display of the same kind that became fast enough for video. Go to the electrowetting display."
msgstr "이 잉크는 느리지만, 지면에는 그것이 어울립니다. 이 전시실의 다음 지점은 같은 종류이면서 동영상을 보여 줄 만큼 빨라진 디스플레이입니다. 전기 습윤 디스플레이로 가세요."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrowetting display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrowetting.title"
msgid "Oil and water"
msgstr "기름과 물"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrowetting display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrowetting.text.1"
msgid "These are 12 cells, greatly enlarged. Each holds a drop of dyed oil under water. With no voltage, the oil spreads out and the cell shows its colour. With a voltage, the water pushes the oil into a corner. Watch one cell switch. The oil does not disappear. It gathers in the corner and stands higher."
msgstr "크게 확대한 셀 12개입니다. 셀마다 물 아래에 색을 입힌 기름방울이 들어 있습니다. 전압이 없으면 기름이 퍼져서 셀이 그 색을 보입니다. 전압이 걸리면 물이 기름을 구석으로 밀어냅니다. 셀 하나가 전환하는 모습을 보세요. 기름은 사라지지 않습니다. 구석에 모여 더 높이 솟습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrowetting display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrowetting.text.2"
msgid "At the first stop a voltage bent a crystal and lifted a pin. Here a voltage makes water push oil aside. Susan Hackwood and Gerardo Beni proposed this display at Bell Laboratories in 1981, and they gave the effect its name: electrowetting. The year on the plate, 2003, is when a laboratory in the Netherlands made it fast enough for video."
msgstr "첫 지점에서는 전압이 결정을 휘게 해서 핀을 들어 올렸습니다. 여기서는 전압이 걸리면 물이 기름을 옆으로 밀어냅니다. 수전 해크우드와 제라르도 베니는 1981년 벨 연구소에서 이 디스플레이를 제안하고, 이 효과에 전기 습윤이라는 이름을 붙였습니다. 명판의 연도인 2003년은 네덜란드의 한 연구소가 이것을 동영상을 보여 줄 만큼 빠르게 만든 해입니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrowetting display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrowetting.text.3"
msgid "There is one more stop in this hall. Go to the electrochromic display, a printed label that shows the minutes."
msgstr "이 전시실에 지점이 하나 더 있습니다. 전기 변색 디스플레이로 가세요. 분을 보여 주는 인쇄 라벨입니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrochromic display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrochromic.title"
msgid "Layer by layer"
msgstr "한 층씩"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrochromic display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrochromic.text.1"
msgid "This printed label shows the minutes past the hour. In this hall a voltage has moved pigment, and then oil. Here it moves atoms. It pushes charged atoms into the printed film, and the film darkens. Wait for the minute to change. The old numeral fades, and the new one darkens over several seconds. The pane of glass behind the label carries the same film."
msgstr "이 인쇄 라벨은 정시에서 몇 분이 지났는지를 보여 줍니다. 이 전시실에서 전압은 안료를 움직였고, 그다음에는 기름을 움직였습니다. 여기서는 원자를 움직입니다. 전하를 띤 원자를 인쇄된 막 안으로 밀어 넣으면 막이 짙어집니다. 분이 바뀌기를 기다리세요. 앞의 숫자가 옅어지고, 새 숫자가 몇 초에 걸쳐 짙어집니다. 라벨 뒤의 유리판에도 같은 막이 입혀져 있습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrochromic display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrochromic.text.2"
msgid "The plate names S. K. Deb, whose work on films of tungsten oxide, in 1969, is where these displays began. At the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Paula Hammond and Dean DeLongchamp built such films in another way: one molecular layer at a time. Their films changed colour faster, and with more contrast, than films made in other ways. The thesis that sets the work out is from 2003, and it points to a display in full colour."
msgstr "명판에는 S. K. 데브의 이름이 있습니다. 산화 텅스텐 막에 관한 그의 1969년 연구에서 이런 디스플레이가 시작되었습니다. 매사추세츠 공과대학교에서 폴라 해먼드와 딘 들롱샴은 그런 막을 다른 방법으로 만들었습니다. 분자층을 한 층씩 쌓은 것입니다. 두 사람의 막은 다른 방법으로 만든 막보다 더 빠르게, 더 높은 대비로 색이 바뀌었습니다. 이 연구를 담은 학위 논문은 2003년에 나왔고, 풀컬러 디스플레이로 가는 길을 가리킵니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrochromic display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.electrochromic.text.3"
msgid "That was the last display that works like print. Next is hall 6, Mid-air. Go to the illusion transmitter, where a picture hangs in the air."
msgstr "인쇄물처럼 작동하는 디스플레이는 이것이 마지막이었습니다. 다음은 제6전시실, 공중입니다. 환영 전송기로 가세요. 그곳에서는 그림이 허공에 떠 있습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Illusion transmitter: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.illusion-transmitter.title"
msgid "A bulb that is not there"
msgstr "거기에 없는 전구"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Illusion transmitter: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.illusion-transmitter.text.1"
msgid "Look at the two boxes. On the left, a real bulb hangs hidden under the socket, and a curved mirror stands its picture in the empty socket above. On the right there is no bulb, only the picture, sent across by a camera and a second mirror. Watch the left bulb go dark. The picture on the right follows."
msgstr "상자 두 개를 보세요. 왼쪽에는 진짜 전구가 소켓 아래에 숨어 매달려 있고, 굽은 거울이 그 상을 위의 빈 소켓에 세웁니다. 오른쪽에는 전구가 없고 상만 있습니다. 카메라와 두 번째 거울이 보내온 상입니다. 왼쪽 전구가 꺼지는 모습을 보세요. 오른쪽의 상도 따라 꺼집니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Illusion transmitter: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.illusion-transmitter.text.2"
msgid "In hall 3 a question was asked at a dinner table. This one was asked at an exhibition, in 1976. Valerie Thomas saw a curved mirror make a bulb seem to glow after it had been taken out of its socket, and she asked whether the picture could be sent. When she invented the illusion transmitter, she was managing the image-processing systems for Landsat at NASA. The patent was granted to her four years after that exhibition."
msgstr "제3전시실에서는 저녁 식탁에서 질문이 나왔습니다. 이번 질문은 1976년, 어느 전시회에서 나왔습니다. 밸러리 토머스는 굽은 거울이 소켓에서 빼낸 전구를 아직 빛나는 것처럼 보이게 하는 것을 보고, 그 상을 보낼 수 있을지 물었습니다. 환영 전송기를 발명할 때 토머스는 NASA에서 랜드샛의 영상 처리 시스템을 관리하고 있었습니다. 특허는 그 전시회로부터 4년 뒤에 그녀에게 등록되었습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Illusion transmitter: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.illusion-transmitter.text.3"
msgid "Stay in hall 6 for the static-volume display, where the picture fills a block of space."
msgstr "제6전시실에 머물러 정적 체적 디스플레이를 보세요. 그곳에서는 그림이 공간 한 덩어리를 채웁니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Static-volume display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.static-volume.title"
msgid "Drawing inside glass"
msgstr "유리 안에 그리기"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Static-volume display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.static-volume.text.1"
msgid "Start with the stack of clear sheets. The figure in it is a knot. One sheet at a time turns milky and catches its slice of the picture. Nothing moves."
msgstr "투명한 판을 쌓은 것부터 보세요. 그 안의 도형은 매듭입니다. 판이 한 번에 한 장씩 뿌옇게 변하며 그림의 단면 하나를 받습니다. 움직이는 것은 아무것도 없습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Static-volume display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.static-volume.text.2"
msgid "Now look beside the stack, at the block of glass. It glows only where two invisible beams cross, and the model draws them in red. A beam of electrons drew in hall 2, and a laser in hall 4. Here the point where two beams cross does the drawing, in three dimensions."
msgstr "이제 쌓은 판 옆의 유리 덩어리를 보세요. 유리는 보이지 않는 두 빔이 엇갈리는 곳에서만 빛나고, 모델은 그 빔을 빨갛게 그려 보입니다. 제2전시실에서는 전자 빔이, 제4전시실에서는 레이저가 그림을 그렸습니다. 여기서는 두 빔이 엇갈리는 점이 3차원으로 그림을 그립니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Static-volume display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.static-volume.text.3"
msgid "Elizabeth Downing and three colleagues drew coloured points of light inside glass in this way. Their work was published from Stanford University in 1996, with Downing as first author. The prototype was a cube about 15 millimetres on each side, and the model shows it many times that size. The last stop is holography, in hall 7."
msgstr "엘리자베스 다우닝과 세 동료는 이 방법으로 유리 안에 색이 있는 빛점을 그렸습니다. 이 연구는 1996년 스탠퍼드 대학교에서 발표되었고, 다우닝이 제1저자였습니다. 시제품은 한 변이 약 15밀리미터인 정육면체였고, 모델은 그것을 여러 배 크게 보여 줍니다. 마지막 지점은 제7전시실의 홀로그래피입니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Holography: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.holography.title"
msgid "The light itself"
msgstr "빛 그 자체"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Holography: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 2. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.holography.text.1"
msgid "Look through the glass plate. A toy engine seems to stand behind it. Move to one side, and you see around the engine. It is not behind the glass. A hologram records the light itself. In hall 4 a laser drew the picture. Here a laser only lights the plate, and the plate sends out the same light waves that the engine did."
msgstr "유리 건판 너머를 보세요. 장난감 기관차가 그 뒤에 서 있는 것처럼 보입니다. 옆으로 움직이면 기관차의 둘레가 보입니다. 기관차는 유리 뒤에 있지 않습니다. 홀로그램은 빛 그 자체를 기록합니다. 제4전시실에서는 레이저가 그림을 그렸습니다. 여기서 레이저는 건판을 비추기만 하고, 건판은 기관차가 내보냈던 것과 같은 빛의 파동을 내보냅니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, at the piece Holography: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 2. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.holography.text.2"
msgid "Dennis Gabor invented holography in 1948. Twenty years later Margaret Benyon, a painter, began to make holograms as art. A hologram like this one is static. The first holographic video display was built at the MIT Media Lab in 1989, and the thesis that Mary Lou Jepsen wrote there that year sets out its design."
msgstr "데니스 가보르는 1948년에 홀로그래피를 발명했습니다. 20년 뒤 화가 마거릿 베니언이 홀로그램을 미술로 만들기 시작했습니다. 이런 홀로그램은 움직이지 않습니다. 최초의 홀로그래피 동영상 디스플레이는 1989년 MIT 미디어랩에서 만들어졌고, 그해에 메리 루 젭슨이 그곳에서 쓴 학위 논문이 그 설계를 담고 있습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, after the last stop: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.women.end.title"
msgid "The end of the green line"
msgstr "초록 선의 끝"

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, after the last stop: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.end.text.1"
msgid "That is the end of the green line. It began with pins that are read by touch. It ends with a toy engine that is not there."
msgstr "초록 선은 여기서 끝납니다. 손끝으로 읽는 핀에서 시작했습니다. 거기에 없는 장난감 기관차에서 끝납니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, after the last stop: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.end.text.2"
msgid "Fifteen women are named at these thirteen stops, and you have now stood at the work of each. In all, the labels in these halls name more than a hundred people."
msgstr "이 열세 지점에는 여성 열다섯 명의 이름이 있고, 여러분은 이제 그 한 사람 한 사람의 작업 앞에 서 보았습니다. 이 전시실들의 라벨에는 모두 백 명이 넘는 사람의 이름이 있습니다."

#: tour-women
#. Guided tour, after the last stop: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.women.end.text.3"
msgid "The line passed more than forty other displays on the way. Leave the tour, and the halls are yours to walk. Every piece has its label, and its people are named on it. Thank you for taking the tour."
msgstr "오는 길에 초록 선은 마흔 점이 넘는 다른 디스플레이를 지나왔습니다. 투어를 그만두면 전시실을 자유롭게 걸을 수 있습니다. 전시품마다 라벨이 있고, 거기에 그 일을 한 사람들의 이름이 있습니다. 투어에 함께해 주셔서 고맙습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Title of a guided tour.
msgctxt "tour.black.title"
msgid "Black inventors and artists"
msgstr "흑인 발명가와 예술가"

#: tour-black
#. One sentence under the title of the tour, saying what it is.
msgctxt "tour.black.lead"
msgid "A guided walk through five halls. Ten displays, from a television tube to a headset, each with a Black inventor or artist on its label."
msgstr "다섯 전시실을 도는 가이드 투어입니다. 텔레비전 브라운관에서 헤드셋까지 디스플레이 열 점을 봅니다. 저마다 라벨에 흑인 발명가나 예술가의 이름이 있습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, before the first stop: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.start.title"
msgid "Before the first stop"
msgstr "첫 지점에 앞서"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, before the first stop: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.start.text.1"
msgid "This tour follows the blue line on the floor. It stops at ten displays in five halls. At each one the label names a Black inventor or a Black artist."
msgstr "이 투어는 바닥의 파란 선을 따라갑니다. 다섯 전시실에서 디스플레이 열 점 앞에 멈춥니다. 어느 디스플레이든 라벨에 흑인 발명가나 흑인 예술가의 이름이 있습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, before the first stop: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.start.text.2"
msgid "Some of them made the display. Some made a part that it could not do without. Some put it to a new use, or made art with it. At each stop I will say which."
msgstr "그 디스플레이를 만든 사람도 있습니다. 그것 없이는 디스플레이가 작동할 수 없는 부품을 만든 사람도 있습니다. 새로운 쓰임새를 찾아낸 사람도, 그것으로 미술 작품을 만든 사람도 있습니다. 어느 쪽인지는 지점마다 말씀드리겠습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, before the first stop: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.start.text.3"
msgid "The first stop is in hall 2. Go to the cathode ray tube."
msgstr "첫 지점은 제2전시실에 있습니다. 음극선관으로 가세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Cathode ray tube: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.cathode-ray-tube.title"
msgid "A camera at the door"
msgstr "현관의 카메라"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Cathode ray tube: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.cathode-ray-tube.text.1"
msgid "Look at the screen. The bright band moving down it is where one beam of electrons has just drawn its lines. The model slows it about 60 times."
msgstr "화면을 보세요. 화면을 따라 내려가는 밝은 띠는 전자 빔 한 줄기가 방금 선을 그린 자리입니다. 모델은 이것을 약 60분의 1 속도로 늦추었습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Cathode ray tube: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.cathode-ray-tube.text.2"
msgid "The plate on the block names Ferdinand Braun, who built the first tube of this kind in 1897. Two people on this label put the tube to work. Marie Van Brittan Brown was a nurse in New York, and her husband Albert was an electronics technician. In 1966 they built a security system for their home. A camera slid between four peepholes in the door, and a television monitor showed who was calling. They patented it in 1969. Mark Dean was an engineer at IBM. Two of his three patents on the IBM personal computer are for the adapters that drove its monitors, one monochrome and one colour."
msgstr "받침대의 명판에는 페르디난트 브라운의 이름이 있습니다. 1897년에 이런 관을 처음 만든 사람입니다. 이 라벨에 있는 두 사람은 이 관을 일에 썼습니다. 마리 밴 브리턴 브라운은 뉴욕의 간호사였고, 남편 앨버트는 전자 기술자였습니다. 1966년에 두 사람은 자기 집을 위한 보안 장치를 만들었습니다. 카메라가 현관문에 뚫린 네 개의 구멍 사이를 오르내리고, 텔레비전 모니터가 찾아온 사람을 비추었습니다. 두 사람은 1969년에 특허를 받았습니다. 마크 딘은 IBM의 기술자였습니다. IBM 개인용 컴퓨터에 관한 그의 특허 세 건 가운데 두 건은 모니터를 구동하는 어댑터에 관한 것입니다. 하나는 흑백용이고 하나는 컬러용입니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Cathode ray tube: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.cathode-ray-tube.text.3"
msgid "The next stop is in this hall as well. Go to the Numitron, the glass bulb with seven small filaments."
msgstr "다음 지점도 이 전시실에 있습니다. 누미트론으로 가세요. 작은 필라멘트 일곱 개가 든 유리구입니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Numitron: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.numitron.title"
msgid "The filament came first"
msgstr "먼저 있었던 필라멘트"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Numitron: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.numitron.text.1"
msgid "Look at the glass bulb. Inside are seven tiny lamp filaments, arranged as the seven bars that form a digit. Current through the right filaments makes them glow in the shape of a numeral."
msgstr "유리구를 보세요. 안에는 아주 작은 전구 필라멘트 일곱 개가, 숫자를 이루는 일곱 개의 막대 모양으로 놓여 있습니다. 알맞은 필라멘트에 전류가 흐르면 숫자 모양으로 빛납니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Numitron: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.numitron.text.2"
msgid "Lewis Latimer did not make this display. It is from 1970, and he worked nearly ninety years earlier. But a display made of filaments needs filaments that last. In 1882 Latimer patented a way of making the carbon filaments of early electric lamps so that fewer broke in the furnace."
msgstr "루이스 래티머는 이 디스플레이를 만든 사람이 아닙니다. 이것은 1970년에 나왔고, 래티머는 그보다 거의 90년 앞서 일했습니다. 하지만 필라멘트로 만든 디스플레이에는 오래가는 필라멘트가 필요합니다. 1882년에 래티머는 초기 전구의 탄소 필라멘트를 가마 안에서 덜 부러지게 만드는 방법의 특허를 받았습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Numitron: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.numitron.text.3"
msgid "Here the filament is the light. At the next stop, also in this hall, the light comes from a coating that is struck by electrons. Go to the field emission display."
msgstr "여기서는 필라멘트가 곧 빛입니다. 이 전시실에 있는 다음 지점에서는 전자가 때린 막에서 빛이 나옵니다. 전계 방출 디스플레이로 가세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Field emission display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.field-emission.title"
msgid "A tube made flat"
msgstr "납작하게 만든 관"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Field emission display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.field-emission.text.1"
msgid "The model shows one corner of a panel, opened and greatly enlarged. Follow the layers upward. First come the tips, then the gate with a hole over each tip. Above them is the front plate with its glowing coating. The lit dots count seconds."
msgstr "모델은 패널의 한 귀퉁이를 열어 크게 확대해 보여 줍니다. 층을 아래에서 위로 따라가 보세요. 먼저 돌기가 있고, 그 위에 돌기마다 구멍이 뚫린 게이트가 있습니다. 그 위가 앞판이고, 빛나는 막이 입혀져 있습니다. 불이 들어온 점은 초를 셉니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Field emission display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.field-emission.text.2"
msgid "This display is a cathode ray tube flattened to a few millimetres, with a tiny source of electrons behind every dot. The way of making the tips was patented at Stanford Research Institute. Akintunde Akinwande was educated in Nigeria and at Stanford. He joined Honeywell in 1986 and worked there on thin-film field emitters and displays. At MIT he worked on arrays of emitters that are switched one by one."
msgstr "이 디스플레이는 몇 밀리미터 두께로 납작하게 누른 음극선관으로, 점마다 뒤에 아주 작은 전자원이 있습니다. 돌기를 만드는 방법은 스탠퍼드 연구소에서 특허를 받았습니다. 아킨툰데 아킨완데는 나이지리아와 스탠퍼드 대학교에서 공부했습니다. 1986년에 허니웰에 들어가 그곳에서 박막 전계 방출 소자와 디스플레이를 연구했습니다. MIT에서는 하나씩 따로 켜고 끄는 방출 소자의 배열을 연구했습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Field emission display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.field-emission.text.3"
msgid "These three displays made their own light. The next one needs only a flame. Walk on to hall 4 and go to the shadow play."
msgstr "이 세 디스플레이는 스스로 빛을 냈습니다. 다음 디스플레이에는 불꽃 하나면 됩니다. 제4전시실로 걸어가 그림자극으로 가세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Shadow play: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.shadow-play.title"
msgid "Cut from black paper"
msgstr "검은 종이를 오려서"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Shadow play: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.shadow-play.text.1"
msgid "Look at the cloth. A flame burns behind it, and three flat figures stand between the flame and the cloth: a tree, a deer and a bird. What you see are their shadows. Switch the daylight off to see them well."
msgstr "천을 보세요. 그 뒤에서 불꽃이 타고, 불꽃과 천 사이에 납작한 인형 셋이 서 있습니다. 나무와 사슴과 새입니다. 보이는 것은 그 그림자입니다. 잘 보이도록 햇빛을 끄세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Shadow play: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.shadow-play.text.2"
msgid "Nobody knows who first did this. It is recorded in Java and in China about a thousand years ago. Kara Walker is an American artist, known for silhouettes cut from black paper. Since 2004 she has made films this way. Her jointed paper puppets, worked by hand on sticks, play out stories of slavery in America."
msgstr "이것을 누가 처음 했는지는 아무도 모릅니다. 약 천 년 전의 자바와 중국에 기록이 있습니다. 카라 워커는 미국의 미술가이고, 검은 종이를 오려 만든 실루엣으로 알려져 있습니다. 2004년부터 이 방식으로 영상 작품을 만들고 있습니다. 막대에 달린 관절 있는 종이 인형을 손으로 움직여 미국 노예제의 이야기를 펼칩니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Shadow play: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.shadow-play.text.3"
msgid "A shadow is a picture made by holding light back. The next stop, in this hall, sends a picture out with the light. Go to the projection mapping."
msgstr "그림자는 빛을 가로막아 만드는 그림입니다. 이 전시실에 있는 다음 지점은 빛에 그림을 실어 보냅니다. 프로젝션 매핑으로 가세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Projection mapping: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.projection-mapping.title"
msgid "A head in a crystal ball"
msgstr "수정 구슬 속의 머리"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Projection mapping: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.projection-mapping.text.1"
msgid "Look at the white plaster shapes and the projector on its tripod. Each shape receives its own pattern, and no light falls on the floor. By day the picture is barely visible, so switch the daylight off."
msgstr "흰 석고 입체들과 삼각대 위의 프로젝터를 보세요. 입체마다 자기 무늬를 받고, 바닥에는 빛이 떨어지지 않습니다. 낮에는 그림이 거의 보이지 않으니 햇빛을 끄세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Projection mapping: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.projection-mapping.text.2"
msgid "The year on the plate, 1969, belongs to the Haunted Mansion at Disneyland. There a filmed face is projected onto a blank head inside a crystal ball. The head is called Madame Leota. Lanny Smoot is a Disney research fellow with more than 100 patents. He is credited with making Madame Leota float in her séance room."
msgstr "명판의 연도인 1969년은 디즈니랜드 헌티드 맨션의 연도입니다. 그곳에서는 촬영한 얼굴이 수정 구슬 안의 민얼굴 머리에 비칩니다. 그 머리를 마담 리오타라고 부릅니다. 래니 스무트는 100건이 넘는 특허를 가진 디즈니의 리서치 펠로입니다. 마담 리오타를 강령회 방에서 떠다니게 만든 사람으로 꼽힙니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Projection mapping: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.projection-mapping.text.3"
msgid "So far every picture needed a lamp or a flame, or made its own light. In hall 5 the pictures use the light of the room. Go to the electrochromic display."
msgstr "지금까지는 그림마다 램프나 불꽃이 필요했거나, 그림이 스스로 빛을 냈습니다. 제5전시실에서는 그림이 주변의 빛을 씁니다. 전기 변색 디스플레이로 가세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrochromic display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.electrochromic.title"
msgid "One layer at a time"
msgstr "한 번에 한 층씩"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrochromic display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.electrochromic.text.1"
msgid "Look at the printed label that shows the minutes in bars. Wait for the minute to change. The old digit fades and the new digit darkens slowly, over several seconds. The window pane behind the label is the same film on glass."
msgstr "분을 막대로 보여 주는 인쇄 라벨을 보세요. 분이 바뀌기를 기다리세요. 앞의 숫자가 옅어지고, 새 숫자가 몇 초에 걸쳐 천천히 짙어집니다. 라벨 뒤의 창유리는 같은 막을 유리에 입힌 것입니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrochromic display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.electrochromic.text.2"
msgid "The plate names S. K. Deb. In 1969 he published the work on tungsten oxide films from which these displays grew. Paula Hammond worked at MIT with Dean DeLongchamp. They built electrochromic films one molecular layer at a time. Their films switched faster and with more contrast than films made in other ways. DeLongchamp's thesis of 2003 points to a full-colour display."
msgstr "명판에는 S. K. 데브의 이름이 있습니다. 1969년에 그는 산화 텅스텐 막에 관한 연구를 발표했고, 이런 디스플레이는 거기에서 자라났습니다. 폴라 해먼드는 MIT에서 딘 들롱샴과 함께 연구했습니다. 두 사람은 전기 변색 막을 분자층을 한 층씩 쌓아 만들었습니다. 이 막은 다른 방법으로 만든 막보다 더 빠르게, 더 높은 대비로 바뀌었습니다. 들롱샴의 2003년 학위 논문은 풀컬러 디스플레이로 가는 길을 가리킵니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Electrochromic display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.electrochromic.text.3"
msgid "This film keeps its colour with the power off. The next picture is not on a surface at all. Go to hall 6 and find the illusion transmitter."
msgstr "이 막은 전원을 꺼도 색을 유지합니다. 다음 그림은 어떤 표면 위에도 있지 않습니다. 제6전시실로 가서 환영 전송기를 찾으세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Illusion transmitter: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.illusion-transmitter.title"
msgid "A picture standing in the air"
msgstr "허공에 서 있는 그림"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Illusion transmitter: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.illusion-transmitter.text.1"
msgid "On the left a real bulb hangs hidden under the socket, and its picture stands in the empty socket above. On the right there is no bulb at all. Watch the left bulb go dark: the picture on the right follows a moment later."
msgstr "왼쪽에는 진짜 전구가 소켓 아래에 숨어 매달려 있고, 그 상이 위의 빈 소켓에 서 있습니다. 오른쪽에는 전구가 아예 없습니다. 왼쪽 전구가 꺼지는 모습을 보세요. 오른쪽의 상도 한순간 뒤에 따라 꺼집니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Illusion transmitter: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.illusion-transmitter.text.2"
msgid "Here the plate names the person this tour came for. Valerie Thomas was born in Maryland in 1943. She was one of only two women to major in physics at Morgan State University. She joined NASA as a data analyst and invented the illusion transmitter there. The plate gives the year 1980."
msgstr "여기 명판에는 이 투어가 찾아온 사람의 이름이 있습니다. 밸러리 토머스는 1943년 메릴랜드에서 태어났습니다. 모건 주립 대학교에서 물리학을 전공한 여성은 그녀를 포함해 단 두 명이었습니다. 데이터 분석가로 NASA에 들어가, 그곳에서 환영 전송기를 발명했습니다. 명판에 적힌 연도는 1980년입니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Illusion transmitter: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.illusion-transmitter.text.3"
msgid "A curved mirror gave that picture its depth. In hall 7 depth comes from giving each eye its own picture. Go to the stereoscope."
msgstr "그 그림에 깊이를 준 것은 굽은 거울이었습니다. 제7전시실에서는 두 눈에 서로 다른 그림을 보여 주는 데서 깊이가 생깁니다. 입체경으로 가세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Stereoscope and 3D glasses: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.stereoscope.title"
msgid "Two pictures, two eyes"
msgstr "그림 두 장, 눈 두 개"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Stereoscope and 3D glasses: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.stereoscope.text.1"
msgid "A hand-held viewer stands on a post, with a stereo card in its holder. Press the Look in button to put an eye to the lens: the flat card becomes a scene with depth. The red and cyan glasses in front do the same job with colour."
msgstr "손에 드는 뷰어가 기둥 위에 서 있고, 입체 카드가 꽂혀 있습니다. 들여다보기 버튼을 눌러 렌즈에 눈을 대 보세요. 평평한 카드가 깊이 있는 장면이 됩니다. 앞에 놓인 빨강과 청록 안경은 같은 일을 색으로 합니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Stereoscope and 3D glasses: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.stereoscope.text.2"
msgid "The plate names Charles Wheatstone, who published the stereoscope in London in 1838. Two people on this label came much later. Charles Harrison was an industrial designer in Chicago. In 1958 he redesigned the View-Master, a stereoscope for the hand, in light moulded plastic. It went from a photographer's accessory to a children's toy. Kenneth Dunkley is a physicist in Pennsylvania. His patent of 1989 is for viewing glasses with no lenses: two pinholes and two empty frames. The patent says that they give an ordinary flat picture the look of depth."
msgstr "명판에는 찰스 휘트스톤의 이름이 있습니다. 1838년 런던에서 입체경을 발표한 사람입니다. 이 라벨에 있는 두 사람은 훨씬 뒤의 사람입니다. 찰스 해리슨은 시카고의 산업 디자이너였습니다. 1958년에 손에 드는 입체경인 View-Master를 가벼운 성형 플라스틱으로 다시 디자인했습니다. 사진가의 부속품이던 것이 어린이의 장난감이 되었습니다. 케네스 던클리는 펜실베이니아의 물리학자입니다. 1989년의 특허는 렌즈가 없는 안경에 관한 것입니다. 작은 구멍 두 개와 빈 틀 두 개가 전부입니다. 특허는 이 안경이 평범한 평면 그림을 깊이가 있는 것처럼 보이게 한다고 밝힙니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Stereoscope and 3D glasses: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.stereoscope.text.3"
msgid "The stereoscope needs a viewer in front of the eyes. The next stop does without one. Stay in this hall and go to the lenticular display."
msgstr "입체경에는 눈앞에 대는 뷰어가 필요합니다. 다음 지점에는 그것이 필요 없습니다. 이 전시실에 머물러 렌티큘러 디스플레이로 가세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Lenticular display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.lenticular.title"
msgid "Words that change as you walk"
msgstr "걸으면 바뀌는 글"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Lenticular display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.lenticular.text.1"
msgid "Look at the card. It shows an eye, printed in nine views. Turn the model: the eye follows you across the room and jumps from one view to the next."
msgstr "카드를 보세요. 아홉 시점으로 인쇄한 눈이 있습니다. 모델을 돌려 보세요. 눈이 방을 가로지르는 여러분을 따라오고, 한 시점에서 다음 시점으로 뜁니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Lenticular display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.lenticular.text.2"
msgid "A sheet of tiny ribbed lenses lies over a picture made of strips, and each rib shows a different strip depending on where you stand. The plate names Walter Hess, who filed his patent in 1912. Hank Willis Thomas is an artist in New York. His lenticular prints carry text that changes as the visitor walks past. In one of them, Black Imitates White becomes White Imitates Black."
msgstr "골이 진 아주 작은 렌즈의 판이 띠로 이루어진 그림 위에 놓여 있고, 서 있는 자리에 따라 골마다 다른 띠를 보여 줍니다. 명판에는 발터 헤스의 이름이 있습니다. 1912년에 특허를 출원한 사람입니다. 행크 윌리스 토머스는 뉴욕의 미술가입니다. 그의 렌티큘러 인쇄 작품에는 관람객이 앞을 지나가면 바뀌는 글이 들어 있습니다. 한 작품에서는 Black Imitates White(검정이 하양을 흉내 낸다)가 White Imitates Black(하양이 검정을 흉내 낸다)으로 바뀝니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Lenticular display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.lenticular.text.3"
msgid "One stop is left, in this hall. Go to the head-mounted display."
msgstr "남은 지점은 하나이고, 이 전시실에 있습니다. 헤드 마운트 디스플레이로 가세요."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Head-mounted display: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.head-mounted.title"
msgid "Goggles for a surgeon"
msgstr "외과 의사를 위한 고글"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Head-mounted display: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.head-mounted.text.1"
msgid "The model shows a headset on a dummy head. Beside it are the parts for one eye: a lens and the small screen behind it. Press the Put it on button to see what the wearer sees."
msgstr "모델은 마네킹 머리에 씌운 헤드셋을 보여 줍니다. 그 옆에는 한쪽 눈의 부품, 곧 렌즈와 그 뒤의 작은 화면이 있습니다. 써 보기 버튼을 누르면 쓴 사람에게 보이는 것을 볼 수 있습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Head-mounted display: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.head-mounted.text.2"
msgid "The plate names Ivan Sutherland and Bob Sproull, who built the first headset driven by computer graphics, at Harvard University in 1968. Samuel Achilefu was born in Nigeria. His team at Washington University in St. Louis built goggles with a screen for surgeons. A dye makes cancer cells glow on that screen. The goggles were first worn in an operation in 2014."
msgstr "명판에는 아이번 서덜랜드와 밥 스프롤의 이름이 있습니다. 1968년 하버드 대학교에서 컴퓨터 그래픽으로 구동하는 최초의 헤드셋을 만든 사람들입니다. 새뮤얼 아칠레푸는 나이지리아에서 태어났습니다. 세인트루이스 워싱턴 대학교의 그의 연구진은 외과 의사를 위해 화면이 달린 고글을 만들었습니다. 염료가 암세포를 그 화면에서 빛나게 합니다. 이 고글은 2014년에 처음으로 수술에서 쓰였습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, at the piece Head-mounted display: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.head-mounted.text.3"
msgid "That was the last stop of the blue line."
msgstr "이것이 파란 선의 마지막 지점이었습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, after the last stop: the title of this part.
msgctxt "tour.black.end.title"
msgid "The end of the blue line"
msgstr "파란 선의 끝"

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, after the last stop: what the guide says, paragraph 1 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.end.text.1"
msgid "That is the end of the blue line. It began with a camera at a front door. It ends with goggles in an operating theatre."
msgstr "파란 선은 여기서 끝납니다. 어느 집 현관의 카메라에서 시작했습니다. 수술실의 고글에서 끝납니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, after the last stop: what the guide says, paragraph 2 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.end.text.2"
msgid "Twelve people are named at these ten stops. Each of them is on this line because a source describes that person as Black, as African American, or as born and raised in an African country. The People page gives the source for every name."
msgstr "이 열 지점에는 열두 사람의 이름이 있습니다. 저마다 이 선에 들어 있는 까닭은, 출처가 그 사람을 흑인, 아프리카계 미국인, 또는 아프리카의 나라에서 태어나 자란 사람이라고 적고 있기 때문입니다. 인물 페이지에 이름마다 그 출처가 있습니다."

#: tour-black
#. Guided tour, after the last stop: what the guide says, paragraph 3 of 3. It is read aloud, so keep the sentences short and write years as figures. The parts call back to each other, so use the same words for the same thing throughout.
msgctxt "tour.black.end.text.3"
msgid "The green line on the floor is another tour, about the women in the museum. Leave this tour, and the halls are yours to walk. Thank you for taking it."
msgstr "바닥의 초록 선은 박물관의 여성들을 다루는 또 다른 투어입니다. 이 투어를 그만두면 전시실을 자유롭게 걸을 수 있습니다. 투어에 함께해 주셔서 고맙습니다."

#: people
#. About Kenyon Taylor, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 1. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenyon-taylor.about.1"
msgid "His name stands on the patents of Ferranti-Packard for magnetically operated signs, one of them with Donald Winrow."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ferdinand Braun, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ferdinand-braun.about.1"
msgid "Born in Fulda, he studied at Marburg and Berlin and taught physics at Karlsruhe, Tübingen and Strasbourg."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ferdinand Braun, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ferdinand-braun.about.2"
msgid "In 1897 he built a cathode-ray oscillograph."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ferdinand Braun, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ferdinand-braun.about.3"
msgid "He shared the Nobel Prize in Physics of 1909 with Guglielmo Marconi, for work on wireless telegraphy."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ferdinand Braun, on that person's own page: paragraph 4 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ferdinand-braun.about.4"
msgid "He died in Brooklyn, held in the United States as a German citizen during the war."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Robert H. Anderson, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.robert-h-anderson.about.1"
msgid "A physicist, he joined Tektronix in 1959, in the research group led by Dick Ropiequet."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Robert H. Anderson, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.robert-h-anderson.about.2"
msgid "He set himself the task of a storage tube of simple build, and arrived at one with no separate storage target inside it."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Robert H. Anderson, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.robert-h-anderson.about.3"
msgid "The Type 564 oscilloscope of 1963 was built around it. He left Tektronix in 1969."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Donald Bitzer, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.donald-bitzer.about.1"
msgid "He took three degrees in electrical engineering at the University of Illinois, the last a doctorate in 1960."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Donald Bitzer, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.donald-bitzer.about.2"
msgid "In 1964, with Gene Slottow and the student Robert Willson, he made the plasma display panel for PLATO, the teaching network of the university."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Donald Bitzer, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.donald-bitzer.about.3"
msgid "The three received an Emmy Award for it in 2002."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gene Slottow, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gene-slottow.about.1"
msgid "He received his doctorate at the University of Illinois in 1964."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gene Slottow, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gene-slottow.about.2"
msgid "In the same year he, Donald Bitzer and Robert Willson made the plasma display panel there, and in 2002 the three received an Emmy Award for it."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Robert Willson, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.robert-willson.about.1"
msgid "A graduate student of Donald Bitzer and Gene Slottow at the University of Illinois, he took his doctorate there in 1966."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Robert Willson, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.robert-willson.about.2"
msgid "With them he made the plasma display panel in 1964, and shared the Emmy Award of 2002."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Nicholas Sheridon, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.nicholas-sheridon.about.1"
msgid "A physicist at the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center, he proposed replacing the monitor with something like paper, and soon had a crude working model."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Nicholas Sheridon, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.nicholas-sheridon.about.2"
msgid "Xerox took him off the work in 1977."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Nicholas Sheridon, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.nicholas-sheridon.about.3"
msgid "By 2001 he was head of research at Gyricon Media, a company Xerox had spun off."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Martin Schadt, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.martin-schadt.about.1"
msgid "A physicist, he was among the earliest hired at the research laboratories of Hoffmann-La Roche in Basel."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Martin Schadt, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.martin-schadt.about.2"
msgid "In the autumn of 1970 he and Wolfgang Helfrich studied twisted layers of liquid crystal there. They filed a patent that December and published in February 1971."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Martin Schadt, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.martin-schadt.about.3"
msgid "In 1972 he built a prototype of a display that used the effect."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Wolfgang Helfrich, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.wolfgang-helfrich.about.1"
msgid "A physicist at Hoffmann-La Roche in Basel, he worked out the twisted nematic effect with Martin Schadt in 1970."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Wolfgang Helfrich, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.wolfgang-helfrich.about.2"
msgid "He left the company soon afterwards for the Freie Universität Berlin."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Fritz Fischer, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.fritz-fischer.about.1"
msgid "Born in Signau, Switzerland, he worked at the institute of technical physics of ETH Zurich."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Fritz Fischer, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.fritz-fischer.about.2"
msgid "He applied for a patent on the Eidophor in November 1939. When he died his second prototype was not yet running."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Fritz Fischer, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.fritz-fischer.about.3"
msgid "His collaborators carried the work on, and the Historical Dictionary of Switzerland dates its full working order to 1959."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Joseph Ostoja-Kotkowski, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.joseph-ostoja-kotkowski.about.1"
msgid "Born in Golub, Poland, he lived and worked in South Australia."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Joseph Ostoja-Kotkowski, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.joseph-ostoja-kotkowski.about.2"
msgid "The History Trust of South Australia says that his Sound and Image of 1968, at the Adelaide Festival of Arts, may have been the first use of a laser in a theatre."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Joseph Ostoja-Kotkowski, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.joseph-ostoja-kotkowski.about.3"
msgid "He went on to stage works of laser light and sound in Canberra, Ballarat, Brisbane and Warsaw."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Joseph Plateau, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.joseph-plateau.about.1"
msgid "A Belgian, born in Brussels."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Joseph Plateau, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.joseph-plateau.about.2"
msgid "The Science Museum Group credits him with the first demonstration of the illusion of a moving picture, with the phenakistoscope of 1832."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gabriel Lippmann, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gabriel-lippmann.about.1"
msgid "He received the Nobel Prize in Physics of 1908 for a way of photographing colours by the interference of light."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gabriel Lippmann, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gabriel-lippmann.about.2"
msgid "In the same year he published his idea of integral photography in the Journal de Physique."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gino Valle, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gino-valle.about.1"
msgid "An architect and industrial designer from Udine, he graduated from the University Institute of Architecture in Venice in 1948 and held a Fulbright scholarship at the Harvard Graduate School of Design in 1951. He taught architecture in Venice from 1954 to 2001."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gino Valle, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gino-valle.about.2"
msgid "For Solari he designed the Cifra 5 clock of 1954 and 1955, which won the Compasso d'Oro in 1956. His alphanumeric boards for airports and stations brought the firm a second Compasso d'Oro in 1962."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gino Valle, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gino-valle.about.3"
msgid "His Cifra 3 clock of 1966, with lettering by Massimo Vignelli, entered the permanent collection of the Museum of Modern Art in New York in 1968."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Myer, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-myer.about.1"
msgid "He worked with Gino Valle on the Cifra 5 clock, which Solari made in the mid 1950s. The ADI Design Museum lists him among its four designers, with Valle, Nani Valle and Michele Provinciali."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Myer, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-myer.about.2"
msgid "According to Finestre sull'Arte, the case could not hold the fifty or so flaps the minutes would need. Myer proposed two numbered rollers whose combinations would show them."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paul Nipkow, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paul-nipkow.about.1"
msgid "Born in Lauenburg in Pomerania, he began studying natural sciences in Berlin in 1882, where Adolf Slaby and Hermann Helmholtz were among his teachers. Lack of money ended his studies."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paul Nipkow, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paul-nipkow.about.2"
msgid "His patent for an electric telescope took effect from 6 January 1884. The German Patent and Trade Mark Office says he probably never tried to build it, and that it could hardly have worked at the time."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paul Nipkow, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paul-nipkow.about.3"
msgid "He worked as an engineer of railway signals for Zimmermann & Buchloh near Berlin. After 1933 the Nazi government made propaganda of him, and the patent office records that the world's first regular television station was named after him."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Logie Baird, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-logie-baird.about.1"
msgid "Born in Helensburgh, he studied at the Glasgow and West of Scotland Technical College, but the war interrupted his course and he did not graduate. In 1915 he joined the Clyde Valley Electrical Power Company."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Logie Baird, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-logie-baird.about.2"
msgid "Working in Hastings, he transmitted the silhouette of a Maltese cross in 1924. In 1927 he sent a picture 438 miles from London to Glasgow by telephone line, and in 1928 to New York by short-wave radio."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Logie Baird, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-logie-baird.about.3"
msgid "The BBC tried his mechanical system against the electronic one of Marconi-EMI and had dropped his by 1937. In the 1940s he worked on colour and stereoscopic television."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniel Rozin, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniel-rozin.about.1"
msgid "He took a degree at the Bezalel Academy of Art and Design in Jerusalem and then spent about ten years as an industrial designer for a large company in Israel."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniel Rozin, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniel-rozin.about.2"
msgid "He came to the Interactive Telecommunications Program at New York University for a master's degree, stayed on after graduating, and teaches there as an arts professor in the Tisch School of the Arts."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniel Rozin, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniel-rozin.about.3"
msgid "His work has been shown at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London and the Israel Museum in Jerusalem. His awards include the Prix Ars Electronica and the Rothschild Prize."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniel Leithinger, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniel-leithinger.about.1"
msgid "He took his bachelor's and master's degrees at the Upper Austria University of Applied Sciences in Hagenberg in 2005 and 2007, and his doctorate at the MIT Media Lab in 2015. The inFORM paper of 2013 was written with Follmer, Ishii and two others."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniel Leithinger, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniel-leithinger.about.2"
msgid "From 2018 to 2023 he was an assistant professor of computer science at the University of Colorado Boulder, where he led the THING Lab at the ATLAS Institute. He then became a Design Tech Innovation Fellow at Cornell University."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniel Leithinger, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniel-leithinger.about.3"
msgid "In 2015 he cofounded Lumii, now Fathom Optics, to develop 3D displays that need no glasses."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Sean Follmer, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.sean-follmer.about.1"
msgid "He took a degree in engineering at Stanford University in 2009, then a master's in 2011 and a doctorate in 2015 at the MIT Media Lab."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Sean Follmer, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.sean-follmer.about.2"
msgid "He returned to Stanford, where he is an associate professor of mechanical engineering and directs the Shape Lab. His research includes haptics, shape-changing interfaces and interfaces for people who are blind or visually impaired."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Sean Follmer, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.sean-follmer.about.3"
msgid "He received a Sloan Research Fellowship in 2021 and an NSF CAREER Award in 2022."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hiroshi Ishii, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hiroshi-ishii.about.1"
msgid "He took degrees in electronic and computer engineering at Hokkaido University, ending with a doctorate in 1992. From 1988 to 1994 he led a research group at NTT Human Interface Laboratories, whose team built TeamWorkStation and ClearBoard."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hiroshi Ishii, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hiroshi-ishii.about.2"
msgid "He joined the MIT Media Lab in October 1995 and set up the Tangible Media Group to pursue an idea he called Tangible Bits, in which people handle digital information through physical objects."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hiroshi Ishii, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hiroshi-ishii.about.3"
msgid "ACM SIGCHI elected him to its CHI Academy in 2006 for creating tangible user interfaces as a field. He became an associate director of the Media Lab in May 2008."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Oleg Tretiakoff, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.oleg-tretiakoff.about.1"
msgid "With Andrée Tretiakoff, both of Sceaux in France, he filed a United States patent in 1975, granted in 1977, for a braille panel. Each dot was a rod that a thin piezoelectric strip pushed up or drew down as it bent."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Oleg Tretiakoff, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.oleg-tretiakoff.about.2"
msgid "The American Printing House for the Blind credits the pair with the first commercially available paperless braille machine, the Digicassette, introduced in Paris in 1976. It kept text on cassette tape and raised one line of braille at a time."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Oleg Tretiakoff, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.oleg-tretiakoff.about.3"
msgid "A later patent of theirs, granted in 1981, was assigned to Triformation Systems. The American Printing House says braille displays of the following decades largely refined the Tretiakoff design."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Andrée Tretiakoff, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.andree-tretiakoff.about.1"
msgid "She and Oleg Tretiakoff, both of Sceaux in France, are the named inventors on a United States patent filed in 1975 and granted in 1977. In it, six rods per braille cell were raised and lowered by bending piezoelectric strips."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Andrée Tretiakoff, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.andree-tretiakoff.about.2"
msgid "The American Printing House for the Blind credits the couple with the first commercially available paperless braille machine, the Digicassette, introduced in Paris in 1976. It stored text on cassette tape."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Andrée Tretiakoff, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.andree-tretiakoff.about.3"
msgid "Their second United States patent, granted in 1981, was assigned to Triformation Systems and also covered reading and recording braille data on magnetic tape."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Klaus Peter Schönherr, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.klaus-peter-schonherr.about.1"
msgid "He founded Schoenherr GmbH in 1974, according to a history of the Braillotron calculator published on facele.eu, which also says he patented in 1975 a braille element able to show and erase characters."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Klaus Peter Schönherr, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.klaus-peter-schonherr.about.2"
msgid "In 1977 Computerwoche reported a braille terminal from the firm, in use at the University of Tübingen computing centre since December 1976. It widened the braille cell to eight dots to cover a set of 96 characters."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Klaus Peter Schönherr, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.klaus-peter-schonherr.about.3"
msgid "He received the Louis Braille Prize in 1980. The American Printing House for the Blind says his company later became Handy Tech."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About James Bryce, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.james-bryce.about.1"
msgid "He joined the Computing-Tabulating-Recording Company in 1917, became its chief engineer in 1923, and in 1926 organised the IBM Patent Department. He held more than 500 patents in the United States and abroad."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About James Bryce, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.james-bryce.about.2"
msgid "His work included an automatic multiplying punch and the use of electron tubes for arithmetic. In 1936 the US Patent Office named him among ten living inventors at its centenary."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About James Bryce, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.james-bryce.about.3"
msgid "The American Printing House for the Blind records a braille display begun by Bryce at IBM. It read text from perforated paper tape and raised the characters on a moving plastic belt. Arnold Grunwald later took it up at Argonne National Laboratory."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Horst Hörtner, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.horst-hortner.about.1"
msgid "In 1990 he co-founded the media art group x-space in Graz. He joined Ars Electronica in 1995 as technical director to help set up the Ars Electronica Center, which opened in 1996."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Horst Hörtner, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.horst-hortner.about.2"
msgid "With Gerfried Stocker he founded the Futurelab, which he ran as managing director from 1996 to 2024 and has since served as senior director. He has also been chief technology officer of Ars Electronica since 2020."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Horst Hörtner, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.horst-hortner.about.3"
msgid "He holds patents on drone swarm flight. Ars Electronica names him as the inventor of the spaxel, the lit drone of its swarm, which flew at the voestalpine Klangwolke in Linz in 2012."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenjiro Takayanagi, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenjiro-takayanagi.about.1"
msgid "Born in what is now Hamamatsu, he trained at the teacher training school of the Tokyo Higher Technical School. In 1924 he became an assistant professor at the new Hamamatsu Higher Technical College and began his television research there."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenjiro Takayanagi, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenjiro-takayanagi.about.2"
msgid "The picture he sent on 25 December 1926 was the Japanese character イ. The IEEE records that in 1935 he made an Iconoscope and showed a television system that was electronic at both ends."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenjiro Takayanagi, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenjiro-takayanagi.about.3"
msgid "At NHK his group set up a television station in 1939. During the war he worked on radar and night vision equipment. Afterwards he joined the Victor Company of Japan, and his team's television set was put on sale by Sharp in 1953."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenjiro Takayanagi, on that person's own page: paragraph 4 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenjiro-takayanagi.about.4"
msgid "The IEEE dedicated a Milestone to his work at Hamamatsu in 2009. He received the Order of Culture in 1981."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Philo Farnsworth, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.philo-farnsworth.about.1"
msgid "Born in Beaver City, Utah, he sketched an electronic camera tube for his school science teacher, Justin Tolman, in 1922. He enrolled briefly at Brigham Young University and left for lack of money."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Philo Farnsworth, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.philo-farnsworth.about.2"
msgid "Backed by the fundraisers George Everson and Leslie Gorrell, he set up a laboratory on the second floor of 202 Green Street, San Francisco. On 7 September 1927 it sent the image of a line drawn on a slide."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Philo Farnsworth, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.philo-farnsworth.about.3"
msgid "RCA challenged his patent in 1932. His lawyers produced Tolman's copy of the 1922 sketch, the court found for him in 1935, and in 1939 RCA agreed to pay him royalties."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Philo Farnsworth, on that person's own page: paragraph 4 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.philo-farnsworth.about.4"
msgid "His company built receivers at Fort Wayne, Indiana, until ITT bought the plant in 1949. He stayed with ITT as a consultant until 1967 and later worked on nuclear fusion as a source of power."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elma Farnsworth, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elma-farnsworth.about.1"
msgid "In the San Francisco laboratory she kept the logbooks and spot welded the elements of the tubes."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elma Farnsworth, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elma-farnsworth.about.2"
msgid "After Philo Farnsworth died in 1971 she spent decades pressing for his place in the history of television. The Television Academy credits her efforts in part for his entry to the National Inventors Hall of Fame and for a postage stamp in his honour."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elma Farnsworth, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elma-farnsworth.about.3"
msgid "In 1990 she published Distant Vision, a book about her husband and his work. The Television Academy's Archive of American Television recorded a seven hour interview with her in 1996. She died in Bountiful, Utah, in 2006."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Marie Van Brittan Brown, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.marie-van-brittan-brown.about.1"
msgid "Born in Jamaica, Queens, she worked long hours as a nurse and often came home late at night. The Lemelson MIT Program says she wanted a way to see who was knocking at her door."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Marie Van Brittan Brown, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.marie-van-brittan-brown.about.2"
msgid "Besides the camera and monitors, the system had two way microphones for speaking to the caller, a remote control to unlock the door from a distance, and an emergency button that raised an alarm with police or security."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Marie Van Brittan Brown, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.marie-van-brittan-brown.about.3"
msgid "The New York Times reported on it, and she received an award from the National Scientists Committee. The Lemelson MIT Program counts 32 later patent applications that cite the patent."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Albert Brown, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.albert-brown.about.1"
msgid "He is named with Marie Van Brittan Brown as joint inventor on United States patent 3,482,037, filed on 1 August 1966 and granted on 2 December 1969. Both are listed as living in Jamaica, New York."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Albert Brown, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.albert-brown.about.2"
msgid "The patent describes a control unit with a microphone and loudspeaker, and a door fitted with a radio controlled lock. The Lemelson MIT Program says he helped his wife build the system."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mark Dean, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mark-dean.about.1"
msgid "He graduated summa cum laude from the University of Tennessee in 1979, then took a master's degree at Florida Atlantic University and a doctorate in electrical engineering at Stanford. He joined IBM at Boca Raton, Florida, in 1979."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mark Dean, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mark-dean.about.2"
msgid "He worked on the IBM PC AT, and the National Inventors Hall of Fame elected him in 1997 with Dennis Moeller for the ISA bus. IBM named him a Fellow in 1995. Later his team built an experimental processor that ran at one gigahertz."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mark Dean, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mark-dean.about.3"
msgid "He became director of IBM's Almaden Research Center and then IBM's chief technology officer for the Middle East and Africa. He left IBM in 2013 to join the University of Tennessee College of Engineering as John Fisher Distinguished Professor."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Guillermo González Camarena, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.guillermo-gonzalez-camarena.about.1"
msgid "Born in Guadalajara, he built a radio transmitter at the age of eight. He graduated in 1939 from the Escuela Superior de Ingeniería Mecánica y Eléctrica of the Instituto Politécnico Nacional in Mexico City."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Guillermo González Camarena, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.guillermo-gonzalez-camarena.about.2"
msgid "He called his colour method the Sistema Tricromático Secuencial de Campos. The NTSC system used in the United States became the standard instead, and in the 1960s he devised a simpler two colour system."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Guillermo González Camarena, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.guillermo-gonzalez-camarena.about.3"
msgid "The Mexico City station XHGC, Channel 5, carries his initials."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About A. Frankenstein, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.a-frankenstein.about.1"
msgid "Histoire de la télévision lists the application as German patent 172 376 of 20 August 1904, under the names W. von Jaworski and A. Frankenstein. It describes selenium cells, three colour filters and mirrors that split the picture into groups of points."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About A. Frankenstein, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.a-frankenstein.about.2"
msgid "The VDE, the German association of electrical engineers, places television proposals by Ré, Jaworsky and Frankenstein in 1903."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Werner von Jaworski, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.werner-von-jaworski.about.1"
msgid "Histoire de la télévision lists the application as German patent 172 376 of 20 August 1904, under the names W. von Jaworski and A. Frankenstein. It describes selenium cells, three colour filters and mirrors that split the picture into groups of points."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Werner von Jaworski, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.werner-von-jaworski.about.2"
msgid "The VDE, the German association of electrical engineers, places television proposals by Ré, Jaworsky and Frankenstein in 1903, spelling his name with a final y."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Peter Goldmark, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.peter-goldmark.about.1"
msgid "Born in Budapest, he studied at the universities of Berlin and Vienna and moved to the United States in 1933. In 1936 he joined CBS as chief engineer of its television department."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Peter Goldmark, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.peter-goldmark.about.2"
msgid "In 1940 he tested his colour system from the top of the Chrysler Building. The Federal Communications Commission approved it, but it never went into commercial use. It served in closed circuit work such as surgery, and sent pictures back from Apollo 15."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Peter Goldmark, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.peter-goldmark.about.3"
msgid "The National Inventors Hall of Fame credits him with the long playing record of 1948 and records 77 patents. He received the National Medal of Science in 1977 and entered the Hall of Fame in 2007."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Andrew Haeff, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.andrew-haeff.about.1"
msgid "Born in Moscow, he qualified as an engineer at the Russian Polytechnic Institute in Harbin in 1928 and took his master's and doctoral degrees at Caltech in 1929 and 1932. He worked at the RCA research laboratories from 1934 to 1941."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Andrew Haeff, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.andrew-haeff.about.2"
msgid "He then joined the Naval Research Laboratory as a consultant in electronics. He was granted a patent for the Memory Tube in August 1947, and a tube of his type was used in MIT's Whirlwind computer."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Andrew Haeff, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.andrew-haeff.about.3"
msgid "In 1950 he moved to Hughes Aircraft, where he set up its Electron Tube Laboratory, developed the Typotron for computer output, and became vice president and director of research. The IEEE History Center records him as the first recipient of the Harry Diamond Memorial Award."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About David Hagelbarger, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.david-hagelbarger.about.1"
msgid "He was a researcher at Bell Telephone Laboratories. A. Michael Noll's history of the laboratories prints a photograph of him with Claude Shannon taken in 1955."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About David Hagelbarger, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.david-hagelbarger.about.2"
msgid "A 1987 profile of Shannon in Omni says Hagelbarger invented a machine that tried to guess whether a player would choose heads or tails. Shannon built a simpler version that beat it."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tadashi Nakamura, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tadashi-nakamura.about.1"
msgid "Born in Ise City, he set up Ise Electronics Corporation there in September 1966, to make and sell display devices. The company dates the basic structure of the display to 1966 and a single digit tube to 1967."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tadashi Nakamura, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tadashi-nakamura.about.2"
msgid "The IEEE dedicated a Milestone to the vacuum fluorescent display on 4 November 2025, with a plaque at the company's main building in Taiki, Mie Prefecture. The company took the name Noritake Itron Corporation in 2002."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tadashi Sasaki, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tadashi-sasaki.about.1"
msgid "Born in Shimane Prefecture, he graduated in electrical engineering from Kyoto Imperial University in 1938. At Kawanishi Machine Works he researched and produced vacuum tubes, and he worked on radar during the Second World War."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tadashi Sasaki, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tadashi-sasaki.about.2"
msgid "He moved to Hayakawa Electric Industry in 1964, at the request of its management. The QT-8D microCompet of 1969, made with North American Rockwell, was built on large-scale integrated circuits. Calculators combining CMOS logic with liquid crystal displays reached mass production in 1973."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tadashi Sasaki, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tadashi-sasaki.about.3"
msgid "He rose to senior managing director and executive vice president of the company, and later served it as an adviser. In 1994 he founded the International Center for Materials Research. The IEEE made him an honorary member in 2003."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Lewis Latimer, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.lewis-latimer.about.1"
msgid "After service in the Navy during the Civil War, he worked at a Boston patent law firm and taught himself mechanical drawing until the partners made him their draftsman. In 1876 he drew the plans for Alexander Graham Bell's telephone."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Lewis Latimer, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.lewis-latimer.about.2"
msgid "In 1880 Hiram Maxim hired him as assistant manager and mechanical draftsman. The National Inventors Hall of Fame says that at the United States Electric Lighting Company he oversaw the setting up of lighting plants in the United States, Canada and England."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Lewis Latimer, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.lewis-latimer.about.3"
msgid "Thomas Edison then took him on as a patent investigator and expert witness. In 1890 he published a book describing the Edison system of incandescent lighting. He retired in 1922. The National Inventors Hall of Fame inducted him in 2006."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tsutae Shinoda, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tsutae-shinoda.about.1"
msgid "He entered the engineering faculty of Hiroshima University in 1968, where his graduation research on protective layers for plasma panels was supervised by Heiju Uchiike. He joined Fujitsu Laboratories in 1973 and worked on AC plasma panels."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tsutae Shinoda, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tsutae-shinoda.about.2"
msgid "After colour trials begun in 1979, he separated the electrodes that selected a cell from those that lit it, in a reflective surface discharge structure with three electrodes. A later stripe rib structure, with ribs on one substrate only, made 21-inch panels feasible."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tsutae Shinoda, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tsutae-shinoda.about.3"
msgid "His awards included the Karl Ferdinand Braun Award of the Society for Information Display in 2003 and honorary membership of the IEEE in 2007. The IEEE recognised the colour plasma display as a Milestone in May 2025."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Spindt, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-spindt.about.1"
msgid "Born in San Jose, California, he left architecture studies at Berkeley for the Air Force and flew as an all-weather fighter interceptor pilot. He then took a degree in electrical engineering at San Jose State."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Spindt, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-spindt.about.2"
msgid "He joined the Applied Physics Lab of Stanford Research Institute in 1959, hired by Kenneth Shoulders. In 1966 he worked out processes for making the gated field emitter array cathode, and published the work in the Journal of Applied Physics in 1968."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Spindt, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-spindt.about.3"
msgid "He co-founded the IEEE vacuum microelectronics conference in 1988. The Society for Information Display gave him its Jan Rajchman Prize in 1996. SRI named him a Fellow in 1992, and he retired in 2002 as director emeritus of its vacuum microelectronics program."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenneth Shoulders, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenneth-shoulders.about.1"
msgid "Born in Texas, he was on the research staff of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology from 1955 to 1958. There he studied microfabrication for field emission amplifiers and magnetic film memories, often working with Dudley A. Buck."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenneth Shoulders, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenneth-shoulders.about.2"
msgid "Stanford Research Institute hired him in 1958 as a senior research engineer. He worked on very small devices, field emission elements among them, and built the vacuum systems for evaporation and multi beam exposure used to develop the Spindt cathode array."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenneth Shoulders, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenneth-shoulders.about.3"
msgid "He hired Charles Spindt at the institute in 1959. After leaving it he studied charge clusters at a private laboratory in Bodega, California. His papers are held by the Science History Institute in Philadelphia."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Louis Heynick, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.louis-heynick.about.1"
msgid "Louis N. Heynick was named with Charles Spindt and Kenneth Shoulders on the United States patent for field emission cathode structures with a cellular array of emission sites. It was filed in February 1970 and issued in August 1973, assigned to Stanford Research Institute."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Louis Heynick, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.louis-heynick.about.2"
msgid "He held a master's degree. In March 1983, with Peter Polson, he wrote a review of the biological effects of radiofrequency radiation for the Air Force School of Aerospace Medicine, as an SRI International project."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Akintunde Akinwande, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.akintunde-akinwande.about.1"
msgid "He moved to the Microsystems Technology Laboratories at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in January 1995. His research there covered smart sensors and actuators, intelligent displays, large area electronics, field ionization devices, mass spectrometry and electric propulsion."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Akintunde Akinwande, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.akintunde-akinwande.about.2"
msgid "He received a National Science Foundation CAREER award in 1996 and was a visiting professor in the Cambridge University Engineering Department in 2002 and 2003. At MIT he held the Thomas and Gerd Perkins professorship of electrical engineering and computer science."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Howard Borden, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.howard-borden.about.1"
msgid "Howard C. Borden began work as a mechanical engineer in 1939. He studied mechanical engineering at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology and electronics at the U.S. Navy Radio Materiel School, and took a BA in economics at Stanford University in 1961."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Howard Borden, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.howard-borden.about.2"
msgid "After Navy service and more engineering work he bought an appliance store, where he introduced television. He then spent 11 years in electro optical and mechanical research and development before joining Hewlett-Packard in 1966."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Howard Borden, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.howard-borden.about.3"
msgid "In February 1969 the Hewlett-Packard Journal gave him as manager of solid-state displays at HP Associates, and as a member of the Society for Information Display."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gerald Pighini, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gerald-pighini.about.1"
msgid "Gerald P. Pighini took a BS in metallurgical engineering at Brooklyn Polytechnic in 1955 and worked in solid-state electronics from then on. Before Hewlett-Packard he was chief manufacturing engineer for a maker of semiconductor devices."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gerald Pighini, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gerald-pighini.about.2"
msgid "He joined Hewlett-Packard in 1965 as chief engineer of HP Associates and then became its production manager for semiconductors, before moving to solid-state displays. He co-wrote a paper on growing large gallium arsenide phosphide wafers for making LEDs."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Isamu Akasaki, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.isamu-akasaki.about.1"
msgid "He graduated from Kyoto University in 1952 and joined the research staff of Kobe Kogyo Corporation. From 1959 he taught in the electronics department of Nagoya University, which awarded him a doctorate in engineering in 1964."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Isamu Akasaki, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.isamu-akasaki.about.2"
msgid "From 1964 he headed a fundamental research laboratory at Matsushita Research Institute Tokyo, and later its semiconductor department. He returned to Nagoya University as professor of electronics in 1981, and moved to Meijo University in 1992."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Isamu Akasaki, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.isamu-akasaki.about.3"
msgid "At Nagoya in the 1980s he and Hiroshi Amano grew thin films of gallium nitride crystal of high quality by metal organic vapour phase epitaxy. From 1987 to 1990 he led a JST project to develop gallium nitride blue LEDs."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hiroshi Amano, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hiroshi-amano.about.1"
msgid "Born in Hamamatsu, he studied electrical engineering at Nagoya University and joined the nitride semiconductor group of Isamu Akasaki as an undergraduate in 1982. He took his degrees there in 1983, 1985 and 1989."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hiroshi Amano, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hiroshi-amano.about.2"
msgid "In 1985, under Akasaki, he grew smooth gallium nitride crystal on sapphire over a buffer layer laid down at low temperature. In 1989 he made a gallium nitride LED with a p-n junction, which the Lindau Nobel Laureate Meetings records as the first."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hiroshi Amano, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hiroshi-amano.about.3"
msgid "He was a research associate at Nagoya University from 1988 and became a professor at Meijo University in 2002. He returned to Nagoya University in 2010, and in 2015 became director of its Center for Integrated Research of Future Electronics."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Shuji Nakamura, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.shuji-nakamura.about.1"
msgid "Born in Ehime, he studied electrical engineering at the University of Tokushima, taking degrees in 1977 and 1979 and a doctorate in 1994. He joined Nichia Chemical Industries in 1979 and spent 1988 at the University of Florida as a visiting research associate."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Shuji Nakamura, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.shuji-nakamura.about.2"
msgid "He began work on blue LEDs of group III nitrides in 1989. In 1990 he built a growth system for gallium nitride that he named Two-Flow MOCVD, and in 1991 he made p-type gallium nitride films by thermal annealing."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Shuji Nakamura, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.shuji-nakamura.about.3"
msgid "Nichia began selling white LEDs based on his blue LEDs in 1996, and violet laser diodes for Blu-ray discs in 1999. From 2000 he was a professor of materials and of electrical and computer engineering at the University of California, Santa Barbara."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ching Wan Tang, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ching-wan-tang.about.1"
msgid "He took a science degree at the University of British Columbia in 1970 and a doctorate in chemistry at Cornell University in 1975."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ching Wan Tang, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ching-wan-tang.about.2"
msgid "He joined Eastman Kodak as a research scientist in 1975. With Steven Van Slyke he found a metal chelate that gave a lasting glow. The National Inventors Hall of Fame lists US patent 4,356,429 for the OLED and inducted him in 2018."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ching Wan Tang, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ching-wan-tang.about.3"
msgid "He retired from Kodak in 2006 as a Distinguished Fellow of the Kodak Research Laboratories and joined the chemical engineering department of the University of Rochester. He received the Wolf Prize in Chemistry in 2011 and the Kyoto Prize in 2019."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Steven Van Slyke, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.steven-van-slyke.about.1"
msgid "He studied chemistry at Ithaca College, graduating in 1978, and later took a master's degree in materials science at the Rochester Institute of Technology. He joined Eastman Kodak straight from college, in 1979."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Steven Van Slyke, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.steven-van-slyke.about.2"
msgid "For about fifteen years he worked on the basic science of electroluminescence. In the mid 1990s he moved to process engineering and helped license the technology for a car audio display that came out in 1996."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Steven Van Slyke, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.steven-van-slyke.about.3"
msgid "The National Inventors Hall of Fame records 40 patents of his on OLED materials and device design. It inducted him, with Tang, in 2018."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Steven Van Slyke, on that person's own page: paragraph 4 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.steven-van-slyke.about.4"
msgid "Kodak sold its OLED intellectual property in 2009. He then joined Kateeva, a start-up in Silicon Valley that worked on inkjet printing of OLED panels, as chief technology officer."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hongxing Jiang, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hongxing-jiang.about.1"
msgid "He took a degree at Fudan University in Shanghai and a doctorate in physics at Syracuse University in 1986. After two years as a research associate at Michigan State University he joined the physics faculty of Kansas State University in 1988."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hongxing Jiang, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hongxing-jiang.about.2"
msgid "At Kansas State he directed the Kansas Advanced Semiconductor Coordinated Laboratory from 1998 to 2008. In 2008 he and Lin moved their group and its microLED research to Texas Tech University, where both later held Horn professorships."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hongxing Jiang, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hongxing-jiang.about.3"
msgid "In the team's own account, they saw blue light from a gallium nitride microLED 12 micrometres across in August 1999. His Texas Tech CV dates a video-capable VGA microLED microdisplay, driven by a silicon CMOS chip, to 2009."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hongxing Jiang, on that person's own page: paragraph 4 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hongxing-jiang.about.4"
msgid "The International SSL Alliance gave him its Global SSL Award of Outstanding Achievements in 2021, citing the invention of the microLED."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Jingyu Lin, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.jingyu-lin.about.1"
msgid "She studied physics at the State University of New York College at Oneonta and at Syracuse University, where she took a doctorate in 1989. She was then a professor of physics at Kansas State University."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Jingyu Lin, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.jingyu-lin.about.2"
msgid "In 2008 she and Jiang moved their group and its microLED research to Texas Tech University. There she held a Horn professorship and the Linda F. Whitacre Chair in Electrical and Computer Engineering, and co-directed the Nano-Photonics Center."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Jingyu Lin, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.jingyu-lin.about.3"
msgid "Her research covered III-nitride semiconductors such as boron nitride, aluminium nitride and gallium nitride, along with radiation sensors and MOCVD crystal growth. She was elected a fellow of the National Academy of Inventors in 2018."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Vicki Colvin, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.vicki-colvin.about.1"
msgid "She took a degree in chemistry and physics at Stanford University in 1988 and a doctorate in chemistry at the University of California, Berkeley, in 1994. Louisiana State University says that doctoral work produced the original technology for using colloidal quantum dots in displays."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Vicki Colvin, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.vicki-colvin.about.2"
msgid "At Rice University she was a professor of chemistry and vice provost for research, and directed the National Science Foundation's Center for Biological and Environmental Nanotechnology."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Vicki Colvin, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.vicki-colvin.about.3"
msgid "She moved to Brown University, where she was provost from July 2015 to July 2016 and directed the Center for Biomedical Engineering. In 2024 Louisiana State University named her dean of its College of Engineering."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About M. C. Schlamp, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.m-c-schlamp.about.1"
msgid "Schlamp worked with Paul Alivisatos in the Department of Chemistry at the University of California, Berkeley, in the 1990s."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About M. C. Schlamp, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.m-c-schlamp.about.2"
msgid "In 1997 Schlamp, Xiaogang Peng and Alivisatos reported diodes in the Journal of Applied Physics that paired the polymer PPV with cadmium selenide nanocrystals in cadmium sulphide shells. They glowed red to green, from as little as 4 volts."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paul Alivisatos, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paul-alivisatos.about.1"
msgid "He studied chemistry at the University of Chicago, graduating in 1981, and took a doctorate at the University of California, Berkeley, in 1986. He taught at Berkeley from 1988 to 2021, latterly as executive vice chancellor and provost."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paul Alivisatos, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paul-alivisatos.about.2"
msgid "From 2009 to 2016 he directed the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory. His group worked on controlling the size and shape of nanocrystals, and he was a founder of two companies, Quantum Dot Corp and Nanosys."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paul Alivisatos, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paul-alivisatos.about.3"
msgid "The University of Chicago says his inventions are used in QLED television displays. He became its fourteenth president on 1 September 2021."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Aleksey Yekimov, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.aleksey-yekimov.about.1"
msgid "He graduated in physics from Leningrad State University in 1967 and took a doctorate at the Ioffe Physical-Technical Institute in 1974. He then did research at the Vavilov State Optical Institute in Leningrad."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Aleksey Yekimov, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.aleksey-yekimov.about.2"
msgid "With his doctoral student Alexei Onushchenko he tinted silicate glass with copper chloride. The temperature of the process set the size of the crystals, from a few to tens of nanometres. In 1981 he traced the effect to quantum confinement."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Aleksey Yekimov, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.aleksey-yekimov.about.3"
msgid "From 1999 he was chief scientist at Nanocrystals Technology Inc. in New York. He shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry of 2023 with Louis Brus and Moungi Bawendi, for the discovery and synthesis of quantum dots."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Louis Brus, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.louis-brus.about.1"
msgid "Born in Cleveland, he studied at Rice University on a Navy scholarship and took a doctorate in physical chemistry at Columbia University in 1969. He then served as a scientific staff officer at the Naval Research Laboratory in Washington."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Louis Brus, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.louis-brus.about.2"
msgid "He worked at Bell Laboratories from 1973 to 1996. There, in 1983, he made the nanocrystals later known as quantum dots."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Louis Brus, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.louis-brus.about.3"
msgid "In 1996 he joined the chemistry department at Columbia University. He shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry of 2023 with Aleksey Yekimov and Moungi Bawendi."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Moungi Bawendi, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.moungi-bawendi.about.1"
msgid "He took an undergraduate degree at Harvard University in 1982 and a doctorate at the University of Chicago in 1988. As a postdoctoral researcher he worked with Louis Brus at AT&T Bell Laboratories."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Moungi Bawendi, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.moungi-bawendi.about.2"
msgid "He joined the faculty of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1990 and became the Lester Wolfe Professor of Chemistry. His group grew dots of a chosen size by varying the conditions under which they crystallised."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Moungi Bawendi, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.moungi-bawendi.about.3"
msgid "He later found ways to control how efficiently the dots gave off light and to curb their habit of blinking on and off."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Xiaogang Peng, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.xiaogang-peng.about.1"
msgid "A chemist, he was a coauthor with M. C. Schlamp and Paul Alivisatos at the University of California, Berkeley, of a paper of 1997 on diodes made with nanocrystals that had a core and a shell."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Xiaogang Peng, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.xiaogang-peng.about.2"
msgid "At the University of Arkansas he held the Scharlau professorship of chemistry and biochemistry and worked on ways to grow nanocrystals of high quality in solution. In 2002 he and colleagues founded NN-Labs to sell nanocrystals."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Xiaogang Peng, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.xiaogang-peng.about.3"
msgid "He later moved to the Department of Chemistry at Zhejiang University. With Yizheng Jin and others he reported in Nature in 2014 a red diode with a thin insulating layer between the dots and the oxide layer that carried electrons."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Yizheng Jin, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.yizheng-jin.about.1"
msgid "A chemist, he was a tenured professor at Zhejiang University and directed the Institute of Advanced Materials Chemistry in its Department of Chemistry. His work covered light-emitting diodes made from solutions, with quantum dots or perovskites, and the oxide layers used in them."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Yizheng Jin, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.yizheng-jin.about.2"
msgid "In the Nature paper of 2014, which names him with Peng among its authors, a layer of PMMA about 6 nanometres thick between the dots and the oxide balanced the charge."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Yizheng Jin, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.yizheng-jin.about.3"
msgid "A profile for the SID conference ICDT lists the work among China's top ten scientific advances of 2014. The Ministry of Education named him a Chang Jiang Scholar Distinguished Professor in 2023."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tuomo Suntola, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tuomo-suntola.about.1"
msgid "Born in Tampere, he took a doctorate at Helsinki University of Technology. When the medical instrument company Instrumentarium asked him for product ideas, he proposed atomic layer deposition, and he filed a patent on it in 1974."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tuomo Suntola, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tuomo-suntola.about.2"
msgid "According to thisisFINLAND, the first panel made this way showed arriving and departing flights at Helsinki Airport, and commercial production followed in the 1980s. He later worked on the method at Microchemistry, a subsidiary of the energy company Neste."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Tuomo Suntola, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.tuomo-suntola.about.3"
msgid "Intel began to use the method in its chips in 2007. Suntola received the Millennium Technology Prize in 2018. He co-owned the ALD company Picosun until it was sold to Applied Materials in 2022."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dawon Kahng, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dawon-kahng.about.1"
msgid "He took a bachelor's degree at Seoul National University in 1955 and his master's and doctoral degrees at Ohio State University. He joined Bell Telephone Laboratories in 1959."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dawon Kahng, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dawon-kahng.about.2"
msgid "The transistor he built with Atalla used Atalla's layer of silicon oxide on the silicon surface. The National Inventors Hall of Fame calls the device, the MOSFET, the most widely used in the computer and electronics industries."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dawon Kahng, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dawon-kahng.about.3"
msgid "He retired from Bell Laboratories in 1988 and then founded the NEC Research Institute, for basic research in computing and communications. The Hall of Fame inducted him in 2009."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ibn al-Haytham, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ibn-al-haytham.about.1"
msgid "Known in Latin as Alhazen, he trained for an official career and was made a minister for Basra and its region. He later turned to science, reading Aristotle above all."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ibn al-Haytham, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ibn-al-haytham.about.2"
msgid "In Egypt the Fatimid caliph al-Hakim set him to regulate the flow of the Nile. Travelling upriver with his engineers, he judged the plan unworkable and said so, and was given an administrative post instead."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ibn al-Haytham, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ibn-al-haytham.about.3"
msgid "His Kitab al-Manazir, the Book of Optics, ran to seven books and rested on experiment. It reached Latin readers in 1270 as Opticae thesaurus Alhazeni. In Cairo he lived by writing, teaching and copying texts."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gemma Frisius, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gemma-frisius.about.1"
msgid "Born in Dokkum in Friesland, he entered the University of Louvain in 1526 as a poor student. He took a medical degree but stayed on for mathematics and astronomy, and became professor of medicine and mathematics there."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gemma Frisius, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gemma-frisius.about.2"
msgid "With the goldsmith Gaspard van der Heyden, and Gerardus Mercator as engraver, he made a terrestrial globe in 1536 and a celestial globe in 1537. In 1530 he proposed finding longitude by comparing local time with a carried clock."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gemma Frisius, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gemma-frisius.about.3"
msgid "MacTutor credits his tract of 1533 with the first proposal to fix places by triangulation. His De radio astronomico of 1545 described a cross staff about one and a half metres long. He died at Louvain."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniele Barbaro, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniele-barbaro.about.1"
msgid "Born in Venice, he studied philosophy, mathematics and optics at the University of Padua. He served the Venetian state as ambassador in London and became patriarch of Aquileia. In 1561 he represented Venice at the Council of Trent."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniele Barbaro, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniele-barbaro.about.2"
msgid "In 1556 he published an Italian translation of Vitruvius on architecture with a long commentary, and a Latin edition in 1567. He was a friend of the architect Andrea Palladio. His treatise on perspective appeared in Venice."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Johannes Kepler, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.johannes-kepler.about.1"
msgid "Born in Weil der Stadt near Stuttgart, he studied at Tübingen under the Copernican Michael Maestlin and taught at Graz from 1594."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Johannes Kepler, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.johannes-kepler.about.2"
msgid "He joined Tycho Brahe in Prague in 1600 and after Brahe died in 1601 succeeded him as mathematician to Emperor Rudolf II. His Astronomiae pars optica of 1604 used reflection and refraction to explain eclipses."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Johannes Kepler, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.johannes-kepler.about.3"
msgid "Astronomia nova of 1609 set out elliptical orbits and the law of equal areas, and Harmonice mundi of 1619 a third law. His Dioptrice of 1611 described a telescope with two convex lenses. He moved to Linz in 1612."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ibn Daniyal, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ibn-daniyal.about.1"
msgid "Doctor and poet as well as a man of the theatre, he came to Cairo after the Mongols took Baghdad in 1258. He wrote his plays out in advance as dialogue with verse and music, and directed the shows himself."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ibn Daniyal, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ibn-daniyal.about.2"
msgid "The plays are Tayf al khayal, Ajib wa gharib and al Mutayyam. The opening play seems to praise Sultan Baybars and his laws against vice, but UNIMA reads it as satire on the Mamluk regime."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ibn Daniyal, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ibn-daniyal.about.3"
msgid "The texts survive in four manuscripts, the oldest of 1425 in the Süleymaniye Library in Istanbul. Paul Kahle edited two of the plays between 1910 and 1912 and found Mamluk leather figures now in Berlin."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About François Dominique Séraphin, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.francois-dominique-seraphin.about.1"
msgid "At twenty three he opened a theatre behind an inn in the Lannion garden at Versailles, showing shadows with changing scenery. He was called to play for the court three times a week in carnival."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About François Dominique Séraphin, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.francois-dominique-seraphin.about.2"
msgid "In Paris he settled in the new galleries of the Palais Royal. His bill mixed comic pieces by Dorvigny and Guillemain with fairy tales such as Cendrillon. Made to swear loyalty to the Republic, he staged La Démonseigneurisation in 1790."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About François Dominique Séraphin, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.francois-dominique-seraphin.about.3"
msgid "His descendants kept the Séraphin shows going until 1870, moving the theatre to the Passage Jouffroy in 1859."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Henri Rivière, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.henri-riviere.about.1"
msgid "Born in Paris, he went to the Collège Rollin, taught himself drawing by copying illustrations by Doré and Vierge, and spent a short time in the studio of Émile Bin."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Henri Rivière, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.henri-riviere.about.2"
msgid "He frequented the Chat Noir from about eighteen and helped start its journal. In 1886 Rodolphe Salis gave him artistic and technical charge of the cabaret's shadow theatre."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Henri Rivière, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.henri-riviere.about.3"
msgid "In the 1890s he made woodcuts after Japanese models, and in 1902 published lithographs, Thirty Six Views of the Eiffel Tower, as a tribute to Hokusai. He collected about 800 Japanese objects and gave up printmaking for watercolour in 1916."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kara Walker, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kara-walker.about.1"
msgid "From Stockton, California, she took a BFA at the Atlanta College of Art and an MFA at the Rhode Island School of Design. Her cut paper wall installation Gone was shown at the Drawing Center in New York in 1994."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kara Walker, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kara-walker.about.2"
msgid "She received a MacArthur Fellowship in 1997 and represented the United States at the Bienal de São Paulo in 2002. Testimony of 2004 is a short silent film on 16mm, made with shadow puppets."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kara Walker, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kara-walker.about.3"
msgid "The Museum of Modern Art holds puppets from Testimony, of cut black paper with pencil, tape and metal fasteners. In 2014 she showed A Subtlety, a sculpture in a former Domino Sugar works in Brooklyn."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About James Fergason, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.james-fergason.about.1"
msgid "Born in Wakenda, Missouri, he took a degree in physics at the University of Missouri in 1956. The Lemelson-MIT Program says he organised the first American research team on liquid crystals, at Westinghouse Research Labs from 1957."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About James Fergason, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.james-fergason.about.2"
msgid "His patent of 1963 used cholesteric liquid crystals to sense temperature. In the 1960s he was associate director of the Liquid Crystal Institute at Kent State University. In 1969 he found the twisted nematic field effect."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About James Fergason, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.james-fergason.about.3"
msgid "Around 1970 he founded ILIXCO to make displays. He held more than 125 United States patents, entered the National Inventors Hall of Fame in 1998 and received the Lemelson-MIT Prize in 2006."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About George Heilmeier, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.george-heilmeier.about.1"
msgid "He studied electrical engineering at the University of Pennsylvania and took a PhD in electronics at Princeton in 1962. He joined RCA Laboratories in 1958 and rose to direct solid state device research."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About George Heilmeier, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.george-heilmeier.about.2"
msgid "In 1964 he found that a voltage changed the colour of a nematic liquid crystal doped with dye. RCA showed prototype liquid crystal displays in 1968."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About George Heilmeier, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.george-heilmeier.about.3"
msgid "He became a White House Fellow in 1970 and director of DARPA in 1974, then worked at Texas Instruments and led Bellcore from 1991. He received the Kyoto Prize in Advanced Technology in 2005."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Sivaramakrishna Chandrasekhar, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.sivaramakrishna-chandrasekhar.about.1"
msgid "After postgraduate study at the University of Cambridge he joined the University of Mysore with the task of building a school of physics. After a further spell in Britain he moved to the Raman Research Institute in Bangalore in 1971."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Sivaramakrishna Chandrasekhar, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.sivaramakrishna-chandrasekhar.about.2"
msgid "There he set up and led a liquid crystal laboratory. The 1977 paper in Pramana reported compounds called benzene hexa n alkanoates, whose disc molecules stacked into columns set in a hexagonal array."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About B. K. Sadashiva, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.b-k-sadashiva.about.1"
msgid "A chemist, he took a master's degree in organic chemistry at Bangalore University and a PhD under S. Chandrasekhar at the Raman Research Institute, where he spent his whole career and retired as a senior professor in 2008."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About B. K. Sadashiva, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.b-k-sadashiva.about.2"
msgid "K. A. Suresh recalls that Sadashiva, then a doctoral student, prepared the pure samples for the disc work after earlier ones proved impure. He later worked on metallomesogens and chiral layered liquid crystals."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About B. K. Sadashiva, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.b-k-sadashiva.about.3"
msgid "He wrote more than 130 papers, received the G. W. Gray Medal of the British Liquid Crystal Society and was a Fellow of the National Academy of Sciences, India. He died in 2020."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About K. A. Suresh, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.k-a-suresh.about.1"
msgid "Kattera A. Suresh took his doctorate at the Raman Research Institute in Bangalore in 1979."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About K. A. Suresh, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.k-a-suresh.about.2"
msgid "Set by Sivaramakrishna Chandrasekhar to study the new compounds, he examined them by X-ray diffraction and proposed a model of discs stacked in columns, which became accepted as the hexagonal columnar structure."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About K. A. Suresh, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.k-a-suresh.about.3"
msgid "He held a Juliet Curie Fellowship at the Collège de France in 1983, then worked for Dowell Schlumberger in France and at Carnegie Mellon University before returning to the institute in 1989."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About K. A. Suresh, on that person's own page: paragraph 4 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.k-a-suresh.about.4"
msgid "He became a senior professor there in 2004 and from 2010 was Scientist of Eminence at the Centre for Nano and Soft Matter Sciences in Bangalore."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mohamed Atalla, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mohamed-atalla.about.1"
msgid "He took a master's degree and a doctorate in mechanical engineering at Purdue University, in 1947 and 1949, and then joined Bell Laboratories."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mohamed Atalla, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mohamed-atalla.about.2"
msgid "There he studied the surface of silicon and grew a layer of silicon dioxide on the wafer to overcome the surface states that blocked current. He proposed a metal, oxide and silicon transistor and gave the work to Dawon Kahng."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mohamed Atalla, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mohamed-atalla.about.3"
msgid "In 1962 he co-founded Hewlett-Packard Associates, and in 1972 he founded the Atalla Corporation, whose Atalla Box encrypted PIN and cash machine messages."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mohamed Atalla, on that person's own page: paragraph 4 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mohamed-atalla.about.4"
msgid "The National Inventors Hall of Fame inducted him in 2009."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Larry Hornbeck, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.larry-hornbeck.about.1"
msgid "Born in St. Louis, he joined Texas Instruments in 1973 and took a doctorate in physics at Case Western Reserve University in 1974."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Larry Hornbeck, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.larry-hornbeck.about.2"
msgid "At the company's Central Research Laboratories he began in 1977 on analogue deformable mirrors, and spent about a decade on them before turning to hinged digital ones. He came to hold 34 United States patents."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Larry Hornbeck, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.larry-hornbeck.about.3"
msgid "He received an Engineering Emmy in 1998 and an Academy Award of Merit in 2015 for the cinema projectors built on the chip. He joined the faculty of the University of Texas at Dallas in 2017."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elsa Garmire, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elsa-garmire.about.1"
msgid "Born in Buffalo, New York, she took degrees in physics at Harvard and at MIT, where she worked with Charles Townes on nonlinear effects of strong laser beams."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elsa Garmire, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elsa-garmire.about.2"
msgid "As a senior research fellow at Caltech from 1966 to 1973 she joined Experiments in Art and Technology and designed the large Mylar mirror of the Pepsi pavilion at Expo 70 in Osaka."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elsa Garmire, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elsa-garmire.about.3"
msgid "She was president of Laser Images, the company she founded with Ivan Dryer, until she handed it to him in 1973. She then taught at the University of Southern California and from 1995 at Dartmouth College."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ivan Dryer, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ivan-dryer.about.1"
msgid "He worked as a guide at Griffith Observatory from 1956, on and off until 1968, and as a filmmaker, including as documentary editor on the feature film Executive Action."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ivan Dryer, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ivan-dryer.about.2"
msgid "After seeing Elsa Garmire show her laser work at a conference in 1970, he began on live laser shows with the filmmaker Dale Pelton. Their company was incorporated in February 1971."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ivan Dryer, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ivan-dryer.about.3"
msgid "Laserium ran at Griffith until January 2002. His company took shows to 46 planetariums and other venues. According to Live Design, the International Laser Display Association gave him its first Career Achievement Award in 1989."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Leota Toombs, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.leota-toombs.about.1"
msgid "She began at the Walt Disney Studios in 1940 in the Ink and Paint department and moved to animation. She returned in 1962 to join WED Enterprises."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Leota Toombs, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.leota-toombs.about.2"
msgid "She worked on attractions for the 1964 to 1965 New York World's Fair, then on Pirates of the Caribbean and the Haunted Mansion, where she also voiced the bride known as Little Leota."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Leota Toombs, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.leota-toombs.about.3"
msgid "In 1971 she moved to Walt Disney World to set up the team that kept its shows running, and from 1979 she trained figure finishers in California."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Michael Naimark, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.michael-naimark.about.1"
msgid "He made Displacements three times between 1980 and 1984, the third at the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art. A 16mm camera on a slow turntable filmed two performers, who looked ghostly when projected because they were not painted."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Michael Naimark, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.michael-naimark.about.2"
msgid "He was on the original design team of the MIT Media Lab in 1980 and a founding member of the Atari Research Lab, the Apple Multimedia Lab and Lucasfilm Interactive. He joined Interval Research in 1992."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Michael Naimark, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.michael-naimark.about.3"
msgid "His work is in the collections of the Exploratorium, the American Museum of the Moving Image and ZKM Karlsruhe."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ramesh Raskar, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ramesh-raskar.about.1"
msgid "He studied engineering in Pune and took his doctorate at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. He then joined Mitsubishi Electric Research Laboratories, where his work led to 40 patents."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ramesh Raskar, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ramesh-raskar.about.2"
msgid "He wrote the shader lamps paper while at Mitsubishi. It covered a plain object with projected images from several projectors so that it took on its own or another appearance."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ramesh Raskar, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ramesh-raskar.about.3"
msgid "In 2008 he founded the Camera Culture group at the MIT Media Lab. He received the Lemelson-MIT Prize in 2016 for femto-photography, which can show objects around a corner."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Greg Welch, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.greg-welch.about.1"
msgid "Before academic work he was at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory and the Defense Systems Division of Northrop Grumman. He was a research professor at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, which the shader lamps paper gives as his affiliation."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Greg Welch, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.greg-welch.about.2"
msgid "He moved to the University of Central Florida, where he became a Pegasus Professor, holder of the AdventHealth Endowed Chair in Healthcare Simulation and co-director of the Synthetic Reality Lab."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kok-Lim Low, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kok-lim-low.about.1"
msgid "He was a graduate student in computer science at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill when the shader lamps paper appeared, and took his doctorate there in 2006."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kok-Lim Low, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kok-lim-low.about.2"
msgid "He joined the School of Computing of the National University of Singapore, where he became a senior lecturer. His work there covered computer graphics, real-time rendering and the automated design of papercraft."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Deepak Bandyopadhyay, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.deepak-bandyopadhyay.about.1"
msgid "He was at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill when the shader lamps paper appeared in 2001."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Deepak Bandyopadhyay, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.deepak-bandyopadhyay.about.2"
msgid "He took his doctorate there in 2006 under Jack Snoeyink, with a thesis on a geometric framework for nearest neighbour analysis of protein structure and function."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Lanny Smoot, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.lanny-smoot.about.1"
msgid "Born in New York City, he took degrees in electrical engineering at Columbia University. From 1978 to 1998 he was at Bell Laboratories and Bellcore, with patents in fibre optics, video on demand and video conferencing."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Lanny Smoot, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.lanny-smoot.about.2"
msgid "He joined Disney in 1998 and worked as a research fellow and head of Imagineering research and development. His work included an extendable lightsaber and the HoloTile floor for shared virtual reality."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Lanny Smoot, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.lanny-smoot.about.3"
msgid "The National Inventors Hall of Fame inducted him in 2024."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Barrett Comiskey, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.barrett-comiskey.about.1"
msgid "He studied mathematics at MIT and worked in the Media Lab, where in 1995 Joseph Jacobson set him the task of a book whose pages could change at the press of a button."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Barrett Comiskey, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.barrett-comiskey.about.2"
msgid "With J. D. Albert he showed on 23 January 1997 that an electric field could move particles inside a microcapsule. He co-founded E Ink in 1997 and spent eight years on the ink in Cambridge, Massachusetts and Shanghai."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Barrett Comiskey, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.barrett-comiskey.about.3"
msgid "He later took an MBA at Stanford and has worked in Asia since 2004. The National Inventors Hall of Fame inducted him in 2016."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. D. Albert, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-d-albert.about.1"
msgid "Jonathan Albert was a mechanical engineering student at MIT when Joseph Jacobson recruited him to his laboratory. With Barrett Comiskey he worked on Jacobson's idea of a book whose pages could be changed electronically, and they had a working prototype in January 1997."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. D. Albert, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-d-albert.about.2"
msgid "Later in 1997 he co-founded E Ink with Comiskey, Jacobson, Russ Wilcox and Jerome Rubin, and he developed the methods used to make electronic ink displays in volume. The National Inventors Hall of Fame inducted him in 2016."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. D. Albert, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-d-albert.about.3"
msgid "He became director of engineering at Bresslergroup, a product design firm, and taught product design, engineering and entrepreneurship in the Integrated Product Design programme at the University of Pennsylvania."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Joseph Jacobson, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.joseph-jacobson.about.1"
msgid "He took a bachelor's degree at Brown University and a doctorate in physics at MIT, and was then a postdoctoral fellow in physics at Stanford University."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Joseph Jacobson, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.joseph-jacobson.about.2"
msgid "In the mid-1990s he proposed a display of black and white particles held in tiny spheres. In 1997 he launched E Ink with his students Barrett Comiskey and J. D. Albert, and the Media Lab lists him as a technical founder of the company."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Joseph Jacobson, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.joseph-jacobson.about.3"
msgid "As a professor at the MIT Media Lab he went on to head its Molecular Machines group, which works on synthetic biology. The National Inventors Hall of Fame inducted him in 2016."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Rogers, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-rogers.about.1"
msgid "He studied chemistry and physics at the University of Texas at Austin, graduating in 1989, and took a doctorate in physical chemistry at MIT in 1995. He was then a Junior Fellow of the Harvard Society of Fellows until 1997."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Rogers, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-rogers.about.2"
msgid "He joined Bell Laboratories in 1997 as a member of technical staff in condensed matter physics research, and directed that department from late 2000 to 2002. He was the lead author of the paper of 2001 in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Rogers, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-rogers.about.3"
msgid "He spent thirteen years on the faculty of the University of Illinois, then moved to Northwestern University in 2016 as professor of materials science and engineering and director of the Querrey Simpson Institute for Bioelectronics. He received a MacArthur Fellowship in 2009."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Zhenan Bao, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.zhenan-bao.about.1"
msgid "She took her doctorate in chemistry at the University of Chicago in 1995 and joined Bell Labs, then part of Lucent Technologies. She became a Distinguished Member of Technical Staff there in 2001, and was second author of that year's paper on the bendable display."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Zhenan Bao, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.zhenan-bao.about.2"
msgid "She moved to Stanford University in 2004, where she became K.K. Lee Professor in Chemical Engineering, chaired the department from 2018 to 2022, and founded the Stanford Wearable Electronics Initiative. Her group works on electronics modelled on skin."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Zhenan Bao, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.zhenan-bao.about.3"
msgid "She co-founded C3 Nano, later acquired by DuPont, and PyrAmes. She is a member of the National Academy of Sciences and the National Academy of Engineering."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Deng-Ke Yang, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.deng-ke-yang.about.1"
msgid "He took a degree in physics at Tsinghua University in 1984 and a doctorate in physics at the University of Hawaii in 1989, and joined Kent State University the same year."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Deng-Ke Yang, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.deng-ke-yang.about.2"
msgid "Kent State gave him its Excellence Award in Invention in 2000 for the bistable reflective cholesteric display. The Society for Information Display recognised his work on polymer stabilised cholesteric devices in 2005 and made him a Fellow in 2007."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Deng-Ke Yang, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.deng-ke-yang.about.3"
msgid "With S. T. Wu he wrote Reflective Liquid Crystal Displays of 2001 and Fundamentals of Liquid Crystal Devices of 2006. The Society gave him its Slottow-Owaki Prize in 2017 for education and training in liquid crystal displays."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. William Doane, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-william-doane.about.1"
msgid "Bill Doane took a doctorate in physics at the University of Missouri-Columbia in 1965 and joined the Kent State University faculty that year."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. William Doane, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-william-doane.about.2"
msgid "He became director of the Liquid Crystal Institute in 1983, and in 1991 was the founding director of the ALCOM Center for liquid crystalline optical materials. In 1995 and 1996 he published on reflective cholesteric displays in the SID Digest and Information Display."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. William Doane, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-william-doane.about.3"
msgid "He co-founded Kent Displays, Inc., where he served as vice president for research and development and chief science officer. He retired from the university in 1996 and was a Fellow of the American Physical Society."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gerardo Beni, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gerardo-beni.about.1"
msgid "He took a laurea in physics at the University of Florence in 1970 and a doctorate in physics at the University of California, Los Angeles, in 1974."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gerardo Beni, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gerardo-beni.about.2"
msgid "At Bell Telephone Laboratories he and Susan Hackwood filed a patent in June 1981 on devices that change the surface tension between two fluids with a voltage. Granted in November 1983, it noted that fluids of different colours give a colour change."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gerardo Beni, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gerardo-beni.about.3"
msgid "He co-founded the Journal of Robotic Systems in 1982, became a professor of electrical and computer engineering at the University of California, Riverside, and later wrote on swarm intelligence and swarm robotics."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Susan Hackwood, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.susan-hackwood.about.1"
msgid "She took a doctorate in solid state ionics and became head of device robotics technology research at AT&T Bell Laboratories. Her patent with Gerardo Beni, filed in June 1981, used a voltage to change the surface tension between two fluids."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Susan Hackwood, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.susan-hackwood.about.2"
msgid "In 1984 she joined the University of California, Santa Barbara, as a professor of electrical and computer engineering, and founded the NSF Engineering Research Center for Robotic Systems in Microelectronics there."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Susan Hackwood, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.susan-hackwood.about.3"
msgid "She became founding dean of the Bourns College of Engineering at the University of California, Riverside, and later executive director of the California Council on Science and Technology. She is a Fellow of the IEEE and the AAAS."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Robert Hayes, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.robert-hayes.about.1"
msgid "A physicist at Philips Research in Eindhoven, he wrote with Johan Feenstra the paper Video-speed electronic paper based on electrowetting, published in Nature in 2003."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Robert Hayes, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.robert-hayes.about.2"
msgid "In each pixel a voltage made water push aside a coloured oil film about 10 microns thick, uncovering a white surface beneath. Physics World reported that a pixel switched in less than 10 milliseconds."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Robert Hayes, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.robert-hayes.about.3"
msgid "The paper said its colour design was four times brighter than reflective liquid crystal displays, with reflectivity and contrast close to those of paper."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Johan Feenstra, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.johan-feenstra.about.1"
msgid "He took a doctorate in solid state physics at the Rijksuniversiteit Groningen in 1997. At Philips Research in the Netherlands he wrote the Nature paper of 2003 on video-speed electrowetting displays with Robert Hayes."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Johan Feenstra, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.johan-feenstra.about.2"
msgid "He co-founded Liquavista, spun out of Philips in 2006, and served as its chief executive and chief technology officer among other roles. Samsung Electronics bought the company in 2011, and it was later sold to Amazon."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Johan Feenstra, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.johan-feenstra.about.3"
msgid "He joined SMART Photonics, a maker of photonic chips, as chief executive in 2019."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mark Miles, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mark-miles.about.1"
msgid "He took a degree in electrical engineering at MIT and spent more than twelve years on development work for clients including ARPA, the NSF and the Air Force Office of Scientific Research. His firm Etalon worked on MEMS devices and thin film processes."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mark Miles, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mark-miles.about.2"
msgid "He co-founded Iridigm Display Corporation in 1997 and, as chief technology officer, directed its technology development for more than five years."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mark Miles, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mark-miles.about.3"
msgid "After the sale he advised Qualcomm MEMS Technologies, the subsidiary formed to sell displays built on the modulator, and ran Mark Miles Consulting in San Francisco. By 2006 he held eleven patents."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About S. K. Deb, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.s-k-deb.about.1"
msgid "Satyen Deb studied at Dacca University, graduating in 1953 and 1955, and took a doctorate in solid state physics at the Cavendish Laboratory of Cambridge University."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About S. K. Deb, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.s-k-deb.about.2"
msgid "He was a corporate research fellow at American Cyanamid in Stamford, Connecticut. His NREL biography says that he discovered the electrochromic phenomenon in transition metal oxides."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About S. K. Deb, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.s-k-deb.about.3"
msgid "He joined what is now the National Renewable Energy Laboratory in 1978, managed its in-house photovoltaic research and directed its Center for Basic Sciences. He was a Fellow of the American Physical Society."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paula Hammond, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paula-hammond.about.1"
msgid "She studied chemical engineering at MIT, graduating in 1984, took a master's degree at Georgia Tech in 1988 and returned to MIT for her doctorate in 1993. She then held an NSF postdoctoral fellowship in chemistry at Harvard."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paula Hammond, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paula-hammond.about.2"
msgid "On the MIT faculty she co-founded the Institute for Soldier Nanotechnology in 2002. Her group's work has included polymers for solar cells, nanoparticles that carry chemotherapy drugs and a spray coating that helps blood clot."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Paula Hammond, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.paula-hammond.about.3"
msgid "She was appointed head of the Department of Chemical Engineering in 2015 and later became Vice Provost for Faculty and an Institute Professor. The Science History Institute gave her its Othmer Gold Medal in 2024."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dean DeLongchamp, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dean-delongchamp.about.1"
msgid "A chemical engineer, he took his doctorate at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 2003, with Paula Hammond as adviser."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dean DeLongchamp, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dean-delongchamp.about.2"
msgid "His thesis also built polymer electrolyte films layer by layer that conducted ions well and held together mechanically, and it used nanocrystals of the Prussian blue family as electrochromic layers."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dean DeLongchamp, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dean-delongchamp.about.3"
msgid "He moved to the National Institute of Standards and Technology as a postdoctoral fellow in 2003. He went on to lead its Polymer Processing Group, and specialised in soft X-ray spectroscopy and scattering. He received a Presidential Early Career Award in 2009."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Henry Dircks, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.henry-dircks.about.1"
msgid "Born in Liverpool, he was apprenticed to a merchant house and then worked as a practical engineer on railways, canals and mines before setting up as a consulting engineer. He took out several patents between 1840 and 1857."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Henry Dircks, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.henry-dircks.about.2"
msgid "Grace's Guide dates his design of the ghost illusion to 1858. He built models and showed them to theatre owners, who declined because their stages would have needed rebuilding."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Henry Dircks, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.henry-dircks.about.3"
msgid "After Pepper adapted one of his models, the patent of 1863 was taken out in both their names, Pepper says at his own request. Dircks objected that the show bore only Pepper's name, and in 1863 published a book defending his claim."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Henry Dircks, on that person's own page: paragraph 4 of 4. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.henry-dircks.about.4"
msgid "He also wrote on perpetual motion and on the second Marquis of Worcester. He died in Brighton."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Henry Pepper, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-henry-pepper.about.1"
msgid "A chemist and lecturer on popular science, he joined the staff of the Royal Polytechnic Institution in 1848 and was its director within ten years. He also wrote books, among them The Playbook of Metals."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Henry Pepper, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-henry-pepper.about.2"
msgid "Around 1862 he took one of Henry Dircks's models and adapted it, hiding the second actor in the orchestra pit. By his own account he gave a private showing just before Christmas 1862, and took the patent the next day."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Henry Pepper, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-henry-pepper.about.3"
msgid "Under an agreement with Dircks, Pepper kept the profits, Dircks kept the credit, and the patent was shared. Pepper gave only his own name when presenting the show, and Dircks protested."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Valerie Thomas, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.valerie-thomas.about.1"
msgid "At NASA she rose to manage the development of image processing systems for Landsat satellite data."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Valerie Thomas, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.valerie-thomas.about.2"
msgid "The idea came from an exhibit she saw in 1976, where a concave mirror made a light bulb appear to glow in an empty socket. She filed her patent in December 1978. It used parabolic mirrors at both ends."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Valerie Thomas, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.valerie-thomas.about.3"
msgid "She later worked on the Space Physics Analysis Network, as its project manager, and was associate chief of the Space Science Data Operations Office when she retired from NASA in 1995."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Karri Palovuori, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.karri-palovuori.about.1"
msgid "A professor at Tampere University of Technology, he worked with Ismo Rakkolainen on the fog screen. The two had described a walk-through virtual environment at the IEEE Virtual Reality conference in Orlando in 2002."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Karri Palovuori, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.karri-palovuori.about.2"
msgid "His later work at Tampere University was in electronics and embedded systems, including full custom VLSI subsystems. His profile there lists about 80 publications and more than ten patents."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ismo Rakkolainen, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ismo-rakkolainen.about.1"
msgid "A senior researcher in the Technology Signal Processing Laboratory at Tampere University of Technology, he built early prototypes of the screen with plastic drinking straws that pushed air through small nozzles."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ismo Rakkolainen, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ismo-rakkolainen.about.2"
msgid "The fog screen was shown at the Turku Science Exhibition in autumn 2002 and at SIGGRAPH Emerging Technologies in San Diego in 2003. FogScreen Inc was founded in 2003 to make and sell it."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ismo Rakkolainen, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ismo-rakkolainen.about.3"
msgid "He stayed at Tampere University as a university research fellow and adjunct professor in the TAUCHI Research Center, working on user interfaces, virtual reality and new displays."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Max Hirsch, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.max-hirsch.about.1"
msgid "He filed the patent in January 1958, in his own name with no company behind it. A cathode-ray tube and its lenses and mirrors turned together with a rear-projection screen, spun by a motor at more than twenty turns a second."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Max Hirsch, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.max-hirsch.about.2"
msgid "A second patent, filed in November 1958 and granted in April 1961, let the tube stay still while circuits turned the picture on its face in step with the screen."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gregg Favalora, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gregg-favalora.about.1"
msgid "He built his display as a senior project at Yale University in 1996, with laser beams reflected onto a fast rotating screen. He took it further as an electrical engineering doctoral student at Harvard, on leave by 1998."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gregg Favalora, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gregg-favalora.about.2"
msgid "He started Actuality Systems with two Harvard students and won the MIT $50K Entrepreneurship Competition in 1997. MIT Technology Review named him among its innovators under 35 in 1999."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Gregg Favalora, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.gregg-favalora.about.3"
msgid "In 2009 he joined Optics for Hire, an optical design consultancy in Arlington, Massachusetts. By 2011 he was named on nine United States patents."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elizabeth Downing, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elizabeth-downing.about.1"
msgid "Before Stanford she worked as an engineer on laser equipment at the FMC Corporation Technology Center in Santa Clara, California."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elizabeth Downing, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elizabeth-downing.about.2"
msgid "As a graduate student she spent eight years on the display, with borrowed laboratory space and donated lasers. The work had a United States Navy grant of 350,000 dollars. The prototype cube was a stack of three doped glass layers, one for each primary colour."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Elizabeth Downing, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.elizabeth-downing.about.3"
msgid "She set up 3D Technology Laboratories in Mountain View, California, to sell the patented device, and by 1997 was planning a larger cube of many thin layers."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Lambertus Hesselink, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.lambertus-hesselink.about.1"
msgid "He studied applied physics and mechanics at Twente University and took his doctorate at the California Institute of Technology in 1977. At Stanford University he became professor of electrical engineering, and supervised Downing's display research there."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Lambertus Hesselink, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.lambertus-hesselink.about.2"
msgid "His research covered ultra high density optical data storage, nonlinear optics and nanophotonics. He founded Siros Technologies in 1997 and cofounded Senvid in 1999. The National Academy of Inventors made him a fellow in 2022."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About John Ralston, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 1. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.john-ralston.about.1"
msgid "In 1996 he worked at SDL, a laser maker in San Jose, California, which supplied the small high power lasers used to draw points inside the glass. He was one of the four authors of the paper in Science."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Roger Macfarlane, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.roger-macfarlane.about.1"
msgid "A physicist at the IBM Almaden Research Center, he studied how atoms and molecules behave in solids, with a view to storing data at very high density."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Roger Macfarlane, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.roger-macfarlane.about.2"
msgid "Much of his work was laser spectroscopy of rare earth ions in crystals, including spectral hole burning. He was still publishing on rare earth doped materials in 2013."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniel Smalley, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniel-smalley.about.1"
msgid "He studied at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, with a degree in electrical engineering in 2005 and a doctorate in holography in 2013. His thesis described a new integrated optics platform, and at MIT he worked on a low cost holographic video monitor."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Daniel Smalley, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.daniel-smalley.about.2"
msgid "At Brigham Young University he became professor of electrical and computer engineering. In the display, lasers trapped and heated a single cellulose particle and steered it through the air while red, green and blue light lit it."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ryuji Hirayama, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ryuji-hirayama.about.1"
msgid "He studied engineering at Chiba University in Japan and took his doctorate there in 2017, with a thesis on volumetric displays that hold several flat images. He then held research fellowships from the Japan Society for the Promotion of Science."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ryuji Hirayama, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ryuji-hirayama.about.2"
msgid "One of those fellowships took him in 2018 to the laboratory of Nobuyuki Masuda at Tokyo University of Science. In 2019 he joined the Interact Lab at Sussex as a Rutherford Fellow, and he is the lead author of the paper in Nature."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ryuji Hirayama, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ryuji-hirayama.about.3"
msgid "He moved to the computer science department of University College London in 2020, became a lecturer in 2022 and an associate professor in 2026. He leads FabDisp, a project on acoustic holography for display and fabrication, and cofounded the company AcoustoFab in 2023."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Diego Martinez Plasencia, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.diego-martinez-plasencia.about.1"
msgid "He taught as an assistant lecturer at UCLM in Spain and was a research associate at the University of Bristol before becoming a lecturer in interactive 3D graphics at Sussex. He later moved to University College London as an associate professor."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Diego Martinez Plasencia, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.diego-martinez-plasencia.about.2"
msgid "At UCL he cofounded the Multi-Sensory Devices Group with Marianna Obrist and Sriram Subramanian. His work joins 3D display methods, human computer interaction and applied physics, from tabletop systems with several views to displays made of moving particles."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Nobuyuki Masuda, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.nobuyuki-masuda.about.1"
msgid "He was a professor of applied electronics at Tokyo University of Science, where his laboratory designed special purpose computing circuits on programmable chips (FPGAs) and used graphics processors to speed up numerical work, much of it for computer generated holograms."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Nobuyuki Masuda, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.nobuyuki-masuda.about.2"
msgid "From 2018 to 2021 he led a project that built a dedicated FPGA computer for digital holography, which his team reported to be faster than a PC. Ryuji Hirayama worked in his laboratory as a research fellow in 2018 before going to Sussex."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Sriram Subramanian, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.sriram-subramanian.about.1"
msgid "When the display was published he was Professor of Informatics at Sussex and held a Royal Academy of Engineering Chair in Emerging Technologies. The university named him as leader of the project."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Sriram Subramanian, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.sriram-subramanian.about.2"
msgid "He moved to University College London, where he leads the Multi-Sensory Devices lab, working on touch in mid air, levitation and contactless control by ultrasound. The company Ultrahaptics, later Ultraleap, grew from his early hardware designs, and he founded Metasonixx and cofounded AcoustoFab."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Sriram Subramanian, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.sriram-subramanian.about.3"
msgid "In 2025 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Academy of Engineering."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Yoichi Ochiai, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.yoichi-ochiai.about.1"
msgid "He studied information media at the University of Tsukuba and took a doctorate in applied computer science at the University of Tokyo in 2015. He then joined Tsukuba's Institute of Library, Information and Media Science, as assistant professor and from 2017 associate professor."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Yoichi Ochiai, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.yoichi-ochiai.about.2"
msgid "The femtosecond display, called Fairy Lights in Femtoseconds, was shown at SIGGRAPH 2015 with colleagues from Utsunomiya University, the Nagoya Institute of Technology, the University of Tokyo and Sony CSL. The team argued that plasma from femtosecond pulses was safer than from nanosecond pulses."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Yoichi Ochiai, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.yoichi-ochiai.about.3"
msgid "He was also chief executive of Pixie Dust Technologies from 2015 and director of Tsukuba's Research and Development Center for Digital Nature from 2020. MIT Technology Review listed him among its Innovators Under 35 for Japan in 2020."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Wheatstone, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-wheatstone.about.1"
msgid "From near Gloucester, he was apprenticed to his uncle's firm of musical instrument makers in London. In 1834 King's College London made him Professor of Experimental Philosophy."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Wheatstone, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-wheatstone.about.2"
msgid "His paper on the stereoscope, Contributions to the Physiology of Vision, appeared in the Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society. He drew simple line figures to view in it and, in 1840, asked William Henry Fox Talbot for stereoscopic photographs."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Wheatstone, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-wheatstone.about.3"
msgid "With William Fothergill Cooke he worked on the electric telegraph, which ran on the Great Western Railway from 1838. He also gave his name to the Wheatstone Bridge for measuring electrical resistance, and was knighted in 1868."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenneth Dunkley, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenneth-dunkley.about.1"
msgid "He took a master's degree in physics and became president of Holospace Laboratories in Camp Hill, Pennsylvania. For several years he ran visual effects workshops at the Museum of Scientific Discovery in Harrisburg, and worked on holography, lasers and electro optics."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Kenneth Dunkley, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 2. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.kenneth-dunkley.about.2"
msgid "The National Business League says the idea came while he was demonstrating holography at the Franklin Institute, when tubes with pinholes gave a flat photograph depth. He filed the patent in April 1986. Its pinholes and frames can be moved apart or together."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Harrison, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-harrison.about.1"
msgid "Born in Shreveport, Louisiana, he took a degree in industrial design at the School of the Art Institute of Chicago in 1954, served in the Army as a mapmaker in Germany, and later earned a master's in art education at night."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Harrison, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-harrison.about.2"
msgid "He took the View-Master job as a freelance designer through Robert Podall Associates, for the maker Sawyer's. Moulding the viewer in thermoplastic rather than Bakelite, he said, let units be made ten times faster. He recalled the work taking under two weeks."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Charles Harrison, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.charles-harrison.about.3"
msgid "Sears, Roebuck and Company hired him as a staff designer in 1961, and he rose to chief of design, with over 750 products. After retiring in 1993 he taught in Chicago. He won the National Design Award for Lifetime Achievement in 2008."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Frederic Ives, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.frederic-ives.about.1"
msgid "Born in Litchfield, Connecticut, he was apprenticed as a boy to a printer and, with no formal schooling, was put in charge of the photographic laboratory at Cornell University while still a teenager."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Frederic Ives, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.frederic-ives.about.2"
msgid "There he worked out a halftone process for printing photographs, in commercial use by 1881, and a crossline screen. He later made the Kromskop, a viewer that gave colour stereo pictures from three pairs of filtered negatives. He held about seventy patents."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Frederic Ives, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.frederic-ives.about.3"
msgid "Living in Philadelphia, he filed his patent for the parallax stereogram in September 1902 and was granted it in April 1903. A screen of opaque lines with clear spaces, set at a calculated distance, lets each eye see only its own picture."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Walter Hess, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.walter-hess.about.1"
msgid "Born in Frauenfeld, Switzerland, he studied medicine at Lausanne, Berlin, Kiel and Zurich and qualified in 1906. He trained in ophthalmology for two years under Otto Haab in Zurich before opening his practice."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Walter Hess, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.walter-hess.about.2"
msgid "His patent prints two stereoscopic negatives in strips behind a ribbed celluloid plate, each rib part of a cylinder, so no stereoscope or spectacles are needed. C. W. Hess writes that his plans to sell it came to nothing when war broke out in 1914."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Walter Hess, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.walter-hess.about.3"
msgid "In 1912 he joined the Physiological Institute of the University of Zurich, and in 1917 became its professor and head, until 1951. He shared the Nobel Prize with Egas Moniz, for work on the diencephalon as a centre coordinating the internal organs."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hank Willis Thomas, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hank-willis-thomas.about.1"
msgid "He took a degree in fine art at New York University in 1998 and a master's at California College of the Arts in 2004. He began as a photographer and went on to make sculpture and installations."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hank Willis Thomas, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hank-willis-thomas.about.2"
msgid "His work turns on perception, context and the reuse of brands, logos and familiar images. He has also printed portraits of activists on coloured retroreflective vinyl."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Hank Willis Thomas, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.hank-willis-thomas.about.3"
msgid "Two of his lenticular prints of 2017, They are Us, Us is Them and Impossibly, hang in Harvey Milk Terminal 1 at San Francisco International Airport. In the second, the words possible, possibly and impossible take turns as the viewer moves."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dennis Gabor, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dennis-gabor.about.1"
msgid "Born in Budapest, he studied electrical engineering in Berlin, taking a doctorate in 1927, and then worked in research at Siemens and Halske. In 1934 he joined the British Thomson-Houston Company at Rugby."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dennis Gabor, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dennis-gabor.about.2"
msgid "In his Nobel lecture he said the aim was a better electron microscope, sharp enough to resolve atomic lattices. His early optical trials used a mercury lamp, whose short coherence kept objects small. The method came back into use with the laser after 1963."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Dennis Gabor, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.dennis-gabor.about.3"
msgid "From 1949 to 1967 he was at Imperial College London, as reader in electronics and then professor of applied electron physics. The Nobel Prize in Physics of 1971 credits him with the invention and development of the holographic method."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Emmett Leith, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.emmett-leith.about.1"
msgid "Born in Detroit, he studied physics at Wayne State University. In 1952 he joined the Radar Laboratory of the University of Michigan's Willow Run Laboratories, where he worked on synthetic aperture radar."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Emmett Leith, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.emmett-leith.about.2"
msgid "He brought Juris Upatnieks onto the holography work in 1960. They called their method lensless photography, and showed a hologram of a toy train at a meeting of the Optical Society of America in 1964."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Emmett Leith, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.emmett-leith.about.3"
msgid "He became a full professor at Michigan in 1968 and received the National Medal of Science in 1979. The Optical Society of America named a medal after him in 2006."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Juris Upatnieks, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.juris-upatnieks.about.1"
msgid "Born in Riga, he left Latvia in 1944 and reached the United States in 1951. He took a degree in electrical engineering at the University of Akron in 1960."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Juris Upatnieks, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.juris-upatnieks.about.2"
msgid "Emmett Leith recruited him that year to Willow Run Laboratories at the University of Michigan. The two patented their holography work jointly as Wavefront Reconstruction Using a Coherent Reference Beam."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Juris Upatnieks, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.juris-upatnieks.about.3"
msgid "From 1973 to 1993 he worked at the University of Michigan, at the Environmental Research Institute and then in electrical and computer engineering. He held 19 United States patents and received the Emmett N. Leith Medal in 2010."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Margaret Benyon, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.margaret-benyon.about.1"
msgid "Her stated aim was to take holography out of the science laboratory and widen what counted as fine art. She lived in Australia in the 1970s, and after returning to Britain in 1980 her holograms dealt with the human body."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Margaret Benyon, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.margaret-benyon.about.2"
msgid "Tigirl, of 1985, set a holographic self portrait against a photograph of a tiger. In 1994 the Royal College of Art in London awarded her a doctorate for her research and practice in art holography."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Margaret Benyon, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.margaret-benyon.about.3"
msgid "She was appointed MBE in 2000 for services to art. Her work is held by the Victoria and Albert Museum and the Australian National Gallery. From 2005 she was a visiting fellow at the University of New South Wales."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Stephen Benton, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.stephen-benton.about.1"
msgid "Born in San Francisco, he studied electrical engineering at MIT, working with Harold Edgerton, and as a student worked in Edwin Land's vision research laboratory at Polaroid. He took his doctorate at Harvard in 1968."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Stephen Benton, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.stephen-benton.about.2"
msgid "MIT News credits him with inventing rainbow holography, which lets a hologram be seen in ordinary white light."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Stephen Benton, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.stephen-benton.about.3"
msgid "He returned to MIT in 1980, founded its Spatial Imaging Group in 1982 and was on the founding faculty of the Media Lab in 1984. He later directed the Center for Advanced Visual Studies."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mary Lou Jepsen, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mary-lou-jepsen.about.1"
msgid "She studied electrical engineering and took a doctorate in optical physics at Brown University. Her master's degree at the MIT Media Lab was in computational holography."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mary Lou Jepsen, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mary-lou-jepsen.about.2"
msgid "She was chief technology officer and chief architect of the One Laptop per Child laptop, and chief technology officer of the display division at Intel. She also ran a display company based in Taipei."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Mary Lou Jepsen, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.mary-lou-jepsen.about.3"
msgid "She directed engineering at Google X and at Facebook, where her work included virtual reality at Oculus. She then founded Openwater, which aims to put imaging of MRI quality into a wearable device."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ali Javan, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ali-javan.about.1"
msgid "He came to the United States in 1949 and took his doctorate in physics at Columbia University in 1954, under Charles Townes, without a bachelor's or master's degree."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ali Javan, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ali-javan.about.2"
msgid "He worked out the principle of the helium and neon laser in 1958. The day after it gave its beam in December 1960, the laser carried a telephone conversation."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ali Javan, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ali-javan.about.3"
msgid "He joined the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1961 and held the Francis Wright Davis chair of physics from 1978 to 1996. He was inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame in 2006."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Nasser Peyghambarian, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.nasser-peyghambarian.about.1"
msgid "He took his doctorate in solid state physics at Indiana University in 1982 and came to the Optical Sciences Center at the University of Arizona as a postdoctoral fellow."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Nasser Peyghambarian, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.nasser-peyghambarian.about.2"
msgid "A professor of optical sciences and of materials science and engineering, he directed a National Science Foundation research centre for integrated access networks and founded two companies, NP Photonics and TIPD."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Nasser Peyghambarian, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.nasser-peyghambarian.about.3"
msgid "He led the team behind the 2010 telepresence display, a 17 inch screen fed by sixteen cameras, and said it could help in surgery. He was named a fellow of the National Academy of Inventors in 2016."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Pierre-Alexandre Blanche, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.pierre-alexandre-blanche.about.1"
msgid "At the University of Arizona's optical sciences college he worked in diffractive optics and holography. He was lead author of the 2010 report on holographic telepresence from the group."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Pierre-Alexandre Blanche, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.pierre-alexandre-blanche.about.2"
msgid "In that system one computer turned the views of sixteen cameras into hogel data and sent it over Ethernet to a second computer, which wrote the holograms onto the polymer screen."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Pierre-Alexandre Blanche, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.pierre-alexandre-blanche.about.3"
msgid "In 2021 he won an Optimax grant toward a curved head-up display built on a waveguide. Optica made him a fellow, citing holographic 3D displays and augmented reality combiners."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ivan Sutherland, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ivan-sutherland.about.1"
msgid "Born in Hastings, Nebraska, he studied at the Carnegie Institute of Technology and Caltech and took his doctorate at MIT in 1963 under Claude Shannon, with a thesis on Sketchpad, a graphical system for drawing with a computer."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ivan Sutherland, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ivan-sutherland.about.2"
msgid "In 1964 he succeeded J. C. R. Licklider as head of the Information Processing Techniques Office at ARPA. The 1968 headset was nicknamed the Sword of Damocles."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Ivan Sutherland, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.ivan-sutherland.about.3"
msgid "He taught at the University of Utah from 1968 to 1974 and cofounded Evans and Sutherland with David Evans. Sun Microsystems bought his firm with Bob Sproull in 1990. He received the Turing Award in 1988."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Bob Sproull, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.bob-sproull.about.1"
msgid "He worked at the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center and was an associate professor of computer science at Carnegie Mellon University, then a principal of Sutherland, Sproull and Associates."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Bob Sproull, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.bob-sproull.about.2"
msgid "He joined Sun Microsystems in 1990 as a vice president and fellow of Sun Microsystems Laboratories, and retired as director of Oracle Labs, which grew out of it."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Bob Sproull, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.bob-sproull.about.3"
msgid "With William Newman he wrote the textbook Principles of Interactive Computer Graphics. His research ran from graphics hardware and page description languages to asynchronous circuits. He was elected to the National Academy of Engineering."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Samuel Achilefu, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.samuel-achilefu.about.1"
msgid "He took a doctorate in molecular and materials chemistry at the University of Nancy and trained at Oxford, then joined the discovery research department of Mallinckrodt Medical in St. Louis in 1993."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Samuel Achilefu, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.samuel-achilefu.about.2"
msgid "At Washington University he was professor of radiology and of biomedical engineering. The goggles were made with Viktor Gruev of the engineering school and Ron Liang of the University of Arizona, with funding from the National Cancer Institute."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About Samuel Achilefu, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.samuel-achilefu.about.3"
msgid "In 2016 he was installed as Michel M. Ter-Pogossian Professor of Radiology. In February 2022 he moved to UT Southwestern Medical Center to chair its department of biomedical engineering."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. M. Naish, on that person's own page: paragraph 1 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-m-naish.about.1"
msgid "He worked at the Royal Aircraft Establishment, Farnborough. A technical note of 1962 set out the form of the steering information his head-up display gave the pilot, and weighed how bright it must be against a desert sky."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. M. Naish, on that person's own page: paragraph 2 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-m-naish.about.2"
msgid "In a paper of 1965 he reported that the display could be learned easily and used accurately, and proposed it as a monitor for automatic landing and take-off."
msgstr ""

#: people
#. About J. M. Naish, on that person's own page: paragraph 3 of 3. Plain statements in the past tense, without praise. Keep the names of people, firms and institutions as they are, and the years as numerals.
msgctxt "person.j-m-naish.about.3"
msgid "By 1979 he was at NASA Ames Research Center as a research associate of the National Research Council. His review of head-up display formats there found neither of the two main kinds fully adequate."
msgstr ""
